window.PHILIS_SEARCH = [{"title":"Audit · What was audited","text":"What was audited Four reviewers divided the supplied sources and checked the handbook’s technical claims, source locations, model assumptions, numerical examples and diagram meaning. The review covered the 30 teaching chapters and 13 source companions, plus the theory in the constraint catalog and glossary. Pre-existing implementation observations and the engine roadmap’s code claims were excluded; no Philis source-code correctness is certified. The frozen pre-correction inventory contains 553 in-article PDF-link occurrences . Every occurrence received a disposition. This includes reading maps and navigation, not just theory assertions, and excludes 13 source-header links. Added pinpoint links and the 115 reusable provenance records were checked during correction. Counts below refer to the original inventory so new links cannot inflate the apparent audit coverage. Auditing a cited assertion is different from reading every line of every book. Books were revisited at the passages supporting the notes; the source companions retain their original reading-depth limits. Broad chapter surveys are qualified or navigation. No source-paper experiments, silicon measurements, foundry qualification or global layout optimality proof were reproduced.","url":"audit.html#scope"},{"title":"Audit · Citation review results","text":"Citation review results Status Occurrences Meaning supported 206 The stated source mechanism/result is supported within its scope. qualified 262 Use requires the stated assumptions, narrower claim or explicit derived/proposed attribution. corrected 44 The notes or locator required a correction, now applied. not_claim 41 Source inventory, reading map or navigation; not certified theory. unresolved 0 A source-supported technical assertion remains unresolved. not_reviewed 0 No semantic disposition was completed. The occurrence-by-occurrence ledger preserves original claims/locations, the disposition, supporting pages and explanation. A supported citation is not a blanket endorsement of every surrounding paragraph. Independent derivations and engine policies require their own attribution. Source Supported Qualified Corrected Navigation An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation 3 4 0 1 Analog Design Centering and Sizing 7 32 2 1 Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability 3 32 3 1 Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches 1 56 3 1 Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead 11 7 2 1 Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs 11 14 1 1 Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design 34 12 3 6 Matching properties of MOS transistors 7 6 0 2 Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions 6 8 0 1 Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design 28 4 9 14 The Art of Analog Layout 71 69 19 9 Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design 9 12 0 2 Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits 15 6 2 1","url":"audit.html#results"},{"title":"Audit · Corrections that matter for implementation","text":"Corrections that matter for implementation Removed a false source-error claim. The Lampaert penalty equation had been called a printing error. Its rendered Eq. (4.11) is correct; the audit removed that allegation and distinguishes the source formula from alternative penalty policies. Narrowed incorrect or incomplete references. Examples include the CC review’s MOM structures (Fig. 5, PDF 4), Hastings LOD illustration (Fig. 13.52, PDF 698), substrate buried shields/dual rings, and the actual topological metaverification and route-integration sections. Corrected classifier interpretation. WAGN’s single-threshold soft classification score is neither a full performance distribution nor evidence of calibrated manufacturing yield. The source definition and training equations now accompany that distinction. Exposed simplified-model assumptions. Single-branch source degeneration, weighted/ratio moments, RC matrix stamps, charge sharing and toy wire-width optima now identify the derivation and its domain rather than implying the entire result was printed in a cited paper. Corrected physical qualifications. Dummy validity depends on qualified geometry/bias; current density is cross-sectional; transient tree-current sums include shunt/displacement loads; net terminal energy flow is not always dissipated heat. Checked apparent source inconsistencies against images and algebra. Hastings Appendix E intermediate signs/prefactors/root, the EM via-temperature multiplier and two centering equations retain explicit qualified corrections. Appendix E also distinguishes total-resistance approximation error from metal-only error. These are apparent internal inconsistencies, not publisher-confirmed errata. Kept distinct metrics distinct. The DAC paper’s N-dependent settling-frequency model is not the ordinary RC −3 dB pole. Runtime percentages/denominators and reported benchmark tradeoffs are scoped to the actual table or figure. Added reusable provenance. Each of the 84 proposed constraint families now links to the mechanisms or original policies supporting it. References identify exact source files, sections/equations/figures and assumptions; they do not turn the proposed catalog into a textbook’s own numbered rule system. Detailed corrections and page reviews: physical-layout textbook · optimization books · physics/reliability sources · matching and performance papers . Independent Appendix E check preserves the boundary-value algebra.","url":"audit.html#corrections"},{"title":"Audit · What remains unverified or incomplete","text":"What remains unverified or incomplete Foundational noise citations: no pinpoint Johnson–Nyquist 4kBTR or sampled kT/C passage was found in the supplied PDFs. The glossary flags the former as a background law; the DAC chapter shows the latter’s conditional equipartition derivation. See D07 and D08 . Do not attribute these formulas to the DAC/layout papers. Production values and validity: actual process limits, mismatch/correlation coefficients, extraction stacks, device qualification, package/thermal models and reliability rules still come from the target PDK and characterization. Exhaustiveness: specialty RF/high-voltage/JFET/memory topics remain surveys of layout implications. The supplied corpus and this audit do not provide a complete qualified generator for every device or cover every modern process effect. Source authority: book/paper examples are evidence under their assumptions. References cited inside a supplied work are not automatically independently reviewed sources. Apparent errors have not been checked against publisher errata. Implementation and experiments: proposed algorithms, schemas, tests and constraint policies are engineering synthesis. Their implementation correctness and measured benefit have not been established by this notes-only audit. There are no original citation occurrences left without an audit disposition. That completeness statement concerns the ledger, not an absolute correctness guarantee for the handbook.","url":"audit.html#limits"},{"title":"Audit · How to use the audited notes","text":"How to use the audited notes Start with the theory records and comment templates . Select the narrow claim matching the implementation, retain its assumptions, and add the design/PDK source for numerical limits. If a record is marked derived or proposed, preserve that wording. Then use the teaching chapter for the causal explanation, tradeoffs and validation cases. Semantic audit data: summary · original citation ledger · 115 reusable records and 84 mappings . Artifact integrity is a separate check: HTML/link/SVG/example validation . A structurally valid link does not by itself establish that a claim is true.","url":"audit.html#use"},{"title":"Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead · What this source establishes","text":"What this source establishes Karmokar and coauthors survey capacitor and transistor common-centroid (CC) methods, then examine cases where clustered or interdigitated patterns perform better. Their argument is physical: CC removes a particular systematic error term, while potentially introducing resistance imbalance and layout-dependent device shifts. The review is evidence for a conditional pattern choice , not for either a universal CC rule or a universal rejection of CC. Introduction, PDF p. 1 §V, PDF pp. 6–7 Engine action and reason. Generate feasible clustered, interdigitated, and CC alternatives for matched structures. Evaluate the extracted circuit and the relevant variation scenarios for each. Retaining alternatives matters because a placement constraint cannot predict which error dominates every device size, bias point, or process.","url":"cc-review.html#result"},{"title":"Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead · The cancellation argument, including its assumptions","text":"The cancellation argument, including its assumptions Let an equal unit of device A or B sample a parameter field p(x,y)=p₀+gₓx+gᵧy . The mean parameter difference is gₓ(x̄A−x̄B)+gᵧ(ȳA−ȳB) . Coincident centroids remove this term for every gradient direction. To translate parameter matching into output matching, the review additionally linearizes performance as ΔP≈(∂P/∂p)Δp . These are two distinct approximations: spatial variation and circuit response. Equations (1)–(5), PDF p. 1 Why the engine needs unit metadata. A macro's center is not the centroid of its electrically contributing units. Store each unit's active region, electrical weight, parent device, orientation, and connection. For unequal units, use weights derived from the quantity being matched; metal padding or a large guard-ring box is not electrical weight. For a ratioed capacitor bank, normalize each member's sum by its nominal capacitance before comparing moments. Merely aligning the centers of the whole A and B boxes does not prove the required cancellation. Scope. Zero first moments do not imply zero quadratic moments. Equal moments of threshold voltage need not imply equal drain current if units experience different bias or sensitivities. A geometrically symmetric arrangement need not match well-edge distance. Conversely, the defining centroid equations alone do not require every possible mirror symmetry: distinguish the algebraic condition from a particular symmetric pattern family. Check. Apply synthetic x and y gradients directly to the unit devices and sum their modeled contributions. A valid CC pattern must reject both linear modes. Test after rotation, routing, dummy insertion, and device-to-cell collapse so later transformations cannot silently invalidate the property.","url":"cc-review.html#centroid"},{"title":"Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead · Random variation requires covariance, not a dispersion slogan","text":"Random variation requires covariance, not a dispersion slogan The review separates global, local spatially correlated, and local uncorrelated variation. Its correlation models and mismatch coefficients are process parameters. Layout can change correlated differential error; rearranging identical independent random units at fixed total active area does not make their intrinsic randomness disappear. Global variation cancels in a difference only to the extent that the compared devices and circuit sensitivities respond alike. §II, PDF pp. 2–3 Proposed general implementation: e = dᵀδp Var(e) = dᵀΣd d = normalized electrical contribution of A minus that of B; Σᵢⱼ = covariance of the corresponding unit parameters. This is our covariance-algebra reformulation of the review's sums: for zero-mean perturbations, E[(dᵀδp)²]=dᵀE[δpδpᵀ]d=dᵀΣd. It makes the reason for dispersion measurable. Mixing members through a shared field can increase covariance between their aggregate values and reduce differential error, but dispersion can also lengthen wires. A process correlation model must say what distance unit it uses and what happens at zero separation. Covariance matrices must remain positive semidefinite; otherwise apparently excellent mismatch numbers may come from an invalid statistical model. Engine proposal. Keep independent mismatch, correlated variation, deterministic gradients, and model uncertainty as separate report entries. Sweep uncertain correlation lengths instead of adopting the review's illustrative long or short correlation length as a foundry fact. Compare layouts using the same random field samples so simulation noise does not masquerade as improvement. Counterexample. Splitting a fixed active area into twice as many independent equal subdevices doubles each subdevice's variance under an inverse-area law, but averaging twice as many leaves the aggregate variance unchanged. The benefit of splitting must come from systematic cancellation, environment balance, routing, or a changed total area—not the count alone.","url":"cc-review.html#variation"},{"title":"Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead · Match the environment that changes device behavior","text":"Match the environment that changes device behavior Effect in the review Reason and required action Evidence to require Well proximity effect (WPE) Scattering near well-mask edges changes channel doping. Match the relevant edge distances or move both units beyond the characterized influence region. Device-model WPE parameters and the actual well geometry, including corners; a shared well name is insufficient. Length of diffusion (LOD) Stress depends on gate-to-active-edge distances SA/SB. Inner B units in ABBA can see a different environment from outer A units despite a perfect centroid. Per-unit SA/SB, finger context, permitted dummies, and extracted/model-evaluated shifts. Oxide-definition spacing/width Active-area dimensions and neighboring gaps alter stress. Reusing a unit footprint helps only if its surroundings also remain consistent. Neighbor distances and OD width after assembling the complete array. Gate pitch Pitch changes can change the stressed volume around the gate. Keep pitch uniform where the characterized device family requires it. Pitch legality and surrounding gate/dummy pattern, not merely matching W/L. §II-A2 and Fig. 2, PDF p. 2 The review's statement that identical units remove several LDEs depends on its row-based, uniform-pitch, diffusion-sharing construction. Philis must verify those premises before reusing that conclusion. A dummy whose geometry, layer or bias differs from the qualified construction does not establish the required boundary environment. Some qualified dummy structures are intentionally floating. Validation. Build ABBA and two-row arrangements with the same total W/L and nominal current. First disable gradients, then enable only LDE models. This isolates whether the layout itself causes a systematic offset. Add gradients afterward to reveal the actual tradeoff.","url":"cc-review.html#lde"},{"title":"Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead · Electrical matching includes the route graph","text":"Electrical matching includes the route graph For transistor arrays, diffusion sharing, source connection resistance, and gate/drain access constrain viable CC patterns. The review describes half-diffusion graphs and Euler paths for constructing diffusion-sharing arrays, then routing that accounts for electromigration and IR drop. These methods provide useful internal macro representations, but capacitor placement algorithms cannot simply be applied to MOS arrays: their adjacency constraints are different. §IV, PDF pp. 5–6 A source lead carrying small-signal current creates local source degeneration. For a simple single-branch model, gₘ,eff≈gₘ/(1+gₘRₛ) . This closed form is our derivation from i=gₘ(vin−iRₛ) , neglecting output conductance and body effect and assuming the branch current is the source-resistor current. The review’s Eq. (25) relates effective transconductance to local source voltage; its array analysis uses the actual unit-current distribution. Wider wires or a different connection tree can improve transconductance, while unequal resistance can create offset. Equal total drawn length does not guarantee equal terminal impedance because layers, vias, branch loading, and neighbors differ. Equation (25) and Fig. 9, PDF pp. 6–7 Engine action. Compare corresponding terminal-to-terminal resistance and capacitance, including internal macro wiring. Count vias by type and track per-layer lengths as intermediate diagnostics. Use the extracted multiport circuit for acceptance. This is justified because a low-resistance trunk cannot repair an asymmetric local source connection hidden inside the macro. Tradeoff. A detour that matches wire lengths may increase both source resistance and victim coupling. In some FinFET layers, a bend also requires a layer transition and a high-resistance via. Allow electrical matching to choose a different layer, contact pattern, topology, or device pattern instead of adding length reflexively.","url":"cc-review.html#routing"},{"title":"Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead · Capacitor construction and routing must be designed together","text":"Capacitor construction and routing must be designed together The review compares MOM finger, sandwich, pillar, and mortise-tenon structures, their density, and their ability to suppress unwanted top-plate coupling. Its preference for MOM in the discussed advanced-node setting is not a universal replacement rule for an available, characterized MIM device. Unit structure determines which parasitics are exposed to the router. §III-B and Fig. 5, PDF p. 4 For a charge-scaling DAC, preserve the distinction between intended capacitance, top-to-substrate capacitance, top-to-bottom coupling, and bottom-plate loading. They affect gain, code-dependent error, power, and settling differently. Two arrays with the same total parasitic capacitance can therefore have very different INL/DNL. Action and reason. Parameterize unit capacitance, array aspect, dispersion, route topology, and shield choices jointly. Better parasitic matching may allow smaller unit capacitors, reducing area and switching energy, but the smaller units then have a stricter relative random mismatch requirement. The unit size must satisfy both statistical and extracted circuit constraints. The paper surveys ILP, pair-sequence search, chessboard construction, and routing-aware stochastic approaches. Retain exclusivity, exact unit ratios, channel capacity, and connectivity as explicit invariants. A visually regular pattern with no legal route is not a usable placement. §III-C, PDF pp. 4–5 See the 2023 DAC paper for a concrete constructive alternative.","url":"cc-review.html#passives"},{"title":"Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead · A decision procedure for Philis","text":"A decision procedure for Philis Identify what must match. Record ratio, circuit mode, terminals, performance metric, and allowable error. Reason: two equal-sized transistors may serve different electrical roles. Generate pattern candidates with valid connectivity. Include clustered, ID, CC, and multirow CC where legal. Reason: the best choice depends on the dominant error mechanism. Reject illegal or environmentally unsupported candidates. Enforce unit count, geometry, allowed orientation, wells, and routability. Reason: a mismatch estimate on unrealizable geometry is meaningless. Evaluate deterministic LDE and routed parasitics before random variation. Reason: Monte Carlo around an already shifted mean can conceal a fixable systematic error. Evaluate gradients and covariance under the stated process model. Reason: compact clustered layouts may perform well under independent variation yet degrade under short-range correlated variation. Keep feasible nondominated alternatives. Reason: one pattern may have lower offset while another has better bandwidth or area. Select according to the circuit's actual specifications. The review's OTA example combines CC for larger structures with a clustered small mirror; its comparator example exposes how small structures can suffer from CC parasitic/LDE mismatch. Those examples establish the existence of the tradeoff, not a size threshold transferable to SKY130. §V-B, PDF p. 7","url":"cc-review.html#decision"},{"title":"Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead · Exact connections to the current code","text":"Exact connections to the current code CentroidGroup::centroid uses layout bounding-box areas and macro centers, and retarget replaces members with collapsed cell IDs. Preserve local unit coordinates and electrical weights so merged groups do not appear matched merely because their representatives coincide. The current placement registers MatchingPair and CentroidGroup in the cost arm. These are guidance, not a completed mismatch feasibility test. enumerate already filters merged macros whose shared pads or gate straps would short different nets. Build on that legality filtering. Report why a candidate was rejected and retain its pattern identity; otherwise an intended CC structure can silently become separate devices and the later score loses the reason. Differential checks relative total length and a deduplicated layer set. Extend the electrical evaluator rather than describing this existing predicate as RC matching. The required test is two routes with equal lengths and identical layer sets but different via counts or layer-length allocation: the electrical check should distinguish them.","url":"cc-review.html#engine"},{"title":"Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead · Reading coverage and limits","text":"Reading coverage and limits Read PDF pp. 1–8: centroid derivation; variation and LDE models; capacitor unit structures and layout algorithms; transistor diffusion-sharing algorithms; non-CC circuit case studies; conclusions and reference list. Equations with damaged extraction were reconstructed only where their meaning was unambiguous; no process constants were imported. The review's cited papers are secondary evidence unless independently supplied elsewhere in this library. The proposals above are Philis adaptations, not implementations claimed by the authors.","url":"cc-review.html#coverage"},{"title":"Analog Design Centering and Sizing · What this book changes about the meaning of a good layout","text":"What this book changes about the meaning of a good layout Helmut E. Graeb, Analog Design Centering and Sizing (2007), supplies the statistical and optimization foundation needed to turn a geometrically attractive placement into an electrically robust design. Its central distinction is between meeting specifications at one nominal point, surviving a prescribed uncertainty region, and maximizing the probability that manufactured circuits satisfy all specifications under all required operating conditions. Those are different objectives. A small, symmetric layout can perform worse on the last two even when its nominal simulation is better. Source finding: design centering means yield optimization in parameter space. It is unrelated to putting devices at the geometric center of a floorplan. Common-centroid placement can improve one physical contributor to yield, but is not itself design centering. The book makes the distinction even between centering performance values, inscribing the largest tolerance ellipsoid, and maximizing actual probability mass. PDF pp. 89–99 , pp. 164–166 , p. 179 . Proposed engine consequence: store nominal performance, robust margins, statistical assumptions, and estimation confidence separately. An optimizer may use a cheap robustness proxy, but its report should name the proxy rather than relabel it yield. The reason is practical: the user must know whether a better score means less wire, larger simulated phase margin, a larger modeled failure distance, or measured improvement in joint passing probability.","url":"centering.html#central-lesson"},{"title":"Analog Design Centering and Sizing · Reading coverage and chapter map","text":"Reading coverage and chapter map The supplied 210-page PDF was extracted completely. These notes are based on substantive reading across all seven chapters and the three appendices, with close reading of parameter classification, covariance transformations, sensitivity scaling, worst-case derivations, yield estimators, tolerance classes, and geometric optimization. Equations on PDF pages 111, 115, 130, and 176 were also checked in rendered page images. Bibliography entries were not independently retrieved; exercises and every historical reference were not reproduced. Reading and mathematical checking are not a reproduction of the book's circuit experiments. Chapter; PDF pages Relevant contribution Use in Philis 1. Introduction; 21–37 Top-down design, bottom-up models, nominal and tolerance design Require parasitic feedback before declaring electrical completion. 2. Introductory example; 38–45 RC example distinguishes several notions of centering Teach why centering a specification interval is insufficient. 3. Problem formulation; 46–67 Design, statistical and range parameters; distributions and transformations Typed uncertainty schema and reproducible sampling. 4. Analog sizing tasks; 68–102 Sensitivity, Pareto objectives, worst-case and yield tasks Choose objectives explicitly; normalize physical units. 5. Worst-case analysis; 103–124 Box, ellipsoid and nonlinear worst cases Cheap analytic estimates with explicit validity domains. 6. Yield analysis; 125–161 Monte Carlo, importance sampling, tolerance classes, failure distances Separate statistically estimated yield from local geometric approximations. 7. Yield optimization/design centering; 162–180 Statistical derivatives, geometric gradients, trust regions and min-max optimization Robust outer-loop sizing and selective expensive re-evaluation. Appendices A–C; 181–197 Moments, estimator uncertainty, constrained optimality Correct covariance propagation and distinguish local stationarity from global optimality.","url":"centering.html#coverage"},{"title":"Analog Design Centering and Sizing · Three parameter classes, three different responsibilities","text":"Three parameter classes, three different responsibilities The book writes performance as f(xd, xs, xr) . Design parameters xd are quantities the designer controls: device dimensions, bias choices and, in a layout extension, legal placement coordinates, fingers, topology, widths and contacts. Statistical parameters xs represent manufacturing variation and have a probability model. Range parameters xr represent operating conditions such as supply and temperature; their specified ranges must be covered, and a probability distribution need not exist. Treating range parameters as convenient random variables silently changes the specification. PDF pp. 46–50 . Action and reason: define a scenario as a combination of an operating condition and a process/mismatch realization, with separate provenance for each. This prevents a Monte Carlo run dominated by room-temperature samples from claiming compliance over a full temperature range. Discrete PDK corner names should remain named model scenarios; they are not automatically independent Gaussian dimensions and do not by themselves define a yield distribution. The introductory circuit uses normalized parameters with time constant τ = RC and illustrative area A = R + C . Centering the allowed time-constant interval [0.5, 2] gives τ = 1.25; minimum illustrative area then gives R = C = √1.25. Centering the feasible segment along R = C instead gives R = C = (√0.5 + √2)/2 and τ = 1.125. Unequal R and C standard deviations change the yield optimum again. This is a pedagogical normalized model, not a physical claim that resistance and capacitance can be added to obtain silicon area. PDF pp. 38–44 . Validation: preserve such a tiny analytic model as an independent optimization oracle. A robustness implementation should distinguish these three solutions, preserve the parameter units, and reproduce an independently integrated yield. This tests the meaning of the objective rather than merely duplicating an optimizer's arithmetic.","url":"centering.html#parameter-model"},{"title":"Analog Design Centering and Sizing · Covariance is part of the physical model","text":"Covariance is part of the physical model For mean μ and positive-definite covariance C, a multivariate Gaussian has equidensity contours characterized by squared distance β² = (xs − μ)ᵀC⁻¹(xs − μ) . With diagonal standard-deviation matrix D and correlation matrix R, C = DRD . A factorization C = AAᵀ allows samples xs = μ + Az with independent standard-normal components in z. Whitening also makes numerical distances meaningful when parameters have different units. PDF pp. 51–63 . Global and local variations must remain separate. A global threshold perturbation shared by two matched transistors largely cancels in their difference; independent local threshold errors do not. Under a simple model q1 = μ1 + g + l1 , q2 = μ2 + g + l2 , independent local terms with variance σl² give Var(q1 − q2) = 2σl² . Counting g independently twice destroys that cancellation. Conversely, assuming every local effect is independent discards spatial correlations. The book introduces global/local decomposition; the exact decomposition used by an engine must come from the PDK or a calibrated model. PDF pp. 63–64 . Proposed action: let placement-dependent mismatch models produce covariance terms, not only one penalty per pair. Propagate them into each sensitive circuit output using Cov(f) ≈ S C Sᵀ . This preserves cancellation and reinforcement shared across constraints. The approximation assumes a valid local linear response and a covariance model appropriate to the layout and bias; final nonlinear simulation must check critical outputs. Numerical safeguards: covariance diagonal entries are variances, not standard deviations. Check symmetry and positive semidefiniteness; pairwise correlation magnitudes below one are insufficient for a valid matrix in three or more dimensions. If C is singular because quantities are exactly shared, sample its nonzero-rank subspace instead of blindly taking an inverse. A nonlinear transformation to Gaussian latent variables changes derivatives and, for density calculations, requires the appropriate Jacobian.","url":"centering.html#covariance"},{"title":"Analog Design Centering and Sizing · Sensitivities, scaling and what the optimizer should improve","text":"Sensitivities, scaling and what the optimizer should improve A local sensitivity matrix has entries Sij = ∂fi/∂xj , giving Δf ≈ SΔx . Rows describe how one output responds to all parameters; columns describe one parameter's effects on all outputs. The book uses these directions to discuss similarity, significance and adjustability. Their numerical lengths and angles are meaningful only after defining parameter and performance scales. Changing nanometers to micrometers must not alter which transistor appears important. PDF pp. 68–75 . For a finite-difference sensitivity, too small a perturbation exposes simulator tolerances; too large a perturbation averages nonlinear behavior. Therefore attach a perturbation size, operating point, extraction version and consistency check to every derivative. Central differences can expose asymmetry but cost additional simulations. A gradient cannot justify an unlimited move: maximizing a linear improvement without a step bound is unbounded. Use a domain or trust region. Pareto dominance compares a complete objective vector: one candidate dominates another only if it is no worse in every retained objective and strictly better in at least one. Weighted sums can miss unsupported portions of nonconvex fronts. Min-max or target-based formulations can explore other compromises, but their weights and reference points still encode user preferences. A zero-weight objective can permit weakly Pareto solutions; retain tie-breaking and dominance checks. PDF pp. 75–83 . Engine mapping: Philis already distinguishes hard constraints, budgets and costs in Requirements<On> (line 18). That is useful infrastructure for avoiding a single uninterpretable score. The geometric finite differences in gradient computations around lines 205–221 differentiate the current placement objective; they are not transistor-to-circuit performance sensitivities. Add a separately identified circuit-response model rather than relabeling the existing derivative.","url":"centering.html#sensitivities-objectives"},{"title":"Analog Design Centering and Sizing · Three worst-case analyses, with explicit assumptions","text":"Three worst-case analyses, with explicit assumptions Box case. Let f ≈ f0 + gᵀδ and independent bounded coordinates satisfy |δk| ≤ ak. Then the upper linear worst case is f0 + Σ|gk|ak ; choose δk = ak sign(gk). The lower case changes the sign. This is appropriate for a specified independent tolerance box and exact for a linear response. If gk = 0, that coordinate is undetermined to first order; it is not proven irrelevant to a nonlinear circuit. PDF pp. 103–108 . Ellipsoid case. For δᵀC⁻¹δ ≤ β² and σf = √(gᵀCg), the linear extrema are: fupper/lower = f0 ± βσf δupper/lower = ±β Cg / σf The equations follow by transforming δ = Az and applying Cauchy–Schwarz to gᵀAz with ||z|| ≤ β. They require σf > 0; otherwise the linear model predicts no variation and a higher-order check is needed. These equations were verified against the rendered source. PDF p. 111 . Nonlinear case. Optimize the real response over the statistical region and operating range. Different specifications generally have different worst-case points; an interior operating point can be worse than all corners. Mismatch can produce multiple opposite failure directions. A single start of an SQP solver is not a global certificate. The book explicitly discusses these difficulties and the nested cost of worst-case analysis. PDF pp. 87–89 , pp. 113–124 , pp. 147–153 . Checked correction: the nonlinear Lagrangian in §5.4, Eq. (189), PDF p. 115 has Hessian ∇²f + 2λC⁻¹ , not merely 2λC⁻¹ as stated in the following paragraph. Positive definiteness of C does not establish second-order sufficiency for an arbitrary nonlinear response. Appendix C.6, Eq. (C.24), PDF p. 195 correctly includes objective and constraint curvature. Never convert that paragraph into a solver acceptance test.","url":"centering.html#worstcase"},{"title":"Analog Design Centering and Sizing · Yield is a joint event, not a collection of good-looking margins","text":"Yield is a joint event, not a collection of good-looking margins Y(xd) = Prxs[for every xr in Tr: fL ≤ f(xd, xs, xr) ≤ fU]. One sample passes only if every specified feature passes throughout the required operating domain. A gain pass probability and a phase-margin pass probability are yield partitions. Their minimum is an upper bound on joint yield; multiplying them is justified only with independence. Because the same devices and parasitics influence both outputs, independence is usually a modeling assumption to test, not a default. PDF pp. 89–96 . A β = 3 statement needs a region definition. For one linear upper-bounded Gaussian output it corresponds to Φ(3), about 99.865% passing. For a symmetric interval on that output it gives 2Φ(3) − 1. For an n-dimensional Gaussian ellipsoid of radius β, enclosed probability is Pr[χ²n ≤ β²] , not Φ(β). The book's table shows that a radius-three ellipsoid encloses about 46.8% in ten dimensions. There is no contradiction: a half-space and a ball are different events. PDF pp. 133–145 . Geometric yield analysis finds the nearest failure boundary in whitened coordinates. Linearizing that boundary at its most probable point gives a half-space approximation and a signed distance: positive for nominally passing, negative for nominally failing. This is more informative than nominal slack alone because it divides a performance margin by sensitivity to variation. However, curved or disconnected boundaries and multiple failure regions can invalidate a single tangent approximation. The book's 1–3% empirical approximation discussion is historical experience, not a guaranteed error bound for Philis or a justification for ppm certification. PDF pp. 146–160 . Action: use the smallest signed failure distance to identify the current bottleneck, retain all active failure modes, then estimate joint yield from their shared latent variables. For finalists, compare this approximation with circuit-based sampling. This directs computation toward the failure boundary without treating the approximation as independent evidence.","url":"centering.html#yield"},{"title":"Analog Design Centering and Sizing · Monte Carlo accuracy and rare failures","text":"Monte Carlo accuracy and rare failures With N independent process samples and K passing samples, Ŷ = K/N . For Bernoulli pass events, Var(Ŷ) = Y(1−Y)/N . The variance depends directly on sample count and yield, while the cost per sample still depends on circuit size, parameter count and operating checks. Accuracy improving like 1/√N explains why a visually convincing handful of simulations cannot validate high yield. PDF pp. 125–133 . A normal confidence approximation is useful away from extreme probabilities and with enough observed successes and failures. It is misleading at zero observed failures: substituting Ŷ = 1 into a variance estimate gives zero, but the underlying failure probability remains uncertain. As an independent derivation, zero failures in N independent trials has probability (1−p)ᴺ. A one-sided 95% upper bound is therefore pupper = 1 − 0.05^(1/N) , approximately 3/N. One thousand clean samples support only an upper failure bound near 0.3%, under the model and independence assumptions. The book's importance-sampling estimator reweights draws from a proposal q by p/q. A proposal must cover all relevant failure support; otherwise omitted failure modes create bias. For rare failures, estimating failure probability directly can be more stable than subtracting a yield estimate from one. Record weights, effective sample behavior and the proposal. A variance formula for unweighted Bernoulli samples cannot be reused unchanged for weighted samples. PDF pp. 127–132 . Validation contract: distinguish optimizer training samples from independent final validation samples; preserve seeds and model hashes; report confidence bounds, scenario coverage and unresolved simulation failures. Failed convergence is an unknown result requiring treatment, not an automatic pass. Common random numbers may help compare nearby designs but do not make correlated comparisons independent validation trials.","url":"centering.html#sampling"},{"title":"Analog Design Centering and Sizing · How the design should move","text":"How the design should move For fixed Gaussian covariance and a fixed acceptance region, the book derives the mean-shift yield gradient ∇μY = YC⁻¹(μaccepted − μ) . This explains the term centering: at an unconstrained stationary point with positive yield, the accepted population's mean matches the manufactured population's mean. It is not a sufficient global optimality condition. More importantly, transistor width, routing topology or finger count generally changes the acceptance region and may change covariance; the formula cannot be applied to those design variables as if they simply shifted a process distribution. PDF pp. 162–170 . The geometric alternative improves signed worst-case distances. A min-max formulation maximizes the weakest distance; a target least-squares formulation balances distances against chosen goals. This can deliberately trade nominal bandwidth for better weak metrics. The source op-amp example raises the minimum distance from 1.1 to 3.9 while reducing nominal transit frequency; it illustrates robust tradeoffs, not an engine-independent guarantee. PDF pp. 173–180 . For a local residual vector e and Jacobian J, the regularized least-squares step solves (JᵀJ + λI)r = −Jᵀe . The transpose on the right is essential; §7.2.3 defines gradients as rows in Eq. (362), but printed Eqs. (365) and (368) on PDF p. 176 omit the right-hand transpose. This is a dimensionally checked correction confirmed against the rendered page. Use numerically stable QR/SVD or an appropriate solver rather than blindly forming normal equations when conditioning is poor. Verify predicted improvement by re-evaluating the true response and shrink the trust region if agreement deteriorates. This prevents large geometry moves from exploiting an obsolete local model. PDF pp. 175–179 . For very high yield, raw Y becomes a flat objective. The book proposes tolerance inflation or distance optimization to expose remaining robustness. Inflation is an optimization aid: results must finally be evaluated under the original process distribution. Layout cannot arbitrarily tune a foundry covariance matrix; any tolerance-assignment variable must correspond to an allowed physical choice such as area, spacing, device class or purchased process option.","url":"centering.html#optimization"},{"title":"Analog Design Centering and Sizing · Concrete integration sequence and meaningful checks","text":"Concrete integration sequence and meaningful checks Represent electrical specifications before adding more weights. Store metric, bound, units, corner/range quantifier and response model. This makes it possible to distinguish an actual performance constraint from a heuristic geometric preference. Extend existing wire budgets into traceable performance reductions. The generic ParasiticBudget::extract helper has a default length budget, while the active annotator already derives lengths from class/load-based capacitance allowances and process wire capacitance. Preserve that path and its assumptions, add circuit sensitivities and available performance margin where characterized, and revise estimates with extracted electrical results. The book does not justify one universal maximum net length. Attach covariance and scenario context to matching budgets. placement (line 53) emits topology-based matching, proximity and thermal requirements. Keep this useful structural inference, but record which uncertainty terms each rule addresses and calibrate numerical thresholds to actual models. Use a staged fidelity loop. Cheap geometric screening, local response estimates, selected extraction/simulation, and independent final yield checks have different jobs. Refit when topology, device variants or active operating conditions change, because those changes can invalidate derivatives discontinuously. Prove numerical meaning with analytic cases. Check Gaussian linear propagation against gᵀCg, box extrema against enumeration, singular shared variation against reduced-rank sampling, and nonlinear functions with known interior or dual failure points. Compare candidate ranking with actual post-layout simulation before increasing optimizer complexity. These are proposed extensions, not claims that Philis currently performs statistical circuit optimization. The book provides the objective definitions and mathematical checks needed to build that capability without confusing a geometry score with measured robustness.","url":"centering.html#engine-actions"},{"title":"Constraints · How to read and instantiate the catalog","text":"How to read and instantiate the catalog L denotes mandatory physical/process legality once the applicable PDK rule is selected. E denotes a required electrical, reliability or statistical acceptance condition in the design contract. C denotes an exact construction or representation invariant chosen to implement intent. B denotes a derived budget or screening proxy whose assumptions must remain valid. P denotes a preference among acceptable alternatives. A row may contain several roles; for example, required offset is E, a derived centroid tolerance is B, and an explicitly selected exact common-centroid pattern is C. Assigning a search tier does not change the underlying physical meaning. Every instance should carry a stable ID, targets and scope, units, scenarios, numerical bounds/tolerances, parent requirement, source and model revision, derivation, and current evidence. Outcomes are pass, fail, unknown, or not applicable . Missing current, temperature, extraction or process data normally makes the dependent check unknown. Not applicable requires a justified scope condition; it cannot mean “the tool does not implement this.” Predicates below are templates, not numerical design rules. Symbols such as limit, tolerance, legal set and supported domain must be instantiated from the design contract or PDK. Evaluate coupled requirements together when they share a performance allowance. Preserve the parent requirement when replacing a geometric proxy, and invalidate evidence when its inputs change. See constraint systems , optimality and verification for the full semantics.","url":"constraints.html#catalog-semantics"},{"title":"Constraints · Electrical intent and physical identity — INT","text":"Electrical intent and physical identity — INT ID, rule and role Why; quantity or predicate Required inputs; enforcement and verification Evidence and origin INT-01 · Terminal connectivity L/E Every intended terminal belongs to its required net; distinct nets remain distinct. Opens and shorts change the circuit rather than merely degrading a score. Elaborated reference netlist, terminal identities, complete conductive geometry and layer connectivity. Preserve during generation; verify extracted connectivity and LVS. S01 · S02 INT-02 · Device electrical identity E/C Extracted device class, model, dimensions and multiplicity match the authorized electrical design within defined tolerances. PDK recognition/model conventions, unitization and reference parameters. Regenerate the reference only for an intentional sizing change; verify extraction/LVS. O15 · H05 · S01 INT-03 · Required device ratios E/C Implement the intended current, resistance or capacitance ratio using valid unit counts and device parameters; geometric area alone need not equal electrical ratio. Circuit ratio requirement and calibrated unit values. Enforce integer/count constraints and verify extracted or simulated ratios. D01 · H09 · H12 INT-04 · Bias and body domains L/E Each body, well, tap and protected region connects to its permitted bias domain; unintended forward bias or domain shorting is forbidden. Device type, voltage ranges, well structure and domain graph. Check structural connectivity, ERC and relevant operating cases. H17 · P08 · S01 INT-05 · Allowed transformations C/E Rotation, reflection, terminal exchange and diffusion sharing stay within the transformations proven valid for the device and electrical role. PDK orientation restrictions, terminal semantics and generator preconditions. Restrict variants/moves; recheck geometry and electrical identity. H04 · H06 · O10 INT-06 · Fixed objects and interfaces C/E Fixed pads, macros, pin locations and required die boundaries remain satisfied; an optimizer cannot move a fixed interface to repair another rule. Parent-level interface contract, fixed/free flags and coordinate transforms. Enforce in construction and certify final positions. O13 · H21 · S01 INT-07 · Realization-map preservation C Splitting, merging and hierarchy changes preserve each electrical member's physical units, weights, terminals and constraints. Stable electrical/physical IDs and explicit many-to-many maps. Audit retargeting and verify no lost, duplicated or misweighted members. O16 · S01 INT-08 · Realizable terminal access L/C/B Each required terminal has a legal connected access compatible with layer, enclosure, neighbors and route direction; rectangle-center reachability is insufficient. Pin polygons, access rules, obstacles and routing layers. Construct candidate accesses, check detailed geometry, then verify actual connection. O08 · H21 · S01","url":"constraints.html#intent-rules"},{"title":"Constraints · Manufacturing geometry and placement legality — GEO","text":"Manufacturing geometry and placement legality — GEO ID, rule and role Why; quantity or predicate Required inputs; enforcement and verification Evidence and origin GEO-01 · Units and legal grids L/C Coordinates, cuts and dimensions lie on their applicable manufacturing lattices; internal unit conversions preserve exact required relationships. PDK grid conventions and per-layer/cut rules. Snap only with verified invariants; check final polygons and doubled-coordinate relations where needed. H21 · S01 GEO-02 · Width and length legality L Each shape satisfies the applicable minimum/maximum dimensions, including conditional wide-wire, device-length and local neck restrictions. Layer/device-specific rules and drawn polygons. Use legal generators and full supported DRC predicates, not only nominal route widths. H21 · P20 GEO-03 · Contextual separation L Spacing meets rules dependent on layer, width, parallel run, voltage, end geometry and object type. PDK rule conditions and neighboring geometry. Construct correct clearance classes and verify exact conditional spacing. H21 · O12 GEO-04 · Enclosure and overlap L Contacts, vias, diffusion, implants and wells have required enclosing material and valid overlap; edge contact alone does not imply a legal connection. Cut/layer stack and enclosure directions. Generate legal landing shapes and verify DRC plus electrical continuity. H21 · P20 GEO-05 · Shape area and topology L Minimum area, hole/notch and forbidden shape conditions pass where specified; an individually legal width can still form an illegal polygon. Complete polygon unions and PDK shape rules. Evaluate after merging and routing, not only per primitive rectangle. H21 · S01 GEO-06 · Occupancy and reserved regions L/C Physical objects avoid prohibited overlap and honor exclusive well, guard-ring, device-environment and routing reservations. Occupied geometry, keepouts and permitted-sharing classes. Pack/compact with reservations; verify the generated shapes. O11 · O13 · S01 GEO-07 · Disjunctive spacing L/C Where the process requires it, a gap belongs to a sharing interval or an isolation interval; forbidden intermediate gaps and excessive total stretch fail. Applicable trench/region rule, bounds and shared-stretch accounting. Use explicit branches or mixed-integer predicates; verify chosen geometry. O12 GEO-08 · Routing-resource feasibility B/C Channels, layer capacities and pin-access corridors can support required nets and protective structures; overflow is a feasibility signal, not electrical success. Real layer/via capacities and blockages. Use global-route feedback and reserve resources; verify detailed routing completion. O13 · O08 · Q10","url":"constraints.html#geometry-rules"},{"title":"Constraints · Matching, orientation and device environment — MAT","text":"Matching, orientation and device environment — MAT ID, rule and role Why; quantity or predicate Required inputs; enforcement and verification Evidence and origin MAT-01 · Random mismatch allowance E/B Propagated local variation meets the required offset/ratio/noise distribution; unit area is a physical lever, not the acceptance metric itself. PDK mismatch model, geometry, bias and circuit sensitivities. Derive area/geometry budgets; verify statistical circuit behavior. P01 · P04 · P05 · H01 MAT-02 · Equivalent matched units C/B Matched contributions use compatible device type, geometry, segmentation and operating environment within the chosen matching strategy. Electrical weights, unitization and allowed variants. Constrain generation; compare extracted device parameters and response. H03 · P04 · Q02 MAT-03 · Weighted first moments C/B Electrically weighted centroids coincide or meet a derived tolerance to cancel a modeled first-order spatial field. Physical unit locations and correct electrical weights. Use exact patterns or moment checks; verify after merges and routing. Q01 · D01 · H02 MAT-04 · Higher-order spatial moments B/P Residual sensitivity to justified second/higher-order field models stays within allocation; equal centroids alone do not cancel curvature. Spatial field basis/domain and unit weights. Compare moments and simulated fields; use only where the field model is supported. Q06 · Q07 · D02 MAT-05 · Physical orientation equivalence C/B Matched units have compatible process orientation and source/drain environment; geometric reflection is not automatically identical orientation. PDK/device orientation effects and electrical terminal roles. Limit transforms; verify generated devices and selected matching metric. H04 · P03 MAT-06 · Selected symmetry relations C/B Chosen paired and self-symmetric objects satisfy the intended shared-axis equations and pin mapping. Group membership, permitted axes and fixed objects. Preserve through compound moves or simultaneous solves; verify exact geometry. O06 · O10 · O11 MAT-07 · Spatial proximity budget B/P Separation stays within a derived gradient/correlation allowance; smaller distance is not universally better when heat or coupling dominates. Calibrated spatial model, footprint and competing environments. Derive pair/group bounds and verify actual circuit impact. P01 · O05 · P11 MAT-08 · Edge and dummy equivalence C Required edge units see equivalent surroundings; dummy presence, geometry and connection implement the intended environment without creating unintended devices. PDK dummy construction, array boundary and circuit domains. Generate and connect dummies explicitly; verify DRC, ERC and extraction. H07 · Q02 MAT-09 · Well-proximity environment B/E Well-edge distances and geometry keep modeled device shifts and mismatch within allocation. Applicable WPE model, well polygons and operating point. Preserve/adjust environment; evaluate model parameters and circuit response. H06 · Q02 MAT-10 · Isolation/stress environment B/E Diffusion lengths, isolation edges and stress-related environment satisfy supported LDE constraints and matching intent. PDK stress/LOD/STI model and actual diffusion geometry. Generate controlled surroundings; re-extract after compaction. H06 · Q02 MAT-11 · Matched electrical interconnect E/B/C Relevant route R, C, coupling and access differences stay within the differential error allocation; equal length alone is insufficient. Paired electrical terminals, extracted topology and aggressor environment. Use matched routing guidance; verify differential response. Q04 · D03 · Q25 MAT-12 · Matched temperature response E/B Temperature differences and device temperature coefficients produce no more than allowed offset/ratio error. Power map, thermal boundary/model and device sensitivities. Budget placement/environment; verify steady and relevant transient thermal cases. H02 · H25 · P25","url":"constraints.html#matching-rules"},{"title":"Constraints · Signal routing and parasitic performance — NET","text":"Signal routing and parasitic performance — NET ID, rule and role Why; quantity or predicate Required inputs; enforcement and verification Evidence and origin NET-01 · Series resistance E/B Branch/path resistance, including contacts and vias, stays within an electrical allowance; drawn length is only a conditional proxy. Layer/width-dependent resistivity, topology, contact models and parent specification. Extract and verify the relevant path response. O04 · O08 · D03 NET-02 · Node loading capacitance E/B Capacitance on each sensitive node fits its gain, bandwidth, settling or energy allowance; total-chip C is insufficient. Node identity, device/internal/external geometry and extraction model. Budget by sensitivity and verify circuit performance. O04 · Q08 · Q25 NET-03 · Aggressor coupling E/B Coupling transfer from defined aggressors remains below noise or error limits under their actual waveforms and victim impedance. Coupling matrix, aggressor timing/spectrum and victim model. Guide spacing/layers/shields; verify AC/transient response. D05 · P15 · H20 NET-04 · Shared performance budget E/B Combined parasitic effects satisfy coupled inequalities or the actual circuit bound; independent children must not spend one allowance repeatedly. Baseline margin, sensitivity/scenario model and allocation provenance. Price or allocate jointly; validate extracted candidates. O01 · O02 · O03 NET-05 · Width/layer choice domain L/C/B Each segment uses a legal realizable width/layer/via combination; electrical gains are assessed with the resulting R and C together. PDK legal widths, transition geometry and segment current. Enumerate legal options; recheck DRC and electrical response. Q20 · Q24 · O04 · P20 NET-06 · Shield connectivity and reference E/C A selected shield connects to the intended reference with adequate impedance and does not create an unintended signal or return path. Reference domain, shield geometry, contacts and frequency/current context. Route the shield as a real net; verify connectivity and transfer. P12 · P13 · H20 · S01 NET-07 · Sensitive-node route environment B/P High-impedance or noise-sensitive routes avoid identified aggressive regions, crossing geometries and unsupported access structures. Circuit sensitivity and aggressor/field maps. Use conditional cost fields or keepouts; confirm actual coupling and loading. H20 · Q08 · Q16 NET-08 · Shared-impedance coupling E/B Shared signal/reference/return segments do not create excessive correlated error through common impedance. Full return graph, branch currents and transfer sensitivity. Split or strengthen paths when justified; simulate shared-path effects. P08 · P12 · H20 NET-09 · Distributed-network adequacy E/C The chosen lumped/distributed R, C and L model resolves behavior over the specified frequency or edge-rate range. Dimensions, material properties, spectrum and extraction capability. Check model domain; promote to distributed/field analysis when needed. D04 · P09 · H23 NET-10 · Complete route realization L/C Every logical route, internal macro connection and required protective conductor appears in final geometry and extracted connectivity. Route graph, cell geometry and emitted output. Compare representation against actual shapes; check opens after all postprocessing. O16 · S02","url":"constraints.html#net-rules"},{"title":"Constraints · Power, current and lifetime — PWR","text":"Power, current and lifetime — PWR ID, rule and role Why; quantity or predicate Required inputs; enforcement and verification Evidence and origin PWR-01 · Branch-current conservation C/E For a tree, shared trunks carry the signed sum of downstream load currents, including interconnect shunt/displacement currents where relevant. Meshed nets require a network solution; one terminal current is not automatically every segment’s current. Connected conductor graph and operating currents/waveforms. Solve or conservatively bound flows; validate conservation and assignments. P19 · D04 PWR-02 · DC supply/reference drop E/B Voltage at every required terminal remains inside its allowance after branch resistance and current are included. Network resistances, pad/source impedances and currents. Budget path drop; solve extracted resistive/circuit network. O08 · P19 · H20 PWR-03 · Dynamic supply/ground error E/B Droop, bounce and reference transients remain within amplitude and settling limits. Switching waveforms, decoupling, package/return impedances and parasitic RLC. Verify relevant transient cases and resonances. P08 · P12 · H23 · S01 PWR-04 · Conductor EM lifetime E/L/B Conductor stress meets the applicable lifetime rule under specified temperature and current history, using the foundry's metric. Cross-section, material/layer, current waveform, temperature and mission profile. Apply calibrated checks; do not substitute an unsupported universal current density. P16 · P17 · P18 · P23 PWR-05 · Contact/via current reliability E/L/B Contact/via arrays and connecting metal support required current with the applicable derating and current-sharing assumptions. Per-cut/array rule, landing geometry and current distribution. Choose legal arrays; verify extraction and reliability checks. P20 · P21 · P22 · H19 PWR-06 · Local current crowding E/B Bends, transitions, bottlenecks and unequal parallel paths do not exceed local stress limits despite acceptable average current. Detailed geometry and appropriate current-distribution model. Refine critical regions or use validated conservative rules. P21 · H19 · H22 PWR-07 · Return-path integrity E/C/B Every signal/current path has an intended continuous return with acceptable impedance and coupling loop behavior. Signal and return graphs, package interface and frequency content. Reserve routes early; verify extracted loop/network behavior. P08 · P12 · H20 · H23 PWR-08 · Device voltage stress E/L Terminal voltages and junction biases stay inside allowed operating/reliability domains during steady state, startup and specified transients. PDK ratings, waveforms and bias domains. ERC screens structure; circuit analysis checks relevant exposure. H18 · H24 · S01","url":"constraints.html#power-rules"},{"title":"Constraints · Thermal, substrate and isolation environment — ENV","text":"Thermal, substrate and isolation environment — ENV ID, rule and role Why; quantity or predicate Required inputs; enforcement and verification Evidence and origin ENV-01 · Dissipation model completeness C/E Every relevant heat source has a justified power estimate and scenario; unknown power cannot establish zero heating. Operating currents/voltages, activity and device map. Build steady/transient power fields with uncertainty flags. H25 · P24 · S02 ENV-02 · Junction-temperature bound E/B Peak junction temperature stays inside the electrical and lifetime contract. Power map, geometry, thermal material/package/boundary model. Use calibrated approximation then verify critical coupled cases. H25 · P24 ENV-03 · Thermal settling and drift E/B Time-dependent temperature shifts do not violate accuracy or settling requirements after changes in power. Thermal time constants, activity transitions and device temperature sensitivity. Verify transient thermal/electrical behavior. H25 · H10 · S01 ENV-04 · Substrate transfer E/B Injected substrate disturbance produces no more than permitted error/noise at sensitive devices. Substrate/epitaxial model, contacts, wells, aggressor injection and victim response. Evaluate transfer; spacing alone is a process-dependent proxy. P08 · P09 · P10 · P11 · P14 ENV-05 · Guard-ring realization L/C/B Selected ring type, continuity, contacts and connection implement the intended collection/isolation function without domain conflicts. Process structure, permitted ring connections and geometry. Generate as real obstacles/nets; verify DRC, ERC and transfer behavior. H17 · P12 · P13 ENV-06 · Guard-return impedance E/B The collected disturbance returns through a sufficiently low and appropriate impedance rather than recoupling into a sensitive reference. Ring/contact network, return routing, injected spectrum and reference topology. Extract and simulate the complete return path. P12 · P14 · P15 ENV-07 · Latch-up prevention L/E Applicable well/tap distances, injection limits and protective structures suppress forbidden parasitic feedback paths. Foundry latch-up rules, substrate/well topology and injection conditions. Apply qualified layout rules and required electrical checks. H17 · P08 · S01 ENV-08 · Isolation-domain compatibility L/C/E Deep wells, trenches, taps and shared isolation structures are valid for each device/domain and do not short or forward-bias incompatible regions. Process options, voltage-domain map and isolation-sharing rules. Restrict grouping; verify physical and electrical structure. H17 · P13 · O12","url":"constraints.html#environment-rules"},{"title":"Constraints · Passive, bipolar and RF device behavior — DEV","text":"Passive, bipolar and RF device behavior — DEV ID, rule and role Why; quantity or predicate Required inputs; enforcement and verification Evidence and origin DEV-01 · Resistor value and ratio E/B/C Sheet, end, contact and bend contributions yield the required resistance/ratio across conditions. Resistor type, geometry, bias/temperature model and contacts. Generate matched segments; extract and verify circuit value. H09 · H11 DEV-02 · Resistor parasitic/environment error E/B Voltage coefficient, thermal effects and capacitive loading stay within the relevant accuracy/noise/bandwidth allocation. Supported resistor models, surroundings, waveform and power. Check model domain and extracted circuit behavior. H10 · H11 · H25 DEV-03 · Capacitor value and terminal roles E/C Realized capacitance, polarity where applicable, and top/bottom-plate assignment match intended circuit behavior. Capacitor structure, unit model, node roles and parasitic asymmetry. Generate legal plates and verify extracted connectivity/value. H12 · H13 · Q08 DEV-04 · Capacitor voltage/frequency domain E/B Bias dependence, loss, leakage and reliability meet the circuit's conditions; nominal area capacitance is insufficient. PDK model, frequency, voltage waveform and temperature. Verify relevant small-signal/transient and reliability metrics. H13 · H23 · S01 DEV-05 · Bipolar/diode construction L/E/C Emitter/base/collector or diode regions, contacts and isolation form the intended device without unintended parasitic conduction. Process-specific device structure and terminal map. Use supported generators; verify extraction, ERC and bias behavior. H14 · H15 · H17 DEV-06 · Bipolar matching and current density E/B Matched devices meet current-density, area/perimeter, base-resistance and thermal requirements for the intended ratio or reference accuracy. Device model, currents, unit geometry and thermal environment. Constrain valid units; simulate mismatch and temperature response. H14 · H15 · H25 DEV-07 · Inductor/RF passive performance E/B Inductance, Q, self-resonance and coupling meet specifications over the operating band. Full geometry, metal/substrate stack and validated electromagnetic model. Use characterized structures and field/extracted verification. H23 DEV-08 · RF interaction and distributed routing E/B Mutual inductance, transmission effects and return-path discontinuities remain within gain, noise, stability or matching limits. Frequency band, neighboring conductors/passives and package/reference model. Co-simulate where lumped RC assumptions fail. H23 · P09 · S01","url":"constraints.html#device-rules"},{"title":"Constraints · DAC and precision-array realization — ARR","text":"DAC and precision-array realization — ARR ID, rule and role Why; quantity or predicate Required inputs; enforcement and verification Evidence and origin ARR-01 · Exact array weights C/E Physical unit counts and assigned terminals implement every intended code weight, including nonbinary or split-array ratios. Architecture, unitization and switching scheme. Enforce integer mapping and verify extracted effective ratios. Q08 · Q09 · D01 ARR-02 · Array spatial error B/C Code-relevant element groups have suitable centroids, dispersion and environment for the specified spatial error model. Code grouping, electrical weights and field/correlation model. Evaluate moments and actual code errors; no universal centroid-only guarantee. Q01 · Q06 · Q09 · Q14 ARR-03 · Code-dependent parasitics E/B Top/bottom-plate and inter-element coupling do not create unacceptable code-dependent transfer errors. Full capacitance matrix, switch states and reference/output impedances. Extract complete array and evaluate architecture-specific codes. Q08 · Q10 ARR-04 · INL/DNL and monotonicity E Static transfer meets the selected INL/DNL convention and monotonicity requirement over the specified code domain. Reference convention, code set, output measurement and variation model. Evaluate the actual transfer; report code coverage. Q11 ARR-05 · Settling and switching transients E/B Specified code transitions settle within error/time limits and meet applicable glitch or reference-disturbance bounds. Switch timing, input transitions, route RC and load/reference network. Simulate relevant worst transitions, not only static codes. Q12 · D10 · Q15 ARR-06 · Array routing realizability C/B/P The assignment/pattern permits legal routes with acceptable congestion, layers and parasitics; a placement-only matching optimum may fail after routing. Actual pin locations, allowed layers, switch/control topology and dummies. Retain routed alternatives and verify complete array behavior. Q09 · Q10 · Q15","url":"constraints.html#array-rules"},{"title":"Constraints · Manufacturability and protection beyond ordinary geometry — MFG","text":"Manufacturability and protection beyond ordinary geometry — MFG ID, rule and role Why; quantity or predicate Required inputs; enforcement and verification Evidence and origin MFG-01 · Density and fill L/E/B Required local/global density windows pass while added fill remains compatible with sensitive-node capacitance and matching. PDK density rules, fill type/connectivity and final geometry. Insert legal fill, rerun density/DRC and relevant extraction. H08 · H21 · S01 MFG-02 · Defect critical area B/P/E Short/open susceptibility meets an explicitly modeled yield/cost objective; spacing and width affect different defect mechanisms. Geometry and calibrated defect-size/density model. Compute critical area and yield under stated assumptions; keep separate from parametric yield. O09 MFG-03 · Process topology robustness L/B/P Applicable lithographic, etch/CMP and redundant-contact conditions avoid fragile structures beyond simple minimum dimensions. Qualified process rules or calibrated hotspot models. Apply supported checks; do not invent universal geometry margins. H21 · H08 · P21 MFG-04 · Antenna exposure L/E Fabrication-stage conductor exposure relative to connected vulnerable gates satisfies the applicable antenna rules. Process sequence, cumulative layer ratios, device protection and connectivity. Check stage-aware antenna conditions after final routing. H16 MFG-05 · ESD discharge path L/E/C Specified discharge paths and clamps protect required terminals without excessive path impedance or unintended weak points. Qualified protection architecture, stress requirements, device/process rules and full pad-to-clamp network. Verify structure and required reliability analyses. H18 · H19 MFG-06 · Test accessibility and discrimination E/B/P Required faults or parameter deviations remain observable under the defined production-test strategy; a geometric proxy alone is not fault coverage. Fault model, test conditions, observable terminals and statistical decision rule. Verify detection/discrimination with the actual test model. O26 · S01 · S02","url":"constraints.html#manufacture-rules"},{"title":"Constraints · Circuit acceptance, robustness and evidence completeness — EVD","text":"Circuit acceptance, robustness and evidence completeness — EVD ID, rule and role Why; quantity or predicate Required inputs; enforcement and verification Evidence and origin EVD-01 · Electrical specification vector E Every required gain, stability, settling, noise, offset, swing, power or architecture-specific metric satisfies its defined bound. Executable testbench, measurement definition, load/bias and complete extracted circuit. Simulate and retain each metric, not only a figure of merit. Q16 · Q25 · S01 EVD-02 · Operating-domain coverage E/C Acceptance holds throughout the required supply, temperature, input/load, mode and transition domain, or the evaluated subset is explicitly reported. Domain contract and evidence for corner reduction/monotonicity. Search or sample appropriately; preserve unresolved interior behavior as unknown. O17 · H24 · S01 EVD-03 · Valid variation model C/E Global/local/spatial distributions and covariance are physically and numerically valid for the geometry, bias and process. PDK statistical model, latent-variable ownership and parameter units. Check covariance validity and geometry-dependent updates. P04 · P05 · O18 · Q14 EVD-04 · Joint parametric yield E/B The probability that all requirements pass under all required operating conditions meets the stated target; per-metric probabilities are not substituted silently. Joint model, complete acceptance event and estimator/approximation. Verify with appropriate sampling or justified analysis. O17 · O19 · O20 EVD-05 · Statistical confidence C/E Yield or failure claims carry valid uncertainty bounds; zero observed failures does not imply zero failure probability. Sample count, independence/weighting assumptions, outcomes and estimator. Use appropriate confidence analysis and report unresolved simulations. O21 · D09 EVD-06 · Proxy/model validity C/B Sensitivity, surrogate and compact models remain within validated topology, geometry, scenario and error domains. Training/reference point, trust region and held-out error. Refresh or promote evaluation when predictions cease to justify decisions. O03 · O15 · Q18 · Q22 EVD-07 · Required checker coverage C/E All mandatory physical/electrical checks have supported evaluators and acceptable outcomes; unsupported effects are not marked passed. Requirement-to-evaluator map and tool/deck capabilities. Report pass/fail/unknown/not-applicable separately with reasons. O16 · S02 EVD-08 · Fresh evidence identity C Results match the delivered geometry, netlist, PDK, models, scenarios and evaluator versions. Immutable revisions/hashes and dependency graph. Invalidate affected extraction, current, thermal, matching and performance evidence after changes. O16 · S02 EVD-09 · Search-domain and proof scope C Claims distinguish a best observed candidate, a restricted optimum, budget exhaustion and proved infeasibility. Representation restrictions, seeds, budgets, solver bounds and termination. Report the actual guarantee; a failed heuristic is not an infeasibility proof. O10 · O11 · O13 · S03 EVD-10 · Independent outcome validation C/E Final acceptance is checked independently enough to expose optimizer/model errors and is not merely a repetition of its scoring function. Reference oracles, held-out cases, qualified external checks where required, and reproducible candidate records. Compare complete realized outcomes. Q23 · Q16 · S02","url":"constraints.html#evidence-rules"},{"title":"Constraints · Turn the catalog into an actual run contract","text":"Turn the catalog into an actual run contract Select only applicable families, then instantiate them with exact targets and values. For each electrical parent, choose a physical construction and any cheap budgets, documenting the implication and assumptions. For example, EVD-01 phase-margin acceptance can generate NET-04 coupled RC budgets, which guide NET-05 width choices; those choices must also satisfy GEO rules and PWR reliability requirements. MAT-03 may be a useful construction for one gradient mechanism while MAT-11 and MAT-12 remain independent obligations. Review the selected contract for contradictions before search. During search, preserve C invariants and mandatory legality, use B/P evaluators with their declared fidelity, and collect candidate-specific evidence. At completion, resolve every mandatory L/E requirement or report the remaining unknowns. The catalog provides traceability and navigation; the linked chapters provide the derivations, examples, competing effects and validation methods that justify each action.","url":"constraints.html#catalog-use"},{"title":"Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs · How to use this paper in the handbook","text":"How to use this paper in the handbook Read DAC arrays for the full construction procedure and worked examples, capacitor devices for unit geometry, and gradient matching for the statistical assumptions. This source page records exactly what the supplied 2023 paper supports.","url":"dac.html#guide"},{"title":"Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs · Problem, assumptions, and modeled circuit","text":"Problem, assumptions, and modeled circuit The authors target binary-weighted and split charge-scaling DACs, especially a commercial 12 nm process with high wire/via resistance and directional routing. The unit count of a conventional N-bit bank with its grounded dummy is 2 N ; a split topology uses two smaller banks and an attenuation capacitor. That architecture reduces unit count but introduces a particularly sensitive bridge element and separate top-plate nodes. Layout generation must preserve the supplied architecture. §I–II, PDF pp. 1–3 §V, commercial 12 nm evaluation setup, PDF p. 10 The model separates intended capacitor values, top-to-bottom parasitics, top-to-substrate parasitics, and bottom-plate loading. The first two can perturb effective ratios; shunt loading and interconnect resistance influence power and settling. Coupling depends on excitation: two terminals at equal potential exchange no incremental charge through their mutual capacitor in that excitation, but this does not mean the physical coupling vanishes under every transition. §II, PDF pp. 2–3 Important implementation consequence. Preserve terminal identities in extraction. A single total-C scalar cannot reproduce these distinctions or calculate code-dependent output error.","url":"dac.html#scope"},{"title":"Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs · The three pattern families and their reasons","text":"The three pattern families and their reasons Family Construction Reason and cost Spiral Place increasing capacitor multiplicities outward, placing each unit together with its centrally reflected partner. Adjacent units of a bit can connect with few bends/vias. Reduced dispersion may worsen variation-induced INL/DNL. Chessboard Distribute the largest bit across one color, then recursively fill remaining positions. High dispersion samples a common spatial field, but disconnected components require substantial routing and many vias. Block chessboard Use a fine chessboard for lower-count capacitors and larger blocks for outer, larger capacitors; vary the block size. Trades dispersion against the RC cost of the critical bits. It creates a family of compromises rather than one winning pattern. §IV-A, PDF pp. 7–8 The unit-count exception is significant: C₀ and C₁ each have one unit in the binary bank, so they cannot both individually occupy the same centroid in a nonoverlapping grid. The paper places them near the center. Call this an approximation for those members, not an exact all-member CC certificate. Split banks have analogous odd-count and bridge-placement exceptions. Odd-count exception, PDF p. 7 Split placement, PDF p. 9","url":"dac.html#methods"},{"title":"Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs · Routing is part of the construction","text":"Routing is part of the construction Neighboring units of a capacitor become connected components found by breadth-first search. Bottom-plate branch wires connect units, vertical trunks connect components, and peripheral bridges join trunks. Each symmetric connection is mirrored. Separate top-plate routing uses a graph/MST to connect electrically common plates while minimizing added top-to-substrate capacitance. In a split DAC the LSB and MSB top-plate graphs remain separate except for the attenuation capacitor. §IV-B–C, PDF pp. 8–10 The authors use parallel wires to reduce resistance on critical bits while respecting discrete wire widths. A widened or parallel-routed critical bit can cause a different bit to become the slowest; therefore repeat the all-bit delay evaluation after every improvement. More parallel routes eventually add enough capacitance and channel demand to give diminishing returns. Results and Fig. 15, PDF pp. 11–12 Philis adaptation. Give capacitor-array macros internal connectivity, route-resource demand, and terminal-specific extracted RC. The current Capacitor::draw constructs each member as a merged plate or comb; that representation is not yet a routed common-centroid unit array. A dedicated generator should coexist with the simple primitive instead of relabeling it.","url":"dac.html#routing"},{"title":"Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs · Metrics and equations that need careful interpretation","text":"Metrics and equations that need careful interpretation Equations (17)–(18) define code-step DNL and ideal-transfer INL in LSB units. Preserve their reference convention: endpoint-fitted or best-fit INL removes different errors. For an N-bit transfer with codes 0…2 N −1 there are 2 N −1 adjacent transitions. Report the worst code and transition, not just an array-level average. §III, PDF p. 4 Paper's frequency metric, Eq. (32): f = 1 / [2 (N+2) ln(2) τ] τ is the worst relevant RC charging delay over the bits. The authors label this a 3-dB frequency, but its N-dependent expression is tied to their charge/discharge settling model. It is not interchangeable with the sinusoidal −3 dB pole of a first-order RC, 1/(2πτ) . Philis should name the reported metric explicitly and validate transient settling to a stated LSB error. This distinction is our interpretation of the displayed equation, visually checked in the PDF. §III-C and Eq. (32), PDF pp. 5–6 For the nonunit MOM bridge capacitor, the paper derives an effective perimeter/area-like condition. With coupling length l and height H, its small edge-shift model gives ΔC/C≈2δ(1/H+1/l) . Matching this quantity to the unit capacitor while solving for target capacitance gives two geometric equations. The result assumes the paper's MOM construction, small common edge shifts, and continuous dimensions; legal pitch quantization and fringe effects require extraction afterward. §III-D, PDF p. 6","url":"dac.html#metrics"},{"title":"Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs · What the results establish—and what remains uncertain","text":"What the results establish—and what remains uncertain The reported experiments show a real tradeoff: spiral layouts favor the modeled frequency, chessboard layouts favor dispersion/linearity, and block chessboards occupy intermediate points. Table II's 8-bit examples, for instance, give about 1411 MHz for spiral and 339 MHz for block chessboard under the paper's frequency model, with different INL/DNL. These values are evidence for the mechanism in that setup, not Philis performance targets. Tables I–II, PDF p. 11 The paper itself warns that its commonly reused gradient and correlation parameters are inherited from earlier work and are not a measured capacitor correlation characterization. This is an especially valuable methodological result: do not turn ρ=0.9 , a 1 mm correlation length, or a 10 ppm gradient into default manufacturing truth. Sweep uncertain values and label them assumed until the PDK or measurements justify them. Parameter discussion, PDF p. 12 Its analytical use of 3σ shifts does not automatically give the joint worst-case yield of all codes and parasitics. The sums share random variables; separately pushing numerator and denominator by the same sign of 3σ is not a general covariance-aware worst case. Use joint samples or sensitivity/covariance analysis for a yield claim. The nominal linearity analysis also assumes an ideal output amplifier, so switch resistance, finite buffer gain, settling, reference impedance, and dynamic error must be added for a complete converter test.","url":"dac.html#evidence"},{"title":"Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs · Reading coverage and limits","text":"Reading coverage and limits Read all 14 PDF pages, including both DAC circuit models, systematic/correlated variation equations, nonunit bridge derivation, placement algorithm, both routing procedures, parameter limitations, results, references, and author material. Visually checked PDF p. 5 for Eq. (32) and PDF p. 11 for the dense tables and routing comparison. The handbook's generator architecture and joint-yield tests are proposed Philis extensions; the paper's experimental improvement ratios have not been reproduced here.","url":"dac.html#coverage"},{"title":"Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design · Source and coverage","text":"Source and coverage All seven supplied PDF pages were read: introduction and local variation (p. 1); parameter choice and model inadequacies (p. 2); propagation/back propagation of variance (p. 3); measured/model comparisons (p. 4); current-mirror geometry and bias (p. 5); polarity and multiple-unit examples, conclusion (p. 6); references and biographies (p. 7). Rendered pp. 1–3 and 6 were checked for equations and table values lost in text extraction. The paper's examples use particular 0.13–0.4 µm technologies; numerical comparisons are examples, not present-day PDK constants. Supplied paper, PDF p. 1 .","url":"drennan.html#coverage"},{"title":"Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design · Coverage and evidence limits","text":"Coverage and evidence limits The supplied 171-page PDF was identified from its title and contents. All main chapters were read: PDF 13–23, 25–69, 73–108, 111–154, and 161–168, with front matter and the contents used to establish the map. Bibliographies and the index were used as navigation, not treated as separately reviewed research papers. Rendered pages 30, 83, 101, 118, 127, 138, and 139 were visually inspected to confirm key equations, via geometry, current classification, and the temperature-factor issue. This note distinguishes the source’s technical conclusions from proposed Philis contracts and illustrative calculations. It does not claim foundry qualification, reproduce every derivation, or validate the source’s historical forecasts as present-day facts.","url":"electromigration.html#coverage"},{"title":"Glossary · Units and naming conventions","text":"Units and naming conventions Carry units and terminal identities through every representation. A geometrically plausible result can be wrong by orders of magnitude after a silent area conversion, multiplicity change or terminal permutation. Quantity Convention to preserve Length and area 1 μm = 1,000 nm; 1 μm² = 10 6 nm². Squaring a length conversion is essential. Name the coordinate system and manufacturing grid. Resistance and conductance Ω and S = Ω −1 . Sheet resistance uses Ω/□; L/W is dimensionless. Contact resistance is not automatically a sheet-resistance term. Capacitance and charge F and C. A capacitance matrix entry needs node identities and a sign convention; a positive physical coupling capacitor produces negative off-diagonal nodal entries. Current and current density A and A/m², or explicitly stated alternatives. Current per drawn width is a process-specific surrogate rather than cross-sectional current density in A/m². Temperature and thermal resistance Use kelvin in physical equations. Temperature differences have the same numerical size in K and °C. K/W requires a stated thermal boundary condition. Error and noise State absolute versus fractional error; 1 ppm = 10 −6 . Distinguish RMS, standard deviation, variance, spectral density and peak excursion. In this handbook, G, D, S and B name MOS gate, drain, source and body. Array index order is never an electrical definition. State whether W means one finger's width or aggregate width, whether nf counts fingers and whether m replicates a modeled device. Their interpretation is PDK/model-specific.","url":"glossary.html#glossary-units"},{"title":"Glossary · Intent, optimization and models","text":"Intent, optimization and models Optimality Best performance under a declared objective, feasible set, scenarios and model. A best observed candidate is not a proof of a global optimum. See optimality . Pareto dominance One feasible candidate dominates another when it is no worse in every selected objective and better in at least one. Nondominated candidates expose genuine tradeoffs. Hard constraint and soft cost A hard requirement defines acceptability. A soft cost guides preference or search. A penalty's small value does not establish that all hard requirements pass. Electrical sensitivity A derivative or measured response of circuit performance to a parameter. It translates geometric or device perturbations into an error budget near a stated operating condition. Operating scenario A jointly allowed set of supplies, signals, currents, temperature, sequencing and timing. Independent extrema can create impossible combinations or omit a dangerous permitted transient. Process design kit (PDK) The process-specific models, layers, rules, extraction data, cells and associated restrictions used to design for a manufacturing technology. Its exact version matters. Parameterized cell (PCell) A generator for a supported device or structure. Legal parameter ranges, recognition, terminal definitions and transform restrictions remain part of its contract. Qualified primitive A physical construction supported by applicable characterization and integration restrictions. Being drawable, extractable or nominally simulatable does not alone establish qualification. Surrogate model A cheaper approximation used to predict or rank outcomes. Its calibration domain and uncertainty limit optimization authority; final evaluation should use the required evidence. Bias or operating point The terminal voltages, currents and device state around which behavior is evaluated. Identical geometry at different bias can have different sensitivity and mismatch.","url":"glossary.html#glossary-intent"},{"title":"Glossary · Geometry and device representation","text":"Geometry and device representation Drawn and effective dimensions Drawn dimensions describe mask intent; effective dimensions describe the model's electrically relevant structure after process and bias effects. Avoid double-counting PCell or model corrections. Active geometry and bounding box Active geometry contributes to device behavior. A bounding box also includes whitespace, guards and access. Its center or area need not represent electrical contribution. Topology, connectivity and geometry Connectivity states what joins what; topology includes branches and shared paths; geometry embeds those paths in layers and coordinates. All influence extracted behavior. Terminal identity and orientation Electrical roles must survive rotation, reflection and cell collapse. A geometric mirror can reverse source/drain direction or alter a qualified asymmetric device. Unitization Decomposition into physical units with an explicit series/parallel composition and parent-device assignment. Equal unit shapes do not imply equal electrical sensitivity. See layout sizing . Multiplicity A model or generator's replication count. Aggregate width is W×nf×m only if those parameters have those meanings; shared fingers can have different parasitics from independent replicas. Diffusion sharing Joining compatible device regions to save area or parasitics. It changes contacts, edge context and possible carrier paths; same net names alone do not prove safety. Well, body and substrate Physical semiconductor regions with distributed resistance, capacitance and junctions. A schematic body net does not make the entire region an ideal equipotential conductor. Dummy structure Material added to control an edge or process environment. Dummies still create fields, capacitance and sometimes devices; their geometry and electrical ties require rules. Density and fill Density measures material coverage within defined windows. Fill helps satisfy process requirements but changes parasitics and device environment. Check the final filled geometry. See fabrication , MOS construction and manufacturability for their physical consequences.","url":"glossary.html#glossary-geometry"},{"title":"Glossary · Matching, spatial fields and device environment","text":"Matching, spatial fields and device environment Matching Agreement of specified electrical quantities or their responses, usually differences or ratios. Equal dimensions are one means; bias, temperature and access also matter. Local random mismatch Stochastic device-to-device differences remaining under the model's shared conditions. Their variance, spatial correlation and geometry dependence require characterization. Systematic spatial variation A position- or environment-dependent field, such as thickness or temperature. Linear gradients are one approximation; nonlinear and discontinuous fields need different treatment. Common centroid Equality of normalized first moments for the relevant contributions. It cancels linear-field mismatch under the assumed response model; it does not certify routing or LDE equality. Electrical weight and moment A contribution or sensitivity determines how each unit samples a field. The weights depend on circuit composition and whether perturbations are absolute or fractional. Higher-order moment A weighted average of coordinate products such as x², xy or y². Matching every relevant monomial cancels the corresponding polynomial-field terms under a linear response. Covariance Joint variation between parameters or units. A valid covariance matrix is positive semidefinite; for linearized error e=dᵀδp, its variance is dᵀΣd. Pelgrom-style area law A characterized inverse-area variance model, often expressed as σ(ΔV TH )=A VT /√(WL). Pair versus individual-device conventions and validity limits must be explicit. Layout-dependent effect (LDE) A device-parameter change caused by surrounding geometry or process context. A zero centroid residual can coexist with substantial LDE mismatch. Well proximity effect (WPE) Device changes related to well-implant processing near well edges. Use the PDK's geometry descriptors and model rather than one universal edge distance. LOD and STI context Length-of-diffusion and shallow-trench-isolation surroundings can alter stress and transistor behavior. Active-edge distances and dummy arrangements differ from well-edge descriptors. Thermomechanical stress Mechanical stress arising from materials, temperature and assembly. Its electrical effect depends on device orientation and process; absolute stress and mismatch-causing stress differences differ. See gradient matching , random mismatch and thermal placement .","url":"glossary.html#glossary-matching"},{"title":"Glossary · Interconnect, noise and passive behavior","text":"Interconnect, noise and passive behavior Sheet resistance Resistance of a square of a uniform conducting film, independent of square size in the ideal model. A strip gives R=R□L/W before access corrections. Contact, via and access resistance Resistance through interfaces, vertical connections and current-spreading regions. N cuts need not behave as N equal parallel resistors when access is nonuniform. Capacitance matrix A node-resolved relationship between charge and voltage. For linear incremental models, a physical capacitor between nodes adds positive diagonal and negative mutual entries. Capacitive coupling Current induced by changing voltage difference across mutual capacitance. Victim voltage depends on impedance, waveform and timing, not only on coupling capacitance. Charge injection and clock feedthrough Switch-channel charge redistribution and capacitive transfer of control transitions, respectively. Their effects depend on switching sequence, terminal impedances and the complete capacitance network. Kelvin and star connections Kelvin wiring separates force and sense paths at a specified physical boundary. A star controls shared impedance by selecting where branch currents join. Shield A deliberately connected conductor that changes field coupling. Its reference impedance, continuity and added loading determine effectiveness; a shield label alone proves nothing. Thermal noise Equilibrium fluctuations associated with dissipation. An ideal resistor's classical one-sided open-circuit voltage-noise density is 4k B TR V²/Hz within that model's frequency range. This is a foundational Johnson–Nyquist law; no exact derivation or equation locator was found in the supplied corpus. See the provenance gap and conditional derivation before citing it. kT/C noise The voltage variance k B T/C for an ideal capacitor thermalized through resistance. It is not a universal bound for every switched-capacitor topology or correlated sampling scheme. Quality factor and self-resonance Q measures reactive storage relative to loss under a stated definition. Parasitic capacitance creates self-resonant behavior; inductance and DC resistance alone are insufficient RF specifications. See parasitics , signal routing , power/reference paths and RF primitives .","url":"glossary.html#glossary-networks"},{"title":"Glossary · Manufacturing, verification and reliability","text":"Manufacturing, verification and reliability DRC Design-rule checking of declared geometric and derived-layer conditions. Passing means the evaluated deck's checks passed on that geometry, not that circuit performance is guaranteed. LVS Layout-versus-schematic comparison of extracted devices and connectivity. Its parameter tolerances, device recognition and treatment of parasitics define the claim it supports. ERC Electrical-rule checking, potentially including floating regions, voltage compatibility and prohibited terminal connections. The actual deck and supplied scenarios determine coverage. PEX Parasitic extraction of layout-dependent electrical networks or parameters. Accuracy depends on the technology model, geometric interpretation, node mapping and retained frequency/physical effects. DFM Design for manufacturability: improving or checking process robustness and yield beyond nominal functional intent. It includes process-specific concerns rather than one generic margin. Antenna damage Plasma-induced charging damage to vulnerable dielectrics during fabrication. Checks depend on process-stage connectivity, exposed geometry, oxide family and qualified discharge structures. Electromigration (EM) Current-driven atomic transport that can produce voids, extrusions or unacceptable resistance change. Local current, material, temperature, time and boundary conditions govern reliability. Blech effect Back-stress can oppose electromigration within a suitably confined diffusion domain. A qualified critical jL condition is not permission to subdivide arbitrary routing segments into “safe” lengths. IR drop Voltage differences caused by current through resistance. A branched supply or signal net requires its current distribution; total wire resistance alone is insufficient. Safe operating area (SOA) A qualified operating envelope over voltage, current, pulse duration, temperature and relevant device state. Static, switching and reverse-bias conditions can have different limits. ESD and EOS Electrostatic discharge is a particular transient stress category; electrical overstress is broader. A qualified ESD rating does not guarantee survival of every sustained fault. Latchup Self-sustaining regenerative conduction through parasitic structures, often involving coupled bipolar action. Injection, well/substrate resistance, supply conditions and physical stack affect susceptibility. Guard ring A qualified surrounding structure used for contacting, collecting or blocking carriers. Carrier type, junction depth, bias and return impedance determine its actual mechanism. TDDB Time-dependent dielectric breakdown under electrical stress. Lifetime evaluation needs the appropriate dielectric, field, area, temperature and stress-history model. Hot-carrier degradation and bias-temperature instability (BTI) Distinct stress-induced device-aging mechanisms. Bias, temperature and duty history influence parameter drift; matched initial devices can age differently under unequal conditions. See verification , EM , protection and substrate isolation .","url":"glossary.html#glossary-checks"},{"title":"Glossary · Circuit metrics and evidence","text":"Circuit metrics and evidence Figure of merit (FOM) A selected scalar combination of performance quantities. Normalization, signs and feasibility handling matter; improving FOM can otherwise conceal failure of one specification. Settling time Time to enter and remain within a specified error band after a defined event. State loading, stimulus, reference value and whether slew or ringing dominates. INL Integral nonlinearity: deviation from a specified ideal or fitted transfer reference, usually in LSB. Endpoint and best-fit conventions can give different values. DNL Differential nonlinearity: DAC step size or ADC code-bin width divided by the specified ideal LSB, minus one. Define transitions and endpoint treatment. CMRR and PSRR Common-mode and power-supply rejection ratios, with specified input/output referencing and frequency. Device and route imbalance can convert shared disturbances into differential output. Noise spectral density Noise power or variance per bandwidth; amplitude density is its square root. State one- versus two-sided convention and integrate with the relevant transfer function. Offset The input or output correction needed to reach a specified balance condition. Separate input-referred, output-referred, systematic, random and temperature/aging-dependent components. Gain, bandwidth and phase margin Different transfer- and loop-response measures. The selected loop break, operating point and load matter; increased gain or bandwidth alone does not establish stability. SNDR and SFDR Signal-to-noise-and-distortion ratio compares signal power to combined unwanted content; spurious-free dynamic range compares it to the largest specified spur. Analysis conventions matter. Predicted yield Estimated probability of meeting the complete specification under a declared variation model. Sampling uncertainty and model uncertainty remain separate from measured manufacturing yield. See performance budgets , DAC arrays , robust design and validation .","url":"glossary.html#glossary-metrics"},{"title":"The Art of Analog Layout · Source, coverage, and limits of these notes","text":"Source, coverage, and limits of these notes Source: Alan Hastings, The Art of Analog Layout , third edition, supplied 2023 Pearson file. This copy contains 1,568 physical PDF pages. The front matter is on PDF pages 1–25, the fifteen chapters on pages 26–828, appendices on pages 829–876, the index on pages 877–976, and accessible long descriptions on pages 977–1568. Those last pages largely describe illustrations already embedded in the book; they are not another 592 pages of independent chapters. Links below use the one-based physical PDF page , not the printed page numbers in the contents. The visible running number in this reflowed file is also often one less than the PDF page. These notes combine a survey of every chapter and appendix with deeper reading of the portions that can guide an automated placer, cell generator, router, and verifier. They are an extensive engineering synthesis, not a claim that every paragraph, exercise, reference, or accessible description was read line by line . Foundation chapters and historical fabrication variants were sampled to understand their mechanisms; matching, device interaction, reliability, power distribution, and top-level interconnection received greater attention. The table records the actual depth. No engine files were changed. Book region Physical PDF pages Coverage in this research pass 1. Device Physics 26–66 Survey, carrier/junction foundations and summary; used to distinguish majority conduction, minority injection, depletion, and contact potentials. 2. Semiconductor Fabrication 67–118 Survey with samples on oxide topography, implantation, isolation, interconnect technologies, CMP, and assembly. 3. Layout 119–153 Survey; focused on rule construction, process bias, mask alignment, grid, hierarchy, and OPC. 4. Representative Processes 154–210 Comparative survey of standard bipolar, poly-gate CMOS, and analog BiCMOS; historical examples treated as explanations, not a deployable PDK. 5. Failure Mechanisms 211–274 Detailed antenna reading, reliability summary, and targeted self-heating, EM, injection, debiasing, and collecting/blocking guard-ring discussions. 6. Resistors 275–325 Targeted geometry, corners/heads, temperature, conductivity modulation, contacts, hydrogenation, parasitics; survey of material choices and trim methods. 7. Capacitors and Inductors 326–366 Survey across capacitor types, dielectric and parasitic mechanisms; focused inductor parasitics and construction guidelines. 8. Matching of Resistors and Capacitors 367–428 Detailed matching rule catalog and centroid/dispersion/segmentation/thermoelectric discussion; targeted stress, hydrogenation, electric-field and package mechanisms. 9. Bipolar Transistors 429–483 Survey with samples on saturation, current hogging, collector/base construction, high-voltage and fast transistor variants. 10. Applications of Bipolar Transistors 484–533 Detailed vertical/lateral matching rules; targeted thermal, emitter sharing, power-device failure and package-stress discussion. 11. Diodes 534–573 Device-family survey and targeted PN, Zener and Schottky matching; no assumption that all diodes obey a common mismatch model. 12. Field-Effect Transistors 574–642 Survey with targeted well isolation, source/drain sharing, body contacts, annular devices, breakdown, NVM and JFET construction. 13. Applications of MOS Transistors 643–721 Detailed MOS matching rules and CC/orientation; targeted LDE, bias/aging, power metallization, SOA, senseFET and high-voltage construction. 14. Special Topics 722–780 Survey throughout; targeted device mergers, process-specific guard rings, ESD behavior, local protection and discharge paths. 15. Assembling the Die 781–828 Survey of area/floorplan/padring; focused routing, Kelvin connections, noise, voltage/current-dependent rules, and final checklist. Appendices A–E 829–876 Acronym/crystal/rule references surveyed; derivation topics inventoried; resistor weighting and distributed metallization equations inspected selectively. Index and accessible descriptions 877–1568 Identified and sampled for navigation and diagram interpretation. Not independently read in full or counted as fresh technical chapters. The PDF text extraction omits many equations because they are rendered as images. Pages 390 , 401 , 650 , 697 , 709 , 822 , and 871–872 were rendered and visually checked for critical equations and diagrams. Numerical tables with missing extraction were not reconstructed by guessing. Proposed formulas below are labeled when they generalize rather than reproduce the source. Applicability boundary. The book explicitly retains older processes to teach physical principles. Its illustrative dimensions, voltage limits, lifetime assumptions, matching grades, doping profiles, packaging conventions, and material comparisons do not become foundry rules merely by appearing in a textbook. These notes identify what to characterize or verify before a rule can support a production claim. This source is particularly valuable for planar analog CMOS, bipolar, BiCMOS, and power processes; it is not a complete treatment of FinFET/GAA layout, modern multi-patterning, or every foundry's reliability qualification.","url":"hastings.html#coverage"},{"title":"The Art of Analog Layout · What “optimal” should mean to the engine","text":"What “optimal” should mean to the engine Source finding. The text repeatedly trades area against precision, compactness against local interactions, shielding against other physical effects, and device size against parasitic load. Its chapter 8 discussion explicitly explains that adding more sections to a fixed-area array can reduce gradient error while increasing random mismatch because spacing consumes active area. It also states that a CC arrangement offers no matching benefit if the relevant gradients are absent. PDF pp. 392–394 Philis interpretation. Define the optimization problem from the circuit's allowed error, operating scenarios, process, package, manufacturing variation, and lifetime. First require electrical connectivity, legal devices, manufacturable geometry, and relevant reliability limits. Then compare feasible candidates on extracted circuit performance, mismatch/yield, area, power loss, and routing resources. Record unresolved checks as unknown rather than silently assuming they passed. “Optimal” should identify the objective, evaluated scenarios, model fidelity, and alternatives considered. For example, minimizing transistor separation helps with a smooth temperature gradient, but placing a hot transistor beside a precision pair can overwhelm that benefit. A grounded shield can reduce capacitive interference while adding load and changing hydrogenation or mechanical surroundings. A small mirror may route beautifully yet fail the current-ratio target. Each objective therefore needs an error mechanism and an operating context; a weighted sum of geometric preferences cannot by itself certify analog performance. Use two levels of explanation for each accepted or rejected move: the local reason —for example, this via arrangement lowers the maximum current density—and the circuit consequence —for example, it preserves the specified current under the hot operating scenario without increasing resistance beyond its voltage-drop budget. Preserve both because a rule can be physically valid yet irrelevant to the circuit being optimized.","url":"hastings.html#optimality"},{"title":"The Art of Analog Layout · Fabrication knowledge must travel with the geometry","text":"Fabrication knowledge must travel with the geometry Chapters 1–4 explain why shapes on different layers are not interchangeable obstacles. Diffusion extends beyond the drawn opening; depletion extends beyond the metallurgical junction; implanted dopants have tails; oxide steps alter topography; local pattern density alters printing, etching, or polishing; contacts and vias depend on the actual metal process. Minimum dimensions include manufacturing tolerances and electrical requirements. Consequently, a device footprint alone cannot express all interactions. §§2.3–2.6, PDF pp. 79–95 , §2.7, PDF pp. 99–110 , §3.2.3, PDF pp. 138–144 Engine action Physical justification Scope and required evidence Store device variant, well/tank, implant, isolation and terminal semantics alongside polygons. The same drawn diffusion can serve as emitter, collector, resistor body, guard ring or unwanted junction depending on surrounding layers and bias. Use foundry-qualified primitives and extraction definitions. A DeviceKind label alone is insufficient. Distinguish drawn geometry from effective electrical geometry. Etch bias, diffusion and depletion change active dimensions; contacts and heads can change the effective length of a resistor. Use PCell/model parameters and fitted bias terms within their characterized geometry range. Attach an allowed-transform set to each primitive and matched group. Rotating a subcell can break relative device orientation. Directional implants and non-self-aligned dimensions can invalidate apparently harmless reflections. Transform terminal directions, active centroids, well-edge distances and environmental descriptors with the geometry. Treat fill, exclusions, slots and protective layers as part of the final candidate. CMP topography, hydrogen diffusion, mechanical stress, and capacitive coupling depend on nearby material, including electrically inactive shapes. Rerun critical checks after fill insertion, slotting and final mask operations, not only before them. Use technology-specific contact/via templates and actual polygon grids. Old aluminum openings and planarized plug vias have different stacking and enclosure constraints; legal path centerlines can still produce off-grid edges. Validate the emitted shapes against the actual rule deck. Do not infer stackability or enclosure symmetry from layer count. Separate process features that exist from fallbacks that merely produce geometry. A deep well, buried layer or trench cannot provide isolation if the process does not fabricate the required continuous structure. Missing characterization should produce a capability limitation or conservative alternative, never a invented default guarantee. Chapter 4's standard bipolar, polysilicon-gate CMOS and analog BiCMOS examples are valuable as contrasting process stacks: their substrate/well topology, available devices and parasitics differ. They argue for a process capability model rather than a single universal rulebook. Appendix C's example rules are teaching data, useful for synthetic generator exercises; they must not override installed PDK rules. PDF pp. 154–210 , 836–844","url":"hastings.html#process"},{"title":"The Art of Analog Layout · Matching requires the right quantity, the right centroid, and the right environment","text":"Matching requires the right quantity, the right centroid, and the right environment Source finding. Random variation, systematic dimensional bias, neighboring-pattern effects, contact/interconnect resistance, temperature, stress and electric fields are distinct mechanisms. Increasing active area averages some random fluctuations; it does not automatically remove systematic error. The simpler inverse-square-root-of-area relation is an approximation when areal variation dominates peripheral variation. The book also distinguishes absolute accuracy from ratio matching and from resolution. PDF pp. 368–375 , 418 For equal, comparable MOS devices in the regime where the fitted model applies, Hastings uses the familiar relation σ(ΔV TH ) = A VT /√(WL). The coefficient is obtained from measurements. The text explicitly cautions against overtrusting its oxide-thickness rule of thumb, discusses backgate-bias dependence, and gives exceptions involving small geometries and pocket implants. That is evidence for storing a mismatch model per device family and operating regime, not treating one dimensionless ratio of gradient to random error as universal. PDF pp. 687–690 Centroids are electrical, not simply centers of occupied rectangles For a conventional MOS device, the active area is the intersection of gate and active silicon. Its centroid can differ from the cell bounding-box center because contacts, guard rings, dummies and routing occupy additional space. For arbitrary resistor networks, equal-sized drawn segments need not have equal effects on the resulting resistance. Hastings gives sensitivity-weighted coordinates: X R = Σ[(∂R/∂R i ) X i ] / Σ(∂R/∂R i ), with the analogous expression for Y R . This transcription was checked visually against equations 8.24–8.25. A series element and an element in a parallel branch can contribute different weights even when their shapes are identical. PDF p. 390 , 704–705 Generalization for Philis. Weight geometry by the sensitivity to the physical field actually being modeled. If a field creates equal absolute changes in segment resistance, the source's ∂R/∂R i weights apply. If it instead produces equal fractional changes, the weights also contain R i ; for other parameters use the corresponding local derivative. Normalize the weights, define the covered operating point, and preserve the same normalization on both matched sides. This avoids a false “zero centroid error” obtained by averaging quantities with different electrical influence. Common centroid is a first-order tool Coincident centroids cancel a linear spatial contribution under the assumptions behind that averaging. They do not cancel all higher-order variation, local discontinuities, arbitrary coupling, or different terminal voltages. Hastings' four passive-device rules are coincidence, symmetry, subdivision into smaller common-centroid groups, and compactness; MOS devices add orientation. The revised third-edition notion of dispersion specifically favors repeatable small balanced subarrays over merely spreading labels uniformly. PDF pp. 392–394 , 709–711 Evaluate a candidate using normalized active first moments, second moments and local environment descriptors, followed by extracted electrical checks. First moments test a linear field; differences in x², xy and y² moments reveal possible quadratic sensitivity. These additional moments are our proposed diagnostic, not a claim that Hastings supplies a complete polynomial optimizer. Verify them against synthetic fields before using them to rank layouts. Compactness is a useful heuristic when the field is unknown; it is not a proof that one arrangement dominates every possible gradient. Counterexample to compulsory segmentation: if device area is fixed, more contacts, dummies and spacing reduce usable active area and increase resistance/capacitance. If random mismatch dominates and local gradients are weak, a simpler arrangement may perform better. Generate a small Pareto set of segment counts and aspect ratios, then compare their estimated and extracted error contributions.","url":"hastings.html#matching-model"},{"title":"The Art of Analog Layout · Resistors: geometry, connectivity and electric fields are inseparable","text":"Resistors: geometry, connectivity and electric fields are inseparable Chapter 6 shows why a resistor is not just sheet resistance multiplied by drawn length/width. It has dimension biases, contact crowding, end effects, corner effects, nonlinear voltage and temperature dependence, distributed capacitance, and sometimes parasitic junctions. For a right-angle equal-width corner, the book gives 0.5587 squares rather than the approximate half-square mnemonic. This small difference matters if the algorithm uses many corners to obtain a precision ratio. Dogbone heads do not inherently make two devices match better: identical systematic head contributions can cancel, while unmatched heads cannot. PDF pp. 279–295 Recommended action Reason Qualification or verification Preserve material, implant, sheet-resistance option, width and unit shape across a matched group. Different materials have different process, temperature, stress and voltage responses. Unequal widths convert a common linewidth error into a ratio error; unequal heads/corners add non-ratiometric resistance. Use identical rectangular units where feasible. If a circuit requires unequal units, evaluate their bias sensitivity and end/contact terms explicitly. Select area and width from an allocated random-error budget. More area averages microscopic fluctuations; wider strips reduce edge and linewidth sensitivity. The smaller resistor can dominate a highly unequal ratio. Fit the material's area/periphery model. The textbook's width multipliers and matching grades are provisional guidance, not process-qualified constants. Keep electrically matched directions parallel. Silicon and poly respond differently to stress along and across current flow. A rotated rectangle can have the correct nominal resistance but a different package shift. Record wafer orientation and material dependence. Do not automatically rotate P-type silicon by 45° without a supported PCell and process permission. Use compact balanced banks with equal pitch, preserving symmetry within each bank. Close proximity reduces field differences; smaller CC subarrays limit nonlinear residues. A long single row can span a larger thermal/stress field. Include the area and routing cost of banks. Prioritize the electrically most critical ratios when several devices share an array. Provide appropriate edge dummies and comparable end extensions. Array boundaries experience different etch loading, diffusion tails and lithographic surroundings. A dummy equalizes the environment of an active edge strip. Match active-to-dummy pitch; use process-characterized dummy extent. Contact/tie dummies where necessary to avoid charge accumulation without disturbing sensitive nodes. Avoid excessively short segments and unnecessary bends. Heads, contacts, dopant encroachment and grain boundaries become a larger share of a short resistor. Serpentines compound corner and mask-alignment terms. Use bends when their area benefit justifies a modeled error. Moderate/exceptional matching in Hastings generally favors arrays of repeatable strips. Search segmentation choices for noninteger ratios. A fixed additive error per segment changes two resistors differently if their segment counts do not scale with their intended values. Evaluate the source's segmentation-sensitivity concept using actual head/contact models. Center partial sections within the surrounding geometry and preserve their local environment. Pair opposite electrical traversals of series sections. Contact potentials vary with contact temperature. A thermal gradient along every strip can make their thermoelectric voltages add even when array centroids coincide. Prefer an even number of series sections where compatible with the circuit; test endpoint temperatures and connection polarity, not only placement labels. Match the substrate/field-oxide context and keep disruptive process boundaries away. Underlying topography affects deposited film thickness and parasitics; oxide steps, STI stress, diffusion tails, doping-mask edges and an NBL shadow can create unequal local properties. Use capability flags and characterized exclusion distances. NBL-shadow precautions are relevant only to processes that actually retain that topography. Control nearby and overlying metal, including automatic fill. Metal changes hydrogen access and electric fields. Folded-in connections over only some strips create a different process environment even if nominal interconnect R is balanced. Compare matched coverage or a qualified field-plate strategy. Folded-out routing reduces coverage asymmetry but may require more vias and resistance. Account for tank/body and shield voltage along every segment. Depletion changes conducting cross-section; a common body tied to one rail can create unequal modulation across a divider. A shared shield can itself impose unequal fields. Use extracted body-connected device models. Separate bodies or segmented field plates may help, at added area and capacitance; do not silently alter circuit connections. Limit self-heating and separate sensitive arrays from external heaters. Deposited resistors sit above thermally resistive oxide; unequal power creates local gradients in addition to package-scale gradients. Compute P = I²R over scenarios and use a qualified thermal model. A common ambient temperature is insufficient. Budget metal, contacts, vias and sense-lead drops as part of the ratio. Equal resistor bodies do not guarantee equal terminal resistance. A 2:1 ratio can require each material's interconnect contribution to scale 2:1, because their temperature coefficients differ. Extract terminal-to-terminal resistance by material and topology; insert physical Kelvin/star connections when circuit intent requires them. Keep accurately matched poly away from process features that perturb coating/etching. The text describes large poly-poly capacitor structures affecting photoresist flow and therefore nearby resistor or gate patterning. Characterize the interaction range for the actual stack; a generic geometric nonoverlap test cannot detect it. The detailed source catalog contains 25 resistor recommendations and explains their matching-grade dependence. The table above groups them by mechanism so Philis can avoid encoding the same physical effect several times under different heuristic names. PDF pp. 418–422 Thermoelectrics are a connectivity test, not a centroid test Equation 8.34 is E T = S ΔT C , where S is the contact Seebeck coefficient and ΔT C is the temperature difference between a segment's contacts. Figure 8.22, visually checked here, contrasts connections that add these voltages with connections that largely cancel them. The engine should trace the ordered path through each series chain and accumulate signed thermoelectric sources. This is separate from matching the average resistance under a temperature field. Close placement of serpentine heads reduces endpoint temperature difference, but their orientation must also address mask-alignment sensitivity. PDF pp. 401–402 Material and trim decisions. Thin-film, poly, diffusion, well, metal and pinched resistors occupy different places in the accuracy/area/power/parasitic trade space. Diffused resistors can dissipate heat more effectively through silicon, while deposited resistors avoid the same junction parasitics. Trimming can compensate a value at its calibration condition, but cannot generally remove thermal gradients, material-dependent temperature behavior, hysteresis or later drift. Preserve trim accessibility, programming-current paths and exclusions when trim structures are present; component substitution or adding trim is a circuit-design choice, not a permissible placement repair. PDF pp. 295–323 , 418–422","url":"hastings.html#resistors"},{"title":"The Art of Analog Layout · Capacitors: match the whole charge-transfer network","text":"Capacitors: match the whole charge-transfer network The book distinguishes junction, MOS, poly-poly, metal-metal, stacked, lateral-flux and trench capacitors. Their voltage dependence, leakage, plate resistance, substrate parasitics, dielectric absorption, breakdown and density differ. Two terminals on a schematic do not imply interchangeable plates in layout. Even when the intended capacitance matches perfectly, bottom-plate or lead coupling can change charge redistribution. PDF pp. 327–354 Action Why it improves the relevant behavior Limit or validation Use identical unit structures for integer ratios, with a consistent plate assignment. A common dimensional bias and peripheral capacitance then scale with unit count rather than creating a different fractional error for each capacitor. Prefer parallel composition where consistent with the schematic. Series combinations expose asymmetric plate parasitics and require circuit-level analysis. For noninteger ratios, preserve the relevant area/perimeter relationship and verify corners. Matching area alone does not match fringe contributions; complex outlines introduce additional OPC and corner effects. Hastings discusses a nonunit capacitor supplement. Use field extraction to validate it instead of assuming equal A/P is exact. Prefer compact square or nearly square units and search unit size. A compact unit lowers perimeter per area; subdividing a large capacitor can reduce gradient error. Excessively small units make edge error and wiring dominant. Choose from PDK-supported cells; measure the ratio of random, peripheral, gradient and routing contributions over candidate sizes. Place matched units adjacently with equal row and column pitch, using compact CC arrangements when justified. Dielectric thickness and other fields vary spatially. Matched first moments suppress their first-order contribution. Row pitch need not equal column pitch. Include access tracks, dummies and routing before evaluating compactness. Preserve the same structure beneath every active unit. Different underlying oxide, moat edges, lower-metal patterns or topography change capacitance and local strain. Reserve controlled under-array regions. Either a qualified continuous plate or a qualified absence of lower material is preferable to accidental asymmetric routing. Give all array edges a characterized dummy environment. Dummies reduce edge etch differences and uncontrolled coupling from neighboring circuitry. Hastings recommends surrounding capacitor arrays on all four sides. Tie dummy electrodes to an intentional node; floating dummies can acquire charge or unpredictable coupling. Shield against external electric fields where the added load is acceptable. A low-impedance shield makes the electrostatic boundary more predictable and can contain fringe fields. Extract shield resistance and capacitance. Do not permit a noisy line over an array merely because a shield exists; attenuation is finite. Match the leads by capacitance, including their surroundings. Lead width, layer, crossings, neighbors and fill alter vertical and lateral coupling. Equal length alone is not equal capacitance. Extract a capacitance matrix. Dummy extensions or jogs are justified only if they reduce the circuit's ratio error without violating total-load, settling or antenna budgets. Assign the more substrate-coupled plate using circuit impedance and noise context. A low-impedance terminal is less disturbed by injected substrate charge than a high-impedance sensitive node. Preserve top/bottom plate intent from the circuit designer. A well shield or isolated structure brings additional junctions, bias constraints and area. Choose dielectric and electrode options using measured mismatch and voltage behavior. Electrode depletion, dielectric variability, dielectric relaxation and stress sensitivity differ strongly among capacitor types. The text favors certain thick, homogeneous dielectrics and metallic electrodes for precision; the actual PDK's characterization decides availability and merit. Separate extreme-accuracy arrays from strong temperature/stress gradients. Even comparatively stable capacitors have residual thermal and mechanical sensitivity; high-permittivity materials can be especially stress-sensitive. Package, temperature and actual capacitor family determine the needed precaution. “All capacitors are temperature-insensitive” is not a safe simplification. These actions synthesize the thirteen matching recommendations and their antecedents. PDF pp. 423–425 , 380–382 Philis acceptance criterion. For a switched-capacitor network, assess the extracted network in each switch phase and under the intended charge-transfer operation. An absolute capacitance error, a ratio error, a parasitic to a quiet rail and a parasitic to a switching aggressor have different consequences. The physical unit pattern should therefore be evaluated together with its route topology and phase-dependent connections.","url":"hastings.html#capacitors"},{"title":"The Art of Analog Layout · MOS: distinguish voltage matching, current matching, orientation and LDE","text":"MOS: distinguish voltage matching, current matching, orientation and LDE Hastings begins MOS matching by distinguishing equal-current voltage matching from equal-gate-voltage current matching. Threshold and current-factor variation enter these cases differently. In the long-channel, strong-inversion, saturation approximation at fixed drain current, increasing width both averages threshold variation and raises transconductance; these effects can cancel in the relative current-error expression. Increasing length can still help. This is the basis of the source's current-matching versus voltage-matching sizing guidance—not a universal theorem for arbitrary compact models, inversion levels or pocket-implanted devices. PDF pp. 686–690 , 712–714 Propagate mismatch through the actual operating-point sensitivities. A useful first-order decomposition is δI ≈ (∂I/∂V TH )δV TH + (∂I/∂β)δβ + (∂I/∂V DS )δV DS + (∂I/∂V BS )δV BS , including covariance where characterized. This is an implementation proposal, not a replacement compact model. Use derivatives from supported device models and simulation; distinguish fixed-current, fixed-voltage and ratioed operation. Orientation is a terminal-direction property Equation 13.61 defines Φ = (1/N)ΣΦ i for identical units, with Φ i = +1 or −1 according to drain-current direction. Equal signed orientation is required; equal absolute magnitude is insufficient. Three right-oriented and one left-oriented unit give +1/2, as do nine right-oriented and three left-oriented units. Zero is convenient but is not compulsory. In two dimensions, track both directional components. The formula and tilted-implant diagram were visually checked. PDF pp. 708–711 During pattern construction, retain source/drain labels and legal sharing boundaries. During hierarchy transforms, rotate or reflect the orientation descriptor consistently. Add a test in which two macros have identical outlines and coincident centroids but opposite normalized source/drain directions: the engine should detect the mismatch. For asymmetric drain-extended devices, use explicitly qualified transformations; apparent geometric mirroring can change a non-self-aligned channel length under mask misalignment. PDF p. 691 Action Physical reason Scope or tradeoff Use identical unit dimensions and variants; preserve intended series/parallel topology. Unequal W/L, oxide options and edge proportions create systematic and random differences. Series-stacked units do not exactly reproduce an arbitrary long device. Use circuit-authorized unitization; validate effective dimensions, terminal voltages and body connections after extraction. Measure equal distances and environments around wells, active edges, implants and STI. Well implant scattering and length-of-diffusion stress change local threshold/current factor. Outer fingers can differ from interior fingers even at equal nominal W/L. Use PDK LDE parameters and known geometry domains. Simple pair spacing cannot encode the full environment. Add end dummies and active extensions appropriate to the technology. They regularize poly etch loading and distance to STI, helping both dimensional and stress matching. Half dummies can save area but may be unrecognized by DRC/LVS or bring a well/moat boundary too close. Full, qualified dummies are often safer to generate. Tie dummy gates to suppress unwanted channels. An electrically active dummy adds leakage and parasitic capacitance; its bias can also change the local electrical environment. Use a valid tie for the device polarity, well and available rails, not an unconditional ground connection. Keep gate overhang, nearby poly and gate-connection patterns comparable. Poly corners and neighboring shapes alter etch behavior. Metal straps can connect gates without creating unequal poly environments beside active channels. Gate-contact placement and strap geometry still require resistance, antenna and matching checks. Avoid contacts over critical active gate regions and control over-gate metal/fill. The source associates local processing and hydrogen passivation differences with increased mismatch. Uniform-looking metal coverage is not necessarily electrically neutral. Hastings discourages over-gate plates for its moderate/exceptional MOS matching classes; many passive devices instead benefit from shielding. Treat this as a family/process-specific rule. Use compact CC subarrays where gradient sensitivity warrants them. Coincidence handles first order; compactness and smaller balanced subarrays reduce exposure to nonlinear fields. Two-dimensional layouts help when they actually become more compact. Complex patterns can impose disproportionate gate-routing and parasitic costs. Use measured bias dependence, including body and drain conditions. Body effect, channel-length modulation and impact ionization create current errors independent of centroid equality. Layout can reduce unequal IR drops and body resistance. It cannot insert a cascode or change device bias without circuit authorization. Account for the planned stress history. Different V DS , V GS , duty cycles and temperatures can produce unequal hot-carrier and bias-temperature aging. Evaluate time-dependent circuit error with the qualified aging model. Initial matching is not a lifetime guarantee. Respect pocket-implant and subthreshold exceptions. Pocket implants can make mismatch stop improving with channel length; edge/stringer conduction can dominate at low currents. Do not infer improvement from a larger W×L alone. Special annular or fin-like layouts require qualified generators and models. Select low-stress, thermally suitable locations using the actual package. Global package stress, bumps/pillars and power devices generate different spatial fields. The preferred central region of a leaded molded package is not a universal rule for a bumped die. Use device-type comparisons only where characterized. The book discusses studies in which NMOS matched better than PMOS and others in which the reverse occurred. Never choose transistor polarity, oxide thickness or diagonal orientation from a universal ranking. Voltage ratings, noise and circuit function also constrain those choices. The detailed mechanism discussions are at PDF pp. 692–706 , and the twenty-four-item matching catalog is at 712–717 . The LOD discussion is on PDF page 697; Figure 13.52 on PDF page 698 was visually inspected: its proposed active extensions and dummies address distance to STI, not an abstract “dummy count.” That distinction matters when comparing two generated arrays with the same number of dummy fingers but different active envelopes.","url":"hastings.html#mos"},{"title":"The Art of Analog Layout · Bipolar transistors and diodes need their own matching and interaction rules","text":"Bipolar transistors and diodes need their own matching and interaction rules Vertical bipolar devices. Match emitter geometry and use identical unit emitters for ratios. Active emitter area and perimeter determine injection, while base resistance, base/emitter contacts, collector resistance, Early effect and saturation alter the terminal currents. Compact clusters and common-centroid arrays help with temperature and stress fields, but contact and base distributions also need balancing. Placing collector contacts outside an array can bring its critical emitters closer together. When emitters share a base, do not blindly exploit the smaller “connected-emitter” spacing: overlapping depletion regions can alter carrier flow even when the terminals share a net. PDF pp. 506–526 Lateral bipolar devices. Both emitter and collector geometry matter because current flows laterally between them. A larger single emitter can reduce current gain, so the source often favors arrays of small emitters rather than simply enlarging one device. Match the collector's inner perimeter, base/contact environment and applicable field plates. A saturated collector can reinject carriers into neighboring collectors; equal nominal base nets do not make shared tanks harmless. Separate isolation regions can be worth their area where collector efficiency or saturation threatens matching. PDF pp. 527–529 Operating-point limits. Keep precision bipolar devices in their characterized current-density range, below problematic high-level injection and away from low-current leakage/recombination dominance. Equalize collector voltages when the circuit requires suppression of Early-effect error. Emitter degeneration transfers sensitivity from a transistor to a resistor and costs voltage headroom; it is an architectural option, not something the placer can add. Guard against reverse emitter-base stress, which can degrade current gain before an obvious catastrophic failure. A ratioed bandgap pair also relies on accurate temperature tracking; a common centroid does not remove all nonlinear thermal behavior. PDF pp. 509–523 , 526 PN diodes. Diode-connected transistors inherit their parent family’s matching limitations. Control the actual regulated current: emitter-current regulation includes base current, so it is not identical to collector-current regulation. Match junction shape, contact arrangement, guard structures, material and surrounding diffusions. Distinguish series resistance from junction-voltage error and include leakage over temperature. PDF pp. 565–571 Zeners. Localized avalanche can let one region with the lowest breakdown voltage dominate the entire junction. Consequently, increasing area does not generally deliver inverse-square-root matching at low current. Surface walkout/walkback and unequal accumulated charge can produce later divergence. Use a characterized structure and lifecycle model; neither a circular outline nor CC placement can promise precise Zener matching. PDF pp. 566–567 Schottky diodes. Barrier material, inhomogeneity, contact perimeter, field relief and series resistance matter. A field-relief guard junction can improve field uniformity but can also inject minority carriers when it forward biases ( §11.2, PDF p. 563 ). The source's comparison of aluminum and noble-silicide Schottkies demonstrates why material-specific primitives are necessary; it is not a license to synthesize an unqualified barrier. PDF pp. 568–571 For Philis, a bipolar/diode matching rule should name the matched output—V BE , current ratio, forward drop or reverse breakdown—and the controlled terminal conditions. Reject the tempting abstraction “all matched devices get the MOS area formula.” Validate geometry through extraction and the circuit's operating scenarios, including potential saturation and transient injection.","url":"hastings.html#bipolar-diodes"},{"title":"The Art of Analog Layout · Inductors, power devices, JFETs and high-voltage structures","text":"Inductors, power devices, JFETs and high-voltage structures Integrated inductors. More turns do not monotonically improve performance. Winding resistance, skin/proximity effects, substrate eddy currents and parasitic capacitance compete with inductance and mutual coupling. High frequency makes conductor shape, neighboring metal and return paths part of the device. The source favors high-level, low-resistance metal, short leads, a controlled empty center and limited unrelated material nearby. A solid shield can create eddy-current loss; a patterned shield must interrupt the relevant current loops. Stacking metal in parallel is different from adding serial winding layers, which can greatly increase interwinding capacitance. PDF pp. 355–363 Treat RF passives as characterized electromagnetic cells with frequency-dependent port models, transform restrictions and exclusion regions. Evaluate Q, self-resonant behavior, loss and coupling across the specified band. Preserve equal terminal environments for differential inductors. Automatic fill and routing must honor these regions or trigger fresh EM extraction. Minimized DC resistance and a correct nominal inductance are insufficient acceptance criteria. Power bipolar devices. Emitter debiasing, thermal runaway and turnoff current focusing distinguish continuous, switching and pulsed operation. Base contact placement and ballast must be designed for those conditions. A structure good for a linear pass element can have poor reverse-bias safe operating area during turnoff. Repeating a small-signal transistor until its total area is large enough does not establish power capability. PDF pp. 485–505 Power MOS devices. Include silicon, metal and package contributions to on-resistance. Limit distributed gate delay, keep source/body resistance under control, and check electrical as well as electrothermal safe operating area. A positive temperature coefficient in some operating regions does not imply universal immunity to current localization: the text discusses impact-ionization feedback and the Spirito effect. SenseFET units must sample comparable temperature, terminal voltage and edge environments; a small sense element at the edge can see first-finger effects absent from the average main transistor. PDF pp. 644–668 The rule of one-third: useful approximation, with a source check For a uniformly distributed, singly terminated metal finger, the source derives an effective added resistance approximately one third of its disconnected end-to-end resistance. That differs from a lead delivering all its current to a load at the far end: current is collected or delivered continuously along the finger. Unequal material coverage, tapering or strong debiasing requires a fuller distributed model. PDF pp. 650–664 , 870–876 Using the visually checked definitions on pages 871–872, let r = R sheet /W metal be resistance per length, g = 1/(L R Si ) be silicon conductance per length, and u = L√(rg). The exact one-finger model gives R total = R Si u coth(u), so R metal ≈ rL/3 for small u. This notation renames the source's finger length W to L to avoid confusion with metal width. Apparent source inconsistency. Equation E.14 as displayed omits the square root implied by E.1, E.2 and E.7 when expressing γW. In the same derivation, the displayed E.6 has a plus sign for the second exponential term although E.3 and E.5 require a minus, and E.8 writes the current prefactor γ/G although differentiation and units require G/γ. Equation E.9 gives the consistent total-resistance expression used above. The audit therefore checks the differential equations and boundary conditions, not an uncorrected transcription of every displayed intermediate equation. Its quoted approximation accuracy also needs care: accuracy of total resistance is not automatically the same as relative accuracy of the much smaller added metal resistance. Implement the exact dimensionless model or validate the approximation against it; do not copy an isolated penetration-distance threshold without reconciling the equations. This is our algebraic check of the supplied edition, not a publisher-confirmed erratum. High-voltage, annular and JFET structures. Drift regions, RESURF, field plates, oxide options, body ties and isolation define their operation. Annular transistors are not ordinary rectangles with an interchangeable source and drain. JFET pinch-off and parasitic junctions depend on a process-specific channel construction. Embedded NVM likewise needs programming/erase voltage, endurance and antenna/protection considerations beyond its static footprint. Restrict automatic transformations and mergers to the characterized variants; offer unsupported structures as explicit capability gaps. PDF pp. 616–639 , 669–685","url":"hastings.html#rf-power"},{"title":"The Art of Analog Layout · Reliability and protection are constraints on paths, scenarios and time","text":"Reliability and protection are constraints on paths, scenarios and time Mechanism Required layout action and reason Evidence to retain Self-heating Size resistors, contacts, wires and power cells for their continuous and transient energy dissipation. Deposited material over oxide and short adiabatic pulses require different thermal reasoning. Current waveform, thermal boundary conditions, temperature rise and qualified material/device limits. Filamentation/current focusing Use qualified distributed contacts, ballasting and device shapes so a small conducting region does not carry destructive current. SOA/TLP or other relevant characterization of the actual structure; total drawn width alone is not proof. Electromigration Check every segment, neck, bend and via transition, accounting for current crowding and temperature. Low total resistance can coexist with one failing bottleneck. Foundry current/lifetime model, material, temperature, waveform and local current distribution. Dielectric breakdown Respect electric-field and lifetime limits, including voltage differences across adjacent shapes and gate/plate dielectrics. PDK voltage-dependent rules and qualified voltage, duty-cycle, area and temperature domain. Antenna damage Check connected conductors as they exist at each process stage, with the required area and perimeter measures. Use higher-layer jumpers or qualified discharge structures where indicated. Stage/layer connectivity, gate oxide family, exposed area/perimeter, diode credit and final foundry antenna results. ESD Place primary devices near pad entry and secondary protection near vulnerable terminals. Route complete low-impedance discharge paths and protect crossings between domains. Qualified clamp behavior, relevant stress model, path R/L, current capability and protected-terminal transient voltage. Latchup/debiasing Control minority injection and well/substrate potential using suitable isolation, contacts and carrier-specific guards. Cross-section connectivity, bias range, contact resistance, injector scenarios and foundry latchup qualification. Hot-carrier/BTI and long-term drift Avoid unequal stress histories where matching matters and preserve qualified bias limits. Include drift in the final error budget. Operating scenarios, duty cycles, aging models and matched differential degradation. Surface charge, contamination and package damage Use appropriate field plates, channel stops, seals, passivation openings and package exclusions. Actual process/assembly rules and supported device structures, rather than historical textbook distances. The physical mechanisms and summary are in PDF pp. 212–274 ; detailed ESD structures and networks are in 748–776 . Antenna is fabrication-history dependent. A long conductor may not yet connect to a gate when its layer is etched. Conversely, a small final connected component can have a problematic exposure history. The book distinguishes areal and peripheral ratios and discusses latent connections during incomplete etching. A higher-layer jumper changes the earlier connected component; it is not merely a change in final total wire area. A top-layer violation cannot be repaired by jumping to a nonexistent higher layer. A diode brings junction capacitance, leakage, placement and sometimes illumination requirements, all of which must be checked. PDF pp. 228–230 ESD protection must remain benign during operation. A snapback clamp has trigger and holding behavior, while a rate-triggered device responds to slew. A normal power-up ramp, a failsafe input above an unpowered supply, or an external source able to sustain holding current can therefore alter device choice. Extra rail width alone cannot repair a clamp that is inappropriate for the application. Primary pad protection also does not ensure a nearby thin gate survives fast local discharge; return points and local secondary clamps matter. The book's illustrated HBM/CDM values are examples from specific structures and eras, not current universal qualification requirements. PDF pp. 749–752 , 766–776","url":"hastings.html#protection"},{"title":"The Art of Analog Layout · Guard rings and device sharing require a cross-sectional model","text":"Guard rings and device sharing require a cross-sectional model A substrate tap usually reduces majority-carrier voltage drop. A minority-carrier collecting ring intercepts carriers at a suitable reverse-biased junction. A blocking ring uses an effective high-low junction or dielectric barrier to obstruct their path. These are different mechanisms even when their top views look like similar rings. The relevant carrier type, depth, buried layer, bias and contact resistance determine whether carriers pass beneath a ring, over a weak junction, or through an opening. PDF pp. 256–270 , 735–742 Engine actions and reasons: identify potential injectors over transient as well as DC scenarios; choose a qualified guard architecture for the actual substrate; ensure its required three-dimensional continuity; connect it to an appropriate low-impedance node; and verify the return path and local debiasing. A nominal enclosure without a sound electrical connection can fail. More width eventually gives diminishing benefit, and a ring that works on a heavily doped substrate may be marginal on a lightly doped one. A blocking ring typically needs complete enclosure; the text also discusses qualified collecting-ring arrangements near die edges that are not closed rectangles. Do not collapse these distinctions into “all rings must look alike.” Sharing is an electrical transformation. Merging devices in a common well/tank saves repeated isolation area but introduces shared resistance, junction capacitance and minority-carrier paths. Net equivalence is necessary for many mergers but not sufficient for safe operation. Saturating lateral PNPs, noisy isolated MOS devices, shared contacts carrying large current, and resistors whose bodies become forward biased illustrate the failure modes. Whitelist qualified merger templates with terminal/bias and injection conditions, and otherwise retain separate regions. A merge should be re-extracted and rechecked under scenarios, not approved merely because DRC and nominal LVS pass. PDF pp. 723–734 , 600","url":"hastings.html#isolation"},{"title":"The Art of Analog Layout · Routing and floorplanning finish the analog circuit","text":"Routing and floorplanning finish the analog circuit Kelvin and star connections. The physical point at which sense and force conductors join determines which voltage drop the circuit measures. If they merge before the intended device or pad, shared current creates an unwanted offset. Separate net names and schematic resistors can assist LVS but do not prove the junction is in the right physical location. Likewise, matched emitters or sources should join an intended star at the actual location where common-mode rather than differential IR drop is desired. Include finite sense current and lead resistance for high accuracy. PDF p. 818 Noise. Coupling depends on aggressor slew, coupling capacitance, victim impedance and timing, not merely the aggressor's average frequency. A configuration bit that switches infrequently can still upset a sensitive measurement. Use circuit roles to separate incompatible nets, controlled crossing geometry, and shields tied to the relevant quiet reference. Check the shield's impedance; a shared noisy return can defeat its purpose. Slowing a signal is a possible circuit-level remedy only when its timing permits it. PDF pp. 819–820 Voltage and current. High-voltage spacing depends on the maximum voltage difference between adjacent conductors under allowed scenarios, not just their voltage to ground. EM is a local bottleneck condition; resistance is an integrated path quantity. The source's via illustration shows why vias near the inside of a corner carry more current than nominally identical outer vias. Prefer appropriate straight transitions and sufficient distributed access, then check the actual current distribution. PDF pp. 821–822 Capacity and access. Route pitch must account for via footprint, enclosure and output-grid legality, not only minimum line spacing. Reserve space for wide supplies, shields, matched routes and local access before filling channels with ordinary signals. At intersecting channels, available tracks can disappear into turning and via demand; a channel-width scalar alone is an imperfect congestion model. Use blockage/access-aware estimation early enough to revise the floorplan. PDF pp. 811–817 Thermal and stress placement. Conventional molded-package stress often has low gradients near the die center despite large absolute stress there. Heat-source avoidance can favor an off-center location. Bumps, pillars, die attach, board loading and multiple changing power sources create different distributions. Optimize the mismatch-relevant field difference over the active region, not simply distance from a heater or absolute stress. Preserve useful symmetry under unequal power scenarios rather than assuming equal power forever. PDF pp. 399–411 , 525 , 715 Whole-die feasibility. Core area and pad-ring requirements can limit die size independently. Pads, ESD devices, guard rings, seal rings, scribe interfaces, bumps, bond trajectories and assembly exclusions all consume resources. Put these structures and the actual pin order into the early floorplan. A smaller core may produce no die-area saving on a pad-limited chip; it may instead free area for lower resistance, extra taps, shielding or matching improvements. PDF pp. 782–810 Finalization. The book ends with a checklist covering DRC/LVS, high-current and resistance checks, antenna, ESD, latchup, dummy exclusions, noisy/sensitive routing, pad requirements, Kelvin locations, corner/slot/seal requirements and archiving. Philis can translate this into a reproducible evidence bundle: final layout and libraries, exact process/rule/model versions, extraction, evaluated scenarios, pass/fail/unknown results and constraint provenance. This is stronger than retaining only a final objective score. PDF pp. 823–825","url":"hastings.html#routing-floorplan"},{"title":"The Art of Analog Layout · Direct implications for the current Philis implementation","text":"Direct implications for the current Philis implementation This is a read-only snapshot of relevant files during this session, not a claim that every backend or uninspected module lacks a feature. Existing structural directives for unitization, dummy requirements, route matching and guard rings are useful foundations. They should acquire evidence that their intended physical behavior survives generation, placement, routing and final verification. Observed implementation What the source changes about its interpretation Concrete extension MatchingPair uses a fixed gradient-to-A VT ratio and geometric separation. Hastings treats mismatch coefficient, geometry, bias, device family and spatial environment as separate influences. Preserve proximity as a heuristic; add PDK/scenario-specific mismatch budgets and report when the heuristic lacks calibrated physical support. CentroidGroup weights Layout cell centers by bounding-box area. The relevant centroid is active/electrical; mixed resistor topologies require sensitivity weighting. Carry active polygons or characterized electrical moments through cell collapse, unitization and transforms; add tests with asymmetric guards and contacts. ThermalGradient checks temperature difference but uses a distance attraction as trial-move cost. Distance is only a proxy; source location, package and spatial thermal response determine temperature difference. Expose model applicability and scenarios; compare actual temperature-field predictions for accepted candidates. Do not infer safety from an unpowered/unknown scenario. Antenna sums routed metal area across layers and divides by total gate area. Foundry antenna checks can be stage-specific, perimeter-dependent and diode-aware. Keep the inexpensive estimate for guidance but add stage connectivity and exact foundry-rule verification before certification. The parser emits MOS terminals as G, D, S, B, and the hypergraph preserves that order; the routing annotator assigns gate area through nets[1] . The observed index is the drain, so a requirement can target the wrong electrical node before any geometric ratio is computed. Resolve the named gate terminal through a shared accessor and regress with different gate and drain nets. A diode-connected test alone can hide the error. This is a proposed code fix; notes generation did not modify the engine. Unitization and GuardRingRequirement express useful structural intent. Same unit shape, dummy count or ring enclosure does not alone establish equal process environment or carrier collection. Connect each directive to qualified templates, electrical ties, transform legality, environment descriptors and a post-generation verifier. Process exposes layer roles, scalar rule lookup and fabrication grid. Many decisive requirements are conditional on bias, material stack, device variant, package, operating history or coupled geometry. Introduce typed capabilities and calibrated model references beside geometric rules; avoid silently defaulting missing physical characterization.","url":"hastings.html#engine-map"},{"title":"The Art of Analog Layout · A practical validation program with discriminating examples","text":"A practical validation program with discriminating examples The following experiments are proposed for Philis. Their purpose is to distinguish superficially similar layouts whose physical consequences differ, rather than testing that an implementation reproduces its own formula. Active centroid versus cell center: add a guard ring or asymmetric pin extension to one matched unit without changing its active channel. Active centroid should remain unchanged while bounding-box centroid changes. Then move the active channel within a fixed outline and require the reverse result. First versus higher moments: compare centroid-equal arrays under linear and quadratic synthetic fields. Confirm first-order cancellation and quantify residual quadratic error; include a compact repeated-subarray alternative and its extra route cost. Terminal orientation: keep A/B placement identical while reversing the source/drain pattern of one side. Require detection even when all bounding boxes and centroids remain equal. Resistor connection polarity: apply a temperature gradient along identical strips and compare series paths that add versus cancel Seebeck sources. The CC score alone should not decide the outcome. LDE environment: give equal-W/L edge and interior MOS fingers different STI/well distances, then add appropriate dummies. Compare extracted model parameters or characterized device currents, not simply geometric regularity. Capacitor routes: produce equal-length leads with different layers, crossings or fill surroundings. Extract their capacitance matrices and show why equal length can fail the intended ratio. Shared-region transient: compare isolated and merged device variants under a scenario that forward biases a normally reverse-biased junction. Nominal connectivity equivalence should not suppress injection/debiasing evaluation. Distributed power metal: compare a one-end finger, two-ended access, a tapered bus and a far-end lumped load. Check the one-third approximation only where its assumptions hold. Antenna history: compare routes with similar final total metal area but different intermediate-layer connections. Verify that a valid jumper changes the appropriate process-stage ratio. ESD and Kelvin path location: keep nominal nets identical while moving a clamp return or force/sense junction. Check protected-terminal voltage or sensed IR drop; LVS equality alone should not pass the test. Post-fill validation: apply fill near a matched MOS group, a capacitor array and an inductor. Recheck their different environment requirements instead of assuming one keepout distance suits all three. Package/scenario robustness: compare one-heater, unequal two-heater and bumped-package cases. Report performance across scenarios, including the models and uncertainties that remain uncharacterized. Before assigning numerical guarantees, collect foundry device/mismatch/LDE and aging models; terminal-current and voltage scenarios; interconnect/contact/via properties and extraction technology; antenna and voltage-dependent rule definitions; qualified guard and ESD structures; thermal and package geometry/materials; and assembly/fill requirements. Where these are absent, the correct deliverable is a justified candidate and an explicit unverified budget, not a fictional universal threshold. Appendix B supports coordinate/crystal-orientation bookkeeping; Appendix C supports understanding where geometric rules originate; Appendix D offers derivations for substrate, fringe, segmentation, stress, junction and device models; Appendix E explains distributed power metallization. They are best used as derivation references with unit and limiting-case checks. Exercise problems are useful future benchmark seeds, but solving or implementing all of them was outside this reading pass. PDF pp. 834–876","url":"hastings.html#validation"},{"title":"Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability · Optimal means electrically feasible, then efficient to manufacture","text":"Optimal means electrically feasible, then efficient to manufacture Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen, and Willy Sansen, Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability (1999). The valuable contribution is the complete chain from circuit specifications to geometry, parasitic estimates, sensitivity-weighted performance changes, placement, routing, and manufacturing objectives. A small layout or short net is a means to that end, not the definition of success. The book argues for optimizing the combined electrical consequences of interconnect, mismatch, and temperature. It explicitly criticizes converting the circuit specification into one fixed allocation of independent parasitic limits: a different allocation can produce an acceptable circuit that the original limits would reject. This is directly relevant to Philis’s per-net length budgets. A length limit is a useful implementable contract, but it is not, by itself, an electrical specification. PDF pp. 41–42 Interpretation limit. The book sometimes calls its output “guaranteed” performance-correct and says impossible specifications can be flagged without iteration. Its working model uses approximate extraction, first-order electrical sensitivities, and finite heuristic search. Those methods justify a prediction within a model; they do not establish global physical feasibility or a proof of infeasibility. Philis should report model feasibility, extracted-circuit feasibility, and search exhaustion separately.","url":"lampaert.html#thesis"},{"title":"Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability · Reading coverage and chapter map","text":"Reading coverage and chapter map This note follows substantive material in every chapter. Detailed reading covered the performance formulation and sensitivities, module alternatives, placement representations and constraint handling, parasitic estimation, routing cost/scheduling, defect models, implementation interfaces, and conclusions. Historical tool surveys and several implementation derivations were surveyed rather than exhaustively reconstructed. Numerical tables are historical demonstrations, not reproduced experiments. The supplied scan contains 185 PDF pages; citations below use one-based PDF positions. Printed-page offsets change within the scan. Chapter and PDF span Material reviewed and consequence for Philis 1. Introduction, 14–33 Hierarchy, top-down specifications and bottom-up verification, macro-cell flow, LAYLA interfaces. Preserve circuit intent across generation and extraction. 2. Performance-driven layout, 34–65 Process margin, sensitivity methods, R/C modeling, mismatch, thermal and substrate mechanisms. Make objectives refer to measured circuit quantities. 3. Module generation, 66–82 Procedural generators, dynamic merging, alternative partitions, technology abstraction and transistor/cascode examples. Keep several electrically characterized realizations. 4. Placement, 83–130 Representation, matching/symmetry moves, terminal geometry, routing reserves, first-order performance, thermal profiles, annealing and examples. Score manufacturable, routable structures. 5. Routing, 131–164 Gridless search, segment widths, path cost, symmetric and multi-terminal routing, net order, critical area, testability and examples. Route against electrical headroom and physical context. 6. Implementation, 165–169 Generator/model synchronization, schematic annotations, framework links, back-annotation. Stable identity and consistent parasitics are essential infrastructure. 7. Conclusions, 170–172 Limitations of RC-only models, placement/routing separation and electrical/physical separation. Add feedback rather than treating a stage’s proxy as final truth. Raster inspection verified the sensitivity equation on PDF p. 42, mismatch equation on p. 53, performance penalty on p. 107, and critical-area diagram/equations on p. 155. The printed penalty in §4.9, Eq. (4.11), correctly subtracts the upper bound in its upper-violation branch. The normalized expression below is a proposed extension, not a correction to that equation. No claim is made to have rerun LAYLA or independently verified its experimental numbers.","url":"lampaert.html#coverage"},{"title":"Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability · 1. Express layout quality through remaining circuit-performance margin","text":"1. Express layout quality through remaining circuit-performance margin Source finding. A performance quantity P has acceptable interval [ P min , P max ]. After considering process variation, the pre-layout design occupies [ P proc,min , P proc,max ]. Under the book’s additive layout-shift approximation, the allowed shift is the difference between those intervals: P min − P proc,min ≤ ΔP layout ≤ P max − P proc,max . For example, if phase margin must exceed 60° and the least favorable pre-layout process result is 64°, the nominal layout has at most 4° of degradation under that approximation. A 10° nominal surplus at the typical process point is not the available robust margin. If parasitic effects vary with corner, evaluate the shift at each corner; subtracting one corner-independent shift from an interval is then insufficient. PDF pp. 34–37 Proposed action and reason. Introduce performance contracts identifying metric, units, lower/upper limits, analysis, load, stimulus, PVT/operating conditions, baseline result, and reserved uncertainty. Keep hard geometric legality independent. Associate each proxy budget with the contract it approximates and its derivation, because otherwise a placer can optimize a number without knowing whether it protects phase margin, settling, offset, or nothing measurable. Tradeoff and validation. Circuit evaluation is expensive and some specifications are discontinuous near failure. Start with a small set of stable metrics and compare proxy-predicted margin against extracted simulation over deliberate placement and routing perturbations. Record false-feasible and false-infeasible decisions separately; a good average error does not establish trustworthy acceptance. The existing Requirements definition already separates hard, budget, and cost tiers. Preserve that separation. ParasiticBudget checks drawn length; although its generic extraction helper contains a placeholder, the active annotator path constructs per-net length limits from class/load-derived capacitance allowances and process wire capacitance. That is useful physical guidance. The remaining gap is a traceable circuit-response constraint and validated stack/topology assumptions, rather than the mere absence of budget generation. Label heuristic length bounds by their calibration and validity.","url":"lampaert.html#performance-contract"},{"title":"Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability · 2. Compute signed, scaled sensitivities and know when they stop being valid","text":"2. Compute signed, scaled sensitivities and know when they stop being valid Source finding. The inner loop estimates geometry-dependent parasitics and applies a first-order performance model. In generalized notation, with p the parasitic vector and a reference p 0 : ΔP j ≈ Σ i S ji (p i − p 0i ), S ji = ∂P j /∂p i . The source writes the zero-reference special case. The sign matters: capacitance can worsen one metric and improve another, and coupling depends on the two connected nodes. A single positive “criticality” scalar cannot represent this entire matrix. The book reviews perturbation, direct sensitivity and adjoint sensitivity; it implements perturbation because of simulator availability. Finite differences trade truncation error against solver noise and require an appropriate perturbation size. Adjoint efficiency comes from many parameters sharing a scalar response; generally each independent scalar response needs its own adjoint solve, rather than accepting the book’s broad wording that two solves cover arbitrary numbers of responses. PDF pp. 42–45 A dimensionless two-sided violation can be implemented as the following proposed normalization , not as an equation copied literally from the source: v j = max(0, (L j − P̂ j )/s j , (P̂ j − U j )/s j ), s j > 0. L and U are specification bounds; s is an explicit engineering scale in the same units. The source’s §4.9, Eq. (4.11), uses the same continuous piecewise bound violation without the proposed per-metric scale. Its upper branch correctly subtracts the upper degradation bound; the earlier edition of these notes misread that superscript. The scale here is an additional normalization, not a source erratum. Do not mix MHz, degrees, and millivolts by an unqualified sum. PDF p. 107 Proposed action and reason. Cache sensitivities by circuit, PVT, device variant, operating point and parasitic reference. Refresh after a material sizing or topology change, after prediction error grows, or after leaving a calibrated neighborhood. Use central finite differences where feasible, compare two perturbation sizes, and retain the estimated numerical error. A speed advantage is valuable only if the model ranks moves reliably. Tradeoff and validation. Refreshing too often erases the speed benefit; refreshing too rarely creates confidently wrong rankings. Benchmark rank agreement and feasibility error, not only regression error. Evaluate several nearby variants with actual extraction and simulation, including a case where a compensation-node capacitance changes the dominant pole. In Philis, global placement lines 205–221 differentiate geometric rule costs; that is not yet a circuit-performance sensitivity calculation.","url":"lampaert.html#sensitivities"},{"title":"Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability · 3. Make primitive alternatives electrically meaningful","text":"3. Make primitive alternatives electrically meaningful Source finding. Module generation returns several realizations of the same electrical intent; placement selects an aspect ratio, orientation and implementation. The source compares fixed procedural subcircuits, dynamic diffusion merging, and precomputed alternative partitions. Fixed generators encode expert structures and reduce search, but miss unanticipated sharing. Dynamic merging explores more choices but complicates legality and runtime. Some common-centroid and interdigitated cascode structures still need dedicated generators. Junction capacitance depends on source/drain area and perimeter, so diffusion sharing changes performance as well as area. PDF pp. 66–74 Proposed action and reason. Store, per variant, legal orientations, terminal polygons, source/drain area and perimeter, internal metal/via parasitics, matching environment, bulk domain and accessibility. Compare variants after adding their internal electrical consequences to external routing estimates. Without those fields, a compact variant can win solely because capacitance or pin-access difficulty has disappeared from the score. Assumptions and tradeoff. Shared diffusion requires compatible device/process type, connectivity, bulk potential and foundry geometry rules. The book’s same-net/layer/bulk overlap conditions are a conceptual minimum, not a modern signoff rule deck. Enumerating all partitions can explode combinatorially; retain an electrical/area/access Pareto subset and permit selected coupled variant changes. Validation. Extract two variants with equal intended W/L and compare junction geometry, terminal connectivity, pin accessibility and the most sensitive circuit metrics. Verify that legal same-net sharing improves the appropriate extracted capacitance and that a similar-looking incompatible merge is rejected. The source separates a fast generator interface mode from detailed layout generation and puts process layers/rules in technology data. It also synchronizes generators with simulation models. For Philis this argues for one authoritative variant descriptor consumed by placement, rendering, extraction and verification, with an explicit convention for parasitics already included in device models. Otherwise back-annotation can double-count them. PDF pp. 73–74 , PDF pp. 167–168","url":"lampaert.html#variants"},{"title":"Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability · 4. Preserve exact intent in moves; estimate actual routing demand","text":"4. Preserve exact intent in moves; estimate actual routing demand Source finding. Position, orientation and shape constraints are different relationships. LAYLA uses separate move groups, allowing a position-symmetric pair to carry its own orientation rule and shape coupling. Symmetric translation, pair swapping, pair flipping, and moving the symmetry axis preserve relationships by construction. Same orientation for matching devices and mirrored orientation for geometric symmetry are explicitly different. The desired electrical correspondence must decide which one applies. PDF pp. 99–105 Proposed action and reason. Preserve hard symmetry and matched variant constraints in coordinated moves, and maintain a compatible terminal correspondence. Check that constraints intersect consistently before optimization. A low scalar cost cannot justify breaking an exact constraint, and independent edits followed by repeated repair can waste time or oscillate. Tradeoff and validation. Restricting moves can disconnect the search space or trap a useful ordering. Include whole-group translation, axis motion, variant replacement and pair-order changes, then verify reachability on small enumerated examples. Use cases with self-symmetric tails, unequal pair sizes, multiple axes and equal-current-direction requirements. Philis already has SymmetryGroup emission around lines 113–130 and detailed placement with legality-first acceptance; the research supports extending those contracts rather than replacing them with one penalty weight. The book’s free-coordinate representation allows useful sharing but also illegal overlap; its claim that this representation is best is a historical engineering choice, not a theorem. Later topological methods in the companion survey remove much illegal search. Whichever representation is used, a terminal is an accessible region, not merely the device center. The book specifically uses terminal-edge Manhattan distances and geometric net approximations to estimate capacitance and coupling. PDF pp. 95–99 , PDF pp. 108–112 Proposed action and reason. Reserve directional space for pin escape, high-current widths, shielding and passing nets; update it using coarse routes. The mechanism is simple: a dense placement can require a long detour or become unroutable, so area saved before routing is not necessarily area saved afterward. The source’s static-plus-dynamic halos are a useful starting abstraction. Tradeoff and validation. Larger halos reduce density and can overconstrain placement. Feed measured global-route congestion back into selected congested boundaries and compare final routed area, detour, opens and electrical margin. Do not validate this change with HPWL alone. The book itself attributes a remaining 10–15% historical area penalty relative to manual layouts largely to separated placement/routing and conservative space reservation. PDF p. 116 , PDF p. 172","url":"lampaert.html#placement"},{"title":"Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability · 5. Distinguish random mismatch, systematic environment and thermal response","text":"5. Distinguish random mismatch, systematic environment and thermal response Source finding. The reviewed pair model is σ²(Δq) = A q ²/(WL) + S q ²D², with process-calibrated constants, device area WL and separation D. It describes a particular decomposition into local random variation and distance-related variation; it is not a universal device law. The book also requires compatible structure, shape, finger count, orientation, local surroundings and temperature. Common-centroid organization targets spatial gradients; it does not eliminate the finite-area random term. PDF pp. 53–55 Its placement mismatch cost sums absolute sensitivities times three-sigma parameter deviations. That is a conservative aggregation, not the same quantity as the standard deviation of a combined circuit response. For statistically modeled parameters, the appropriate linear covariance propagation is σ²(P) ≈ SΣSᵀ. An arithmetic sum of “three sigma” contributions has no universal three-sigma yield interpretation. The book’s area/distance separation is helpful for responsibility boundaries, but a square root of summed variances is not generally additive in area and distance. PDF pp. 112–113 Proposed action and reason. Annotate a matching rule with its protected response, process model and systematic mechanism. Retain exact common structure/orientation/environment requirements where justified; optimize proximity only against the part of error it can influence. For thermal matching, evaluate device temperatures from power sources and their thermal transfer rather than treating Euclidean closeness as proof of equal temperature. Assumptions and validation. Thermal superposition assumes an adequately linear thermal model with known stack and boundary conditions; power can itself depend on temperature. Calibrate a fast kernel against a more detailed solution and validate temperature differences at sensitive pairs as well as maximum temperature. Compare two equidistant pairs placed along versus across a thermal gradient. Update the field after materially changing sources or placement. Philis’s emitted thermal budget uses a tuning default at lines 37–40; its number should ultimately come from response sensitivity and technology, not from the book’s examples. Substrate isolation is likewise technology-dependent: spacing can be effective in one substrate and weak in another; guard-ring performance depends on contact location and return impedance. The book introduces these mechanisms but does not supply a universal foundry rule. Its frequency examples and broad temperature/reliability rules should not be carried into modern PDK defaults. PDF pp. 59–64","url":"lampaert.html#matching-thermal"},{"title":"Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability · 6. Route with segment physics and a shared performance budget","text":"6. Route with segment physics and a shared performance budget Source finding. Routing evaluates segment resistance R = R □,layer ℓ/w and overlap, lateral and fringe capacitances. Here R □ is sheet resistance, ℓ and w use identical length units, and via/contact resistance needs separate treatment. Different portions of a branched net can carry different currents; width belongs to the segment. The book sizes widths from current per allowed current-per-width and sizes via arrays from via current capability. This is a physical reason to preserve branch currents, not a justification for applying one nominal DC limit to every waveform and temperature. PDF p. 143 , PDF pp. 147–148 Proposed action and reason. Add layer/width/via-aware R/C and coupling estimates to incremental routing costs, with a circuit-level budget evaluator at route acceptance. Wider wires reduce R but can add C and congestion; shielding can reduce aggressor coupling while increasing ground capacitance. The right decision depends on the sensitivity matrix and actual available margin. Tradeoff and validation. Incremental estimates require a consistent ownership rule for shared coupling terms, or a net pair’s capacitance can be counted twice. Compare complete-route recomputation with incremental updates after insertion, rip-up and width changes. Use two equal-length routes on different layers, two equal-length routes with different neighbors, and a multi-terminal tree with changing branch currents. LAYLA preroutes nets independently, estimates their performance impact, preserves critical routes and reroutes less influential nets to remove conflicts. This suggests an urgency based on remaining performance margin and avoidable damage, not a permanent ordering by fanout or wirelength. In Philis, route_net (around lines 635–710) already combines base path cost, congestion history, present overuse and a penalty field; electrical price fields can enter there, but final acceptance must still evaluate the coupled state. PDF pp. 150–152 Search caveat. The source discusses A* with a lower-bound predictor. A geometric lower bound is not automatically an admissible bound on a signed, coupled performance objective. Negative incremental sensitivities or route-history-dependent coupling can also invalidate ordinary shortest-path assumptions. Use a nonnegative surrogate for path generation plus full-state electrical acceptance, or explicitly implement the richer search state and prove the required cost properties. Similarly, pruning a partial path for exceeding a budget is safe only when all completions cannot restore feasibility. Symmetric routing should compare electrical consequences and surroundings, including layers, vias, endpoints and nearby conductors. Mirroring centerlines is useful but not sufficient when surrounding objects are asymmetric. Where exact mirroring is impossible, expose an electrical matching budget and document the permitted escape rather than silently accepting a large geometric penalty. PDF pp. 148–150","url":"lampaert.html#routing"},{"title":"Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability · 7. Spend justified margin on manufacturability and testability","text":"7. Spend justified margin on manufacturability and testability Source finding. The source separates local defect faults from parametric variation. For a defect diameter χ bridging two long parallel conductors of overlap length L and spacing s, its simplified critical area is A(χ) = L·max(0, χ−s). Combining this with a defect-size density D(χ) gives expected fault count λ = ∫A(χ)D(χ)dχ; under the stated Poisson assumptions, the no-fault probability is exp(−λ). These formulas require calibrated defect mechanisms and geometrical approximations. They are not equivalent to mismatch yield. PDF pp. 153–157 The manufacturability phase protects the performance limits while reducing defect exposure and preferentially avoiding difficult-to-detect bridging faults. The testability weighting uses separation of good/faulty response distributions. An inverse statistical distance is an optimization proxy, not itself a probability that a fault escapes testing. Nor does accepting a statistical hypothesis establish the posterior probability that an individual manufactured circuit is good. PDF pp. 158–160 Proposed action and reason. Once extracted electrical specifications and required guard margins pass, explore spacing, route arrangement and via redundancy using calibrated reliability/manufacturing objectives. Preserve performance as a constraint. This recognizes that many electrically equivalent layouts differ in production quality. Tradeoff and validation. Without foundry defect data and a production test model, report geometry-based exposure only; do not label it yield. Benchmark exposure changes and any added area/C/R. Use fault injection with the intended tests before claiming improved test coverage. Keep deterministic DRC, electrical yield, defect yield and escaped-fault risk as separate outputs.","url":"lampaert.html#manufacturing"},{"title":"Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability · Engine work justified by this book, in dependency order","text":"Engine work justified by this book, in dependency order Make performance contracts and budget provenance explicit. This gives each existing heuristic a falsifiable purpose; validate with a pre-layout and extracted reference circuit. Characterize variants and routing parasitics consistently. Electrical feedback is meaningless if internal parasitics are omitted or double-counted; validate with standalone primitive extraction. Connect signed sensitivities to shared circuit budgets. This recovers valid tradeoffs excluded by fixed per-net bounds; validate prediction errors near specification boundaries. Feed routing congestion and extracted error back to placement. This repairs the separated-stage weakness identified in the conclusions; validate final routed area and performance across multiple seeds. Add manufacturing/testability optimization only when its inputs exist. Otherwise a numerically precise score would imply knowledge the engine does not possess. The strongest success criterion is a reproducible, electrically verified feasible layout with measured tradeoffs and known model limits. This book supplies the architecture of that objective, while modern PDK data, extraction, statistical models and independent verification must supply its calibration.","url":"lampaert.html#implementation-sequence"},{"title":"An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation · The precise claim","text":"The precise claim Dai, He, Xing, Chen, and Geiger construct a recursively symmetric layout for two identically designed devices. Starting with two units per device in a first-order pattern, the nth-order construction uses 2 n units per device and cancels polynomial parameter gradients through order n under the paper's additive unit model. The superscript matters: text extraction flattens it to “2n.” The claim concerns model cancellation; it does not establish best circuit performance or a universal minimum unit count. Abstract and gradient model, PDF pp. 1–2 Action and reason. Add a moment certificate to generated matching patterns. It provides an independently checkable invariant and identifies what the pattern actually cancels. A pattern label such as “CC” or “symmetric” cannot communicate its second- or third-order properties.","url":"nth-order.html#claim"},{"title":"An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation · Derivation suitable for implementation","text":"Derivation suitable for implementation p(x,y) = c + Σ₁≤a+b≤n gₐᵦ xᵃyᵇ Mₐᵦ(D) = (1/mD) Σᵢ xᵢᵃyᵢᵇ Δp̄ = Σ gₐᵦ [Mₐᵦ(A) − Mₐᵦ(B)] The paper sums unit values for equal-size devices; the normalized moments above express the same result for equal counts and make ratio comparison easier to state. Every unknown coefficient gₐᵦ disappears from the mismatch if its corresponding moment difference is zero. First order needs equal x and y centroids. Second order also needs equal x², xy, and y² moments. Testing just radial distance checks x²+y² and can miss anisotropic x²−y² or xy fields. §II, Eqs. (4)–(6), additive unit model, PDF p. 2 §III, Eqs. (15)–(23), cancellation proof, PDF p. 3 Why translations are safe. Translating a degree-n monomial produces the same degree-n part plus lower-degree terms. Once the lower-degree differences already vanish, translating two lower-order patterns does not spoil their lower-order cancellation. This is the induction step behind the recursive construction; it is why arbitrary placement of the recursion centers can work within the model. Finite units. The paper approximates a unit's area-averaged parameter by its value at one representative point because the unit is small. For large or differently shaped units, compute area-integrated moments, or show that their identical translated footprints make the relevant internal moments cancel. Do not replace electrically weighted active areas with bounding boxes.","url":"nth-order.html#derivation"},{"title":"An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation · Recursive construction and its reason","text":"Recursive construction and its reason Start with a verified first-order pattern. For example ABBA on a line, or the two-dimensional diagonal pair. Reason: the induction requires all first-order terms already canceled. Create a reflected copy of the previous-order pattern around a new center. Choose disjoint, legal unit positions on the manufacturing grid. Reason: paired coordinates gain a predictable sign under central reflection. For odd target order, retain device labels in the reflected copy. A homogeneous odd-degree term changes sign under reflection, so the two contributions of each device cancel. For even target order, interchange A and B in the reflected copy. An even-degree term keeps its sign, so matching it across opposite labels cancels the A−B difference. Recheck every monomial up to the target order. Reason: the proof assumes exact reflection and preserved lower-order structure; integer-grid placement, variant dimensions, or an implementation error can violate those premises. §III recursive definition, PDF pp. 2–3 If the base uses k units per device, the construction uses k·2^(n−1) ; the paper's 2 n count follows from k=2. Keep this distinction if Philis uses a different legal base pattern. For ratioed devices, more than two device types, heterogeneous footprints, or different electrical sensitivities, the paper's 1:1 construction is not automatically applicable. The general moment equations still suggest a solver formulation, but that extension must be separately justified and checked.","url":"nth-order.html#construction"},{"title":"An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation · Why ABBA is insufficient for quadratic gradients","text":"Why ABBA is insufficient for quadratic gradients Place A at x=−1.5,+1.5 and B at x=−0.5,+0.5 in unit-pitch coordinates. Both centroids are zero. Under p(x)=p₀+q x² , the mean A−B error is q(2.25−0.25)=2q . It has perfect first-order cancellation and a nonzero second-order error. A two-row ABBA / BAAB pattern at y=±0.5 equalizes x², xy, and y² as well as x and y. It can still respond to a cubic term such as x²y. This illustrates why each claim should name the highest verified order rather than call the pattern “gradient-free.” These examples are our algebraic checks, not silicon results. Explore the moment differences Equal electrical units; coordinates in illustrative unit pitches. Sliders define p−p₀=gₓx+gᵧy+qₓₓx²+qₓᵧxy+qᵧᵧy²+c₂₁x²y. The final column is the mean parameter error A−B. Pattern sizes differ; this demonstrates cancellation only, not a fixed-area performance comparison. Pattern to draw Common-centroid ABBA Clustered AABB Interdigitated ABAB Second-order, two rows Third-order, four rows gₓ: 0.0 gᵧ: 0.0 qₓₓ: 1.0 qₓᵧ: 0.0 qᵧᵧ: 0.0 c₂₁: 0.0 ABBA first-order common-centroid pattern Four identical units arranged A, B, B, A. Their centroids coincide, but their second x moments differ. A B B A Download this SVG Normalized moment differences, A minus B Pattern Δx Δy Δx² Δxy Δy² Δx²y Error ABBA, qₓₓ=1 0 0 2 0 0 0 2 The static ABBA example above remains valid without JavaScript. Enable JavaScript locally to compare all five patterns.","url":"nth-order.html#counterexample"},{"title":"An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation · What cancellation does not guarantee","text":"What cancellation does not guarantee Nonlinear device response. If oxide thickness varies linearly but capacitance is proportional to its reciprocal, capacitance itself contains higher-order terms. Cancellation in the thickness field does not prove exact capacitance matching. Likewise, a linear threshold-voltage field passed through a nonlinear current law can leave residual current mismatch. Local discontinuities and LDE. A well edge, active edge, hot spot, or abrupt fill boundary need not resemble a low-degree polynomial across the array. Higher-order symmetry does not automatically equalize these environments. Use the review's LDE analysis and Hastings alongside the moment certificate. Random mismatch. The proof removes deterministic polynomial mismatch. It does not remove uncorrelated unit variation. At fixed total area, increasing unit count does not necessarily improve the random mismatch floor. Routing and size. Each order doubles units in this construction. More contacts, edges, channels, vias, and internal routing can degrade noise, bandwidth, area, and even systematic matching. The simulated zeroes in Table I validate the polynomial model, not a routed PDK implementation. §IV, Table I and conclusion, PDF p. 4","url":"nth-order.html#bounds"},{"title":"An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation · Practical implementation and acceptance tests","text":"Practical implementation and acceptance tests Proposed data. A generated pattern should expose its unit coordinates, electrical weights, orientation classes, parent-device map, exact ratios, and certified moments. Put the certificate at the cell generator boundary so cell collapse retains what CentroidGroup cannot infer from macro centers. Make the cancellation order a chosen capability, not an inferred promise from a pattern name. Proposed search policy. First evaluate lower-order, compact, routable patterns. Add higher-order alternatives only when a characterized nonlinear field or a measured sensitivity sweep shows a benefit worth their implementation cost. This follows directly from the exponential unit count and the competing route penalties. Certificate algorithm. Accumulate monomial sums separately for each device, then compare cross-multiplied normalized sums to avoid dividing by unit counts. Use translated local coordinates to reduce numeric growth. For integral grid coordinates, wide integers or arbitrary-precision arithmetic can establish exact low-order equalities; explicitly bound overflow. Electrical weights derived from a simulator require tolerances and uncertainty, so label those numerical certificates differently. Reproduce ABBA's zero x/y moments and nonzero x² difference. Check every xᵃyᵇ with 1≤a+b≤n for each claimed order, including mixed terms. Translate and rotate patterns, then repeat the checks; preserve terminal and orientation semantics. Move one unit by one grid step and verify a previously canceled mode becomes detectable. Generate legal contacts and routes, extract the circuit, and compare offset/ratio error versus lower-order alternatives at equal total device area and at separately reported equal unit geometry. These tests address the actual theorem and its physical usefulness. Testing that a generator reproduces its own string template would not independently validate either.","url":"nth-order.html#engine"},{"title":"An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation · Reading coverage and limits","text":"Reading coverage and limits Read all four PDF pages, including the polynomial expansion, induction, pattern diagrams, simulation table, and references. Visually verified PDF pp. 1 and 3 to resolve the 2 n notation and parity-dependent pattern construction. The normalized moment formulation and interactive examples are derived here from the paper's additive equal-unit model. No numerical mismatch percentages from its synthetic fields are treated as process predictions.","url":"nth-order.html#coverage"},{"title":"Matching properties of MOS transistors · Source and coverage","text":"Source and coverage All four supplied PDF pages were read. PDF pp. 1–2 contain the statistical derivation, transistor model, test structure and measured dependencies; p. 3 contains correlation, the bandgap application and conclusions; p. 4 contains the measurement flow and figures. Equations (1)–(3), coefficient units and orientation discussion were checked in rendered pp. 1–2 because text extraction corrupts symbols. The figure page is the original experimental evidence, not a modern-process calibration. Open supplied paper, PDF p. 1 .","url":"pelgrom.html#coverage"},{"title":"Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions · Teaching chapters connected to this source","text":"Teaching chapters connected to this source Use the definition of optimal layout , the performance feedback loop , search techniques , and experimental validation for the full teaching treatment. This page preserves the paper-specific findings and limits.","url":"performance.html#guide"},{"title":"Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions · Constraints are useful proxies, with a limited guarantee","text":"Constraints are useful proxies, with a limited guarantee The survey traces conventional geometric objectives and constraints—area, wire length, symmetry, proximity, common centroid, shielding, current flow, and LDE-aware placement. Each protects some aspect of behavior, but satisfying them does not generally prove gain, bandwidth, noise, or offset requirements. The missing step is an electrical evaluation of the realized layout. §I–II, PDF pp. 1–3 Action and reason. Keep geometric rules as efficient guidance and legality constraints, then evaluate the actual performance specifications. This does not require abandoning Philis's constraint system. It requires distinguishing a proxy residual from the specification that motivated it. In the current lex_key , the final scalar objective is signoff's extracted total capacitance. That is a physically informed scalar, but it cannot distinguish capacitance on a high-impedance pole from capacitance on an insensitive rail. The paper motivates adding per-metric electrical results and a feasible candidate archive.","url":"performance.html#main-result"},{"title":"Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions · Three ways to bring performance into search","text":"Three ways to bring performance into search Approach What it contributes What it does not establish Additional physical constraints Signal flow, LDE, route matching, and power routing reduce known failure mechanisms. They do not provide a universal analog equivalent of digital static timing analysis. Direct black-box optimization Generate layouts, extract, simulate, and tune a smaller control space such as net weights. The expensive simulator limits evaluations; the chosen control space may exclude a better topology. Learned performance prediction Predict circuit metrics or quality from schematic, placement, and route features, enabling many cheap proposals. Prediction errors, distribution shift, and omitted features can mis-rank layouts; a prediction is not signoff. §II-A–C, PDF pp. 2–3 Implementation lesson. Treat a net-weight optimizer as tuning one generator, not as optimizing all possible physical layouts. Record the generator version, seed, and resulting geometry so repeated controls do not get mistaken for independent design-space exploration.","url":"performance.html#methods"},{"title":"Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions · The expensive part is obtaining trustworthy labels","text":"The expensive part is obtaining trustworthy labels The text reports data acquisition as 92.89% of a modeling lifecycle and training plus inference as 7.11%. However, the percentages printed in Fig. 2 are internally inconsistent with that split: 22.55% PNR + 75.42% PEX/simulation + 7% training + 0.11% inference sum above 100%. The figure was visually checked. Preserve the qualitative conclusion—extraction and simulation dominate this example—without copying its contradictory percentages into a runtime budget. §III-A, PDF pp. 3–4 Fig. 2, PDF p. 5 Action and reason. Instrument Philis's own PNR, extraction, simulation, model-training, and candidate-selection costs. Cache results by geometry, model, and testbench hashes. Use cheap legality and surrogate checks to screen candidates, then spend expensive evaluations on uncertain or promising candidates. Report both wall time and computational work, because parallelism changes their relationship.","url":"performance.html#cost"},{"title":"Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions · Transfer learning needs a domain check","text":"Transfer learning needs a domain check The paper compares prediction from scratch with transfer between same-topology sizing variations and different topologies. Table I shows metric-dependent degradation; a model that transfers adequately for bandwidth can transfer poorly for CMRR or noise. This is evidence to measure transferability per metric and design domain, not to assume an analog model generalizes because the circuit is still called an OTA. §III-B, Table I, PDF p. 4 Proposed evaluator. Attach process, device families, topology, size range, route rules, circuit modes, and training-distribution metadata to every surrogate. Require local validation or fallback simulation when the candidate leaves that domain. Use separate held-out topologies and sizing ranges to distinguish interpolation from transfer. Calibration and false-feasible error matter more than a single average accuracy number.","url":"performance.html#transfer"},{"title":"Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions · Preserve the tradeoffs","text":"Preserve the tradeoffs The paper's MOBO versus weighted-BO case study argues for retaining multiple competing performance objectives. A fixed weighted sum can hide important alternatives, and counting top-ranked metrics does not prove any one layout is best in all metrics. Requirements such as minimum phase margin remain acceptance constraints even when gain or bandwidth is being optimized. §III-C, PDF pp. 4–5 Philis action. First discard failed physical/electrical candidates; then retain nondominated feasible results in the chosen metric space. Present worst-case margins, area, power, and runtime with the selected layout. Keep the selection policy explicit. A Pareto archive is a record of tradeoffs among evaluated candidates, not proof of the global Pareto frontier.","url":"performance.html#multiobjective"},{"title":"Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions · Future directions and a practical adoption order","text":"Future directions and a practical adoption order The authors discuss active learning, faster simulation, model transfer, multimodal schematic/layout features, improved physical representations, and multiobjective optimization. For Philis, a justified order is: establish trustworthy testbenches and extraction; collect repeatable candidate data; test simple sensitivity and surrogate baselines; then adopt more complex ML only where its total evaluation cost and false-feasible rate improve. A sophisticated optimizer cannot repair incorrect circuit labels. §IV–V, PDF pp. 5–6 The review describes the field as of its publication, rather than providing an exhaustive account of later work. References to MAGICAL, ALIGN, and other systems here are the authors' reports; their current external implementations have not been independently audited for these notes.","url":"performance.html#research"},{"title":"Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions · Reading coverage and limits","text":"Reading coverage and limits Read all seven PDF pages, including the survey, MAGICAL case studies, transfer table, runtime discussion, future directions, and references. Visually checked PDF p. 5 to inspect the runtime chart and multiobjective figure. The figures do not support a universal speedup or a guaranteed optimum; the notes explicitly avoid those interpretations.","url":"performance.html#coverage"},{"title":"Roadmap · Code-review scope and the existing foundation","text":"Code-review scope and the existing foundation The reviewed workspace is based on commit dad330c42f129cb0bca51427d308745f2d168324 , with the user's existing uncommitted changes included in the inspection. The handbook did not modify engine code. Paths and symbol names below describe this snapshot; later changes may supersede the observations. The full research inventory is in source coverage . Philis already separates hard constraints, budget residuals and cost; recognizes analog structures; creates legal cell variants and some merged matched macros; checks route and placement properties; and evaluates drawn-geometry verification during its epoch loop. The runtime assigns supply/ground net classes to the detailed router. These are existing capabilities to extend, not missing features inferred from older documentation. The source consensus suggests three improvements in sequence: preserve the electrical meaning of each physical object and rule; make model coverage and final-circuit evaluation explicit; then expand the search space and optimize using those trustworthy results. The sequence follows dependencies, not a claim that any one topic is universally more important for every circuit.","url":"roadmap.html#snapshot"},{"title":"Roadmap · First: repair semantic and evidence gaps","text":"First: repair semantic and evidence gaps Observed issue / proposed change Why it affects quality Concrete acceptance evidence Antenna gate identity. extract assigns MOS gate area using nets[1] . The parser emits G,D,S,B, and the hypergraph preserves that order. Use named terminal G or the validated index 0. The current index associates the gate's oxide area with the drain net. A diode-connected test hides the error because G=D. A MOS with distinct G/D nets contributes oxide area to G alone; diode-connected and non-MOS cases remain correct. Then extend stage-aware exposure separately. See protection . Verification coverage. signoff logs unsupported LVS devices and skipped in-stage rules. Add structured per-candidate coverage and required-device checks. A clean supported subgraph or partial rule set can otherwise be mistaken for a complete certificate. Removing a required recognizer/rule changes coverage status and blocks that certification, with the omitted objects named. Existing engine/stage failure handling stays visible. Unknown physical inputs. Record whether current, power, temperature, variation and stack data are measured/model-derived, assumed or absent. Missing current or power represented numerically as zero can make reliability or thermal evaluation look favorable. An absent operating-point result yields unknown for dependent rules. A physically zero-current mode remains distinguishable from missing data. See constraint systems . Stable rule identity and units. Retain raw measurements, normalized residuals, source/provenance and object mappings across stages. Ranking and diagnostics need to distinguish the same physical requirement evaluated twice from two independent risks. Unit changes leave normalized ordering invariant; macro retargeting retains the intended identities; a required boolean failure cannot disappear through a zero residual. These are narrowly reviewable changes. None requires an elaborate optimizer. Their benefit is that subsequent performance claims refer to the correct circuit, objects and checked conditions.","url":"roadmap.html#p0"},{"title":"Roadmap · Second: preserve internal units and characterize matching models","text":"Second: preserve internal units and characterize matching models CentroidGroup::centroid weights cell centers by bounding-box area, while retargeting maps devices into merged cells. This is a geometric proxy unless the generator guarantees that outline area represents the intended electrical weights and preserves all internal-unit positions. Device matching needs active-unit moments and orientation/environment descriptors, including when several schematic members share one macro. Proposed interface. A generated macro should expose each physical unit's schematic owner, electrical weight, active polygon or moments, terminal mapping, legal orientation, process environment and route-access contract. Rotate/reflect those descriptors together with geometry. Keep original device and bit identities through merging. The first acceptance test changes only the halo or aspect of an enclosing box: electrical moments should stay fixed if internal active geometry stays fixed. MatchingPair uses a fixed gradient-to-area-coefficient ratio. The supplied Pelgrom and Drennan sources do not establish a universal process-independent constant for that ratio or a universal 1/√area law over all geometry and bias domains. Add PDK/model-qualified coefficients and their domains, then propagate variation through the actual matched electrical quantity. Use spatial covariance when available and expose uncertain coefficients as assumptions. See random mismatch and gradient matching . Current annotation registers matching, proximity and centroid terms as costs, while selected symmetry and isolation constraints have stronger roles. Do not describe every existing geometric preference as a hard certificate. Allow circuit-specific required offset/ratio constraints to motivate justified geometric budgets, while preserving alternatives when a costly centroid pattern is unnecessary or electrically worse. Build family-specific environment descriptors after the unit mapping is sound: MOS LOD/well/poly/dummy environment; resistor contacts, bends and thermoelectric symmetry; capacitor perimeter/plate environment; bipolar emitter/base/collector geometry and temperature. The device chapters explain why a single “matched device” abstraction needs qualified family details.","url":"roadmap.html#p1"},{"title":"Roadmap · Third: make parasitic evaluation terminal-aware and close the circuit loop","text":"Third: make parasitic evaluation terminal-aware and close the circuit loop Verification currently uses total extracted capacitance as its cost, and lex_key uses it in epoch selection after violation tiers. Preserve this useful extraction path but add an electrical result vector under declared testbenches. Two layouts with equal total C can have very different pole locations, offset, settling or noise when the capacitance is on different nodes. The active parasitic budget construction converts a class/load-derived capacitance allowance into a length bound using per-length wire capacitance. That is already more informative than an arbitrary fixed length. Improve it by retaining the stack/layer/width and terminal-network assumptions, then update local performance sensitivities after meaningful layout changes. Shared circuit margins should constrain combinations of parasitics, rather than independently giving every net the entire allowance. A first integration target is one fixed-schematic OTA with qualified operating conditions, loads and gain/UGF/phase-margin/noise/offset/power measurements. Archive several feasible candidates and their margins. Cache by geometry, extraction settings, model and testbench identity. Promote uncertain candidates to higher-fidelity evaluation; do not let a cheap estimate certify what it cannot observe. See the performance loop . Acceptance. A pair of equal-total-C layouts with different node loading should be distinguished electrically. A layout with improved UGF but failed required phase margin should be rejected. Changes to a load or model must invalidate cached results. Only after these work should a surrogate or Bayesian optimizer be judged by how cheaply it finds better verified candidates.","url":"roadmap.html#p2"},{"title":"Roadmap · Fourth: strengthen routing quality and resource-aware search","text":"Fourth: strengthen routing quality and resource-aware search Current scope Next useful capability Reason and acceptance test Differential routing compares total length and the set of layers used. Per-layer lengths, via/cut structure, terminal topology, resistance/capacitance and neighbor environment. Equal length and identical layer sets can hide unequal RC. Use an equal-length counterexample with different via counts or layer allocation. Coupling estimates same-layer parallel overlap using a stack-independent constant; crosstalk is a geometric spacing check. Qualified layer-pair/width/spacing coupling plus aggressor/victim transfer constraints. Cross-layer zero in a scoped estimator is not physical zero. Check parallel, crossing and shielded structures, then the induced victim error. Detailed routing already supports configurable widths, supply widening, width-dependent spacing and via arrays. Discrete performance-driven width/layer choices with local route feasibility and terminal RC feedback. Uniform widening can hurt high-impedance nodes or congestion. Compare selective sizing to narrow/wide baselines on the same circuit. Placement chooses variants before complete detailed-route information is available. Reserve route corridors, pin access, symmetric channels and power/guard returns; preserve multiple candidate topologies. A compact placement with no legal route is not a good final layout. Track post-route feasibility and electrical quality at equal search budgets. Do not optimize these dimensions independently when they share resources. A shield consumes a track and adds load; wider power wires change nearby coupling; more dispersed matched units create internal route demand. Each mutation should invalidate the affected geometry and performance checks, then return a clear reason for acceptance or rejection.","url":"roadmap.html#p3"},{"title":"Roadmap · Fifth: connect current, power, isolation and reliability models","text":"Fifth: connect current, power, isolation and reliability models The library already populates supply_nets from annotated supply/ground classes. The reviewed runtime did not show an assignment of per-net operating currents into the detailed router's net_current_ua vector. The detailed router contains a fixed EM_UA_PER_UM rule-of-thumb constant. A meaningful EM contract needs layer/process/temperature/lifetime-qualified capacity and currents for branches, vias and bottlenecks, not only a supply class or net-wide width preference. Use the appropriate DC/average, RMS and peak information under the PDK rule's semantics. Propagate current through actual route branches and verify current crowding and unequal via sharing where relevant. Iterate electrical heating with temperature-sensitive capacity when required. Keep Blech or other stress-based exemptions conditional on their physical applicability. The EM chapter and power chapter derive the reasons. The thermal estimate uses superposed half-space point sources with an effective near-field radius and fixed silicon conductivity. It is useful as a placement estimate but lacks detailed stack/package boundaries and transient heat flow. Calibrate its domain; attach confidence; promote sensitive candidates to a qualified model. Preserve the distinction between power from a real operating-point analysis and a fallback/probe assumption. Validate differential temperature at matched devices, not just distance from a hot box. Substrate isolation similarly needs injection/collection contacts, substrate layers and electrical return paths. Guard geometry with a weak or shared noisy return can provide less isolation than its shape suggests. Add a contact-network/transfer model at a useful fidelity and distinguish latch-up, substrate-noise and ESD mechanisms. The same ring shape does not automatically solve all three.","url":"roadmap.html#p4"},{"title":"Roadmap · Sixth: specialized generators and manufacturing closure","text":"Sixth: specialized generators and manufacturing closure A dedicated capacitor-array generator should preserve bit/unit identities, odd-count centroid residuals, separate plate graphs and bridge semantics, then enumerate spiral/chessboard/block alternatives. This is a new capability alongside the current merged plate/comb primitive , not a relabeling of it. Its acceptance criteria are connectivity, code INL/DNL, worst-bit settling and qualified manufacturing rules. See DAC arrays . Device sizing or refolding should become a search dimension only with an explicit equivalence contract and synchronized reference netlist. The MOS generator intentionally preserves schematic finger information and restricts patterns that would short terminals. Expand its choices through verified generators, not arbitrary permutations. New RF, high-voltage, bipolar or specialty structures similarly need qualified device models, extraction and recognition before optimization can claim their behavior. Close the manufacturing loop with voltage-dependent spacing, density/fill, antenna stage checks, redundant contacts, qualified dummy/tie rules, package/ESD interfaces and final geometry verification as applicable to the target process. Re-extract after parasitic-affecting repairs. Track exceptions as explicit approved design choices, not silently relaxed penalties. Foundry-specific numbers remain required inputs; the textbooks explain mechanisms and techniques but do not replace a PDK.","url":"roadmap.html#p5"},{"title":"Roadmap · Milestones that demonstrate better layouts","text":"Milestones that demonstrate better layouts Trustworthy certificates: terminal identities, model availability and checker coverage are correct and visible. Trustworthy matching: emitted unit geometry and characterized statistics predict the intended ratio/offset behavior within stated limits. Trustworthy electrical selection: candidate ranking respects required post-layout circuit specifications, with reproducible testbenches. Broader feasible search: route-aware placement, selective widths and specialized generators improve measured margins or tradeoffs at a reported compute cost. Robust closure: required variation, mission and manufacturing checks apply to the final emitted geometry, with remaining uncertainty named. Use the experiments in validation to demonstrate these milestones. The intended outcome is an engine that can explain why a constraint exists, which physical quantity it controls, where its model applies, and what evidence makes the selected layout acceptable.","url":"roadmap.html#delivery"},{"title":"Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design · Coverage and navigation","text":"Coverage and navigation The extracted main text of all nine chapters and Appendices A–D was read. The many low-text pages were inspected rather than treated as blanks: all thirteen main-content pages containing essentially only running headers were rendered and checked (PDF pp. 37, 68, 77, 83, 103, 105, 126, 128, 129, 131, 133, 137 and 141). They contain circuit cross-sections, plots and tables. Additional rendered checks covered the governing transport equation, sensitivity constraints, RC sizing algorithm, guard-ring return model, and Appendix A/B equations. This is comprehensive engineering coverage, not a line-by-line independent rederivation of every numerical method. Some fine plot labels are low-resolution in the original; no new quantitative rule is extracted from an unreadable label. Part / one-based PDF pages What to retain for the engine 1: Introduction, 24–32 Injection, transport and reception must be modeled together; local geometric changes can alter global coupling. 2: Mechanisms, 34–42 Body effect, junction capacitance, impact ionization, well/supply bounce, substrate contacts and delay modulation. 3: Analysis, 44–54 Boundary conditions, finite-element/finite-difference versus boundary-element formulations, layered Green functions and solver acceleration. 4: Modeling, 56–68 Activity-dependent injection signatures; multi-port extraction; timing/supply-noise iteration and convergence limits. 5: Constraints, 70–83 Local noise generators, performance sensitivity, worst-case timing, budget allocation and PLL example. 6: Optimization, 84–106 Incremental matrix updates, local sensitivities, exact rechecks, template reuse and performance-weighted error. 7: Circuit impact, 108–120 Feedback/stability, DC bias changes, thermal noise, passive losses, spurs, mixing and ESD model boundaries. 8: Physical guidance, 122–156 Substrate type, distance saturation, package inductance, differential balance, guard rings and buried shields. 9: Conclusion, 158 Noise-aware physical design must include packaging and calibrated technology models. Appendix A, 160–162 ; B, 164–166 Finite contact depth, induction-matrix scaling, derivatives with respect to layer properties and contact depth. Appendix C, 168–169 ; D, 170–182 Conditional annealing convergence; DC, RF, transient and frequency-translated measurement/calibration. Front matter, references and index were used as navigation and provenance. References cited by the book are not automatically independently read sources.","url":"substrate.html#coverage"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · What a trustworthy code comment should preserve","text":"What a trustworthy code comment should preserve Source result means the identified passage supports the stated mechanism within its assumptions. Derived result means the handbook performs additional algebra or extends a model; cite it as a derivation and preserve the premises. Foundation with a reference gap identifies a background law lacking a verified locator in the supplied PDFs. Proposed policy is our engineering contract, not a rule authored or proven in a textbook. Do not paste a source citation onto a broader claim: a centroid equation does not prove post-layout offset, a Black-law fit does not establish a universal current limit, and a classifier score does not establish yield. Numerical limits still need the actual PDK/model revision and design requirement. Physical PDF positions are one-based; printed page numbers are included only where checked. File hashes in the JSON and source inventory disambiguate editions. The records below are curated reusable justifications, not a claim that every sentence of the handbook is a source theorem. Existing Philis implementation observations were excluded from this theory audit.","url":"theory-provenance.html#use"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Which theory supports each proposed constraint?","text":"Which theory supports each proposed constraint? All 84 rows are handbook proposals. The references identify mechanisms or methodological foundations; the mapping does not make every part of a row an externally proven rule. Read the scope column before citing a whole predicate. Constraint Theory records What the references justify INT-01 · Terminal connectivity S01 · S02 Original terminal-connectivity acceptance contract; these are methodology records, not an externally numbered analog-law constraint. INT-02 · Device electrical identity O15 · H05 · S01 Layout-aware geometry/device-model consistency motivates the requirement; exact LVS tolerances and authorized sizing are design/PDK contracts. INT-03 · Required device ratios D01 · H09 · H12 Additive electrical composition, resistor end terms and capacitor perimeter effects explain why drawn-area ratios alone are insufficient. INT-04 · Bias and body domains H17 · P08 · S01 Carrier/substrate/contact physics motivates bias-domain preservation; actual legal voltages and well ties require qualified process rules. INT-05 · Allowed transformations H04 · H06 · O10 Signed orientation, local environment and restricted symmetry representations explain why geometric transforms require electrical preconditions. INT-06 · Fixed objects and interfaces O13 · H21 · S01 Hierarchical alternatives and process-aware interfaces motivate preserving fixed boundaries; the fixed-object contract is selected by the designer. INT-07 · Realization-map preservation O16 · S01 The source constraint lifecycle motivates preserving meaning through hierarchy; the explicit unit-to-macro identity map is our proposed representation. INT-08 · Realizable terminal access O08 · H21 · S01 Physical access, layer/contact geometry and interconnect capacity motivate legal pin access; the access predicate is PDK/generator-specific. GEO-01 · Units and legal grids H21 · S01 Manufacturing rule construction motivates legal geometry; lattice values and exact unit conversion are process/software contracts. GEO-02 · Width and length legality H21 · P20 Process-rule construction and manufactured cross-section motivate dimension limits; the numerical and conditional predicates come from the PDK. GEO-03 · Contextual separation H21 · O12 Conditional registration/spacing and explicit DTI examples motivate context-aware predicates; they do not define universal spacing values. GEO-04 · Enclosure and overlap H21 · P20 Registration/enclosure and terminal metal cross-section justify checking actual landing geometry and continuity. GEO-05 · Shape area and topology H21 · S01 General rule-construction rationale; exact area/notch/hole rule families and values require the target rule deck. GEO-06 · Occupancy and reserved regions O11 · O13 · S01 Island/hierarchy representations and selected keepouts motivate occupancy constraints; overlap permission remains device/process-specific. GEO-07 · Disjunctive spacing O12 The source explicitly models disjunctive DTI separation and coupled stretch; apply only to a process with those rules. GEO-08 · Routing-resource feasibility O13 · O08 · Q10 Hierarchical alternative retention, routing capacity and constructive array routing explain the need for route resources; a capacity estimate is a proxy. MAT-01 · Random mismatch allowance P01 · P04 · P05 · H01 Characterized area/distance laws, independent physical causes and sensitivity/covariance determine a mismatch allowance; circuit yield requires more than area. MAT-02 · Equivalent matched units H03 · P04 · Q02 Matched response requires compatible units, bias and environment; identical drawn dimensions alone do not establish it. MAT-03 · Weighted first moments Q01 · D01 · H02 Equal-unit linear cancellation is sourced; arbitrary ratio/weight normalization is a stated derivation, with a weighted-resistor example in Hastings. MAT-04 · Higher-order spatial moments Q06 · Q07 · D02 Polynomial cancellation and its recursive construction are sourced; weighted/finite-footprint extension and the selected order require explicit assumptions. MAT-05 · Physical orientation equivalence H04 · P03 Signed MOS orientation and process-specific orientation evidence motivate the comparison; legal rotation/reflection still depends on the model. MAT-06 · Selected symmetry relations O06 · O10 · O11 Source encodings/moves preserve selected symmetry relations; they may restrict the feasible search space and do not establish electrical matching alone. MAT-07 · Spatial proximity budget P01 · O05 · P11 Spatial mismatch and substrate transport have different distance dependence; proximity is a conditional proxy rather than a universal priority. MAT-08 · Edge and dummy equivalence H07 · Q02 Qualified dummy geometry/bias and edge context motivate environmental equivalence; floating structures are not universally invalid. MAT-09 · Well-proximity environment H06 · Q02 Well-proximity mechanisms and descriptor differences are sourced; actual coefficients and safe distances require the device model. MAT-10 · Isolation/stress environment H06 · Q02 LOD/STI and active-edge context can shift parameters at identical centroids; constrain actual modeled surroundings. MAT-11 · Matched electrical interconnect Q04 · D03 · Q25 Unit-current paths and competing RC effects motivate electrical matching; tolerance allocation is a circuit-specific derivation. MAT-12 · Matched temperature response H02 · H25 · P25 Temperature-weighted centroid matching is conditional on stated thermal/device assumptions; actual thermal mismatch needs source/boundary data. NET-01 · Series resistance O04 · O08 · D03 Sheet resistance, series paths and source-degeneration mechanisms justify a terminal/path allowance; meshes require a network solve. NET-02 · Node loading capacitance O04 · Q08 · Q25 Capacitance models and terminal/metric-specific effects justify node-resolved loading budgets instead of total C. NET-03 · Aggressor coupling D05 · P15 · H20 Charge conservation and source coupling/sensitivity analysis justify transfer-based noise limits; the simple fast-step ratio is derived, not a general noise bound. NET-04 · Shared performance budget O01 · O02 · O03 Shared performance allowance and signed sensitivity constraints are source-grounded; allocations must remain inside the model domain. NET-05 · Width/layer choice domain Q20 · Q24 · O04 · P20 Source width formulations and realized parallel tracks motivate legal option sets; electrical and reliability effects must be recomputed after realization. NET-06 · Shield connectivity and reference P12 · P13 · H20 · S01 Shield/guard/reference-impedance mechanisms motivate the requirement; detailed signal-shield connectivity and limits are our contract applied to qualified geometry. NET-07 · Sensitive-node route environment H20 · Q08 · Q16 Victim impedance, capacitor terminal role and proxy limitations motivate sensitive-route environments; numerical keepouts need a circuit/process basis. NET-08 · Shared-impedance coupling P08 · P12 · H20 Contact/return networks and Kelvin/shared-impedance mechanisms motivate the error budget; use the complete electrical return graph. NET-09 · Distributed-network adequacy D04 · P09 · H23 Lumped RC, electroquasistatic substrate assumptions and RF surroundings have different validity domains; promotion to stronger models is our evidence policy. NET-10 · Complete route realization O16 · S02 Constraint propagation and evidence coverage motivate verifying the final route graph; the exact realization certificate is original policy. PWR-01 · Branch-current conservation P19 · D04 Current-aware route topology is sourced; KCL tree sums are derived and require all downstream currents, including displacement/shunt loads; meshes require a solve. PWR-02 · DC supply/reference drop O08 · P19 · H20 Segment current, resistance and physical sense junctions motivate terminal drop checks; the allowed voltage error is a circuit requirement. PWR-03 · Dynamic supply/ground error P08 · P12 · H23 · S01 Shared/package return and RF mechanisms motivate dynamic analysis; a particular droop/bounce limit or decoupling design is not established by this row. PWR-04 · Conductor EM lifetime P16 · P17 · P18 · P23 Flux divergence, calibrated Black-law metrics, waveform classes and Blech-domain limits justify conditional lifetime checks. PWR-05 · Contact/via current reliability P20 · P21 · P22 · H19 Actual cut/landing geometry, unequal sharing and fixed-lifetime temperature scaling justify via/contact reliability checks. PWR-06 · Local current crowding P21 · H19 · H22 Local crowding and distributed access can dominate despite acceptable aggregate width/cut count; simplified models retain their limits. PWR-07 · Return-path integrity P08 · P12 · H20 · H23 Full return/reference impedance and RF surroundings justify considering complete loops; the routing contract remains circuit/package-specific. PWR-08 · Device voltage stress H18 · H24 · S01 ESD coordination and power-device safe operating behavior support mode-specific stress checks; numerical ratings come from qualified device data. ENV-01 · Dissipation model completeness H25 · P24 · S02 Thermal equations require actual dissipated heat and boundary conditions; missing-power result states are our original evidence policy. ENV-02 · Junction-temperature bound H25 · P24 Finite-source heat conduction is sourced; the half-space point-source expression is explicitly derived and unsuitable near a finite source or unmatched package boundary. ENV-03 · Thermal settling and drift H25 · H10 · S01 Transient/self-heating and thermoelectric mechanisms justify drift analysis; time-domain accuracy/settling limits are a selected circuit contract. ENV-04 · Substrate transfer P08 · P09 · P10 · P11 · P14 Injection/transport/reception, loading and differential transfer are sourced; distance alone is not a process-independent isolation measure. ENV-05 · Guard-ring realization H17 · P12 · P13 Guard/buried-shield mechanisms differ by carriers, depth, bias and return path; the actual ring must be a qualified structure. ENV-06 · Guard-return impedance P12 · P14 · P15 Grounding scheme, victim reference and correlated/phase-dependent injection determine useful isolation. ENV-07 · Latch-up prevention H17 · P08 · S01 Carrier collection and substrate feedback motivate latch-up prevention; foundry injection/tap/spacing rules supply the actual predicate. ENV-08 · Isolation-domain compatibility H17 · P13 · O12 Different isolation structures have different domain and geometric premises; selected process-specific compatibility must be preserved. DEV-01 · Resistor value and ratio H09 · H11 Resistor contacts, ends, bends and material-specific lead scaling explain why a simple body-area ratio is insufficient. DEV-02 · Resistor parasitic/environment error H10 · H11 · H25 Thermoelectric paths, interconnection parasitics and thermal conditions justify environment checks; voltage/noise/frequency models require qualified resistor data. DEV-03 · Capacitor value and terminal roles H12 · H13 · Q08 Perimeter and plate-specific parasitic behavior justify preserving capacitor construction, polarity/terminal roles and extracted value. DEV-04 · Capacitor voltage/frequency domain H13 · H23 · S01 MOS capacitor operation and RF passive loss/context support model-domain requirements; exact voltage/frequency/reliability limits remain process-specific. DEV-05 · Bipolar/diode construction H14 · H15 · H17 Device-family construction, injection and isolation justify preserving physical device identity; no universal BJT/diode generator is inferred. DEV-06 · Bipolar matching and current density H14 · H15 · H25 BJT/diode family-specific matching and thermal behavior motivate current-density and geometry conditions; source families must not be conflated. DEV-07 · Inductor/RF passive performance H23 Inductor geometry, substrate and surrounding conductors jointly determine useful RF behavior; field/characterization evidence is still required. DEV-08 · RF interaction and distributed routing H23 · P09 · S01 RF and substrate model domains motivate distributed/field treatment where needed; complete RF signoff is outside the handbook’s survey scope. ARR-01 · Exact array weights Q08 · Q09 · D01 Circuit bank counts and split-node topology must be preserved; arbitrary nonbinary constructions require their own additive/composition derivation. ARR-02 · Array spatial error Q01 · Q06 · Q09 · Q14 Moment cancellation, pattern tradeoffs and uncertainty in correlation data motivate code-relevant spatial evaluation. ARR-03 · Code-dependent parasitics Q08 · Q10 Terminal-specific parasitics and internal routing affect the code transfer under stated architecture/excitation assumptions. ARR-04 · INL/DNL and monotonicity Q11 INL/DNL definitions support code/transition evaluation; the reference convention and required monotonicity are explicit design choices. ARR-05 · Settling and switching transients Q12 · D10 · Q15 Separate paper-specific frequency, derived single-pole settling and full transient requirements; dynamic reference/glitch checks are proposed extensions. ARR-06 · Array routing realizability Q09 · Q10 · Q15 Constructive placement/routing and bottleneck movement support evaluating routed alternatives rather than placement moments alone. MFG-01 · Density and fill H08 · H21 · S01 Fill can alter passivation/matching and process density constraints require actual rules; final-fill evidence invalidation is our proposed lifecycle policy. MFG-02 · Defect critical area O09 Defect-size-dependent critical area and defect-yield models are distinct from parametric yield; distribution/density need characterization. MFG-03 · Process topology robustness H21 · H08 · P21 Process construction, fill environment and redundant-via/current sharing motivate robustness checks; arbitrary geometric safety factors are not sourced rules. MFG-04 · Antenna exposure H16 Antenna damage depends on conductor connectivity at fabrication stages, rather than final-layer connectivity alone. MFG-05 · ESD discharge path H18 · H19 Clamp coordination and path current density motivate complete discharge-network checks; qualified stress architecture supplies numeric limits. MFG-06 · Test accessibility and discrimination O26 · S01 · S02 The source distinguishes fault incidence from detectability and uses a response-separation proxy. Actual test coverage requires the selected fault model, stimulus and statistical decision rule; it is not proven by the proxy. EVD-01 · Electrical specification vector Q16 · Q25 · S01 Proxy limitations and competing performance metrics justify explicit specification acceptance; the metric set is circuit-specific. EVD-02 · Operating-domain coverage O17 · H24 · S01 Operating conditions and dynamic device state differ from manufacturing randomness; actual scenario reduction needs evidence. EVD-03 · Valid variation model P04 · P05 · O18 · Q14 Independent physical causes, covariance transformations and statistical-data limits motivate valid dependence/domain checks. EVD-04 · Joint parametric yield O17 · O19 · O20 Joint yield, local ellipsoidal worst cases and probability-region interpretation justify preserving the full event and its approximation limits. EVD-05 · Statistical confidence O21 · D09 Source sampling uncertainty plus explicit binomial inversion explain why zero failures do not prove perfect yield. EVD-06 · Proxy/model validity O03 · O15 · Q18 · Q22 Sensitivity locality, sizing/extraction domain, transfer results and classifier scope motivate revalidation; domain metadata is original policy. EVD-07 · Required checker coverage O16 · S02 Constraint metaverification motivates consistency/coverage; pass/fail/unknown schema and required-evaluator semantics are our proposed contract. EVD-08 · Fresh evidence identity O16 · S02 Source constraint lifecycle motivates freshness; exact hashes, dependency invalidation and cache keys are original implementation policy. EVD-09 · Search-domain and proof scope O10 · O11 · O13 · S03 Representation restrictions and conditional search guarantees justify distinguishing a restricted optimum from best observed or failed search. EVD-10 · Independent outcome validation Q23 · Q16 · S02 Final simulated evaluation and the proxy-performance gap motivate independent outcome checks; the complete acceptance method is proposed engineering policy.","url":"theory-provenance.html#constraint-map"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Device construction and physical layout","text":"Device construction and physical layout H01 · Random matching is not one universal area law Source result. Separate statistical variation from systematic offsets; areal and peripheral variation contribute differently, with inverse-square-root area only in the areal-dominant regime. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §8.1; §8.2.1 Random Variation, Eqs8.6–8.10. PDF p. 368 · PDF pp. 371–374 . Assumptions and limits: Process/material/model coefficients and valid device dimensions must be characterized; differently sized units can be dominated by the smaller unit. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H01; source]: Separate statistical variation from systematic offsets; areal and // peripheral variation contribute differently, with inverse-square-root area only in the // areal-dominant regime. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.1; // §8.2.1 Random Variation, Eqs8.6–8.10. Physical PDF pp. 368, 371–374. // Applies when: Process/material/model coefficients and valid device dimensions must be // characterized; differently sized units can be dominated by the smaller unit. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H01 H02 · Electrical sensitivity defines the relevant centroid Source result. A resistor-network centroid is weighted by its sensitivity to unit resistance, rather than by cell outline or instance count. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §8.2.7 Temperature, Eqs8.24–8.25. PDF p. 390 . Assumptions and limits: Source example uses identical nominal resistors subject to equal absolute perturbation. A fractional perturbation generalization weights Ri·∂Req/∂Ri and is a chain-rule extension, not Eq8.24 verbatim. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H02; source]: A resistor-network centroid is weighted by its sensitivity to unit // resistance, rather than by cell outline or instance count. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.7 // Temperature, Eqs8.24–8.25. Physical PDF pp. 390. // Applies when: Source example uses identical nominal resistors subject to equal absolute // perturbation. A fractional perturbation generalization weights Ri·∂Req/∂Ri and is a chain-rule // extension, not Eq8.24 verbatim. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H02 H03 · Common centroid has conditions and implementation costs Source result. Coincidence, symmetry, compactness and balanced subarrays reduce specified gradient errors; excessive segmentation can reduce active area and worsen random matching. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §8.2.7, Table8.4 and discussion following Table8.4. PDF pp. 392–394 . Assumptions and limits: Linear-field cancellation is first order. Curved fields, routing/end terms and fixed footprint require a separate error budget; no-gradient cases gain no gradient benefit. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H03; source]: Coincidence, symmetry, compactness and balanced subarrays reduce specified // gradient errors; excessive segmentation can reduce active area and worsen random matching. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.7, // Table8.4 and discussion following Table8.4. Physical PDF pp. 392–394. // Applies when: Linear-field cancellation is first order. Curved fields, routing/end terms and // fixed footprint require a separate error budget; no-gradient cases gain no gradient benefit. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H03 H04 · MOS signed orientation must match Source result. For identical MOS sections with horizontal channel current, orientation is the mean of ±1 current directions. Matched arrays require equal signed means; equal nonzero means are allowed. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §13.2.5 Gradients, Eq13.61 and Table13.2. PDF pp. 708–709 . Assumptions and limits: Identical unit sections and electrical terminal directions; in two dimensions compare both components. Relative geometric rotation alone is insufficient. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H04; source]: For identical MOS sections with horizontal channel current, orientation is // the mean of ±1 current directions. Matched arrays require equal signed means; equal nonzero // means are allowed. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.2.5 // Gradients, Eq13.61 and Table13.2. Physical PDF pp. 708–709. // Applies when: Identical unit sections and electrical terminal directions; in two dimensions // compare both components. Relative geometric rotation alone is insufficient. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H04 H05 · MOS current matching depends on bias and geometry Source result. Long-channel saturation has ID=kVov²/2, gm=√(2kID), k=k′We/Le; the mismatch observable differs between fixed gate voltage and fixed drain current. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §12.1 Eqs12.3–12.4; §12.1.1 Eqs12.6–12.9; §12.1.3 limits; §13.2 and §13.2.1. PDF pp. 576–577 · PDF pp. 584–585 · PDF pp. 686–690 . Assumptions and limits: Strong inversion, long-channel model; ignore CLM/velocity saturation only when justified. Use effective rather than blindly drawn geometry. Current/gate bias assumptions must accompany derived sensitivities. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H05; source]: Long-channel saturation has ID=kVov²/2, gm=√(2kID), k=k′We/Le; the // mismatch observable differs between fixed gate voltage and fixed drain current. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §12.1 // Eqs12.3–12.4; §12.1.1 Eqs12.6–12.9; §12.1.3 limits; §13.2 and §13.2.1. Physical PDF pp. 576–577, // 584–585, 686–690. // Applies when: Strong inversion, long-channel model; ignore CLM/velocity saturation only when // justified. Use effective rather than blindly drawn geometry. Current/gate bias assumptions must // accompany derived sensitivities. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H05 H06 · WPE and LOD encode different local environments Source result. Well implant scattering, trench stress and length-of-diffusion/end geometry can change otherwise nominally identical MOS devices. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §13.2.2 Diffusion, Implantation, Etch, and Trench Effects; Figs13.49–13.52. PDF pp. 695–698 . Assumptions and limits: PDK-specific mechanism magnitude/sign and device polarity; preserve actual edge distances or qualified dummies. Fig13.52 is physical PDF698. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H06; source]: Well implant scattering, trench stress and length-of-diffusion/end // geometry can change otherwise nominally identical MOS devices. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.2.2 // Diffusion, Implantation, Etch, and Trench Effects; Figs13.49–13.52. Physical PDF pp. 695–698. // Applies when: PDK-specific mechanism magnitude/sign and device polarity; preserve actual edge // distances or qualified dummies. Fig13.52 is physical PDF698. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H06 H07 · Dummies need process and electrical meaning Source result. Matched MOS edges require suitable gate/dummy neighborhood and overhang; half/full dummies and inactive terminal ties are not interchangeable. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §13.2.2, Figs13.45–13.46; §13.3 dummy recommendations. PDF pp. 692–693 · PDF pp. 713–714 . Assumptions and limits: Use qualified dummy extent/pitch and safe terminal bias. Added rectangles do not automatically reproduce the active device environment. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H07; source]: Matched MOS edges require suitable gate/dummy neighborhood and overhang; // half/full dummies and inactive terminal ties are not interchangeable. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.2.2, // Figs13.45–13.46; §13.3 dummy recommendations. Physical PDF pp. 692–693, 713–714. // Applies when: Use qualified dummy extent/pitch and safe terminal bias. Added rectangles do not // automatically reproduce the active device environment. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H07 H08 · Metal fill can perturb passivation and matching Source result. Metal above or near matched MOS regions can change hydrogenation/passivation and environmental symmetry. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §13.2.4 Hydrogenation; §13.3 rules17–18. PDF pp. 702–704 · PDF p. 716 . Assumptions and limits: Process-dependent hydrogen transport/material stack; source numerical distances and preferences are not generic modern-node mandates. Fill also has extraction-dependent electrical effects. Handbook workflow implication: final fill must be included when rechecking affected geometry and electrical behavior; that lifecycle policy is our derivation from the physical mechanism. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H08; source]: Metal above or near matched MOS regions can change // hydrogenation/passivation and environmental symmetry. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.2.4 // Hydrogenation; §13.3 rules17–18. Physical PDF pp. 702–704, 716. // Applies when: Process-dependent hydrogen transport/material stack; source numerical distances // and preferences are not generic modern-node mandates. Fill also has extraction-dependent // electrical effects. Handbook workflow implication: final fill must be included when rechecking // affected geometry and electrical behavior; that lifecycle policy is our derivation from the // physical mechanism. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H08 H09 · Resistor physical geometry includes end and corner terms Source result. Unequal widths turn common process bias into ratio error; contacts/heads/corners must be matched or modeled. An ideal equal-width right-angle corner is 0.5587 squares. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §6.2 Resistor Layout, Eqs6.5–6.10; §8.2.2 Eq8.18. PDF pp. 279–282 · PDF p. 374 . Assumptions and limits: The corner result assumes the source ideal sheet/current-boundary geometry. Real contact and process-biased corners require calibrated models; identical systematic heads can cancel. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H09; source]: Unequal widths turn common process bias into ratio error; // contacts/heads/corners must be matched or modeled. An ideal equal-width right-angle corner is // 0.5587 squares. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §6.2 // Resistor Layout, Eqs6.5–6.10; §8.2.2 Eq8.18. Physical PDF pp. 279–282, 374. // Applies when: The corner result assumes the source ideal sheet/current-boundary geometry. Real // contact and process-biased corners require calibrated models; identical systematic heads can // cancel. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H09 H10 · Thermoelectric cancellation is a connection problem Source result. ET=SΔTC describes the net contact-temperature EMF; series sections can add or cancel it depending on electrical traversal. Common-centroid placement alone cannot cancel it. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §8.2.7, Eq8.34 and Figs8.22–8.23. PDF pp. 401–402 . Assumptions and limits: Matched material Seebeck behavior and temperature drops; opposite path directions must be paired. An even segment count alone does not guarantee cancellation in an arbitrary field. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H10; source]: ET=SΔTC describes the net contact-temperature EMF; series sections can add // or cancel it depending on electrical traversal. Common-centroid placement alone cannot cancel // it. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.7, // Eq8.34 and Figs8.22–8.23. Physical PDF pp. 401–402. // Applies when: Matched material Seebeck behavior and temperature drops; opposite path directions // must be paired. An even segment count alone does not guarantee cancellation in an arbitrary // field. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H10 H11 · Precision resistor leads must scale by material Source result. Metal, contact and via contributions can change an intended resistor ratio; for ratioed devices each material contribution should preserve the ratio or be budgeted separately. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §8.2.4 Interconnection Parasitics; §8.3.1 rule23. PDF pp. 380–381 · PDF p. 422 . Assumptions and limits: Different lead materials have different temperature coefficients. Equal body R ratios or equal route lengths alone do not establish terminal ratios. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H11; source]: Metal, contact and via contributions can change an intended resistor // ratio; for ratioed devices each material contribution should preserve the ratio or be budgeted // separately. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.4 // Interconnection Parasitics; §8.3.1 rule23. Physical PDF pp. 380–381, 422. // Applies when: Different lead materials have different temperature coefficients. Equal body R // ratios or equal route lengths alone do not establish terminal ratios. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H11 H12 · Capacitor ratios need perimeter and topology control Source result. Identical parallel units control ratio errors from area and periphery; series interconnection introduces different electrode-parasitic behavior. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §8.2.2 Process Biases; §8.3.2 rules1–3. PDF pp. 374–375 · PDF p. 423 . Assumptions and limits: Fixed capacitor family and actual plate connections; nonunit geometries must match sensitivity rather than merely nominal C. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H12; source]: Identical parallel units control ratio errors from area and periphery; // series interconnection introduces different electrode-parasitic behavior. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.2 // Process Biases; §8.3.2 rules1–3. Physical PDF pp. 374–375, 423. // Applies when: Fixed capacitor family and actual plate connections; nonunit geometries must match // sensitivity rather than merely nominal C. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H12 H13 · Capacitor plate roles and surroundings matter Source result. Capacitor electrodes can have asymmetric substrate/interconnect parasitics; edge dummies and shields need intentional quiet connections and balanced surroundings. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §7.1 MOS capacitor construction; §8.2.3 Fig8.5; §8.3.2 rules5–10. PDF pp. 339–340 · PDF p. 378 · PDF pp. 424–425 . Assumptions and limits: Real terminal roles, finite shield impedance, bias and surrounding noise matter. Dummies cannot be assigned arbitrary floating or noisy potentials. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H13; source]: Capacitor electrodes can have asymmetric substrate/interconnect // parasitics; edge dummies and shields need intentional quiet connections and balanced // surroundings. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §7.1 MOS // capacitor construction; §8.2.3 Fig8.5; §8.3.2 rules5–10. Physical PDF pp. 339–340, 378, 424–425. // Applies when: Real terminal roles, finite shield impedance, bias and surrounding noise matter. // Dummies cannot be assigned arbitrary floating or noisy potentials. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H13 H14 · BJT matching is device-family specific Source result. Vertical emitters, lateral emitter perimeters, beta/base-current errors, collector bias and thermal gradients demand different matching checks. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §10.2.1–3; §10.2.6; §10.3.1–2. PDF pp. 506–509 · PDF pp. 522–523 · PDF pp. 526–529 . Assumptions and limits: Identify matched observable and operating region; same total area does not make a lateral device equivalent, and reverse-BE stress can degrade accuracy. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H14; source]: Vertical emitters, lateral emitter perimeters, beta/base-current errors, // collector bias and thermal gradients demand different matching checks. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. // §10.2.1–3; §10.2.6; §10.3.1–2. Physical PDF pp. 506–509, 522–523, 526–529. // Applies when: Identify matched observable and operating region; same total area does not make a // lateral device equivalent, and reverse-BE stress can degrade accuracy. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H14 H15 · Do not merge diode matching models Source result. PN current definition, Schottky field/barrier construction and nonuniform Zener avalanche produce different matching/injection behavior. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §11.2 Schottky field-relief discussion; §11.3.1–3 diode matching. PDF p. 563 · PDF pp. 565–570 . Assumptions and limits: Schottky guard-junction forward bias can inject minority carriers; Zener segments may current-hog. Do not assume ordinary unit-area averaging applies to avalanche. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H15; source]: PN current definition, Schottky field/barrier construction and nonuniform // Zener avalanche produce different matching/injection behavior. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §11.2 // Schottky field-relief discussion; §11.3.1–3 diode matching. Physical PDF pp. 563, 565–570. // Applies when: Schottky guard-junction forward bias can inject minority carriers; Zener segments // may current-hog. Do not assume ordinary unit-area averaging applies to avalanche. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H15 H16 · Antenna checking follows fabrication stages Source result. Plasma damage depends on the conductive geometry connected to a gate during each process stage; later-layer jumpers and protective diodes address different exposed-node situations. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §5.1.6 The Antenna Effect. PDF pp. 228–230 . Assumptions and limits: Foundry defines gate denominator, exposed area/perimeter, layer accumulation and diode credit. Final net connectivity is insufficient; stage-graph implementation is handbook synthesis. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H16; source]: Plasma damage depends on the conductive geometry connected to a gate // during each process stage; later-layer jumpers and protective diodes address different // exposed-node situations. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §5.1.6 // The Antenna Effect. Physical PDF pp. 228–230. // Applies when: Foundry defines gate denominator, exposed area/perimeter, layer accumulation and // diode credit. Final net connectivity is insufficient; stage-graph implementation is handbook // synthesis. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H16 H17 · Guard rings require carrier, depth and return-path context Source result. Collecting PN junctions, high-low blocking regions and majority taps have different functions; minority carriers can bypass shallow guards or debias weak returns. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §5.4.4 Guard Rings; §14.2.1–5 process examples. PDF pp. 264–270 · PDF pp. 736–738 · PDF p. 741 . Assumptions and limits: Qualified geometry/bias/substrate stack required. Blocking guards need appropriate complete 3D barriers; special collecting L/U guards near die edges are not generalized to blockers. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H17; source]: Collecting PN junctions, high-low blocking regions and majority taps have // different functions; minority carriers can bypass shallow guards or debias weak returns. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §5.4.4 // Guard Rings; §14.2.1–5 process examples. Physical PDF pp. 264–270, 736–738, 741. // Applies when: Qualified geometry/bias/substrate stack required. Blocking guards need appropriate // complete 3D barriers; special collecting L/U guards near die edges are not generalized to // blockers. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H17 H18 · ESD protection must coordinate actual terminal voltages Source result. Clipping, snapback and rate-triggered protectors require different operating constraints; secondary protection must use the vulnerable device local reference and qualified limiting paths. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §14.4.1 Primary Protection; §14.4.2 Secondary Protection, Fig14.36. PDF pp. 750–752 · PDF pp. 765–769 . Assumptions and limits: Normal supply/slew/failsafe conditions, stress polarity and tested cell topology matter. Multifinger width does not imply uniform current; local ballast may be intentional. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H18; source]: Clipping, snapback and rate-triggered protectors require different // operating constraints; secondary protection must use the vulnerable device local reference and // qualified limiting paths. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §14.4.1 // Primary Protection; §14.4.2 Secondary Protection, Fig14.36. Physical PDF pp. 750–752, 765–769. // Applies when: Normal supply/slew/failsafe conditions, stress polarity and tested cell topology // matter. Multifinger width does not imply uniform current; local ballast may be intentional. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H18 H19 · Local current density sets reliability bottlenecks Source result. Via arrays and bent leads need current-distribution analysis: inner-corner vias can carry far more current than outer ones. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §5.1.3 Electromigration; §15.4.4, Eqs15.24–15.25 and Fig15.29. PDF pp. 217–220 · PDF pp. 821–822 . Assumptions and limits: Foundry lifetime model, material/interface, local temperature and waveform determine limits. Total via count and average wire width are insufficient. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H19; source]: Via arrays and bent leads need current-distribution analysis: inner-corner // vias can carry far more current than outer ones. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §5.1.3 // Electromigration; §15.4.4, Eqs15.24–15.25 and Fig15.29. Physical PDF pp. 217–220, 821–822. // Applies when: Foundry lifetime model, material/interface, local temperature and waveform // determine limits. Total via count and average wire width are insufficient. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H19 H20 · Physical Kelvin junctions and victim impedance matter Source result. Shared-path voltage drops persist on one named net; capacitive disturbance depends on aggressor slew and victim impedance, while shields need an effective reference. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §15.4.4 Special Routing Techniques, Figs15.26–15.28 and Eq15.23. PDF pp. 818–820 . Assumptions and limits: Use actual branch topology and local references. Eq15.23 is a simplified lumped relation; frequency-dependent impedances and return paths may require extraction. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H20; source]: Shared-path voltage drops persist on one named net; capacitive disturbance // depends on aggressor slew and victim impedance, while shields need an effective reference. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §15.4.4 // Special Routing Techniques, Figs15.26–15.28 and Eq15.23. Physical PDF pp. 818–820. // Applies when: Use actual branch topology and local references. Eq15.23 is a simplified lumped // relation; frequency-dependent impedances and return paths may require extraction. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H20 H21 · Manufacturable pitch includes enclosure and registration Source result. Design-rule margins account for fabrication deviations; routing pitch must allow legal via/contact landing geometry, not only drawn line width plus nominal line spacing. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §3.2.3 Design Rule Construction; §15.4.2 Eqs15.18–15.20; AppendixC disclaimer. PDF pp. 138–144 · PDF pp. 812–814 · PDF p. 836 . Assumptions and limits: Numeric textbook rules are teaching examples. Use actual PDK shape/grid/stacking rules and qualified waivers; geometry needs to be checked after generation. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H21; source]: Design-rule margins account for fabrication deviations; routing pitch must // allow legal via/contact landing geometry, not only drawn line width plus nominal line spacing. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §3.2.3 // Design Rule Construction; §15.4.2 Eqs15.18–15.20; AppendixC disclaimer. Physical PDF pp. // 138–144, 812–814, 836. // Applies when: Numeric textbook rules are teaching examples. Use actual PDK shape/grid/stacking // rules and qualified waivers; geometry needs to be checked after generation. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H21 H22 · Exact distributed-finger model before the one-third limit Derived result. For constant r,g and a singly terminated finger of length L, Rtotal=RSi·u·coth(u), u=L√(rg); small u gives added metal R≈rL/3. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §13.1.1 Eq13.11; AppendixE EqsE.1–E.4, E.7, E.9–E.13. PDF p. 650 · PDF pp. 871–872 . Assumptions and limits: Uniform geometry and distributed silicon conductance; v(0)=V, i(L)=0. Not a generic bound for tapered/strongly nonuniform devices. Supplied E.6 sign, E.8 prefactor and E.14 sqrt appear inconsistent. Derivation: Solve v′=−ri and i′=−gv with open far-end boundary: i(0)=√(g/r)V tanh(L√(rg)); invert and use g=1/(LRSi). Expand ucothu=1+u²/3−u⁴/45+…. This agrees with source E.9; correction is audit algebra, not publisher-confirmed erratum. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H22; derived]: For constant r,g and a singly terminated finger of length L, // Rtotal=RSi·u·coth(u), u=L√(rg); small u gives added metal R≈rL/3. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 // catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.1.1 Eq13.11; AppendixE EqsE.1–E.4, E.7, E.9–E.13. // Physical PDF pp. 650, 871–872. // Applies when: Uniform geometry and distributed silicon conductance; v(0)=V, i(L)=0. Not a // generic bound for tapered/strongly nonuniform devices. Supplied E.6 sign, E.8 prefactor and E.14 // sqrt appear inconsistent. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H22 H23 · RF passive surroundings are part of the device Source result. Skin/proximity loss, eddy currents, capacitance and surrounding metal determine frequency-dependent inductor quality; patterned shields must interrupt induced loops. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §7.2.1 Inductor Parasitics and §7.2.2 Inductor Construction. PDF pp. 358–363 . Assumptions and limits: Use frequency-dependent port/EM models and qualified fill exclusions. Parallel metal stacking differs from series winding layers; more turns/width is not universally better. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H23; source]: Skin/proximity loss, eddy currents, capacitance and surrounding metal // determine frequency-dependent inductor quality; patterned shields must interrupt induced loops. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §7.2.1 // Inductor Parasitics and §7.2.2 Inductor Construction. Physical PDF pp. 358–363. // Applies when: Use frequency-dependent port/EM models and qualified fill exclusions. Parallel // metal stacking differs from series winding layers; more turns/width is not universally better. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H23 H24 · Power MOS layout needs dynamic and thermal checks Source result. Distributed gate resistance/capacitance causes nonuniform switching; body resistance and electrothermal feedback can localize current even when on-resistance has a positive temperature coefficient in another regime. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §13.1.2 Switching Losses, Eqs13.21–13.23/Figs13.8–13.10; §13.1.3 SOA; §13.1.4 SenseFET discussion. PDF pp. 652–655 · PDF pp. 659–660 · PDF pp. 667–668 . Assumptions and limits: Waveform, bias, package heat path and process determine SOA. SenseFET layout must sample representative terminal voltages/temperature; repeated small-signal units alone do not establish power capability. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H24; source]: Distributed gate resistance/capacitance causes nonuniform switching; body // resistance and electrothermal feedback can localize current even when on-resistance has a // positive temperature coefficient in another regime. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.1.2 // Switching Losses, Eqs13.21–13.23/Figs13.8–13.10; §13.1.3 SOA; §13.1.4 SenseFET discussion. // Physical PDF pp. 652–655, 659–660, 667–668. // Applies when: Waveform, bias, package heat path and process determine SOA. SenseFET layout must // sample representative terminal voltages/temperature; repeated small-signal units alone do not // establish power capability. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H24 H25 · Thermal-gradient theory needs boundary conditions Source result. Heat flux follows Fourier’s law q=−κ∇T; package-dependent 3D heat flow and active-region weighting determine how temperature gradients affect matching. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §5.1.1 Self-Heating, Eq5.6 finite source and Eqs5.7–5.9; §8.2.7 Temperature Eq8.22 and Eq8.23 context. PDF pp. 213–214 · PDF pp. 386–388 . Assumptions and limits: Active-centroid model assumes local/planar contribution, suitable linear temperature response and a linear field. P/(2πκr) is not found verbatim in this book; a half-space point-source use must be labeled an independent Fourier-law derivation with its own boundaries. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H25; source]: Heat flux follows Fourier’s law q=−κ∇T; package-dependent 3D heat flow and // active-region weighting determine how temperature gradients affect matching. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §5.1.1 // Self-Heating, Eq5.6 finite source and Eqs5.7–5.9; §8.2.7 Temperature Eq8.22 and Eq8.23 context. // Physical PDF pp. 213–214, 386–388. // Applies when: Active-centroid model assumes local/planar contribution, suitable linear // temperature response and a linear field. P/(2πκr) is not found verbatim in this book; a // half-space point-source use must be labeled an independent Fourier-law derivation with its own // boundaries. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H25","url":"theory-provenance.html#hastings-records"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H01 · Random matching is not one universal area law","text":"H01 · Random matching is not one universal area law Source result. Separate statistical variation from systematic offsets; areal and peripheral variation contribute differently, with inverse-square-root area only in the areal-dominant regime. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §8.1; §8.2.1 Random Variation, Eqs8.6–8.10. PDF p. 368 · PDF pp. 371–374 . Assumptions and limits: Process/material/model coefficients and valid device dimensions must be characterized; differently sized units can be dominated by the smaller unit. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H01; source]: Separate statistical variation from systematic offsets; areal and // peripheral variation contribute differently, with inverse-square-root area only in the // areal-dominant regime. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.1; // §8.2.1 Random Variation, Eqs8.6–8.10. Physical PDF pp. 368, 371–374. // Applies when: Process/material/model coefficients and valid device dimensions must be // characterized; differently sized units can be dominated by the smaller unit. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H01","url":"theory-provenance.html#H01"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H02 · Electrical sensitivity defines the relevant centroid","text":"H02 · Electrical sensitivity defines the relevant centroid Source result. A resistor-network centroid is weighted by its sensitivity to unit resistance, rather than by cell outline or instance count. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §8.2.7 Temperature, Eqs8.24–8.25. PDF p. 390 . Assumptions and limits: Source example uses identical nominal resistors subject to equal absolute perturbation. A fractional perturbation generalization weights Ri·∂Req/∂Ri and is a chain-rule extension, not Eq8.24 verbatim. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H02; source]: A resistor-network centroid is weighted by its sensitivity to unit // resistance, rather than by cell outline or instance count. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.7 // Temperature, Eqs8.24–8.25. Physical PDF pp. 390. // Applies when: Source example uses identical nominal resistors subject to equal absolute // perturbation. A fractional perturbation generalization weights Ri·∂Req/∂Ri and is a chain-rule // extension, not Eq8.24 verbatim. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H02","url":"theory-provenance.html#H02"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H03 · Common centroid has conditions and implementation costs","text":"H03 · Common centroid has conditions and implementation costs Source result. Coincidence, symmetry, compactness and balanced subarrays reduce specified gradient errors; excessive segmentation can reduce active area and worsen random matching. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §8.2.7, Table8.4 and discussion following Table8.4. PDF pp. 392–394 . Assumptions and limits: Linear-field cancellation is first order. Curved fields, routing/end terms and fixed footprint require a separate error budget; no-gradient cases gain no gradient benefit. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H03; source]: Coincidence, symmetry, compactness and balanced subarrays reduce specified // gradient errors; excessive segmentation can reduce active area and worsen random matching. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.7, // Table8.4 and discussion following Table8.4. Physical PDF pp. 392–394. // Applies when: Linear-field cancellation is first order. Curved fields, routing/end terms and // fixed footprint require a separate error budget; no-gradient cases gain no gradient benefit. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H03","url":"theory-provenance.html#H03"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H04 · MOS signed orientation must match","text":"H04 · MOS signed orientation must match Source result. For identical MOS sections with horizontal channel current, orientation is the mean of ±1 current directions. Matched arrays require equal signed means; equal nonzero means are allowed. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §13.2.5 Gradients, Eq13.61 and Table13.2. PDF pp. 708–709 . Assumptions and limits: Identical unit sections and electrical terminal directions; in two dimensions compare both components. Relative geometric rotation alone is insufficient. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H04; source]: For identical MOS sections with horizontal channel current, orientation is // the mean of ±1 current directions. Matched arrays require equal signed means; equal nonzero // means are allowed. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.2.5 // Gradients, Eq13.61 and Table13.2. Physical PDF pp. 708–709. // Applies when: Identical unit sections and electrical terminal directions; in two dimensions // compare both components. Relative geometric rotation alone is insufficient. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H04","url":"theory-provenance.html#H04"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H05 · MOS current matching depends on bias and geometry","text":"H05 · MOS current matching depends on bias and geometry Source result. Long-channel saturation has ID=kVov²/2, gm=√(2kID), k=k′We/Le; the mismatch observable differs between fixed gate voltage and fixed drain current. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §12.1 Eqs12.3–12.4; §12.1.1 Eqs12.6–12.9; §12.1.3 limits; §13.2 and §13.2.1. PDF pp. 576–577 · PDF pp. 584–585 · PDF pp. 686–690 . Assumptions and limits: Strong inversion, long-channel model; ignore CLM/velocity saturation only when justified. Use effective rather than blindly drawn geometry. Current/gate bias assumptions must accompany derived sensitivities. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H05; source]: Long-channel saturation has ID=kVov²/2, gm=√(2kID), k=k′We/Le; the // mismatch observable differs between fixed gate voltage and fixed drain current. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §12.1 // Eqs12.3–12.4; §12.1.1 Eqs12.6–12.9; §12.1.3 limits; §13.2 and §13.2.1. Physical PDF pp. 576–577, // 584–585, 686–690. // Applies when: Strong inversion, long-channel model; ignore CLM/velocity saturation only when // justified. Use effective rather than blindly drawn geometry. Current/gate bias assumptions must // accompany derived sensitivities. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H05","url":"theory-provenance.html#H05"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H06 · WPE and LOD encode different local environments","text":"H06 · WPE and LOD encode different local environments Source result. Well implant scattering, trench stress and length-of-diffusion/end geometry can change otherwise nominally identical MOS devices. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §13.2.2 Diffusion, Implantation, Etch, and Trench Effects; Figs13.49–13.52. PDF pp. 695–698 . Assumptions and limits: PDK-specific mechanism magnitude/sign and device polarity; preserve actual edge distances or qualified dummies. Fig13.52 is physical PDF698. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H06; source]: Well implant scattering, trench stress and length-of-diffusion/end // geometry can change otherwise nominally identical MOS devices. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.2.2 // Diffusion, Implantation, Etch, and Trench Effects; Figs13.49–13.52. Physical PDF pp. 695–698. // Applies when: PDK-specific mechanism magnitude/sign and device polarity; preserve actual edge // distances or qualified dummies. Fig13.52 is physical PDF698. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H06","url":"theory-provenance.html#H06"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H07 · Dummies need process and electrical meaning","text":"H07 · Dummies need process and electrical meaning Source result. Matched MOS edges require suitable gate/dummy neighborhood and overhang; half/full dummies and inactive terminal ties are not interchangeable. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §13.2.2, Figs13.45–13.46; §13.3 dummy recommendations. PDF pp. 692–693 · PDF pp. 713–714 . Assumptions and limits: Use qualified dummy extent/pitch and safe terminal bias. Added rectangles do not automatically reproduce the active device environment. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H07; source]: Matched MOS edges require suitable gate/dummy neighborhood and overhang; // half/full dummies and inactive terminal ties are not interchangeable. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.2.2, // Figs13.45–13.46; §13.3 dummy recommendations. Physical PDF pp. 692–693, 713–714. // Applies when: Use qualified dummy extent/pitch and safe terminal bias. Added rectangles do not // automatically reproduce the active device environment. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H07","url":"theory-provenance.html#H07"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H08 · Metal fill can perturb passivation and matching","text":"H08 · Metal fill can perturb passivation and matching Source result. Metal above or near matched MOS regions can change hydrogenation/passivation and environmental symmetry. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §13.2.4 Hydrogenation; §13.3 rules17–18. PDF pp. 702–704 · PDF p. 716 . Assumptions and limits: Process-dependent hydrogen transport/material stack; source numerical distances and preferences are not generic modern-node mandates. Fill also has extraction-dependent electrical effects. Handbook workflow implication: final fill must be included when rechecking affected geometry and electrical behavior; that lifecycle policy is our derivation from the physical mechanism. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H08; source]: Metal above or near matched MOS regions can change // hydrogenation/passivation and environmental symmetry. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.2.4 // Hydrogenation; §13.3 rules17–18. Physical PDF pp. 702–704, 716. // Applies when: Process-dependent hydrogen transport/material stack; source numerical distances // and preferences are not generic modern-node mandates. Fill also has extraction-dependent // electrical effects. Handbook workflow implication: final fill must be included when rechecking // affected geometry and electrical behavior; that lifecycle policy is our derivation from the // physical mechanism. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H08","url":"theory-provenance.html#H08"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H09 · Resistor physical geometry includes end and corner terms","text":"H09 · Resistor physical geometry includes end and corner terms Source result. Unequal widths turn common process bias into ratio error; contacts/heads/corners must be matched or modeled. An ideal equal-width right-angle corner is 0.5587 squares. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §6.2 Resistor Layout, Eqs6.5–6.10; §8.2.2 Eq8.18. PDF pp. 279–282 · PDF p. 374 . Assumptions and limits: The corner result assumes the source ideal sheet/current-boundary geometry. Real contact and process-biased corners require calibrated models; identical systematic heads can cancel. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H09; source]: Unequal widths turn common process bias into ratio error; // contacts/heads/corners must be matched or modeled. An ideal equal-width right-angle corner is // 0.5587 squares. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §6.2 // Resistor Layout, Eqs6.5–6.10; §8.2.2 Eq8.18. Physical PDF pp. 279–282, 374. // Applies when: The corner result assumes the source ideal sheet/current-boundary geometry. Real // contact and process-biased corners require calibrated models; identical systematic heads can // cancel. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H09","url":"theory-provenance.html#H09"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H10 · Thermoelectric cancellation is a connection problem","text":"H10 · Thermoelectric cancellation is a connection problem Source result. ET=SΔTC describes the net contact-temperature EMF; series sections can add or cancel it depending on electrical traversal. Common-centroid placement alone cannot cancel it. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §8.2.7, Eq8.34 and Figs8.22–8.23. PDF pp. 401–402 . Assumptions and limits: Matched material Seebeck behavior and temperature drops; opposite path directions must be paired. An even segment count alone does not guarantee cancellation in an arbitrary field. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H10; source]: ET=SΔTC describes the net contact-temperature EMF; series sections can add // or cancel it depending on electrical traversal. Common-centroid placement alone cannot cancel // it. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.7, // Eq8.34 and Figs8.22–8.23. Physical PDF pp. 401–402. // Applies when: Matched material Seebeck behavior and temperature drops; opposite path directions // must be paired. An even segment count alone does not guarantee cancellation in an arbitrary // field. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H10","url":"theory-provenance.html#H10"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H11 · Precision resistor leads must scale by material","text":"H11 · Precision resistor leads must scale by material Source result. Metal, contact and via contributions can change an intended resistor ratio; for ratioed devices each material contribution should preserve the ratio or be budgeted separately. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §8.2.4 Interconnection Parasitics; §8.3.1 rule23. PDF pp. 380–381 · PDF p. 422 . Assumptions and limits: Different lead materials have different temperature coefficients. Equal body R ratios or equal route lengths alone do not establish terminal ratios. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H11; source]: Metal, contact and via contributions can change an intended resistor // ratio; for ratioed devices each material contribution should preserve the ratio or be budgeted // separately. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.4 // Interconnection Parasitics; §8.3.1 rule23. Physical PDF pp. 380–381, 422. // Applies when: Different lead materials have different temperature coefficients. Equal body R // ratios or equal route lengths alone do not establish terminal ratios. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H11","url":"theory-provenance.html#H11"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H12 · Capacitor ratios need perimeter and topology control","text":"H12 · Capacitor ratios need perimeter and topology control Source result. Identical parallel units control ratio errors from area and periphery; series interconnection introduces different electrode-parasitic behavior. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §8.2.2 Process Biases; §8.3.2 rules1–3. PDF pp. 374–375 · PDF p. 423 . Assumptions and limits: Fixed capacitor family and actual plate connections; nonunit geometries must match sensitivity rather than merely nominal C. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H12; source]: Identical parallel units control ratio errors from area and periphery; // series interconnection introduces different electrode-parasitic behavior. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.2 // Process Biases; §8.3.2 rules1–3. Physical PDF pp. 374–375, 423. // Applies when: Fixed capacitor family and actual plate connections; nonunit geometries must match // sensitivity rather than merely nominal C. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H12","url":"theory-provenance.html#H12"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H13 · Capacitor plate roles and surroundings matter","text":"H13 · Capacitor plate roles and surroundings matter Source result. Capacitor electrodes can have asymmetric substrate/interconnect parasitics; edge dummies and shields need intentional quiet connections and balanced surroundings. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §7.1 MOS capacitor construction; §8.2.3 Fig8.5; §8.3.2 rules5–10. PDF pp. 339–340 · PDF p. 378 · PDF pp. 424–425 . Assumptions and limits: Real terminal roles, finite shield impedance, bias and surrounding noise matter. Dummies cannot be assigned arbitrary floating or noisy potentials. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H13; source]: Capacitor electrodes can have asymmetric substrate/interconnect // parasitics; edge dummies and shields need intentional quiet connections and balanced // surroundings. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §7.1 MOS // capacitor construction; §8.2.3 Fig8.5; §8.3.2 rules5–10. Physical PDF pp. 339–340, 378, 424–425. // Applies when: Real terminal roles, finite shield impedance, bias and surrounding noise matter. // Dummies cannot be assigned arbitrary floating or noisy potentials. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H13","url":"theory-provenance.html#H13"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H14 · BJT matching is device-family specific","text":"H14 · BJT matching is device-family specific Source result. Vertical emitters, lateral emitter perimeters, beta/base-current errors, collector bias and thermal gradients demand different matching checks. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §10.2.1–3; §10.2.6; §10.3.1–2. PDF pp. 506–509 · PDF pp. 522–523 · PDF pp. 526–529 . Assumptions and limits: Identify matched observable and operating region; same total area does not make a lateral device equivalent, and reverse-BE stress can degrade accuracy. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H14; source]: Vertical emitters, lateral emitter perimeters, beta/base-current errors, // collector bias and thermal gradients demand different matching checks. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. // §10.2.1–3; §10.2.6; §10.3.1–2. Physical PDF pp. 506–509, 522–523, 526–529. // Applies when: Identify matched observable and operating region; same total area does not make a // lateral device equivalent, and reverse-BE stress can degrade accuracy. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H14","url":"theory-provenance.html#H14"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H15 · Do not merge diode matching models","text":"H15 · Do not merge diode matching models Source result. PN current definition, Schottky field/barrier construction and nonuniform Zener avalanche produce different matching/injection behavior. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §11.2 Schottky field-relief discussion; §11.3.1–3 diode matching. PDF p. 563 · PDF pp. 565–570 . Assumptions and limits: Schottky guard-junction forward bias can inject minority carriers; Zener segments may current-hog. Do not assume ordinary unit-area averaging applies to avalanche. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H15; source]: PN current definition, Schottky field/barrier construction and nonuniform // Zener avalanche produce different matching/injection behavior. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §11.2 // Schottky field-relief discussion; §11.3.1–3 diode matching. Physical PDF pp. 563, 565–570. // Applies when: Schottky guard-junction forward bias can inject minority carriers; Zener segments // may current-hog. Do not assume ordinary unit-area averaging applies to avalanche. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H15","url":"theory-provenance.html#H15"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H16 · Antenna checking follows fabrication stages","text":"H16 · Antenna checking follows fabrication stages Source result. Plasma damage depends on the conductive geometry connected to a gate during each process stage; later-layer jumpers and protective diodes address different exposed-node situations. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §5.1.6 The Antenna Effect. PDF pp. 228–230 . Assumptions and limits: Foundry defines gate denominator, exposed area/perimeter, layer accumulation and diode credit. Final net connectivity is insufficient; stage-graph implementation is handbook synthesis. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H16; source]: Plasma damage depends on the conductive geometry connected to a gate // during each process stage; later-layer jumpers and protective diodes address different // exposed-node situations. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §5.1.6 // The Antenna Effect. Physical PDF pp. 228–230. // Applies when: Foundry defines gate denominator, exposed area/perimeter, layer accumulation and // diode credit. Final net connectivity is insufficient; stage-graph implementation is handbook // synthesis. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H16","url":"theory-provenance.html#H16"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H17 · Guard rings require carrier, depth and return-path context","text":"H17 · Guard rings require carrier, depth and return-path context Source result. Collecting PN junctions, high-low blocking regions and majority taps have different functions; minority carriers can bypass shallow guards or debias weak returns. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §5.4.4 Guard Rings; §14.2.1–5 process examples. PDF pp. 264–270 · PDF pp. 736–738 · PDF p. 741 . Assumptions and limits: Qualified geometry/bias/substrate stack required. Blocking guards need appropriate complete 3D barriers; special collecting L/U guards near die edges are not generalized to blockers. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H17; source]: Collecting PN junctions, high-low blocking regions and majority taps have // different functions; minority carriers can bypass shallow guards or debias weak returns. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §5.4.4 // Guard Rings; §14.2.1–5 process examples. Physical PDF pp. 264–270, 736–738, 741. // Applies when: Qualified geometry/bias/substrate stack required. Blocking guards need appropriate // complete 3D barriers; special collecting L/U guards near die edges are not generalized to // blockers. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H17","url":"theory-provenance.html#H17"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H18 · ESD protection must coordinate actual terminal voltages","text":"H18 · ESD protection must coordinate actual terminal voltages Source result. Clipping, snapback and rate-triggered protectors require different operating constraints; secondary protection must use the vulnerable device local reference and qualified limiting paths. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §14.4.1 Primary Protection; §14.4.2 Secondary Protection, Fig14.36. PDF pp. 750–752 · PDF pp. 765–769 . Assumptions and limits: Normal supply/slew/failsafe conditions, stress polarity and tested cell topology matter. Multifinger width does not imply uniform current; local ballast may be intentional. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H18; source]: Clipping, snapback and rate-triggered protectors require different // operating constraints; secondary protection must use the vulnerable device local reference and // qualified limiting paths. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §14.4.1 // Primary Protection; §14.4.2 Secondary Protection, Fig14.36. Physical PDF pp. 750–752, 765–769. // Applies when: Normal supply/slew/failsafe conditions, stress polarity and tested cell topology // matter. Multifinger width does not imply uniform current; local ballast may be intentional. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H18","url":"theory-provenance.html#H18"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H19 · Local current density sets reliability bottlenecks","text":"H19 · Local current density sets reliability bottlenecks Source result. Via arrays and bent leads need current-distribution analysis: inner-corner vias can carry far more current than outer ones. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §5.1.3 Electromigration; §15.4.4, Eqs15.24–15.25 and Fig15.29. PDF pp. 217–220 · PDF pp. 821–822 . Assumptions and limits: Foundry lifetime model, material/interface, local temperature and waveform determine limits. Total via count and average wire width are insufficient. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H19; source]: Via arrays and bent leads need current-distribution analysis: inner-corner // vias can carry far more current than outer ones. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §5.1.3 // Electromigration; §15.4.4, Eqs15.24–15.25 and Fig15.29. Physical PDF pp. 217–220, 821–822. // Applies when: Foundry lifetime model, material/interface, local temperature and waveform // determine limits. Total via count and average wire width are insufficient. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H19","url":"theory-provenance.html#H19"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H20 · Physical Kelvin junctions and victim impedance matter","text":"H20 · Physical Kelvin junctions and victim impedance matter Source result. Shared-path voltage drops persist on one named net; capacitive disturbance depends on aggressor slew and victim impedance, while shields need an effective reference. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §15.4.4 Special Routing Techniques, Figs15.26–15.28 and Eq15.23. PDF pp. 818–820 . Assumptions and limits: Use actual branch topology and local references. Eq15.23 is a simplified lumped relation; frequency-dependent impedances and return paths may require extraction. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H20; source]: Shared-path voltage drops persist on one named net; capacitive disturbance // depends on aggressor slew and victim impedance, while shields need an effective reference. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §15.4.4 // Special Routing Techniques, Figs15.26–15.28 and Eq15.23. Physical PDF pp. 818–820. // Applies when: Use actual branch topology and local references. Eq15.23 is a simplified lumped // relation; frequency-dependent impedances and return paths may require extraction. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H20","url":"theory-provenance.html#H20"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H21 · Manufacturable pitch includes enclosure and registration","text":"H21 · Manufacturable pitch includes enclosure and registration Source result. Design-rule margins account for fabrication deviations; routing pitch must allow legal via/contact landing geometry, not only drawn line width plus nominal line spacing. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §3.2.3 Design Rule Construction; §15.4.2 Eqs15.18–15.20; AppendixC disclaimer. PDF pp. 138–144 · PDF pp. 812–814 · PDF p. 836 . Assumptions and limits: Numeric textbook rules are teaching examples. Use actual PDK shape/grid/stacking rules and qualified waivers; geometry needs to be checked after generation. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H21; source]: Design-rule margins account for fabrication deviations; routing pitch must // allow legal via/contact landing geometry, not only drawn line width plus nominal line spacing. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §3.2.3 // Design Rule Construction; §15.4.2 Eqs15.18–15.20; AppendixC disclaimer. Physical PDF pp. // 138–144, 812–814, 836. // Applies when: Numeric textbook rules are teaching examples. Use actual PDK shape/grid/stacking // rules and qualified waivers; geometry needs to be checked after generation. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H21","url":"theory-provenance.html#H21"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H22 · Exact distributed-finger model before the one-third limit","text":"H22 · Exact distributed-finger model before the one-third limit Derived result. For constant r,g and a singly terminated finger of length L, Rtotal=RSi·u·coth(u), u=L√(rg); small u gives added metal R≈rL/3. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §13.1.1 Eq13.11; AppendixE EqsE.1–E.4, E.7, E.9–E.13. PDF p. 650 · PDF pp. 871–872 . Assumptions and limits: Uniform geometry and distributed silicon conductance; v(0)=V, i(L)=0. Not a generic bound for tapered/strongly nonuniform devices. Supplied E.6 sign, E.8 prefactor and E.14 sqrt appear inconsistent. Derivation: Solve v′=−ri and i′=−gv with open far-end boundary: i(0)=√(g/r)V tanh(L√(rg)); invert and use g=1/(LRSi). Expand ucothu=1+u²/3−u⁴/45+…. This agrees with source E.9; correction is audit algebra, not publisher-confirmed erratum. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H22; derived]: For constant r,g and a singly terminated finger of length L, // Rtotal=RSi·u·coth(u), u=L√(rg); small u gives added metal R≈rL/3. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 // catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.1.1 Eq13.11; AppendixE EqsE.1–E.4, E.7, E.9–E.13. // Physical PDF pp. 650, 871–872. // Applies when: Uniform geometry and distributed silicon conductance; v(0)=V, i(L)=0. Not a // generic bound for tapered/strongly nonuniform devices. Supplied E.6 sign, E.8 prefactor and E.14 // sqrt appear inconsistent. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H22","url":"theory-provenance.html#H22"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H23 · RF passive surroundings are part of the device","text":"H23 · RF passive surroundings are part of the device Source result. Skin/proximity loss, eddy currents, capacitance and surrounding metal determine frequency-dependent inductor quality; patterned shields must interrupt induced loops. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §7.2.1 Inductor Parasitics and §7.2.2 Inductor Construction. PDF pp. 358–363 . Assumptions and limits: Use frequency-dependent port/EM models and qualified fill exclusions. Parallel metal stacking differs from series winding layers; more turns/width is not universally better. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H23; source]: Skin/proximity loss, eddy currents, capacitance and surrounding metal // determine frequency-dependent inductor quality; patterned shields must interrupt induced loops. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §7.2.1 // Inductor Parasitics and §7.2.2 Inductor Construction. Physical PDF pp. 358–363. // Applies when: Use frequency-dependent port/EM models and qualified fill exclusions. Parallel // metal stacking differs from series winding layers; more turns/width is not universally better. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H23","url":"theory-provenance.html#H23"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H24 · Power MOS layout needs dynamic and thermal checks","text":"H24 · Power MOS layout needs dynamic and thermal checks Source result. Distributed gate resistance/capacitance causes nonuniform switching; body resistance and electrothermal feedback can localize current even when on-resistance has a positive temperature coefficient in another regime. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §13.1.2 Switching Losses, Eqs13.21–13.23/Figs13.8–13.10; §13.1.3 SOA; §13.1.4 SenseFET discussion. PDF pp. 652–655 · PDF pp. 659–660 · PDF pp. 667–668 . Assumptions and limits: Waveform, bias, package heat path and process determine SOA. SenseFET layout must sample representative terminal voltages/temperature; repeated small-signal units alone do not establish power capability. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H24; source]: Distributed gate resistance/capacitance causes nonuniform switching; body // resistance and electrothermal feedback can localize current even when on-resistance has a // positive temperature coefficient in another regime. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.1.2 // Switching Losses, Eqs13.21–13.23/Figs13.8–13.10; §13.1.3 SOA; §13.1.4 SenseFET discussion. // Physical PDF pp. 652–655, 659–660, 667–668. // Applies when: Waveform, bias, package heat path and process determine SOA. SenseFET layout must // sample representative terminal voltages/temperature; repeated small-signal units alone do not // establish power capability. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H24","url":"theory-provenance.html#H24"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · H25 · Thermal-gradient theory needs boundary conditions","text":"H25 · Thermal-gradient theory needs boundary conditions Source result. Heat flux follows Fourier’s law q=−κ∇T; package-dependent 3D heat flow and active-region weighting determine how temperature gradients affect matching. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §5.1.1 Self-Heating, Eq5.6 finite source and Eqs5.7–5.9; §8.2.7 Temperature Eq8.22 and Eq8.23 context. PDF pp. 213–214 · PDF pp. 386–388 . Assumptions and limits: Active-centroid model assumes local/planar contribution, suitable linear temperature response and a linear field. P/(2πκr) is not found verbatim in this book; a half-space point-source use must be labeled an independent Fourier-law derivation with its own boundaries. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [H25; source]: Heat flux follows Fourier’s law q=−κ∇T; package-dependent 3D heat flow and // active-region weighting determine how temperature gradients affect matching. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §5.1.1 // Self-Heating, Eq5.6 finite source and Eqs5.7–5.9; §8.2.7 Temperature Eq8.22 and Eq8.23 context. // Physical PDF pp. 213–214, 386–388. // Applies when: Active-centroid model assumes local/planar contribution, suitable linear // temperature response and a linear field. P/(2πκr) is not found verbatim in this book; a // half-space point-source use must be labeled an independent Fourier-law derivation with its own // boundaries. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H25","url":"theory-provenance.html#H25"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Optimization, budgets, representations and yield","text":"Optimization, budgets, representations and yield O01 · Additive performance allowance Source result. If pre-layout process values lie in [Pmin_proc,Pmax_proc] and every value receives the same shift dP, specification [L,U] requires L-Pmin_proc <= dP <= U-Pmax_proc. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §2.2, Eqs. (2.1)–(2.7), printed pp.21–24. PDF pp. 34–37 . Assumptions and limits: A common additive shift; use scenario-specific response otherwise. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O01; source]: If pre-layout process values lie in [Pmin_proc,Pmax_proc] and every value // receives the same shift dP, specification [L,U] requires L-Pmin_proc <= dP <= U-Pmax_proc. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §2.2, Eqs. (2.1)–(2.7), printed pp.21–24. Physical PDF pp. // 34–37. // Applies when: A common additive shift; use scenario-specific response otherwise. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O01 O02 · Coupled parasitic feasible region Source result. Independent allocations of per-parasitic limits can exclude electrically acceptable combinations; direct performance constraints preserve those tradeoffs. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §2.3.3 and §2.4; Fig.2.4 and direct-performance discussion. PDF pp. 40–42 . Assumptions and limits: The response model and specifications define feasibility; a linearized region is only local. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O02; source]: Independent allocations of per-parasitic limits can exclude electrically // acceptable combinations; direct performance constraints preserve those tradeoffs. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §2.3.3 and §2.4; Fig.2.4 and direct-performance discussion. // Physical PDF pp. 40–42. // Applies when: The response model and specifications define feasibility; a linearized region is // only local. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O02 O03 · Signed local sensitivities and adjoints Source result. dPj is approximated by sum_i Sji dXi. An adjoint computes derivatives with respect to many parameters for one scalar response. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §2.4.1 Eqs. (2.12)–(2.13); §2.4.2.3 Eqs. (2.18)–(2.24). PDF pp. 42–44 . Assumptions and limits: Differentiable local response; suitable simulator convergence; independent responses generally need independent adjoints. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O03; source]: dPj is approximated by sum_i Sji dXi. An adjoint computes derivatives with // respect to many parameters for one scalar response. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §2.4.1 Eqs. (2.12)–(2.13); §2.4.2.3 Eqs. (2.18)–(2.24). // Physical PDF pp. 42–44. // Applies when: Differentiable local response; suitable simulator convergence; independent // responses generally need independent adjoints. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O03 O04 · Resistance and geometric capacitance models Source result. A uniform rectangular segment has R=Rsheet L/W; overlap, lateral and fringing capacitance have distinct geometry-dependent contributions. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §2.5.3.1 Eqs. (2.29)–(2.32); §2.5.3.2 Eq. (2.33), printed pp.36–38. PDF pp. 49–51 . Assumptions and limits: Calibrated material/layer/environment; L and W use the same units; contacts/vias and nonuniform current need additional modeling. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O04; source]: A uniform rectangular segment has R=Rsheet L/W; overlap, lateral and // fringing capacitance have distinct geometry-dependent contributions. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §2.5.3.1 Eqs. (2.29)–(2.32); §2.5.3.2 Eq. (2.33), printed // pp.36–38. Physical PDF pp. 49–51. // Applies when: Calibrated material/layer/environment; L and W use the same units; contacts/vias // and nonuniform current need additional modeling. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O04 O05 · Area and distance mismatch terms Source result. The cited pair model is variance=Aq^2/(WL)+Sq^2 D^2, with structure/orientation/environment constraints used alongside it. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §2.7.1 Eq. (2.35); §2.7.2 matching guidelines. PDF pp. 53–55 . Assumptions and limits: Process-calibrated pair model and its geometry domain; coefficients are not universal foundry constants; common centroid does not remove the finite-area random term. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O05; source]: The cited pair model is variance=Aq^2/(WL)+Sq^2 D^2, with // structure/orientation/environment constraints used alongside it. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §2.7.1 Eq. (2.35); §2.7.2 matching guidelines. Physical PDF // pp. 53–55. // Applies when: Process-calibrated pair model and its geometry domain; coefficients are not // universal foundry constants; common centroid does not remove the finite-area random term. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O05 O06 · Separate symmetry move groups Source result. Position, orientation and shape relationships are distinct; coordinated translations, swaps, flips and axis movement can preserve chosen relationships by construction. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §§4.6–4.7: constraint groups and translation, swap, flip and symmetry-axis moves. PDF pp. 99–105 . Assumptions and limits: Compatible group membership, fixed-object conditions and legal variants; preserving one relationship does not prove neighbor clearance or electrical matching. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O06; source]: Position, orientation and shape relationships are distinct; coordinated // translations, swaps, flips and axis movement can preserve chosen relationships by construction. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §§4.6–4.7: constraint groups and translation, swap, flip and // symmetry-axis moves. Physical PDF pp. 99–105. // Applies when: Compatible group membership, fixed-object conditions and legal variants; // preserving one relationship does not prove neighbor clearance or electrical matching. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O06 O07 · Annealing temperature calibration Source result. For fixed energy and symmetric proposals, uphill acceptance is exp(-dE/T); T0≈-mean(positive dE)/ln(p0) is an initial calibration heuristic. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §4.4.6 Metropolis criterion, PDF89–90; §4.11 Eqs. (4.37)–(4.38), PDF117–118. PDF pp. 89–90 · PDF pp. 117–118 . Assumptions and limits: T>0, 0<p0<1, consistent energy units, finite search; exp of mean does not equal mean acceptance. No finite-run global optimum guarantee. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O07; source]: For fixed energy and symmetric proposals, uphill acceptance is exp(-dE/T); // T0≈-mean(positive dE)/ln(p0) is an initial calibration heuristic. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §4.4.6 Metropolis criterion, PDF89–90; §4.11 Eqs. // (4.37)–(4.38), PDF117–118. Physical PDF pp. 89–90, 117–118. // Applies when: T>0, 0<p0<1, consistent energy units, finite search; exp of mean does not equal // mean acceptance. No finite-run global optimum guarantee. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O07 O08 · Segment current and via capability Source result. Required wire width and via count depend on current carried by each route segment and on process current capability. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §5.6.2 Eqs. (5.1)–(5.2); §5.7.1 Eq. (5.9). PDF pp. 143–147 . Assumptions and limits: Current scenario and branch topology must be known; waveform, temperature and current sharing require the relevant reliability model. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O08; source]: Required wire width and via count depend on current carried by each route // segment and on process current capability. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §5.6.2 Eqs. (5.1)–(5.2); §5.7.1 Eq. (5.9). Physical PDF pp. // 143–147. // Applies when: Current scenario and branch topology must be known; waveform, temperature and // current sharing require the relevant reliability model. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O08 O09 · Defect critical area is a separate yield model Source result. For long parallel wires with length L and spacing s, bridging critical area is L max(0,chi-s); expected faults integrate critical area against defect density, with Poisson no-fault probability exp(-lambda). Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §5.9.1 Eqs. (5.16)–(5.18), Eq. (5.24), Fig.5.15; printed pp.141–143. PDF pp. 153–155 . Assumptions and limits: chi is defect diameter; simplified long-wire geometry omits detailed end effects; density and Poisson assumptions must be calibrated; not parametric mismatch yield. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O09; source]: For long parallel wires with length L and spacing s, bridging critical // area is L max(0,chi-s); expected faults integrate critical area against defect density, with // Poisson no-fault probability exp(-lambda). // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §5.9.1 Eqs. (5.16)–(5.18), Eq. (5.24), Fig.5.15; printed // pp.141–143. Physical PDF pp. 153–155. // Applies when: chi is defect diameter; simplified long-wire geometry omits detailed end effects; // density and Poisson assumptions must be calibrated; not parametric mismatch yield. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O09 O10 · Sequence-pair symmetry is a sufficient subset Source result. For one common vertical symmetry axis, pos_alpha(x)<pos_alpha(y) iff pos_beta(sym(y))<pos_beta(sym(x)) is a sufficient symmetric-feasible sequence-pair condition, not a necessary condition for physical symmetry. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches . Balasa, Chapter1 §1.3, Eq. (1.1); sufficient-not-necessary discussion PDF50. PDF pp. 48–51 . Assumptions and limits: Source sequence convention, distinct group members, reflected partner mapping; multiple groups need compatible embedding (§1.3.2 PDF60–61). Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O10; source]: For one common vertical symmetry axis, pos_alpha(x)<pos_alpha(y) iff // pos_beta(sym(y))<pos_beta(sym(x)) is a sufficient symmetric-feasible sequence-pair condition, // not a necessary condition for physical symmetry. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological // Approaches. Balasa, Chapter1 §1.3, Eq. (1.1); sufficient-not-necessary discussion PDF50. // Physical PDF pp. 48–51. // Applies when: Source sequence convention, distinct group members, reflected partner mapping; // multiple groups need compatible embedding (§1.3.2 PDF60–61). // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O10 O11 · Connected symmetry islands restrict the search Source result. A symmetry island is an abutting connected group. ASF-B-star stores representative half-groups and reconstructs counterparts; self-symmetric representatives touch the axis. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches . Lin and Chang, §§2.2.2–2.3.1, Definition2.1, Lemma2.1 and Property2.1. PDF pp. 79–85 . Assumptions and limits: Legal module envelopes and chapter-specific B-star convention; connectivity/abutment is stronger than geometric symmetry. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O11; source]: A symmetry island is an abutting connected group. ASF-B-star stores // representative half-groups and reconstructs counterparts; self-symmetric representatives touch // the axis. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological // Approaches. Lin and Chang, §§2.2.2–2.3.1, Definition2.1, Lemma2.1 and Property2.1. Physical PDF // pp. 79–85. // Applies when: Legal module envelopes and chapter-specific B-star convention; // connectivity/abutment is stronger than geometric symmetry. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O11 O12 · DTI legality is disjunctive and coupled Source result. The DTI example permits a shared range or a sufficiently isolated range, excludes intermediate gaps, and limits a middle device total left-plus-right stretch. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches . Strasser et al., §3.1.2 Fig.3.3; §3.3.2 Eqs. (3.36)–(3.40). PDF p. 115 · PDF pp. 135–137 . Assumptions and limits: Technology-specific DTI semantics and finite geometry bounds; not every isolation rule has these branches. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O12; source]: The DTI example permits a shared range or a sufficiently isolated range, // excludes intermediate gaps, and limits a middle device total left-plus-right stretch. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological // Approaches. Strasser et al., §3.1.2 Fig.3.3; §3.3.2 Eqs. (3.36)–(3.40). Physical PDF pp. 115, // 135–137. // Applies when: Technology-specific DTI semantics and finite geometry bounds; not every isolation // rule has these branches. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O12 O13 · Shape functions preserve alternatives within a decomposition Source result. Ordinary horizontal composition gives (w1+w2,max(h1,h2)); enhanced shapes retain B-star topology and can interlock contours. Hierarchical enumeration does not enumerate all circuit layouts. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches . Strasser et al., §§3.4–3.5, Figs.3.23–3.33, Algorithm3.4. PDF pp. 137–146 . Assumptions and limits: Fixed hierarchy and variants; composition constraint-preservation assumes no incompatible added parent constraints; geometric dominance is not automatically electrical dominance. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O13; source]: Ordinary horizontal composition gives (w1+w2,max(h1,h2)); enhanced shapes // retain B-star topology and can interlock contours. Hierarchical enumeration does not enumerate // all circuit layouts. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological // Approaches. Strasser et al., §§3.4–3.5, Figs.3.23–3.33, Algorithm3.4. Physical PDF pp. 137–146. // Applies when: Fixed hierarchy and variants; composition constraint-preservation assumes no // incompatible added parent constraints; geometric dominance is not automatically electrical // dominance. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O13 O14 · Difference constraints versus general linear relations Source result. An acyclic fixed-order graph solves xj-xi>=dij by longest paths; symmetry relations involving sums/differences of more variables require a richer linear representation. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches . Said et al., §5.4.1 Eqs. (5.1)–(5.2); §5.4.2 Eq. (5.6); §5.5.1 Definition5.2. PDF pp. 219–229 . Assumptions and limits: One-dimensional fixed ordering with appropriate graph feasibility; LP optimality concerns the stated model, not all topology choices or nonlinear electrical effects. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O14; source]: An acyclic fixed-order graph solves xj-xi>=dij by longest paths; symmetry // relations involving sums/differences of more variables require a richer linear representation. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological // Approaches. Said et al., §5.4.1 Eqs. (5.1)–(5.2); §5.4.2 Eq. (5.6); §5.5.1 Definition5.2. // Physical PDF pp. 219–229. // Applies when: One-dimensional fixed ordering with appropriate graph feasibility; LP optimality // concerns the stated model, not all topology choices or nonlinear electrical effects. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O14 O15 · Layout-aware sizing and surrogate domain Source result. Each sizing can select geometric parameters, instantiate or model layout parasitics, and evaluate the resulting circuit. A naive table with m samples in n dimensions has m^n entries. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches . Castro-López et al., §6.3.3 flow; §6.4.2 shape propagation; §6.5.1 extraction approaches A/B; Table6.7. PDF p. 263 · PDF pp. 267–268 · PDF pp. 272–275 . Assumptions and limits: Fixed characterized template/technology/model domain; sensitivity screening reduces sampling but cannot certify omitted parasitics in all future contexts. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O15; source]: Each sizing can select geometric parameters, instantiate or model layout // parasitics, and evaluate the resulting circuit. A naive table with m samples in n dimensions has // m^n entries. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological // Approaches. Castro-López et al., §6.3.3 flow; §6.4.2 shape propagation; §6.5.1 extraction // approaches A/B; Table6.7. Physical PDF pp. 263, 267–268, 272–275. // Applies when: Fixed characterized template/technology/model domain; sensitivity screening // reduces sampling but cannot certify omitted parasitics in all future contexts. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O15 O16 · Constraint lifecycle and meta-verification Source result. Management, derivation, transformation, sensitivity analysis and verification are distinct services; static final verification uses fixed design and constraint data. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches . Jerke, Lienig and Freuer, §7.4, Fig.7.4; §§7.4.1–7.4.5. PDF pp. 285–293 . Assumptions and limits: Applicable rules and model coverage must be defined; DRC/LVS alone do not express all analog intent; transformations can restrict the remaining design space. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O16; source]: Management, derivation, transformation, sensitivity analysis and // verification are distinct services; static final verification uses fixed design and constraint // data. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological // Approaches. Jerke, Lienig and Freuer, §7.4, Fig.7.4; §§7.4.1–7.4.5. Physical PDF pp. 285–293. // Applies when: Applicable rules and model coverage must be defined; DRC/LVS alone do not express // all analog intent; transformations can restrict the remaining design space. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O16 O17 · Yield over all operating conditions Source result. Yield is probability over statistical fabrication variables that every specified performance bound holds for every operating-range condition. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §§3.1–3.5 parameter classes; §4.8.1 Eq. (114), §§4.8.2–4.8.4. PDF pp. 46–50 · PDF pp. 89–95 . Assumptions and limits: Declared joint statistical distribution and complete range contract; a sampled operating point or process-corner list alone is a different analysis. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O17; source]: Yield is probability over statistical fabrication variables that every // specified performance bound holds for every operating-range condition. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing. §§3.1–3.5 parameter classes; // §4.8.1 Eq. (114), §§4.8.2–4.8.4. Physical PDF pp. 46–50, 89–95. // Applies when: Declared joint statistical distribution and complete range contract; a sampled // operating point or process-corner list alone is a different analysis. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O17 O18 · Covariance propagation and valid dependence Source result. For local response f≈f0+S(x-mu), Cov(f)≈S C S-transpose; a scalar sensitivity column g gives variance g-transpose C g. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §5.2 Eq. (175); AppendixA.9 Eqs. (A.13) and (A.15). PDF p. 111 · PDF p. 183 . Assumptions and limits: Finite second moments; exact for affine response, approximate for nonlinear response. C must be PSD, inverse whitening requires positive definiteness or a reduced-rank subspace. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O18; source]: For local response f≈f0+S(x-mu), Cov(f)≈S C S-transpose; a scalar // sensitivity column g gives variance g-transpose C g. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing. §5.2 Eq. (175); AppendixA.9 // Eqs. (A.13) and (A.15). Physical PDF pp. 111, 183. // Applies when: Finite second moments; exact for affine response, approximate for nonlinear // response. C must be PSD, inverse whitening requires positive definiteness or a reduced-rank // subspace. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O18 O19 · Linear ellipsoidal worst case Source result. For delta-transpose C-inverse delta<=beta^2 and f=f0+g-transpose delta, extrema occur at delta=±beta Cg/sqrt(g-transpose Cg) and f=f0±beta sqrt(g-transpose Cg). Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §5.2.1–5.2.2, Eqs. (171)–(179), printed p.93. PDF p. 111 . Assumptions and limits: Positive-definite C, beta>=0 and g-transpose Cg>0; zero gradient/variance requires separate treatment; nonlinear circuits need validated approximation. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O19; source]: For delta-transpose C-inverse delta<=beta^2 and f=f0+g-transpose delta, // extrema occur at delta=±beta Cg/sqrt(g-transpose Cg) and f=f0±beta sqrt(g-transpose Cg). // Source: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing. §5.2.1–5.2.2, Eqs. // (171)–(179), printed p.93. Physical PDF pp. 111. // Applies when: Positive-definite C, beta>=0 and g-transpose Cg>0; zero gradient/variance requires // separate treatment; nonlinear circuits need validated approximation. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O19 O20 · A sigma distance needs its probability region Source result. A standardized n-dimensional Gaussian radius-beta ball encloses P(chi-square_n<=beta^2); a symmetric independent box encloses [2Phi(beta)-1]^n; one linear upper bound has probability Phi(beta). Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §§6.2.2–6.2.4, Eq. (240), Eqs. (242)–(248), Eqs. (258)–(259), Tables11–12. PDF pp. 133–143 . Assumptions and limits: Independent standardized coordinates for box product; positive signed margin for usual passing example; joint circuit yield is an intersection, not automatically any of these regions. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O20; source]: A standardized n-dimensional Gaussian radius-beta ball encloses // P(chi-square_n<=beta^2); a symmetric independent box encloses [2Phi(beta)-1]^n; one linear upper // bound has probability Phi(beta). // Source: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing. §§6.2.2–6.2.4, Eq. (240), // Eqs. (242)–(248), Eqs. (258)–(259), Tables11–12. Physical PDF pp. 133–143. // Applies when: Independent standardized coordinates for box product; positive signed margin for // usual passing example; joint circuit yield is an intersection, not automatically any of these // regions. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O20 O21 · No-failure confidence is not perfect yield Derived result. With N independent Bernoulli trials and no failures, a one-sided 95% upper failure-probability bound is 1-0.05^(1/N); ordinary Monte Carlo yield variance is Y(1-Y)/N. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §6.1.1 Eq. (219), §6.1.3 Eqs. (225)–(226); confidence limit is derived here. PDF pp. 125–132 . Assumptions and limits: Independent, identically distributed complete pass/fail events; not the variance formula for weighted importance samples or unresolved simulation outcomes. Derivation: P(no failures|p)=(1-p)^N; solve (1-p)^N=.05. N=3000 gives 0.00099808; a 1 ppm upper bound requires at least 2,995,731 clean independent trials. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O21; derived]: With N independent Bernoulli trials and no failures, a one-sided 95% // upper failure-probability bound is 1-0.05^(1/N); ordinary Monte Carlo yield variance is // Y(1-Y)/N. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design // Centering and Sizing. §6.1.1 Eq. (219), §6.1.3 Eqs. (225)–(226); confidence limit is derived // here. Physical PDF pp. 125–132. // Applies when: Independent, identically distributed complete pass/fail events; not the variance // formula for weighted importance samples or unresolved simulation outcomes. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O21 O22 · Mean-shift centering has a limited parameter interpretation Source result. For Gaussian process covariance fixed and acceptance region fixed, gradient_mu Y=Y C-inverse(mu_accepted-mu). A stationary positive-yield point matches the accepted and original means. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §7.1.2, Eq. (324), printed pp.145–146. PDF pp. 163–164 . Assumptions and limits: Unconstrained mean variation, fixed covariance and fixed region; necessary stationarity only. Width/topology changes normally alter the region and cannot be substituted without chain rules. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O22; source]: For Gaussian process covariance fixed and acceptance region fixed, // gradient_mu Y=Y C-inverse(mu_accepted-mu). A stationary positive-yield point matches the // accepted and original means. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing. §7.1.2, Eq. (324), printed // pp.145–146. Physical PDF pp. 163–164. // Applies when: Unconstrained mean variation, fixed covariance and fixed region; necessary // stationarity only. Width/topology changes normally alter the region and cannot be substituted // without chain rules. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O22 O23 · Correct regularized least-squares transpose Derived result. With residual e in R^m and row-gradient Jacobian J in R^(m by n), the regularized linear least-squares step satisfies (J-transpose J+lambda I)r=-J-transpose e. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §7.2.3 Eq. (362) and printed Eqs. (365),(368); printed pp.158–159. PDF pp. 175–176 . Assumptions and limits: Local residual linearization, lambda>=0 and suitable solve/rank treatment; source RHS omits transpose, verified in actual raster. Not a global circuit-optimum certificate. Derivation: Differentiate one-half ||e+Jr||^2 + lambda/2 ||r||^2. Gradient J-transpose(e+Jr)+lambda r=0. RHS must have n components. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O23; derived]: With residual e in R^m and row-gradient Jacobian J in R^(m by n), the // regularized linear least-squares step satisfies (J-transpose J+lambda I)r=-J-transpose e. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design // Centering and Sizing. §7.2.3 Eq. (362) and printed Eqs. (365),(368); printed pp.158–159. // Physical PDF pp. 175–176. // Applies when: Local residual linearization, lambda>=0 and suitable solve/rank treatment; source // RHS omits transpose, verified in actual raster. Not a global circuit-optimum certificate. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O23 O24 · Full nonlinear Lagrangian curvature Derived result. For L=f-lambda(beta^2-delta-transpose C-inverse delta), Hessian_delta L=Hessian f+2lambda C-inverse; covariance curvature alone does not establish nonlinear second-order sufficiency. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §5.4 Eq. (189), prose following Eq. (191); AppendixC.6 Eq. (C.24). PDF p. 115 · PDF p. 195 . Assumptions and limits: Twice differentiable f, constant positive-definite C and fixed multiplier for Hessian; constrained sufficiency concerns appropriate feasible/critical directions. The prose omission was verified in raster. Derivation: Differentiate the displayed Lagrangian twice with respect to delta. The objective-curvature term vanishes for affine f, or at a point where its Hessian happens to be zero; it cannot generally be omitted. Also, lambda>=0 alone is not strict positive curvature. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O24; derived]: For L=f-lambda(beta^2-delta-transpose C-inverse delta), Hessian_delta // L=Hessian f+2lambda C-inverse; covariance curvature alone does not establish nonlinear // second-order sufficiency. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design // Centering and Sizing. §5.4 Eq. (189), prose following Eq. (191); AppendixC.6 Eq. (C.24). // Physical PDF pp. 115, 195. // Applies when: Twice differentiable f, constant positive-definite C and fixed multiplier for // Hessian; constrained sufficiency concerns appropriate feasible/critical directions. The prose // omission was verified in raster. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O24 O25 · Electrical centroid uses normalized comparison weights Derived result. For the difference of weighted responses to a shared affine spatial field, equal zeroth and first moments cancel that field. Ratio comparisons first normalize each group by its intended total; equal raw device counts are not necessary. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §2.7.2 common-centroid motivation; contrasting geometric area-weighted group center in Topological §3.1.1 Eq. (3.12), PDF112. PDF pp. 53–55 . Assumptions and limits: Same local field and proportional unit response; comparison weights encode the intended ratio. Second-order fields, unequal environments and routing bias are not canceled by first moments alone. Derivation: For q(r)=q0+g dot r, weighted difference=sum_i a_i q(r_i)-sum_j b_j q(r_j)=q0(sum a-sum b)+g dot(sum a r-sum b r). Both coefficients must vanish; normalize group weights for mean/ratio comparison. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O25; derived]: For the difference of weighted responses to a shared affine spatial // field, equal zeroth and first moments cancel that field. Ratio comparisons first normalize each // group by its intended total; equal raw device counts are not necessary. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and // Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability. §2.7.2 // common-centroid motivation; contrasting geometric area-weighted group center in Topological // §3.1.1 Eq. (3.12), PDF112. Physical PDF pp. 53–55. // Applies when: Same local field and proportional unit response; comparison weights encode the // intended ratio. Second-order fields, unequal environments and routing bias are not canceled by // first moments alone. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O25 O26 · Fault detectability differs from fault incidence Source result. Layouts with equal expected defect faults can differ in detectability; the source weights routing exposure using good/faulty response separation. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §5.9.2, Fig.5.18 and Eqs.(5.32)–(5.34), printed pp.145–147. PDF pp. 157–159 . Assumptions and limits: A specified fault model, stimulus and response distribution are required. Inverse statistical distance is a cost proxy, not a calibrated escape probability; accepting a hypothesis does not establish posterior probability that an individual circuit is good. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O26; source]: Layouts with equal expected defect faults can differ in detectability; the // source weights routing exposure using good/faulty response separation. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §5.9.2, Fig.5.18 and Eqs.(5.32)–(5.34), printed pp.145–147. // Physical PDF pp. 157–159. // Applies when: A specified fault model, stimulus and response distribution are required. Inverse // statistical distance is a cost proxy, not a calibrated escape probability; accepting a // hypothesis does not establish posterior probability that an individual circuit is good. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O26","url":"theory-provenance.html#optimization-records"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O01 · Additive performance allowance","text":"O01 · Additive performance allowance Source result. If pre-layout process values lie in [Pmin_proc,Pmax_proc] and every value receives the same shift dP, specification [L,U] requires L-Pmin_proc <= dP <= U-Pmax_proc. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §2.2, Eqs. (2.1)–(2.7), printed pp.21–24. PDF pp. 34–37 . Assumptions and limits: A common additive shift; use scenario-specific response otherwise. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O01; source]: If pre-layout process values lie in [Pmin_proc,Pmax_proc] and every value // receives the same shift dP, specification [L,U] requires L-Pmin_proc <= dP <= U-Pmax_proc. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §2.2, Eqs. (2.1)–(2.7), printed pp.21–24. Physical PDF pp. // 34–37. // Applies when: A common additive shift; use scenario-specific response otherwise. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O01","url":"theory-provenance.html#O01"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O02 · Coupled parasitic feasible region","text":"O02 · Coupled parasitic feasible region Source result. Independent allocations of per-parasitic limits can exclude electrically acceptable combinations; direct performance constraints preserve those tradeoffs. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §2.3.3 and §2.4; Fig.2.4 and direct-performance discussion. PDF pp. 40–42 . Assumptions and limits: The response model and specifications define feasibility; a linearized region is only local. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O02; source]: Independent allocations of per-parasitic limits can exclude electrically // acceptable combinations; direct performance constraints preserve those tradeoffs. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §2.3.3 and §2.4; Fig.2.4 and direct-performance discussion. // Physical PDF pp. 40–42. // Applies when: The response model and specifications define feasibility; a linearized region is // only local. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O02","url":"theory-provenance.html#O02"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O03 · Signed local sensitivities and adjoints","text":"O03 · Signed local sensitivities and adjoints Source result. dPj is approximated by sum_i Sji dXi. An adjoint computes derivatives with respect to many parameters for one scalar response. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §2.4.1 Eqs. (2.12)–(2.13); §2.4.2.3 Eqs. (2.18)–(2.24). PDF pp. 42–44 . Assumptions and limits: Differentiable local response; suitable simulator convergence; independent responses generally need independent adjoints. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O03; source]: dPj is approximated by sum_i Sji dXi. An adjoint computes derivatives with // respect to many parameters for one scalar response. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §2.4.1 Eqs. (2.12)–(2.13); §2.4.2.3 Eqs. (2.18)–(2.24). // Physical PDF pp. 42–44. // Applies when: Differentiable local response; suitable simulator convergence; independent // responses generally need independent adjoints. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O03","url":"theory-provenance.html#O03"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O04 · Resistance and geometric capacitance models","text":"O04 · Resistance and geometric capacitance models Source result. A uniform rectangular segment has R=Rsheet L/W; overlap, lateral and fringing capacitance have distinct geometry-dependent contributions. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §2.5.3.1 Eqs. (2.29)–(2.32); §2.5.3.2 Eq. (2.33), printed pp.36–38. PDF pp. 49–51 . Assumptions and limits: Calibrated material/layer/environment; L and W use the same units; contacts/vias and nonuniform current need additional modeling. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O04; source]: A uniform rectangular segment has R=Rsheet L/W; overlap, lateral and // fringing capacitance have distinct geometry-dependent contributions. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §2.5.3.1 Eqs. (2.29)–(2.32); §2.5.3.2 Eq. (2.33), printed // pp.36–38. Physical PDF pp. 49–51. // Applies when: Calibrated material/layer/environment; L and W use the same units; contacts/vias // and nonuniform current need additional modeling. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O04","url":"theory-provenance.html#O04"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O05 · Area and distance mismatch terms","text":"O05 · Area and distance mismatch terms Source result. The cited pair model is variance=Aq^2/(WL)+Sq^2 D^2, with structure/orientation/environment constraints used alongside it. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §2.7.1 Eq. (2.35); §2.7.2 matching guidelines. PDF pp. 53–55 . Assumptions and limits: Process-calibrated pair model and its geometry domain; coefficients are not universal foundry constants; common centroid does not remove the finite-area random term. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O05; source]: The cited pair model is variance=Aq^2/(WL)+Sq^2 D^2, with // structure/orientation/environment constraints used alongside it. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §2.7.1 Eq. (2.35); §2.7.2 matching guidelines. Physical PDF // pp. 53–55. // Applies when: Process-calibrated pair model and its geometry domain; coefficients are not // universal foundry constants; common centroid does not remove the finite-area random term. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O05","url":"theory-provenance.html#O05"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O06 · Separate symmetry move groups","text":"O06 · Separate symmetry move groups Source result. Position, orientation and shape relationships are distinct; coordinated translations, swaps, flips and axis movement can preserve chosen relationships by construction. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §§4.6–4.7: constraint groups and translation, swap, flip and symmetry-axis moves. PDF pp. 99–105 . Assumptions and limits: Compatible group membership, fixed-object conditions and legal variants; preserving one relationship does not prove neighbor clearance or electrical matching. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O06; source]: Position, orientation and shape relationships are distinct; coordinated // translations, swaps, flips and axis movement can preserve chosen relationships by construction. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §§4.6–4.7: constraint groups and translation, swap, flip and // symmetry-axis moves. Physical PDF pp. 99–105. // Applies when: Compatible group membership, fixed-object conditions and legal variants; // preserving one relationship does not prove neighbor clearance or electrical matching. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O06","url":"theory-provenance.html#O06"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O07 · Annealing temperature calibration","text":"O07 · Annealing temperature calibration Source result. For fixed energy and symmetric proposals, uphill acceptance is exp(-dE/T); T0≈-mean(positive dE)/ln(p0) is an initial calibration heuristic. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §4.4.6 Metropolis criterion, PDF89–90; §4.11 Eqs. (4.37)–(4.38), PDF117–118. PDF pp. 89–90 · PDF pp. 117–118 . Assumptions and limits: T>0, 0<p0<1, consistent energy units, finite search; exp of mean does not equal mean acceptance. No finite-run global optimum guarantee. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O07; source]: For fixed energy and symmetric proposals, uphill acceptance is exp(-dE/T); // T0≈-mean(positive dE)/ln(p0) is an initial calibration heuristic. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §4.4.6 Metropolis criterion, PDF89–90; §4.11 Eqs. // (4.37)–(4.38), PDF117–118. Physical PDF pp. 89–90, 117–118. // Applies when: T>0, 0<p0<1, consistent energy units, finite search; exp of mean does not equal // mean acceptance. No finite-run global optimum guarantee. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O07","url":"theory-provenance.html#O07"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O08 · Segment current and via capability","text":"O08 · Segment current and via capability Source result. Required wire width and via count depend on current carried by each route segment and on process current capability. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §5.6.2 Eqs. (5.1)–(5.2); §5.7.1 Eq. (5.9). PDF pp. 143–147 . Assumptions and limits: Current scenario and branch topology must be known; waveform, temperature and current sharing require the relevant reliability model. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O08; source]: Required wire width and via count depend on current carried by each route // segment and on process current capability. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §5.6.2 Eqs. (5.1)–(5.2); §5.7.1 Eq. (5.9). Physical PDF pp. // 143–147. // Applies when: Current scenario and branch topology must be known; waveform, temperature and // current sharing require the relevant reliability model. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O08","url":"theory-provenance.html#O08"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O09 · Defect critical area is a separate yield model","text":"O09 · Defect critical area is a separate yield model Source result. For long parallel wires with length L and spacing s, bridging critical area is L max(0,chi-s); expected faults integrate critical area against defect density, with Poisson no-fault probability exp(-lambda). Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §5.9.1 Eqs. (5.16)–(5.18), Eq. (5.24), Fig.5.15; printed pp.141–143. PDF pp. 153–155 . Assumptions and limits: chi is defect diameter; simplified long-wire geometry omits detailed end effects; density and Poisson assumptions must be calibrated; not parametric mismatch yield. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O09; source]: For long parallel wires with length L and spacing s, bridging critical // area is L max(0,chi-s); expected faults integrate critical area against defect density, with // Poisson no-fault probability exp(-lambda). // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §5.9.1 Eqs. (5.16)–(5.18), Eq. (5.24), Fig.5.15; printed // pp.141–143. Physical PDF pp. 153–155. // Applies when: chi is defect diameter; simplified long-wire geometry omits detailed end effects; // density and Poisson assumptions must be calibrated; not parametric mismatch yield. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O09","url":"theory-provenance.html#O09"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O10 · Sequence-pair symmetry is a sufficient subset","text":"O10 · Sequence-pair symmetry is a sufficient subset Source result. For one common vertical symmetry axis, pos_alpha(x)<pos_alpha(y) iff pos_beta(sym(y))<pos_beta(sym(x)) is a sufficient symmetric-feasible sequence-pair condition, not a necessary condition for physical symmetry. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches . Balasa, Chapter1 §1.3, Eq. (1.1); sufficient-not-necessary discussion PDF50. PDF pp. 48–51 . Assumptions and limits: Source sequence convention, distinct group members, reflected partner mapping; multiple groups need compatible embedding (§1.3.2 PDF60–61). Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O10; source]: For one common vertical symmetry axis, pos_alpha(x)<pos_alpha(y) iff // pos_beta(sym(y))<pos_beta(sym(x)) is a sufficient symmetric-feasible sequence-pair condition, // not a necessary condition for physical symmetry. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological // Approaches. Balasa, Chapter1 §1.3, Eq. (1.1); sufficient-not-necessary discussion PDF50. // Physical PDF pp. 48–51. // Applies when: Source sequence convention, distinct group members, reflected partner mapping; // multiple groups need compatible embedding (§1.3.2 PDF60–61). // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O10","url":"theory-provenance.html#O10"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O11 · Connected symmetry islands restrict the search","text":"O11 · Connected symmetry islands restrict the search Source result. A symmetry island is an abutting connected group. ASF-B-star stores representative half-groups and reconstructs counterparts; self-symmetric representatives touch the axis. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches . Lin and Chang, §§2.2.2–2.3.1, Definition2.1, Lemma2.1 and Property2.1. PDF pp. 79–85 . Assumptions and limits: Legal module envelopes and chapter-specific B-star convention; connectivity/abutment is stronger than geometric symmetry. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O11; source]: A symmetry island is an abutting connected group. ASF-B-star stores // representative half-groups and reconstructs counterparts; self-symmetric representatives touch // the axis. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological // Approaches. Lin and Chang, §§2.2.2–2.3.1, Definition2.1, Lemma2.1 and Property2.1. Physical PDF // pp. 79–85. // Applies when: Legal module envelopes and chapter-specific B-star convention; // connectivity/abutment is stronger than geometric symmetry. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O11","url":"theory-provenance.html#O11"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O12 · DTI legality is disjunctive and coupled","text":"O12 · DTI legality is disjunctive and coupled Source result. The DTI example permits a shared range or a sufficiently isolated range, excludes intermediate gaps, and limits a middle device total left-plus-right stretch. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches . Strasser et al., §3.1.2 Fig.3.3; §3.3.2 Eqs. (3.36)–(3.40). PDF p. 115 · PDF pp. 135–137 . Assumptions and limits: Technology-specific DTI semantics and finite geometry bounds; not every isolation rule has these branches. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O12; source]: The DTI example permits a shared range or a sufficiently isolated range, // excludes intermediate gaps, and limits a middle device total left-plus-right stretch. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological // Approaches. Strasser et al., §3.1.2 Fig.3.3; §3.3.2 Eqs. (3.36)–(3.40). Physical PDF pp. 115, // 135–137. // Applies when: Technology-specific DTI semantics and finite geometry bounds; not every isolation // rule has these branches. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O12","url":"theory-provenance.html#O12"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O13 · Shape functions preserve alternatives within a decomposition","text":"O13 · Shape functions preserve alternatives within a decomposition Source result. Ordinary horizontal composition gives (w1+w2,max(h1,h2)); enhanced shapes retain B-star topology and can interlock contours. Hierarchical enumeration does not enumerate all circuit layouts. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches . Strasser et al., §§3.4–3.5, Figs.3.23–3.33, Algorithm3.4. PDF pp. 137–146 . Assumptions and limits: Fixed hierarchy and variants; composition constraint-preservation assumes no incompatible added parent constraints; geometric dominance is not automatically electrical dominance. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O13; source]: Ordinary horizontal composition gives (w1+w2,max(h1,h2)); enhanced shapes // retain B-star topology and can interlock contours. Hierarchical enumeration does not enumerate // all circuit layouts. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological // Approaches. Strasser et al., §§3.4–3.5, Figs.3.23–3.33, Algorithm3.4. Physical PDF pp. 137–146. // Applies when: Fixed hierarchy and variants; composition constraint-preservation assumes no // incompatible added parent constraints; geometric dominance is not automatically electrical // dominance. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O13","url":"theory-provenance.html#O13"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O14 · Difference constraints versus general linear relations","text":"O14 · Difference constraints versus general linear relations Source result. An acyclic fixed-order graph solves xj-xi>=dij by longest paths; symmetry relations involving sums/differences of more variables require a richer linear representation. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches . Said et al., §5.4.1 Eqs. (5.1)–(5.2); §5.4.2 Eq. (5.6); §5.5.1 Definition5.2. PDF pp. 219–229 . Assumptions and limits: One-dimensional fixed ordering with appropriate graph feasibility; LP optimality concerns the stated model, not all topology choices or nonlinear electrical effects. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O14; source]: An acyclic fixed-order graph solves xj-xi>=dij by longest paths; symmetry // relations involving sums/differences of more variables require a richer linear representation. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological // Approaches. Said et al., §5.4.1 Eqs. (5.1)–(5.2); §5.4.2 Eq. (5.6); §5.5.1 Definition5.2. // Physical PDF pp. 219–229. // Applies when: One-dimensional fixed ordering with appropriate graph feasibility; LP optimality // concerns the stated model, not all topology choices or nonlinear electrical effects. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O14","url":"theory-provenance.html#O14"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O15 · Layout-aware sizing and surrogate domain","text":"O15 · Layout-aware sizing and surrogate domain Source result. Each sizing can select geometric parameters, instantiate or model layout parasitics, and evaluate the resulting circuit. A naive table with m samples in n dimensions has m^n entries. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches . Castro-López et al., §6.3.3 flow; §6.4.2 shape propagation; §6.5.1 extraction approaches A/B; Table6.7. PDF p. 263 · PDF pp. 267–268 · PDF pp. 272–275 . Assumptions and limits: Fixed characterized template/technology/model domain; sensitivity screening reduces sampling but cannot certify omitted parasitics in all future contexts. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O15; source]: Each sizing can select geometric parameters, instantiate or model layout // parasitics, and evaluate the resulting circuit. A naive table with m samples in n dimensions has // m^n entries. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological // Approaches. Castro-López et al., §6.3.3 flow; §6.4.2 shape propagation; §6.5.1 extraction // approaches A/B; Table6.7. Physical PDF pp. 263, 267–268, 272–275. // Applies when: Fixed characterized template/technology/model domain; sensitivity screening // reduces sampling but cannot certify omitted parasitics in all future contexts. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O15","url":"theory-provenance.html#O15"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O16 · Constraint lifecycle and meta-verification","text":"O16 · Constraint lifecycle and meta-verification Source result. Management, derivation, transformation, sensitivity analysis and verification are distinct services; static final verification uses fixed design and constraint data. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches . Jerke, Lienig and Freuer, §7.4, Fig.7.4; §§7.4.1–7.4.5. PDF pp. 285–293 . Assumptions and limits: Applicable rules and model coverage must be defined; DRC/LVS alone do not express all analog intent; transformations can restrict the remaining design space. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O16; source]: Management, derivation, transformation, sensitivity analysis and // verification are distinct services; static final verification uses fixed design and constraint // data. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological // Approaches. Jerke, Lienig and Freuer, §7.4, Fig.7.4; §§7.4.1–7.4.5. Physical PDF pp. 285–293. // Applies when: Applicable rules and model coverage must be defined; DRC/LVS alone do not express // all analog intent; transformations can restrict the remaining design space. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O16","url":"theory-provenance.html#O16"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O17 · Yield over all operating conditions","text":"O17 · Yield over all operating conditions Source result. Yield is probability over statistical fabrication variables that every specified performance bound holds for every operating-range condition. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §§3.1–3.5 parameter classes; §4.8.1 Eq. (114), §§4.8.2–4.8.4. PDF pp. 46–50 · PDF pp. 89–95 . Assumptions and limits: Declared joint statistical distribution and complete range contract; a sampled operating point or process-corner list alone is a different analysis. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O17; source]: Yield is probability over statistical fabrication variables that every // specified performance bound holds for every operating-range condition. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing. §§3.1–3.5 parameter classes; // §4.8.1 Eq. (114), §§4.8.2–4.8.4. Physical PDF pp. 46–50, 89–95. // Applies when: Declared joint statistical distribution and complete range contract; a sampled // operating point or process-corner list alone is a different analysis. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O17","url":"theory-provenance.html#O17"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O18 · Covariance propagation and valid dependence","text":"O18 · Covariance propagation and valid dependence Source result. For local response f≈f0+S(x-mu), Cov(f)≈S C S-transpose; a scalar sensitivity column g gives variance g-transpose C g. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §5.2 Eq. (175); AppendixA.9 Eqs. (A.13) and (A.15). PDF p. 111 · PDF p. 183 . Assumptions and limits: Finite second moments; exact for affine response, approximate for nonlinear response. C must be PSD, inverse whitening requires positive definiteness or a reduced-rank subspace. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O18; source]: For local response f≈f0+S(x-mu), Cov(f)≈S C S-transpose; a scalar // sensitivity column g gives variance g-transpose C g. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing. §5.2 Eq. (175); AppendixA.9 // Eqs. (A.13) and (A.15). Physical PDF pp. 111, 183. // Applies when: Finite second moments; exact for affine response, approximate for nonlinear // response. C must be PSD, inverse whitening requires positive definiteness or a reduced-rank // subspace. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O18","url":"theory-provenance.html#O18"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O19 · Linear ellipsoidal worst case","text":"O19 · Linear ellipsoidal worst case Source result. For delta-transpose C-inverse delta<=beta^2 and f=f0+g-transpose delta, extrema occur at delta=±beta Cg/sqrt(g-transpose Cg) and f=f0±beta sqrt(g-transpose Cg). Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §5.2.1–5.2.2, Eqs. (171)–(179), printed p.93. PDF p. 111 . Assumptions and limits: Positive-definite C, beta>=0 and g-transpose Cg>0; zero gradient/variance requires separate treatment; nonlinear circuits need validated approximation. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O19; source]: For delta-transpose C-inverse delta<=beta^2 and f=f0+g-transpose delta, // extrema occur at delta=±beta Cg/sqrt(g-transpose Cg) and f=f0±beta sqrt(g-transpose Cg). // Source: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing. §5.2.1–5.2.2, Eqs. // (171)–(179), printed p.93. Physical PDF pp. 111. // Applies when: Positive-definite C, beta>=0 and g-transpose Cg>0; zero gradient/variance requires // separate treatment; nonlinear circuits need validated approximation. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O19","url":"theory-provenance.html#O19"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O20 · A sigma distance needs its probability region","text":"O20 · A sigma distance needs its probability region Source result. A standardized n-dimensional Gaussian radius-beta ball encloses P(chi-square_n<=beta^2); a symmetric independent box encloses [2Phi(beta)-1]^n; one linear upper bound has probability Phi(beta). Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §§6.2.2–6.2.4, Eq. (240), Eqs. (242)–(248), Eqs. (258)–(259), Tables11–12. PDF pp. 133–143 . Assumptions and limits: Independent standardized coordinates for box product; positive signed margin for usual passing example; joint circuit yield is an intersection, not automatically any of these regions. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O20; source]: A standardized n-dimensional Gaussian radius-beta ball encloses // P(chi-square_n<=beta^2); a symmetric independent box encloses [2Phi(beta)-1]^n; one linear upper // bound has probability Phi(beta). // Source: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing. §§6.2.2–6.2.4, Eq. (240), // Eqs. (242)–(248), Eqs. (258)–(259), Tables11–12. Physical PDF pp. 133–143. // Applies when: Independent standardized coordinates for box product; positive signed margin for // usual passing example; joint circuit yield is an intersection, not automatically any of these // regions. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O20","url":"theory-provenance.html#O20"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O21 · No-failure confidence is not perfect yield","text":"O21 · No-failure confidence is not perfect yield Derived result. With N independent Bernoulli trials and no failures, a one-sided 95% upper failure-probability bound is 1-0.05^(1/N); ordinary Monte Carlo yield variance is Y(1-Y)/N. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §6.1.1 Eq. (219), §6.1.3 Eqs. (225)–(226); confidence limit is derived here. PDF pp. 125–132 . Assumptions and limits: Independent, identically distributed complete pass/fail events; not the variance formula for weighted importance samples or unresolved simulation outcomes. Derivation: P(no failures|p)=(1-p)^N; solve (1-p)^N=.05. N=3000 gives 0.00099808; a 1 ppm upper bound requires at least 2,995,731 clean independent trials. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O21; derived]: With N independent Bernoulli trials and no failures, a one-sided 95% // upper failure-probability bound is 1-0.05^(1/N); ordinary Monte Carlo yield variance is // Y(1-Y)/N. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design // Centering and Sizing. §6.1.1 Eq. (219), §6.1.3 Eqs. (225)–(226); confidence limit is derived // here. Physical PDF pp. 125–132. // Applies when: Independent, identically distributed complete pass/fail events; not the variance // formula for weighted importance samples or unresolved simulation outcomes. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O21","url":"theory-provenance.html#O21"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O22 · Mean-shift centering has a limited parameter interpretation","text":"O22 · Mean-shift centering has a limited parameter interpretation Source result. For Gaussian process covariance fixed and acceptance region fixed, gradient_mu Y=Y C-inverse(mu_accepted-mu). A stationary positive-yield point matches the accepted and original means. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §7.1.2, Eq. (324), printed pp.145–146. PDF pp. 163–164 . Assumptions and limits: Unconstrained mean variation, fixed covariance and fixed region; necessary stationarity only. Width/topology changes normally alter the region and cannot be substituted without chain rules. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O22; source]: For Gaussian process covariance fixed and acceptance region fixed, // gradient_mu Y=Y C-inverse(mu_accepted-mu). A stationary positive-yield point matches the // accepted and original means. // Source: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing. §7.1.2, Eq. (324), printed // pp.145–146. Physical PDF pp. 163–164. // Applies when: Unconstrained mean variation, fixed covariance and fixed region; necessary // stationarity only. Width/topology changes normally alter the region and cannot be substituted // without chain rules. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O22","url":"theory-provenance.html#O22"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O23 · Correct regularized least-squares transpose","text":"O23 · Correct regularized least-squares transpose Derived result. With residual e in R^m and row-gradient Jacobian J in R^(m by n), the regularized linear least-squares step satisfies (J-transpose J+lambda I)r=-J-transpose e. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §7.2.3 Eq. (362) and printed Eqs. (365),(368); printed pp.158–159. PDF pp. 175–176 . Assumptions and limits: Local residual linearization, lambda>=0 and suitable solve/rank treatment; source RHS omits transpose, verified in actual raster. Not a global circuit-optimum certificate. Derivation: Differentiate one-half ||e+Jr||^2 + lambda/2 ||r||^2. Gradient J-transpose(e+Jr)+lambda r=0. RHS must have n components. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O23; derived]: With residual e in R^m and row-gradient Jacobian J in R^(m by n), the // regularized linear least-squares step satisfies (J-transpose J+lambda I)r=-J-transpose e. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design // Centering and Sizing. §7.2.3 Eq. (362) and printed Eqs. (365),(368); printed pp.158–159. // Physical PDF pp. 175–176. // Applies when: Local residual linearization, lambda>=0 and suitable solve/rank treatment; source // RHS omits transpose, verified in actual raster. Not a global circuit-optimum certificate. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O23","url":"theory-provenance.html#O23"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O24 · Full nonlinear Lagrangian curvature","text":"O24 · Full nonlinear Lagrangian curvature Derived result. For L=f-lambda(beta^2-delta-transpose C-inverse delta), Hessian_delta L=Hessian f+2lambda C-inverse; covariance curvature alone does not establish nonlinear second-order sufficiency. Reference: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing . §5.4 Eq. (189), prose following Eq. (191); AppendixC.6 Eq. (C.24). PDF p. 115 · PDF p. 195 . Assumptions and limits: Twice differentiable f, constant positive-definite C and fixed multiplier for Hessian; constrained sufficiency concerns appropriate feasible/critical directions. The prose omission was verified in raster. Derivation: Differentiate the displayed Lagrangian twice with respect to delta. The objective-curvature term vanishes for affine f, or at a point where its Hessian happens to be zero; it cannot generally be omitted. Also, lambda>=0 alone is not strict positive curvature. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O24; derived]: For L=f-lambda(beta^2-delta-transpose C-inverse delta), Hessian_delta // L=Hessian f+2lambda C-inverse; covariance curvature alone does not establish nonlinear // second-order sufficiency. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design // Centering and Sizing. §5.4 Eq. (189), prose following Eq. (191); AppendixC.6 Eq. (C.24). // Physical PDF pp. 115, 195. // Applies when: Twice differentiable f, constant positive-definite C and fixed multiplier for // Hessian; constrained sufficiency concerns appropriate feasible/critical directions. The prose // omission was verified in raster. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O24","url":"theory-provenance.html#O24"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O25 · Electrical centroid uses normalized comparison weights","text":"O25 · Electrical centroid uses normalized comparison weights Derived result. For the difference of weighted responses to a shared affine spatial field, equal zeroth and first moments cancel that field. Ratio comparisons first normalize each group by its intended total; equal raw device counts are not necessary. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §2.7.2 common-centroid motivation; contrasting geometric area-weighted group center in Topological §3.1.1 Eq. (3.12), PDF112. PDF pp. 53–55 . Assumptions and limits: Same local field and proportional unit response; comparison weights encode the intended ratio. Second-order fields, unequal environments and routing bias are not canceled by first moments alone. Derivation: For q(r)=q0+g dot r, weighted difference=sum_i a_i q(r_i)-sum_j b_j q(r_j)=q0(sum a-sum b)+g dot(sum a r-sum b r). Both coefficients must vanish; normalize group weights for mean/ratio comparison. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O25; derived]: For the difference of weighted responses to a shared affine spatial // field, equal zeroth and first moments cancel that field. Ratio comparisons first normalize each // group by its intended total; equal raw device counts are not necessary. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and // Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability. §2.7.2 // common-centroid motivation; contrasting geometric area-weighted group center in Topological // §3.1.1 Eq. (3.12), PDF112. Physical PDF pp. 53–55. // Applies when: Same local field and proportional unit response; comparison weights encode the // intended ratio. Second-order fields, unequal environments and routing bias are not canceled by // first moments alone. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O25","url":"theory-provenance.html#O25"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · O26 · Fault detectability differs from fault incidence","text":"O26 · Fault detectability differs from fault incidence Source result. Layouts with equal expected defect faults can differ in detectability; the source weights routing exposure using good/faulty response separation. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . §5.9.2, Fig.5.18 and Eqs.(5.32)–(5.34), printed pp.145–147. PDF pp. 157–159 . Assumptions and limits: A specified fault model, stimulus and response distribution are required. Inverse statistical distance is a cost proxy, not a calibrated escape probability; accepting a hypothesis does not establish posterior probability that an individual circuit is good. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [O26; source]: Layouts with equal expected defect faults can differ in detectability; the // source weights routing exposure using good/faulty response separation. // Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for // Performance and Manufacturability. §5.9.2, Fig.5.18 and Eqs.(5.32)–(5.34), printed pp.145–147. // Physical PDF pp. 157–159. // Applies when: A specified fault model, stimulus and response distribution are required. Inverse // statistical distance is a cost proxy, not a calibrated escape probability; accepting a // hypothesis does not establish posterior probability that an individual circuit is good. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O26","url":"theory-provenance.html#O26"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Mismatch, substrate, thermal and reliability","text":"Mismatch, substrate, thermal and reliability P01 · Area and distance contributions to MOS pair mismatch Source result. For the characterized equal-device pair model, variance is approximated by A_P²/(WL)+S_P²D². Reference: Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988. Matching properties of MOS transistors . Analysis, Eq.(1), printed pp.327–328. PDF pp. 1–2 . Assumptions and limits: Many small additive local events with correlation distance much smaller than device dimensions, plus a fitted long-range component; process/parameter-specific coefficients. A refers to the pair-difference statistic here. A single-device coefficient requires its own convention conversion. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P01; source]: For the characterized equal-device pair model, variance is approximated by // A_P²/(WL)+S_P²D². // Source: Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988. Matching properties of MOS // transistors. Analysis, Eq.(1), printed pp.327–328. Physical PDF pp. 1–2. // Applies when: Many small additive local events with correlation distance much smaller than // device dimensions, plus a fitted long-range component; process/parameter-specific coefficients. // A refers to the pair-difference statistic here. A single-device coefficient requires its own // convention conversion. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P01 P02 · Edge variation prevents a universal area-only beta law Source result. Current-factor variance contains width-edge, length-edge, mobility and oxide terms before an approximate A_beta²/(WL) reduction. Reference: Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988. Matching properties of MOS transistors . Matching of MOS transistors, Eq.(3), printed p.328. PDF p. 2 . Assumptions and limits: Area-dependent contribution only; same defined process parameters; authors say simplification becomes less valid as W and L decrease. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P02; source]: Current-factor variance contains width-edge, length-edge, mobility and // oxide terms before an approximate A_beta²/(WL) reduction. // Source: Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988. Matching properties of MOS // transistors. Matching of MOS transistors, Eq.(3), printed p.328. Physical PDF pp. 2. // Applies when: Area-dependent contribution only; same defined process parameters; authors say // simplification becomes less valid as W and L decrease. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P02 P03 · Orientation evidence is process-specific Source result. The studied 90-degree rotation affected current-factor mismatch and varied by wafer/batch; this experiment did not establish arbitrary transform equivalence. Reference: Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988. Matching properties of MOS transistors . Orientation discussion, Fig.3(d), printed pp.328–330. PDF pp. 2–4 . Assumptions and limits: 2.5µm n-well CMOS experiment; no universal rotational/reflection/source-drain symmetry rule for another process. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P03; source]: The studied 90-degree rotation affected current-factor mismatch and varied // by wafer/batch; this experiment did not establish arbitrary transform equivalence. // Source: Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988. Matching properties of MOS // transistors. Orientation discussion, Fig.3(d), printed pp.328–330. Physical PDF pp. 2–4. // Applies when: 2.5µm n-well CMOS experiment; no universal rotational/reflection/source-drain // symmetry rule for another process. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P03 P04 · Use independent physical causes in mismatch models Source result. Treating threshold and gain-factor mismatch as independent can double count an underlying oxide-thickness fluctuation; bias and geometry matter. Reference: Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design . Section II, Table I and discussion surrounding Eqs.(4)–(6), printed p.451. PDF p. 2 . Assumptions and limits: Do not apply this critique as a prohibition on all calibrated local empirical fits; inspect whether the statistical basis and correlations are already handled. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P04; source]: Treating threshold and gain-factor mismatch as independent can double // count an underlying oxide-thickness fluctuation; bias and geometry matter. // Source: Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for // analog design. Section II, Table I and discussion surrounding Eqs.(4)–(6), printed p.451. // Physical PDF pp. 2. // Applies when: Do not apply this critique as a prohibition on all calibrated local empirical // fits; inspect whether the statistical basis and correlations are already handled. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P04 P05 · Propagate mismatch with covariance and circuit sensitivity Derived result. For a first-order expansion Δe≈JΔp, Var(e)≈J C Jᵀ; Drennan Eq.(8) is the independent-parameter special case. Reference: Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design . Section II, Eqs.(7)–(11), printed p.452. PDF p. 3 . Assumptions and limits: Small perturbations around the specified bias/geometry, valid compact model, and covariance C for the same physical variables; nonlinear tails need additional treatment. Derivation: Cov(JΔp)=J Cov(Δp) Jᵀ. If C is diagonal, this reduces to Σ_i (∂e/∂p_i)² σ_i², matching Eq.(8). Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P05; derived]: For a first-order expansion Δe≈JΔp, Var(e)≈J C Jᵀ; Drennan Eq.(8) is the // independent-parameter special case. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. // McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design. Section II, Eqs.(7)–(11), // printed p.452. Physical PDF pp. 3. // Applies when: Small perturbations around the specified bias/geometry, valid compact model, and // covariance C for the same physical variables; nonlinear tails need additional treatment. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P05 P06 · Fixed-current width can trade averaging against sensitivity Derived result. In a long-channel saturation, threshold-only toy model at fixed current, relative current variance from threshold mismatch is proportional to 1/L² and independent of W. Reference: Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design . Eq.(9) and §III.A Geometry and Bias Interrelationship, printed pp.452,454. PDF p. 3 · PDF p. 5 . Assumptions and limits: This is an illustrative derivation, not a modern compact-model law; ignore mobility/edge/series/short-channel contributions and use the stated area coefficient. Recheck headroom. Derivation: σ²(ΔI/I)≈4A²/(WL Vov²), while Vov²=2IL/(μCox W); substitution gives 2A² μCox/(I L²). The source supports the more general observation that changing W changes both physical variation and sensitivity. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P06; derived]: In a long-channel saturation, threshold-only toy model at fixed current, // relative current variance from threshold mismatch is proportional to 1/L² and independent of W. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. // McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design. Eq.(9) and §III.A // Geometry and Bias Interrelationship, printed pp.452,454. Physical PDF pp. 3, 5. // Applies when: This is an illustrative derivation, not a modern compact-model law; ignore // mobility/edge/series/short-channel contributions and use the stated area coefficient. Recheck // headroom. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P06 P07 · Splitting preserves ideal total-area random mismatch Source result. Four independent 20µm-wide units can reproduce the ideal mismatch of one 80µm-wide device at the same length; there is no free fixed-total-width averaging gain. Reference: Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design . §III.C Multiple-Unit Devices, printed p.455. PDF p. 6 . Assumptions and limits: Width effects and altered electrical/parasitic context neglected; compare equal total width, not an array with one small unit. Circuit-role differences are separately shown in Table III. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P07; source]: Four independent 20µm-wide units can reproduce the ideal mismatch of one // 80µm-wide device at the same length; there is no free fixed-total-width averaging gain. // Source: Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for // analog design. §III.C Multiple-Unit Devices, printed p.455. Physical PDF pp. 6. // Applies when: Width effects and altered electrical/parasitic context neglected; compare equal // total width, not an array with one small unit. Circuit-role differences are separately shown in // Table III. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P07 P08 · Substrate coupling is a complete contact-network problem Source result. Injected substrate current paths depend on injector, receiver, other contacts, doping profile and backplate potential; taps/wells may inject return noise. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . §1.1 and Chapter 2 §§2–3, printed pp.2,15–17. PDF p. 25 · PDF pp. 38–40 . Assumptions and limits: State injector mechanisms, reference connections and receiving impedance/body bias; a distance-only pair model omits third-contact loading. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P08; source]: Injected substrate current paths depend on injector, receiver, other // contacts, doping profile and backplate potential; taps/wells may inject return noise. // Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto // Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization // for IC Design. §1.1 and Chapter 2 §§2–3, printed pp.2,15–17. Physical PDF pp. 25, 38–40. // Applies when: State injector mechanisms, reference connections and receiving impedance/body // bias; a distance-only pair model omits third-contact loading. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P08 P09 · Electroquasistatic substrate equation and boundaries Source result. Conductive and displacement currents obey ∇·(σ∇φ)+∂t[∇·(ε∇φ)]=0; the resistive limit and explicit contact/edge/interface boundaries determine the model. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . Chapter 3 §2 Electromagnetic Formulation, Eqs.(3.1)–(3.3), printed pp.22–23. PDF pp. 45–46 . Assumptions and limits: Electroquasistatic continuum regime. Resistive approximation requires ε/σ small relative to times of interest; contact equipotential and insulating/grounded boundaries must match the physical setup. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P09; source]: Conductive and displacement currents obey ∇·(σ∇φ)+∂t[∇·(ε∇φ)]=0; the // resistive limit and explicit contact/edge/interface boundaries determine the model. // Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto // Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization // for IC Design. Chapter 3 §2 Electromagnetic Formulation, Eqs.(3.1)–(3.3), printed pp.22–23. // Physical PDF pp. 45–46. // Applies when: Electroquasistatic continuum regime. Resistive approximation requires ε/σ small // relative to times of interest; contact equipotential and insulating/grounded boundaries must // match the physical setup. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P09 P10 · Isolation includes loading and sign convention Source result. The book defines contact isolation as V_receiver/V_injector; it depends on contact capacitance, backside impedance, frequency and receiver load, not only substrate resistance. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . Chapter 8 §2 Characterizing Isolation and §4 Effects of Load Impedance, printed pp.101–102,111–112. PDF pp. 124–125 · PDF pp. 134–135 . Assumptions and limits: Use a specified source/reference/loading convention. A negative-dB transfer and a positive attenuation magnitude have opposite reported signs. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P10; source]: The book defines contact isolation as V_receiver/V_injector; it depends on // contact capacitance, backside impedance, frequency and receiver load, not only substrate // resistance. // Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto // Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization // for IC Design. Chapter 8 §2 Characterizing Isolation and §4 Effects of Load Impedance, printed // pp.101–102,111–112. Physical PDF pp. 124–125, 134–135. // Applies when: Use a specified source/reference/loading convention. A negative-dB transfer and a // positive attenuation magnitude have opposite reported signs. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P10 P11 · Separation can saturate in low-resistivity substrates Source result. Finite backplate impedance can make isolation approximately independent of further contact separation in the book’s low-resistivity examples. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . §8.2.1 Low-Resistivity Substrates, Figs.8.6–8.7; contrast §8.2.2, printed pp.102–107. PDF pp. 125–127 · PDF p. 130 . Assumptions and limits: Depends on epitaxial profile, area, frequency and backplate connection. Do not use its multiple-of-epitaxial-thickness example as a universal spacing rule. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P11; source]: Finite backplate impedance can make isolation approximately independent of // further contact separation in the book’s low-resistivity examples. // Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto // Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization // for IC Design. §8.2.1 Low-Resistivity Substrates, Figs.8.6–8.7; contrast §8.2.2, printed // pp.102–107. Physical PDF pp. 125–127, 130. // Applies when: Depends on epitaxial profile, area, frequency and backplate connection. Do not use // its multiple-of-epitaxial-thickness example as a universal spacing rule. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P11 P12 · Guard-ring return and observation reference matter Source result. A ring can worsen received noise through finite/noisy grounding; internal versus external signal reference and ring width/location change the result. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . §8.6.1 Grounding Schemes and §8.6.2 Optimization of Guard Ring Widths, Figs.8.16–8.23, printed pp.116–124. PDF pp. 139–140 · PDF pp. 142–147 . Assumptions and limits: The numerical studies use specific high-resistivity substrates and lumped package models. Compare actual reference voltage and include ring-return inductance and pickup. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P12; source]: A ring can worsen received noise through finite/noisy grounding; internal // versus external signal reference and ring width/location change the result. // Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto // Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization // for IC Design. §8.6.1 Grounding Schemes and §8.6.2 Optimization of Guard Ring Widths, // Figs.8.16–8.23, printed pp.116–124. Physical PDF pp. 139–140, 142–147. // Applies when: The numerical studies use specific high-resistivity substrates and lumped package // models. Compare actual reference voltage and include ring-return inductance and pickup. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P12 P13 · Buried shields have distinct mechanisms and limits Source result. Conductive shields provide an alternate current return, dielectric isolation raises impedance, and buried opposite-type wells combine junction isolation with conductive shielding. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . §8.9 Buried Substrate Shields, Figs.8.27–8.30, printed pp.128–133. PDF pp. 151–156 . Assumptions and limits: The process must support the structure and bias. Conductive return impedance, finite shield resistivity, junction capacitance and frequency limit isolation. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P13; source]: Conductive shields provide an alternate current return, dielectric // isolation raises impedance, and buried opposite-type wells combine junction isolation with // conductive shielding. // Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto // Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization // for IC Design. §8.9 Buried Substrate Shields, Figs.8.27–8.30, printed pp.128–133. Physical PDF // pp. 151–156. // Applies when: The process must support the structure and bias. Conductive return impedance, // finite shield resistivity, junction capacitance and frequency limit isolation. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P13 P14 · Differential substrate error is transfer imbalance Derived result. In a linear receiver difference, v_diff=(H1−H2)v_aggressor; equal common-mode transfer cancels only while the two complete paths and receiver responses match. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . §8.3 Improving Isolation Using Differential Circuits, Figs.8.9–8.10, printed pp.107–111. PDF pp. 130–134 . Assumptions and limits: Complex transfer functions with common reference. The source explicitly studies a 5% receiving-capacitor mismatch; phase, loading and circuit mismatch must remain included. Derivation: Subtract v1=H1 va and v2=H2 va. A 1% relative imbalance of a 0.01 transfer gives 10^-4 differential transfer only for the stated approximately equal-phase case. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P14; derived]: In a linear receiver difference, v_diff=(H1−H2)v_aggressor; equal // common-mode transfer cancels only while the two complete paths and receiver responses match. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, // Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. // Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design. §8.3 Improving Isolation Using // Differential Circuits, Figs.8.9–8.10, printed pp.107–111. Physical PDF pp. 130–134. // Applies when: Complex transfer functions with common reference. The source explicitly studies a // 5% receiving-capacitor mismatch; phase, loading and circuit mismatch must remain included. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P14 P15 · Local substrate-noise budgets need phase and sensitivity Source result. Local noise generators permit performance-to-parasitic budget generation; the PLL example shows jitter depends on the phase of injection relative to transitions. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . Chapter 5 §§1–3, Eqs.(5.1)–(5.5), §5.3 case study, printed pp.47–53,58. PDF pp. 70–73 · PDF pp. 75–76 · PDF p. 81 . Assumptions and limits: Local linearization or explicitly maximized sensitivity over a defined interval; source waveform/phase assumptions and circuit operating state must be retained. Shared budget allocation is not unique. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P15; source]: Local noise generators permit performance-to-parasitic budget generation; // the PLL example shows jitter depends on the phase of injection relative to transitions. // Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto // Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization // for IC Design. Chapter 5 §§1–3, Eqs.(5.1)–(5.5), §5.3 case study, printed pp.47–53,58. Physical // PDF pp. 70–73, 75–76, 81. // Applies when: Local linearization or explicitly maximized sensitivity over a defined interval; // source waveform/phase assumptions and circuit operating state must be retained. Shared budget // allocation is not unique. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P15 P16 · Migration damage is flux divergence and coupled driving forces Source result. Electrical, thermal and stress driving forces produce atomic flux; accumulation/depletion and coupled heating/stress determine vulnerable regions. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . §2.2 and §2.5, especially Eqs.(2.15)–(2.18), printed pp.18,35–43. PDF p. 30 · PDF pp. 47–48 · PDF pp. 52–55 . Assumptions and limits: Material paths, interface boundary conditions, temperature and mechanical state matter; average current density alone is not a damage-location model. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P16; source]: Electrical, thermal and stress driving forces produce atomic flux; // accumulation/depletion and coupled heating/stress determine vulnerable regions. // Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware // Integrated Circuit Design. §2.2 and §2.5, especially Eqs.(2.15)–(2.18), printed pp.18,35–43. // Physical PDF pp. 30, 47–48, 52–55. // Applies when: Material paths, interface boundary conditions, temperature and mechanical state // matter; average current density alone is not a damage-location model. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P16 P17 · Black law describes a calibrated median Source result. t50=C j^-n exp(Ea/kT) is a generalized empirical median-lifetime relation; a product reliability target also needs mission profile and failure statistics. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . Eq.(2.1), generalized Eq.(3.8), §§3.4.1–3.4.2, printed pp.18–19,79–86. PDF pp. 30–31 · PDF pp. 91–98 . Assumptions and limits: Same characterized process, geometry, mechanism, temperature/current range and failure criterion. Median is not low-failure-probability lifetime of every segment; do not extrapolate across mechanism changes. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P17; source]: t50=C j^-n exp(Ea/kT) is a generalized empirical median-lifetime relation; // a product reliability target also needs mission profile and failure statistics. // Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware // Integrated Circuit Design. Eq.(2.1), generalized Eq.(3.8), §§3.4.1–3.4.2, printed // pp.18–19,79–86. Physical PDF pp. 30–31, 91–98. // Applies when: Same characterized process, geometry, mechanism, temperature/current range and // failure criterion. Median is not low-failure-probability lifetime of every segment; do not // extrapolate across mechanism changes. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P17 P18 · Current metrics are not interchangeable Source result. RMS, effective/directional average and peak currents correspond to different mechanisms; all applicable dimensioning limits must be considered. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . §2.4.3 and §3.3.1 Current Types, printed pp.29–32,70–71. PDF pp. 41–44 · PDF pp. 82–83 . Assumptions and limits: RMS heating assumes resistance treated consistently; self-healing depends on characterized waveform/frequency/material behavior. Zero signed average does not imply zero damage. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P18; source]: RMS, effective/directional average and peak currents correspond to // different mechanisms; all applicable dimensioning limits must be considered. // Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware // Integrated Circuit Design. §2.4.3 and §3.3.1 Current Types, printed pp.29–32,70–71. Physical PDF // pp. 41–44, 82–83. // Applies when: RMS heating assumes resistance treated consistently; self-healing depends on // characterized waveform/frequency/material behavior. Zero signed average does not imply zero // damage. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P18 P19 · Topology and current-aware wire area Source result. Signed terminal-current bounds/scenarios determine tree segment demand; current-driven planning can minimize required interconnect area rather than only length. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . §§3.3.2–3.3.3, Eqs.(3.1)–(3.7), Figs.3.4–3.10, printed pp.71–78. PDF pp. 83–90 . Assumptions and limits: For transient cut sums include relevant interconnect shunt currents; retain mode/correlation assumptions. Meshes require a network current-sharing solution. The handbook’s exact waveform sum is a KCL expression, distinct from the book’s equivalent-current bounds. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P19; source]: Signed terminal-current bounds/scenarios determine tree segment demand; // current-driven planning can minimize required interconnect area rather than only length. // Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware // Integrated Circuit Design. §§3.3.2–3.3.3, Eqs.(3.1)–(3.7), Figs.3.4–3.10, printed pp.71–78. // Physical PDF pp. 83–90. // Applies when: For transient cut sums include relevant interconnect shunt currents; retain // mode/correlation assumptions. Meshes require a network current-sharing solution. The handbook’s // exact waveform sum is a KCL expression, distinct from the book’s equivalent-current bounds. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P19 P20 · The metal path includes terminals and manufactured cross-section Source result. Width must meet the largest applicable current/manufacturing requirement; pin landing regions and internal pin geometry need ampacity verification. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . §§3.5.1–3.5.3, Eqs.(3.21)–(3.24), Fig.3.15, printed pp.87–90. PDF pp. 99–102 . Assumptions and limits: Application-robust per-layer rules, conducting thickness/width corrections and actual terminal current. mA/µm and A/cm² are distinct rule conventions. Via-temperature Eq.(3.25) requires the separate caveat in P22. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P20; source]: Width must meet the largest applicable current/manufacturing requirement; // pin landing regions and internal pin geometry need ampacity verification. // Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware // Integrated Circuit Design. §§3.5.1–3.5.3, Eqs.(3.21)–(3.24), Fig.3.15, printed pp.87–90. // Physical PDF pp. 99–102. // Applies when: Application-robust per-layer rules, conducting thickness/width corrections and // actual terminal current. mA/µm and A/cm² are distinct rule conventions. Via-temperature // Eq.(3.25) requires the separate caveat in P22. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P20 P21 · Via capacity depends on current sharing Source result. Via arrays at direction changes can overload inner cuts; equal cut count is not equal maximum-cut utilization. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . §4.6 Multiple Vias, especially §4.6.4 and Figs.4.25–4.29, printed pp.122–128. PDF pp. 134–140 . Assumptions and limits: Include both connected metal layers, contact resistances and reservoir/mechanical consequences. The source’s geometric equalization examples are conditional templates, not universal equal-sharing proofs. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P21; source]: Via arrays at direction changes can overload inner cuts; equal cut count // is not equal maximum-cut utilization. // Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware // Integrated Circuit Design. §4.6 Multiple Vias, especially §4.6.4 and Figs.4.25–4.29, printed // pp.122–128. Physical PDF pp. 134–140. // Applies when: Include both connected metal layers, contact resistances and reservoir/mechanical // consequences. The source’s geometric equalization examples are conditional templates, not // universal equal-sharing proofs. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P21 P22 · Correct fixed-lifetime temperature scaling Derived result. For unchanged Black parameters and target lifetime, j_allow(T)/j_allow(Tref)=exp[(Ea/(nk))(1/T−1/Tref)]. Required identical-via count grows as the inverse capacity factor. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . Derived from Eq.(3.8); compare printed Eqs.(3.25)–(3.26), printed pp.81,89. PDF p. 93 · PDF p. 101 . Assumptions and limits: Absolute Kelvin temperatures, positive Ea and n, unchanged geometry/failure mechanism. Printed via-count equations exhibit apparent inverse-factor inconsistency; no publisher erratum or foundry qualification was established. Derivation: Set C j_T^-n exp(Ea/kT)=C j_ref^-n exp(Ea/kTref), take logarithms, solve n ln(j_T/j_ref)=Ea/k(1/T−1/Tref). A smaller per-via allowable current requires more cuts, not fewer. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P22; derived]: For unchanged Black parameters and target lifetime, // j_allow(T)/j_allow(Tref)=exp[(Ea/(nk))(1/T−1/Tref)]. Required identical-via count grows as the // inverse capacity factor. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · // 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design. Derived from Eq.(3.8); // compare printed Eqs.(3.25)–(3.26), printed pp.81,89. Physical PDF pp. 93, 101. // Applies when: Absolute Kelvin temperatures, positive Ea and n, unchanged geometry/failure // mechanism. Printed via-count equations exhibit apparent inverse-factor inconsistency; no // publisher erratum or foundry qualification was established. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P22 P23 · Blech protection requires a physical stress/diffusion domain Source result. The one-dimensional balance gives |jL|crit=ΩΔσ/(e|Z*|ρ), subject to confinement, boundary and failure assumptions; void-growth saturation is a distinct criterion. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . §4.3, Eq.(4.1), discussion of Eq.(4.2); §4.5.1 neighboring segments, printed pp.105–108,111,115,118. PDF pp. 117–120 · PDF p. 123 · PDF p. 127 · PDF p. 130 . Assumptions and limits: Physical boundaries and material flow, not arbitrary route vertices. Simple isolated segment model; branched/reservoir structures and transients require additional analysis. A safe-looking steady state is not proof of a damage-free transition. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P23; source]: The one-dimensional balance gives |jL|crit=ΩΔσ/(e|Z*|ρ), subject to // confinement, boundary and failure assumptions; void-growth saturation is a distinct criterion. // Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware // Integrated Circuit Design. §4.3, Eq.(4.1), discussion of Eq.(4.2); §4.5.1 neighboring segments, // printed pp.105–108,111,115,118. Physical PDF pp. 117–120, 123, 127, 130. // Applies when: Physical boundaries and material flow, not arbitrary route vertices. Simple // isolated segment model; branched/reservoir structures and transients require additional // analysis. A safe-looking steady state is not proof of a damage-free transition. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P23 P24 · Half-space thermal spreading is a derived ideal model Derived result. For a surface point heater in a homogeneous isotropic semi-infinite body with an otherwise adiabatic surface and fixed far-field temperature, ΔT=P/(2πkr). Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . Fourier-law basis: §8.2.7 Thermal Gradients, Eq.(8.22), printed p.385. The half-space result is derived in the handbook, not printed as this equation in the book.. PDF p. 386 . Assumptions and limits: Constant k, steady state, hemispherical spreading, ideal point source, no finite die/package/layer effects. Singular near-source field requires finite-source treatment or labeled regularization. Derivation: Conservation on a hemisphere gives heat flux P/(2πr²). Fourier law gives dT/dr=−P/(2πkr²); integrate from r to infinity where T=T∞. Full unbounded space instead has 4π. The transient PDE adds local energy storage to the same flux balance. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P24; derived]: For a surface point heater in a homogeneous isotropic semi-infinite body // with an otherwise adiabatic surface and fixed far-field temperature, ΔT=P/(2πkr). // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 // catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. Fourier-law basis: §8.2.7 Thermal Gradients, // Eq.(8.22), printed p.385. The half-space result is derived in the handbook, not printed as this // equation in the book.. Physical PDF pp. 386. // Applies when: Constant k, steady state, hemispherical spreading, ideal point source, no finite // die/package/layer effects. Singular near-source field requires finite-source treatment or // labeled regularization. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P24 P25 · Centroid temperature needs four assumptions Source result. A device’s average temperature equals its active-area centroid temperature only under the stated active-area, equal-contribution, linear electrical-temperature and linear spatial-temperature assumptions. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §8.2.7 Centroids, followed by Eq.(8.23), printed pp.386–387. PDF pp. 387–388 . Assumptions and limits: Package and power conditions determine the field. Unequal electrical weighting, finite nonlinear fields or nonlinear device temperature dependence require more detailed evaluation. The handbook’s general Σs_i ΔT_i is a first-order sensitivity extension. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P25; source]: A device’s average temperature equals its active-area centroid temperature // only under the stated active-area, equal-contribution, linear electrical-temperature and linear // spatial-temperature assumptions. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.7 // Centroids, followed by Eq.(8.23), printed pp.386–387. Physical PDF pp. 387–388. // Applies when: Package and power conditions determine the field. Unequal electrical weighting, // finite nonlinear fields or nonlinear device temperature dependence require more detailed // evaluation. The handbook’s general Σs_i ΔT_i is a first-order sensitivity extension. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P25","url":"theory-provenance.html#physics-records"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P01 · Area and distance contributions to MOS pair mismatch","text":"P01 · Area and distance contributions to MOS pair mismatch Source result. For the characterized equal-device pair model, variance is approximated by A_P²/(WL)+S_P²D². Reference: Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988. Matching properties of MOS transistors . Analysis, Eq.(1), printed pp.327–328. PDF pp. 1–2 . Assumptions and limits: Many small additive local events with correlation distance much smaller than device dimensions, plus a fitted long-range component; process/parameter-specific coefficients. A refers to the pair-difference statistic here. A single-device coefficient requires its own convention conversion. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P01; source]: For the characterized equal-device pair model, variance is approximated by // A_P²/(WL)+S_P²D². // Source: Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988. Matching properties of MOS // transistors. Analysis, Eq.(1), printed pp.327–328. Physical PDF pp. 1–2. // Applies when: Many small additive local events with correlation distance much smaller than // device dimensions, plus a fitted long-range component; process/parameter-specific coefficients. // A refers to the pair-difference statistic here. A single-device coefficient requires its own // convention conversion. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P01","url":"theory-provenance.html#P01"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P02 · Edge variation prevents a universal area-only beta law","text":"P02 · Edge variation prevents a universal area-only beta law Source result. Current-factor variance contains width-edge, length-edge, mobility and oxide terms before an approximate A_beta²/(WL) reduction. Reference: Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988. Matching properties of MOS transistors . Matching of MOS transistors, Eq.(3), printed p.328. PDF p. 2 . Assumptions and limits: Area-dependent contribution only; same defined process parameters; authors say simplification becomes less valid as W and L decrease. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P02; source]: Current-factor variance contains width-edge, length-edge, mobility and // oxide terms before an approximate A_beta²/(WL) reduction. // Source: Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988. Matching properties of MOS // transistors. Matching of MOS transistors, Eq.(3), printed p.328. Physical PDF pp. 2. // Applies when: Area-dependent contribution only; same defined process parameters; authors say // simplification becomes less valid as W and L decrease. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P02","url":"theory-provenance.html#P02"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P03 · Orientation evidence is process-specific","text":"P03 · Orientation evidence is process-specific Source result. The studied 90-degree rotation affected current-factor mismatch and varied by wafer/batch; this experiment did not establish arbitrary transform equivalence. Reference: Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988. Matching properties of MOS transistors . Orientation discussion, Fig.3(d), printed pp.328–330. PDF pp. 2–4 . Assumptions and limits: 2.5µm n-well CMOS experiment; no universal rotational/reflection/source-drain symmetry rule for another process. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P03; source]: The studied 90-degree rotation affected current-factor mismatch and varied // by wafer/batch; this experiment did not establish arbitrary transform equivalence. // Source: Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988. Matching properties of MOS // transistors. Orientation discussion, Fig.3(d), printed pp.328–330. Physical PDF pp. 2–4. // Applies when: 2.5µm n-well CMOS experiment; no universal rotational/reflection/source-drain // symmetry rule for another process. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P03","url":"theory-provenance.html#P03"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P04 · Use independent physical causes in mismatch models","text":"P04 · Use independent physical causes in mismatch models Source result. Treating threshold and gain-factor mismatch as independent can double count an underlying oxide-thickness fluctuation; bias and geometry matter. Reference: Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design . Section II, Table I and discussion surrounding Eqs.(4)–(6), printed p.451. PDF p. 2 . Assumptions and limits: Do not apply this critique as a prohibition on all calibrated local empirical fits; inspect whether the statistical basis and correlations are already handled. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P04; source]: Treating threshold and gain-factor mismatch as independent can double // count an underlying oxide-thickness fluctuation; bias and geometry matter. // Source: Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for // analog design. Section II, Table I and discussion surrounding Eqs.(4)–(6), printed p.451. // Physical PDF pp. 2. // Applies when: Do not apply this critique as a prohibition on all calibrated local empirical // fits; inspect whether the statistical basis and correlations are already handled. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P04","url":"theory-provenance.html#P04"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P05 · Propagate mismatch with covariance and circuit sensitivity","text":"P05 · Propagate mismatch with covariance and circuit sensitivity Derived result. For a first-order expansion Δe≈JΔp, Var(e)≈J C Jᵀ; Drennan Eq.(8) is the independent-parameter special case. Reference: Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design . Section II, Eqs.(7)–(11), printed p.452. PDF p. 3 . Assumptions and limits: Small perturbations around the specified bias/geometry, valid compact model, and covariance C for the same physical variables; nonlinear tails need additional treatment. Derivation: Cov(JΔp)=J Cov(Δp) Jᵀ. If C is diagonal, this reduces to Σ_i (∂e/∂p_i)² σ_i², matching Eq.(8). Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P05; derived]: For a first-order expansion Δe≈JΔp, Var(e)≈J C Jᵀ; Drennan Eq.(8) is the // independent-parameter special case. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. // McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design. Section II, Eqs.(7)–(11), // printed p.452. Physical PDF pp. 3. // Applies when: Small perturbations around the specified bias/geometry, valid compact model, and // covariance C for the same physical variables; nonlinear tails need additional treatment. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P05","url":"theory-provenance.html#P05"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P06 · Fixed-current width can trade averaging against sensitivity","text":"P06 · Fixed-current width can trade averaging against sensitivity Derived result. In a long-channel saturation, threshold-only toy model at fixed current, relative current variance from threshold mismatch is proportional to 1/L² and independent of W. Reference: Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design . Eq.(9) and §III.A Geometry and Bias Interrelationship, printed pp.452,454. PDF p. 3 · PDF p. 5 . Assumptions and limits: This is an illustrative derivation, not a modern compact-model law; ignore mobility/edge/series/short-channel contributions and use the stated area coefficient. Recheck headroom. Derivation: σ²(ΔI/I)≈4A²/(WL Vov²), while Vov²=2IL/(μCox W); substitution gives 2A² μCox/(I L²). The source supports the more general observation that changing W changes both physical variation and sensitivity. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P06; derived]: In a long-channel saturation, threshold-only toy model at fixed current, // relative current variance from threshold mismatch is proportional to 1/L² and independent of W. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. // McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design. Eq.(9) and §III.A // Geometry and Bias Interrelationship, printed pp.452,454. Physical PDF pp. 3, 5. // Applies when: This is an illustrative derivation, not a modern compact-model law; ignore // mobility/edge/series/short-channel contributions and use the stated area coefficient. Recheck // headroom. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P06","url":"theory-provenance.html#P06"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P07 · Splitting preserves ideal total-area random mismatch","text":"P07 · Splitting preserves ideal total-area random mismatch Source result. Four independent 20µm-wide units can reproduce the ideal mismatch of one 80µm-wide device at the same length; there is no free fixed-total-width averaging gain. Reference: Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design . §III.C Multiple-Unit Devices, printed p.455. PDF p. 6 . Assumptions and limits: Width effects and altered electrical/parasitic context neglected; compare equal total width, not an array with one small unit. Circuit-role differences are separately shown in Table III. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P07; source]: Four independent 20µm-wide units can reproduce the ideal mismatch of one // 80µm-wide device at the same length; there is no free fixed-total-width averaging gain. // Source: Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for // analog design. §III.C Multiple-Unit Devices, printed p.455. Physical PDF pp. 6. // Applies when: Width effects and altered electrical/parasitic context neglected; compare equal // total width, not an array with one small unit. Circuit-role differences are separately shown in // Table III. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P07","url":"theory-provenance.html#P07"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P08 · Substrate coupling is a complete contact-network problem","text":"P08 · Substrate coupling is a complete contact-network problem Source result. Injected substrate current paths depend on injector, receiver, other contacts, doping profile and backplate potential; taps/wells may inject return noise. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . §1.1 and Chapter 2 §§2–3, printed pp.2,15–17. PDF p. 25 · PDF pp. 38–40 . Assumptions and limits: State injector mechanisms, reference connections and receiving impedance/body bias; a distance-only pair model omits third-contact loading. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P08; source]: Injected substrate current paths depend on injector, receiver, other // contacts, doping profile and backplate potential; taps/wells may inject return noise. // Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto // Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization // for IC Design. §1.1 and Chapter 2 §§2–3, printed pp.2,15–17. Physical PDF pp. 25, 38–40. // Applies when: State injector mechanisms, reference connections and receiving impedance/body // bias; a distance-only pair model omits third-contact loading. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P08","url":"theory-provenance.html#P08"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P09 · Electroquasistatic substrate equation and boundaries","text":"P09 · Electroquasistatic substrate equation and boundaries Source result. Conductive and displacement currents obey ∇·(σ∇φ)+∂t[∇·(ε∇φ)]=0; the resistive limit and explicit contact/edge/interface boundaries determine the model. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . Chapter 3 §2 Electromagnetic Formulation, Eqs.(3.1)–(3.3), printed pp.22–23. PDF pp. 45–46 . Assumptions and limits: Electroquasistatic continuum regime. Resistive approximation requires ε/σ small relative to times of interest; contact equipotential and insulating/grounded boundaries must match the physical setup. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P09; source]: Conductive and displacement currents obey ∇·(σ∇φ)+∂t[∇·(ε∇φ)]=0; the // resistive limit and explicit contact/edge/interface boundaries determine the model. // Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto // Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization // for IC Design. Chapter 3 §2 Electromagnetic Formulation, Eqs.(3.1)–(3.3), printed pp.22–23. // Physical PDF pp. 45–46. // Applies when: Electroquasistatic continuum regime. Resistive approximation requires ε/σ small // relative to times of interest; contact equipotential and insulating/grounded boundaries must // match the physical setup. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P09","url":"theory-provenance.html#P09"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P10 · Isolation includes loading and sign convention","text":"P10 · Isolation includes loading and sign convention Source result. The book defines contact isolation as V_receiver/V_injector; it depends on contact capacitance, backside impedance, frequency and receiver load, not only substrate resistance. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . Chapter 8 §2 Characterizing Isolation and §4 Effects of Load Impedance, printed pp.101–102,111–112. PDF pp. 124–125 · PDF pp. 134–135 . Assumptions and limits: Use a specified source/reference/loading convention. A negative-dB transfer and a positive attenuation magnitude have opposite reported signs. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P10; source]: The book defines contact isolation as V_receiver/V_injector; it depends on // contact capacitance, backside impedance, frequency and receiver load, not only substrate // resistance. // Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto // Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization // for IC Design. Chapter 8 §2 Characterizing Isolation and §4 Effects of Load Impedance, printed // pp.101–102,111–112. Physical PDF pp. 124–125, 134–135. // Applies when: Use a specified source/reference/loading convention. A negative-dB transfer and a // positive attenuation magnitude have opposite reported signs. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P10","url":"theory-provenance.html#P10"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P11 · Separation can saturate in low-resistivity substrates","text":"P11 · Separation can saturate in low-resistivity substrates Source result. Finite backplate impedance can make isolation approximately independent of further contact separation in the book’s low-resistivity examples. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . §8.2.1 Low-Resistivity Substrates, Figs.8.6–8.7; contrast §8.2.2, printed pp.102–107. PDF pp. 125–127 · PDF p. 130 . Assumptions and limits: Depends on epitaxial profile, area, frequency and backplate connection. Do not use its multiple-of-epitaxial-thickness example as a universal spacing rule. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P11; source]: Finite backplate impedance can make isolation approximately independent of // further contact separation in the book’s low-resistivity examples. // Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto // Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization // for IC Design. §8.2.1 Low-Resistivity Substrates, Figs.8.6–8.7; contrast §8.2.2, printed // pp.102–107. Physical PDF pp. 125–127, 130. // Applies when: Depends on epitaxial profile, area, frequency and backplate connection. Do not use // its multiple-of-epitaxial-thickness example as a universal spacing rule. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P11","url":"theory-provenance.html#P11"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P12 · Guard-ring return and observation reference matter","text":"P12 · Guard-ring return and observation reference matter Source result. A ring can worsen received noise through finite/noisy grounding; internal versus external signal reference and ring width/location change the result. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . §8.6.1 Grounding Schemes and §8.6.2 Optimization of Guard Ring Widths, Figs.8.16–8.23, printed pp.116–124. PDF pp. 139–140 · PDF pp. 142–147 . Assumptions and limits: The numerical studies use specific high-resistivity substrates and lumped package models. Compare actual reference voltage and include ring-return inductance and pickup. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P12; source]: A ring can worsen received noise through finite/noisy grounding; internal // versus external signal reference and ring width/location change the result. // Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto // Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization // for IC Design. §8.6.1 Grounding Schemes and §8.6.2 Optimization of Guard Ring Widths, // Figs.8.16–8.23, printed pp.116–124. Physical PDF pp. 139–140, 142–147. // Applies when: The numerical studies use specific high-resistivity substrates and lumped package // models. Compare actual reference voltage and include ring-return inductance and pickup. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P12","url":"theory-provenance.html#P12"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P13 · Buried shields have distinct mechanisms and limits","text":"P13 · Buried shields have distinct mechanisms and limits Source result. Conductive shields provide an alternate current return, dielectric isolation raises impedance, and buried opposite-type wells combine junction isolation with conductive shielding. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . §8.9 Buried Substrate Shields, Figs.8.27–8.30, printed pp.128–133. PDF pp. 151–156 . Assumptions and limits: The process must support the structure and bias. Conductive return impedance, finite shield resistivity, junction capacitance and frequency limit isolation. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P13; source]: Conductive shields provide an alternate current return, dielectric // isolation raises impedance, and buried opposite-type wells combine junction isolation with // conductive shielding. // Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto // Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization // for IC Design. §8.9 Buried Substrate Shields, Figs.8.27–8.30, printed pp.128–133. Physical PDF // pp. 151–156. // Applies when: The process must support the structure and bias. Conductive return impedance, // finite shield resistivity, junction capacitance and frequency limit isolation. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P13","url":"theory-provenance.html#P13"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P14 · Differential substrate error is transfer imbalance","text":"P14 · Differential substrate error is transfer imbalance Derived result. In a linear receiver difference, v_diff=(H1−H2)v_aggressor; equal common-mode transfer cancels only while the two complete paths and receiver responses match. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . §8.3 Improving Isolation Using Differential Circuits, Figs.8.9–8.10, printed pp.107–111. PDF pp. 130–134 . Assumptions and limits: Complex transfer functions with common reference. The source explicitly studies a 5% receiving-capacitor mismatch; phase, loading and circuit mismatch must remain included. Derivation: Subtract v1=H1 va and v2=H2 va. A 1% relative imbalance of a 0.01 transfer gives 10^-4 differential transfer only for the stated approximately equal-phase case. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P14; derived]: In a linear receiver difference, v_diff=(H1−H2)v_aggressor; equal // common-mode transfer cancels only while the two complete paths and receiver responses match. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, // Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. // Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design. §8.3 Improving Isolation Using // Differential Circuits, Figs.8.9–8.10, printed pp.107–111. Physical PDF pp. 130–134. // Applies when: Complex transfer functions with common reference. The source explicitly studies a // 5% receiving-capacitor mismatch; phase, loading and circuit mismatch must remain included. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P14","url":"theory-provenance.html#P14"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P15 · Local substrate-noise budgets need phase and sensitivity","text":"P15 · Local substrate-noise budgets need phase and sensitivity Source result. Local noise generators permit performance-to-parasitic budget generation; the PLL example shows jitter depends on the phase of injection relative to transitions. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . Chapter 5 §§1–3, Eqs.(5.1)–(5.5), §5.3 case study, printed pp.47–53,58. PDF pp. 70–73 · PDF pp. 75–76 · PDF p. 81 . Assumptions and limits: Local linearization or explicitly maximized sensitivity over a defined interval; source waveform/phase assumptions and circuit operating state must be retained. Shared budget allocation is not unique. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P15; source]: Local noise generators permit performance-to-parasitic budget generation; // the PLL example shows jitter depends on the phase of injection relative to transitions. // Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto // Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization // for IC Design. Chapter 5 §§1–3, Eqs.(5.1)–(5.5), §5.3 case study, printed pp.47–53,58. Physical // PDF pp. 70–73, 75–76, 81. // Applies when: Local linearization or explicitly maximized sensitivity over a defined interval; // source waveform/phase assumptions and circuit operating state must be retained. Shared budget // allocation is not unique. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P15","url":"theory-provenance.html#P15"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P16 · Migration damage is flux divergence and coupled driving forces","text":"P16 · Migration damage is flux divergence and coupled driving forces Source result. Electrical, thermal and stress driving forces produce atomic flux; accumulation/depletion and coupled heating/stress determine vulnerable regions. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . §2.2 and §2.5, especially Eqs.(2.15)–(2.18), printed pp.18,35–43. PDF p. 30 · PDF pp. 47–48 · PDF pp. 52–55 . Assumptions and limits: Material paths, interface boundary conditions, temperature and mechanical state matter; average current density alone is not a damage-location model. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P16; source]: Electrical, thermal and stress driving forces produce atomic flux; // accumulation/depletion and coupled heating/stress determine vulnerable regions. // Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware // Integrated Circuit Design. §2.2 and §2.5, especially Eqs.(2.15)–(2.18), printed pp.18,35–43. // Physical PDF pp. 30, 47–48, 52–55. // Applies when: Material paths, interface boundary conditions, temperature and mechanical state // matter; average current density alone is not a damage-location model. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P16","url":"theory-provenance.html#P16"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P17 · Black law describes a calibrated median","text":"P17 · Black law describes a calibrated median Source result. t50=C j^-n exp(Ea/kT) is a generalized empirical median-lifetime relation; a product reliability target also needs mission profile and failure statistics. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . Eq.(2.1), generalized Eq.(3.8), §§3.4.1–3.4.2, printed pp.18–19,79–86. PDF pp. 30–31 · PDF pp. 91–98 . Assumptions and limits: Same characterized process, geometry, mechanism, temperature/current range and failure criterion. Median is not low-failure-probability lifetime of every segment; do not extrapolate across mechanism changes. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P17; source]: t50=C j^-n exp(Ea/kT) is a generalized empirical median-lifetime relation; // a product reliability target also needs mission profile and failure statistics. // Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware // Integrated Circuit Design. Eq.(2.1), generalized Eq.(3.8), §§3.4.1–3.4.2, printed // pp.18–19,79–86. Physical PDF pp. 30–31, 91–98. // Applies when: Same characterized process, geometry, mechanism, temperature/current range and // failure criterion. Median is not low-failure-probability lifetime of every segment; do not // extrapolate across mechanism changes. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P17","url":"theory-provenance.html#P17"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P18 · Current metrics are not interchangeable","text":"P18 · Current metrics are not interchangeable Source result. RMS, effective/directional average and peak currents correspond to different mechanisms; all applicable dimensioning limits must be considered. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . §2.4.3 and §3.3.1 Current Types, printed pp.29–32,70–71. PDF pp. 41–44 · PDF pp. 82–83 . Assumptions and limits: RMS heating assumes resistance treated consistently; self-healing depends on characterized waveform/frequency/material behavior. Zero signed average does not imply zero damage. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P18; source]: RMS, effective/directional average and peak currents correspond to // different mechanisms; all applicable dimensioning limits must be considered. // Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware // Integrated Circuit Design. §2.4.3 and §3.3.1 Current Types, printed pp.29–32,70–71. Physical PDF // pp. 41–44, 82–83. // Applies when: RMS heating assumes resistance treated consistently; self-healing depends on // characterized waveform/frequency/material behavior. Zero signed average does not imply zero // damage. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P18","url":"theory-provenance.html#P18"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P19 · Topology and current-aware wire area","text":"P19 · Topology and current-aware wire area Source result. Signed terminal-current bounds/scenarios determine tree segment demand; current-driven planning can minimize required interconnect area rather than only length. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . §§3.3.2–3.3.3, Eqs.(3.1)–(3.7), Figs.3.4–3.10, printed pp.71–78. PDF pp. 83–90 . Assumptions and limits: For transient cut sums include relevant interconnect shunt currents; retain mode/correlation assumptions. Meshes require a network current-sharing solution. The handbook’s exact waveform sum is a KCL expression, distinct from the book’s equivalent-current bounds. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P19; source]: Signed terminal-current bounds/scenarios determine tree segment demand; // current-driven planning can minimize required interconnect area rather than only length. // Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware // Integrated Circuit Design. §§3.3.2–3.3.3, Eqs.(3.1)–(3.7), Figs.3.4–3.10, printed pp.71–78. // Physical PDF pp. 83–90. // Applies when: For transient cut sums include relevant interconnect shunt currents; retain // mode/correlation assumptions. Meshes require a network current-sharing solution. The handbook’s // exact waveform sum is a KCL expression, distinct from the book’s equivalent-current bounds. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P19","url":"theory-provenance.html#P19"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P20 · The metal path includes terminals and manufactured cross-section","text":"P20 · The metal path includes terminals and manufactured cross-section Source result. Width must meet the largest applicable current/manufacturing requirement; pin landing regions and internal pin geometry need ampacity verification. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . §§3.5.1–3.5.3, Eqs.(3.21)–(3.24), Fig.3.15, printed pp.87–90. PDF pp. 99–102 . Assumptions and limits: Application-robust per-layer rules, conducting thickness/width corrections and actual terminal current. mA/µm and A/cm² are distinct rule conventions. Via-temperature Eq.(3.25) requires the separate caveat in P22. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P20; source]: Width must meet the largest applicable current/manufacturing requirement; // pin landing regions and internal pin geometry need ampacity verification. // Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware // Integrated Circuit Design. §§3.5.1–3.5.3, Eqs.(3.21)–(3.24), Fig.3.15, printed pp.87–90. // Physical PDF pp. 99–102. // Applies when: Application-robust per-layer rules, conducting thickness/width corrections and // actual terminal current. mA/µm and A/cm² are distinct rule conventions. Via-temperature // Eq.(3.25) requires the separate caveat in P22. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P20","url":"theory-provenance.html#P20"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P21 · Via capacity depends on current sharing","text":"P21 · Via capacity depends on current sharing Source result. Via arrays at direction changes can overload inner cuts; equal cut count is not equal maximum-cut utilization. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . §4.6 Multiple Vias, especially §4.6.4 and Figs.4.25–4.29, printed pp.122–128. PDF pp. 134–140 . Assumptions and limits: Include both connected metal layers, contact resistances and reservoir/mechanical consequences. The source’s geometric equalization examples are conditional templates, not universal equal-sharing proofs. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P21; source]: Via arrays at direction changes can overload inner cuts; equal cut count // is not equal maximum-cut utilization. // Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware // Integrated Circuit Design. §4.6 Multiple Vias, especially §4.6.4 and Figs.4.25–4.29, printed // pp.122–128. Physical PDF pp. 134–140. // Applies when: Include both connected metal layers, contact resistances and reservoir/mechanical // consequences. The source’s geometric equalization examples are conditional templates, not // universal equal-sharing proofs. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P21","url":"theory-provenance.html#P21"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P22 · Correct fixed-lifetime temperature scaling","text":"P22 · Correct fixed-lifetime temperature scaling Derived result. For unchanged Black parameters and target lifetime, j_allow(T)/j_allow(Tref)=exp[(Ea/(nk))(1/T−1/Tref)]. Required identical-via count grows as the inverse capacity factor. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . Derived from Eq.(3.8); compare printed Eqs.(3.25)–(3.26), printed pp.81,89. PDF p. 93 · PDF p. 101 . Assumptions and limits: Absolute Kelvin temperatures, positive Ea and n, unchanged geometry/failure mechanism. Printed via-count equations exhibit apparent inverse-factor inconsistency; no publisher erratum or foundry qualification was established. Derivation: Set C j_T^-n exp(Ea/kT)=C j_ref^-n exp(Ea/kTref), take logarithms, solve n ln(j_T/j_ref)=Ea/k(1/T−1/Tref). A smaller per-via allowable current requires more cuts, not fewer. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P22; derived]: For unchanged Black parameters and target lifetime, // j_allow(T)/j_allow(Tref)=exp[(Ea/(nk))(1/T−1/Tref)]. Required identical-via count grows as the // inverse capacity factor. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · // 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design. Derived from Eq.(3.8); // compare printed Eqs.(3.25)–(3.26), printed pp.81,89. Physical PDF pp. 93, 101. // Applies when: Absolute Kelvin temperatures, positive Ea and n, unchanged geometry/failure // mechanism. Printed via-count equations exhibit apparent inverse-factor inconsistency; no // publisher erratum or foundry qualification was established. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P22","url":"theory-provenance.html#P22"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P23 · Blech protection requires a physical stress/diffusion domain","text":"P23 · Blech protection requires a physical stress/diffusion domain Source result. The one-dimensional balance gives |jL|crit=ΩΔσ/(e|Z*|ρ), subject to confinement, boundary and failure assumptions; void-growth saturation is a distinct criterion. Reference: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design . §4.3, Eq.(4.1), discussion of Eq.(4.2); §4.5.1 neighboring segments, printed pp.105–108,111,115,118. PDF pp. 117–120 · PDF p. 123 · PDF p. 127 · PDF p. 130 . Assumptions and limits: Physical boundaries and material flow, not arbitrary route vertices. Simple isolated segment model; branched/reservoir structures and transients require additional analysis. A safe-looking steady state is not proof of a damage-free transition. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P23; source]: The one-dimensional balance gives |jL|crit=ΩΔσ/(e|Z*|ρ), subject to // confinement, boundary and failure assumptions; void-growth saturation is a distinct criterion. // Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware // Integrated Circuit Design. §4.3, Eq.(4.1), discussion of Eq.(4.2); §4.5.1 neighboring segments, // printed pp.105–108,111,115,118. Physical PDF pp. 117–120, 123, 127, 130. // Applies when: Physical boundaries and material flow, not arbitrary route vertices. Simple // isolated segment model; branched/reservoir structures and transients require additional // analysis. A safe-looking steady state is not proof of a damage-free transition. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P23","url":"theory-provenance.html#P23"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P24 · Half-space thermal spreading is a derived ideal model","text":"P24 · Half-space thermal spreading is a derived ideal model Derived result. For a surface point heater in a homogeneous isotropic semi-infinite body with an otherwise adiabatic surface and fixed far-field temperature, ΔT=P/(2πkr). Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . Fourier-law basis: §8.2.7 Thermal Gradients, Eq.(8.22), printed p.385. The half-space result is derived in the handbook, not printed as this equation in the book.. PDF p. 386 . Assumptions and limits: Constant k, steady state, hemispherical spreading, ideal point source, no finite die/package/layer effects. Singular near-source field requires finite-source treatment or labeled regularization. Derivation: Conservation on a hemisphere gives heat flux P/(2πr²). Fourier law gives dT/dr=−P/(2πkr²); integrate from r to infinity where T=T∞. Full unbounded space instead has 4π. The transient PDE adds local energy storage to the same flux balance. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P24; derived]: For a surface point heater in a homogeneous isotropic semi-infinite body // with an otherwise adiabatic surface and fixed far-field temperature, ΔT=P/(2πkr). // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 // catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. Fourier-law basis: §8.2.7 Thermal Gradients, // Eq.(8.22), printed p.385. The half-space result is derived in the handbook, not printed as this // equation in the book.. Physical PDF pp. 386. // Applies when: Constant k, steady state, hemispherical spreading, ideal point source, no finite // die/package/layer effects. Singular near-source field requires finite-source treatment or // labeled regularization. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P24","url":"theory-provenance.html#P24"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · P25 · Centroid temperature needs four assumptions","text":"P25 · Centroid temperature needs four assumptions Source result. A device’s average temperature equals its active-area centroid temperature only under the stated active-area, equal-contribution, linear electrical-temperature and linear spatial-temperature assumptions. Reference: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout . §8.2.7 Centroids, followed by Eq.(8.23), printed pp.386–387. PDF pp. 387–388 . Assumptions and limits: Package and power conditions determine the field. Unequal electrical weighting, finite nonlinear fields or nonlinear device temperature dependence require more detailed evaluation. The handbook’s general Σs_i ΔT_i is a first-order sensitivity extension. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [P25; source]: A device’s average temperature equals its active-area centroid temperature // only under the stated active-area, equal-contribution, linear electrical-temperature and linear // spatial-temperature assumptions. // Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.7 // Centroids, followed by Eq.(8.23), printed pp.386–387. Physical PDF pp. 387–388. // Applies when: Package and power conditions determine the field. Unequal electrical weighting, // finite nonlinear fields or nonlinear device temperature dependence require more detailed // evaluation. The handbook’s general Σs_i ΔT_i is a first-order sensitivity extension. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P25","url":"theory-provenance.html#P25"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Matching arrays and performance-driven research","text":"Matching arrays and performance-driven research Q01 · First moments and first-order performance Source result. Coincident normalized x/y centroids remove linear spatial parameter mismatch; translating parameter mismatch into performance uses a separate sensitivity approximation. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead . Eqs.(1)–(5), printed p.114. PDF p. 1 . Assumptions and limits: Equal-unit or explicitly normalized additive responses; shared parameter field; circuit linearization valid locally. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q01; source]: Coincident normalized x/y centroids remove linear spatial parameter // mismatch; translating parameter mismatch into performance uses a separate sensitivity // approximation. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and // Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. Eqs.(1)–(5), printed p.114. Physical PDF pp. 1. // Applies when: Equal-unit or explicitly normalized additive responses; shared parameter field; // circuit linearization valid locally. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q01 Q02 · Centroid does not equal device environment Source result. WPE, LOD, OD dimensions and gate pitch can shift device parameters even when centroids coincide. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead . §II-A2/Fig.2 and §V-A/Fig.9, printed pp.115,119. PDF p. 2 · PDF p. 6 . Assumptions and limits: Relevant process/LDE model; review’s identical-unit simplifications presume its row, pitch and diffusion-sharing construction. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q02; source]: WPE, LOD, OD dimensions and gate pitch can shift device parameters even // when centroids coincide. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and // Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. §II-A2/Fig.2 and §V-A/Fig.9, printed pp.115,119. // Physical PDF pp. 2, 6. // Applies when: Relevant process/LDE model; review’s identical-unit simplifications presume its // row, pitch and diffusion-sharing construction. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q02 Q03 · Pattern choice is conditional Source result. The OTA/comparator cases show circumstances in which clustered/interdigitated patterns outperform some CC structures. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead . §V, Figs.9–10, printed pp.119–120. PDF pp. 6–7 . Assumptions and limits: Reported device/circuit/process assumptions; not a universal CC prohibition or a portable size threshold. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q03; source]: The OTA/comparator cases show circumstances in which // clustered/interdigitated patterns outperform some CC structures. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and // Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. §V, Figs.9–10, printed pp.119–120. Physical PDF // pp. 6–7. // Applies when: Reported device/circuit/process assumptions; not a universal CC prohibition or a // portable size threshold. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q03 Q04 · MOS internal topology and source paths Source result. Half-diffusion graphs/Euler paths express compatible sharing; source-current distribution and route resistance affect array transconductance. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead . §IV; §V-A Eq.(25)/Fig.9, printed pp.118–120. PDF pp. 5–7 . Assumptions and limits: Keep actual net labels and unit paths; generalized routing acceptance is a handbook adaptation. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q04; source]: Half-diffusion graphs/Euler paths express compatible sharing; // source-current distribution and route resistance affect array transconductance. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and // Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. §IV; §V-A Eq.(25)/Fig.9, printed pp.118–120. // Physical PDF pp. 5–7. // Applies when: Keep actual net labels and unit paths; generalized routing acceptance is a // handbook adaptation. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q04 Q05 · MOM unit construction exposes different parasitics Source result. MOM finger/sandwich/pillar/mortise-tenon constructions trade density and terminal coupling. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead . §III-B, Fig.5, printed p.117. PDF p. 4 . Assumptions and limits: A source-specific advanced-node comparison; selecting a qualified PDK primitive still requires its own model. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q05; source]: MOM finger/sandwich/pillar/mortise-tenon constructions trade density and // terminal coupling. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and // Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. §III-B, Fig.5, printed p.117. Physical PDF pp. 4. // Applies when: A source-specific advanced-node comparison; selecting a qualified PDK primitive // still requires its own model. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q05 Q06 · Polynomial additive-unit model Source result. Under an additive identical-unit parameter model, equal spatial moments through n cancel every polynomial component of degree at most n. Reference: Xin Dai, Chengming He, Hanqing Xing, Degang Chen and Randall Geiger · ISCAS 2005. An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation . §II Eqs.(4)–(6); §III Eqs.(15)–(23), printed pp.4836–4837. PDF pp. 2–3 . Assumptions and limits: Two identically designed devices; parameter additivity; unit center approximates area average, or finite-footprint equivalence is independently justified. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q06; source]: Under an additive identical-unit parameter model, equal spatial moments // through n cancel every polynomial component of degree at most n. // Source: Xin Dai, Chengming He, Hanqing Xing, Degang Chen and Randall Geiger · ISCAS 2005. An // Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation. §II // Eqs.(4)–(6); §III Eqs.(15)–(23), printed pp.4836–4837. Physical PDF pp. 2–3. // Applies when: Two identically designed devices; parameter additivity; unit center approximates // area average, or finite-footprint equivalence is independently justified. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q06 Q07 · Recursive central-symmetry parity Source result. Reflect the prior pattern, retaining labels for odd target order and exchanging labels for even target order; the specified base leads to 2^n units per device. Reference: Xin Dai, Chengming He, Hanqing Xing, Degang Chen and Randall Geiger · ISCAS 2005. An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation . §III recursive construction and induction, printed pp.4836–4837. PDF pp. 2–3 . Assumptions and limits: First-order base has two units per device; legal disjoint placement; not a minimal-count or circuit-performance theorem. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q07; source]: Reflect the prior pattern, retaining labels for odd target order and // exchanging labels for even target order; the specified base leads to 2^n units per device. // Source: Xin Dai, Chengming He, Hanqing Xing, Degang Chen and Randall Geiger · ISCAS 2005. An // Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation. §III // recursive construction and induction, printed pp.4836–4837. Physical PDF pp. 2–3. // Applies when: First-order base has two units per device; legal disjoint placement; not a // minimal-count or circuit-performance theorem. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q07 Q08 · DAC plate identities and parasitic classes Source result. Binary and split DAC networks respond differently to top/substrate, top/bottom and bottom/substrate parasitics; split banks retain two top-plate nodes. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . §II-A–B, Figs.1–2, printed pp.2783–2784. PDF pp. 2–3 . Assumptions and limits: Specified charge-scaling architecture and ideal static excitation conditions; dynamic reference/switch effects require extensions. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q08; source]: Binary and split DAC networks respond differently to top/substrate, // top/bottom and bottom/substrate parasitics; split banks retain two top-plate nodes. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §II-A–B, // Figs.1–2, printed pp.2783–2784. Physical PDF pp. 2–3. // Applies when: Specified charge-scaling architecture and ideal static excitation conditions; // dynamic reference/switch effects require extensions. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q08 Q09 · Constructive DAC families and odd counts Source result. Spiral/chessboard/block choices trade connected components against dispersion; the two one-unit members are deliberately only near the common center. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . §IV-A and §IV-C1, printed pp.2788–2790. PDF pp. 7–9 . Assumptions and limits: Identical nonoverlapping grid units; preserve exact electrical counts and quantify CC exceptions. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q09; source]: Spiral/chessboard/block choices trade connected components against // dispersion; the two one-unit members are deliberately only near the common center. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §IV-A and // §IV-C1, printed pp.2788–2790. Physical PDF pp. 7–9. // Applies when: Identical nonoverlapping grid units; preserve exact electrical counts and quantify // CC exceptions. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q09 Q10 · DAC routing construction Source result. Same-bit components are joined by branch/trunk/bridge routes; top plates use graph/MST routing, separately for split-bank nodes. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . §IV-B–C, printed pp.2789–2791. PDF pp. 8–10 . Assumptions and limits: Specified layer/pin/array model; MST wire cost does not prove minimum electrical delay. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q10; source]: Same-bit components are joined by branch/trunk/bridge routes; top plates // use graph/MST routing, separately for split-bank nodes. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §IV-B–C, printed // pp.2789–2791. Physical PDF pp. 8–10. // Applies when: Specified layer/pin/array model; MST wire cost does not prove minimum electrical // delay. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q10 Q11 · DAC INL and DNL definitions Source result. DNL evaluates adjacent output steps relative to one ideal LSB; INL evaluates deviation from the declared ideal transfer. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . §III, Eqs.(17)–(18), printed p.2785. PDF p. 4 . Assumptions and limits: Record reference convention, code endpoints and normalization; endpoint or best-fit corrections change the metric. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q11; source]: DNL evaluates adjacent output steps relative to one ideal LSB; INL // evaluates deviation from the declared ideal transfer. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §III, // Eqs.(17)–(18), printed p.2785. Physical PDF pp. 4. // Applies when: Record reference convention, code endpoints and normalization; endpoint or // best-fit corrections change the metric. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q11 Q12 · Paper-specific DAC frequency Source result. The paper’s Eq.(32) defines f=1/[2(N+2)ln(2)τ] from its charging/discharging model. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . §III-C, Eq.(32), printed p.2786. PDF p. 5 . Assumptions and limits: τ is the worst modeled charging-path delay. This N-dependent settling measure must not be equated to 1/(2πτ). Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q12; source]: The paper’s Eq.(32) defines f=1/[2(N+2)ln(2)τ] from its // charging/discharging model. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §III-C, Eq.(32), // printed p.2786. Physical PDF pp. 5. // Applies when: τ is the worst modeled charging-path delay. This N-dependent settling measure must // not be equated to 1/(2πτ). // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q12 Q13 · MOM bridge edge sensitivity Source result. For a common small edge displacement δ, the specified MOM geometry gives ΔC/C≈2δ(1/H+1/l), enabling joint nominal-C and fractional-sensitivity matching. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . §III-D, Eqs.(39)–(45), printed p.2787. PDF p. 6 . Assumptions and limits: H and l are in-plane dimensions; common small perturbation, continuous geometry, and simplified coupling model. Quantization/fringing require extraction. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q13; source]: For a common small edge displacement δ, the specified MOM geometry gives // ΔC/C≈2δ(1/H+1/l), enabling joint nominal-C and fractional-sensitivity matching. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §III-D, // Eqs.(39)–(45), printed p.2787. Physical PDF pp. 6. // Applies when: H and l are in-plane dimensions; common small perturbation, continuous geometry, // and simplified coupling model. Quantization/fringing require extraction. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q13 Q14 · Do not promote illustrative correlation to process data Source result. The DAC evaluation’s gradient/correlation constants are inherited assumptions, not a measured capacitor statistical characterization. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . §V setup and parameter discussion, printed pp.2791,2793. PDF p. 10 · PDF p. 12 . Assumptions and limits: Yield/correlation claims require qualified process data; parameter sweeps study conditional behavior only. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q14; source]: The DAC evaluation’s gradient/correlation constants are inherited // assumptions, not a measured capacitor statistical characterization. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §V setup and // parameter discussion, printed pp.2791,2793. Physical PDF pp. 10, 12. // Applies when: Yield/correlation claims require qualified process data; parameter sweeps study // conditional behavior only. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q14 Q15 · Widening shifts the critical bit Source result. Parallel critical-bit routes can improve delay but change the bottleneck and eventually have diminishing returns. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . Tables I–II; Fig.15/discussion, printed pp.2792–2793. PDF pp. 11–12 . Assumptions and limits: Re-evaluate all bits with changed capacitance, vias and routing capacity; numerical gains belong to that setup. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q15; source]: Parallel critical-bit routes can improve delay but change the bottleneck // and eventually have diminishing returns. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. Tables I–II; // Fig.15/discussion, printed pp.2792–2793. Physical PDF pp. 11–12. // Applies when: Re-evaluate all bits with changed capacitance, vias and routing capacity; // numerical gains belong to that setup. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q15 Q16 · Electrical feedback closes a proxy gap Source result. Geometric constraints and scalar physical objectives do not generally guarantee post-layout circuit performance. Reference: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions . §I–II, Fig.1, printed pp.679–681. PDF pp. 1–3 . Assumptions and limits: Contract and testbench determine required metrics. An extracted/simulated result is conditional on model coverage. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q16; source]: Geometric constraints and scalar physical objectives do not generally // guarantee post-layout circuit performance. // Source: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven // Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions. §I–II, Fig.1, printed // pp.679–681. Physical PDF pp. 1–3. // Applies when: Contract and testbench determine required metrics. An extracted/simulated result // is conditional on model coverage. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q16 Q17 · Model cost needs consistent denominators Source result. The survey’s text and Fig.2 have inconsistent runtime fractions; only the qualitative data-acquisition-dominance lesson is safely reused. Reference: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions . §III-A; Fig.2, printed pp.681,683. PDF p. 3 · PDF p. 5 . Assumptions and limits: Apparent internal inconsistency, visually checked; not a publisher-verified erratum or an engine runtime budget. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q17; source]: The survey’s text and Fig.2 have inconsistent runtime fractions; only the // qualitative data-acquisition-dominance lesson is safely reused. // Source: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven // Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions. §III-A; Fig.2, printed // pp.681,683. Physical PDF pp. 3, 5. // Applies when: Apparent internal inconsistency, visually checked; not a publisher-verified // erratum or an engine runtime budget. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q17 Q18 · Transfer accuracy is metric/domain dependent Source result. The transfer example behaves differently for bandwidth, CMRR and noise across sizing/topology changes. Reference: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions . §III-B, Table I, printed p.682. PDF p. 4 . Assumptions and limits: Reported circuits and train/test domains; no generalization guarantee beyond them. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q18; source]: The transfer example behaves differently for bandwidth, CMRR and noise // across sizing/topology changes. // Source: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven // Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions. §III-B, Table I, printed p.682. // Physical PDF pp. 4. // Applies when: Reported circuits and train/test domains; no generalization guarantee beyond them. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q18 Q19 · Retain competing objectives Source result. The MOBO/weighted-BO comparison motivates considering tradeoffs among circuit metrics. Reference: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions . §III-C and related figures, printed pp.682–683. PDF pp. 4–5 . Assumptions and limits: Reported case study. A feasible Pareto archive is our software policy and is not a global-frontier proof. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q19; source]: The MOBO/weighted-BO comparison motivates considering tradeoffs among // circuit metrics. // Source: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven // Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions. §III-C and related figures, // printed pp.682–683. Physical PDF pp. 4–5. // Applies when: Reported case study. A feasible Pareto archive is our software policy and is not a // global-frontier proof. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q19 Q20 · Discrete per-net wire sizing Source result. The manuscript assigns integer width choices per net after global routing; symmetric nets receive equal width choices. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . §§2–3, Eqs.(1)–(3), supplied manuscript pp.5–6. PDF pp. 5–6 . Assumptions and limits: Metrics in normalized ratio scores must have meaningful positive values; final feasibility remains separate from weighted FOM. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q20; source]: The manuscript assigns integer width choices per net after global routing; // symmetric nets receive equal width choices. // Source: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh // Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire // Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits. §§2–3, Eqs.(1)–(3), supplied manuscript pp.5–6. Physical // PDF pp. 5–6. // Applies when: Metrics in normalized ratio scores must have meaningful positive values; final // feasibility remains separate from weighted FOM. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q20 Q21 · WAGN physical graph features Source result. Pins and route Steiner nodes carry device/role/geometry features; edges include distance, via count, widths, pin dimensions and terminal types. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . §4.2, supplied manuscript p.8. PDF p. 8 . Assumptions and limits: These are the reported model inputs, not proof that any particular graph network is needed for this engine. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q21; source]: Pins and route Steiner nodes carry device/role/geometry features; edges // include distance, via count, widths, pin dimensions and terminal types. // Source: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh // Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire // Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits. §4.2, supplied manuscript p.8. Physical PDF pp. 8. // Applies when: These are the reported model inputs, not proof that any particular graph network // is needed for this engine. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q21 Q22 · WAGN output is a threshold classifier Source result. The model output is denoted P(FOM<T) at one specified threshold and trained with binary labels/cross entropy. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . §4.3–4.5, Eqs.(17)–(19), supplied manuscript pp.10–11. PDF pp. 10–11 . Assumptions and limits: Not a full CDF, metric regression or established calibration/yield model; observed classification accuracy does not supply those guarantees. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q22; source]: The model output is denoted P(FOM<T) at one specified threshold and // trained with binary labels/cross entropy. // Source: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh // Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire // Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits. §4.3–4.5, Eqs.(17)–(19), supplied manuscript pp.10–11. // Physical PDF pp. 10–11. // Applies when: Not a full CDF, metric regression or established calibration/yield model; observed // classification accuracy does not supply those guarantees. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q22 Q23 · Simulated shortlist after surrogate search Source result. Relax/search widths through fixed WAGN, discretize, and simulate the top shortlist before final selection. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . §5, Eqs.(20)–(21), Algorithm1, supplied manuscript pp.12–14. PDF pp. 12–14 . Assumptions and limits: Multistart/local/BO methods are heuristic; finite penalty, rounding and route fallback require actual feasibility checks. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q23; source]: Relax/search widths through fixed WAGN, discretize, and simulate the top // shortlist before final selection. // Source: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh // Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire // Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits. §5, Eqs.(20)–(21), Algorithm1, supplied manuscript // pp.12–14. Physical PDF pp. 12–14. // Applies when: Multistart/local/BO methods are heuristic; finite penalty, rounding and route // fallback require actual feasibility checks. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q23 Q24 · Parallel routes implement width options Source result. The experiments implement 1×, 2× and 3× width choices using parallel routes. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . §6, Fig.8, supplied manuscript pp.15–16. PDF pp. 15–16 . Assumptions and limits: The realized route graph may differ from a single wide conductor; routability and access remain conditional. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q24; source]: The experiments implement 1×, 2× and 3× width choices using parallel // routes. // Source: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh // Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire // Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits. §6, Fig.8, supplied manuscript pp.15–16. Physical PDF pp. // 15–16. // Applies when: The realized route graph may differ from a single wide conductor; routability and // access remain conditional. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q24 Q25 · Wider can improve one metric and hurt another Source result. Table7’s CM-OTA 1×→3× change raises UGF 451.0→511.4 MHz while reducing gain 33.1→31.1 dB and FOM 0.92→0.90. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . Table7, supplied manuscript p.19. PDF p. 19 . Assumptions and limits: One reported circuit/setup; do not reuse the numbers as a target-process forecast. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q25; source]: Table7’s CM-OTA 1×→3× change raises UGF 451.0→511.4 MHz while reducing // gain 33.1→31.1 dB and FOM 0.92→0.90. // Source: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh // Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire // Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits. Table7, supplied manuscript p.19. Physical PDF pp. 19. // Applies when: One reported circuit/setup; do not reuse the numbers as a target-process forecast. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q25 Q26 · WAGN evidence and stated limits Source result. Runtime/gain claims compare specified BO budgets; the paper limits cross-family transfer, large circuits, process variation and classification-only prediction. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . §6.2/Table6; §7, supplied manuscript pp.17–19,23. PDF pp. 17–19 · PDF p. 23 . Assumptions and limits: Training-data cost, parallel hardware and circuit family must accompany comparisons; no experiments reproduced here. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q26; source]: Runtime/gain claims compare specified BO budgets; the paper limits // cross-family transfer, large circuits, process variation and classification-only prediction. // Source: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh // Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire // Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits. §6.2/Table6; §7, supplied manuscript pp.17–19,23. // Physical PDF pp. 17–19, 23. // Applies when: Training-data cost, parallel hardware and circuit family must accompany // comparisons; no experiments reproduced here. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q26","url":"theory-provenance.html#paper-records"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q01 · First moments and first-order performance","text":"Q01 · First moments and first-order performance Source result. Coincident normalized x/y centroids remove linear spatial parameter mismatch; translating parameter mismatch into performance uses a separate sensitivity approximation. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead . Eqs.(1)–(5), printed p.114. PDF p. 1 . Assumptions and limits: Equal-unit or explicitly normalized additive responses; shared parameter field; circuit linearization valid locally. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q01; source]: Coincident normalized x/y centroids remove linear spatial parameter // mismatch; translating parameter mismatch into performance uses a separate sensitivity // approximation. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and // Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. Eqs.(1)–(5), printed p.114. Physical PDF pp. 1. // Applies when: Equal-unit or explicitly normalized additive responses; shared parameter field; // circuit linearization valid locally. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q01","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q01"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q02 · Centroid does not equal device environment","text":"Q02 · Centroid does not equal device environment Source result. WPE, LOD, OD dimensions and gate pitch can shift device parameters even when centroids coincide. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead . §II-A2/Fig.2 and §V-A/Fig.9, printed pp.115,119. PDF p. 2 · PDF p. 6 . Assumptions and limits: Relevant process/LDE model; review’s identical-unit simplifications presume its row, pitch and diffusion-sharing construction. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q02; source]: WPE, LOD, OD dimensions and gate pitch can shift device parameters even // when centroids coincide. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and // Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. §II-A2/Fig.2 and §V-A/Fig.9, printed pp.115,119. // Physical PDF pp. 2, 6. // Applies when: Relevant process/LDE model; review’s identical-unit simplifications presume its // row, pitch and diffusion-sharing construction. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q02","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q02"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q03 · Pattern choice is conditional","text":"Q03 · Pattern choice is conditional Source result. The OTA/comparator cases show circumstances in which clustered/interdigitated patterns outperform some CC structures. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead . §V, Figs.9–10, printed pp.119–120. PDF pp. 6–7 . Assumptions and limits: Reported device/circuit/process assumptions; not a universal CC prohibition or a portable size threshold. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q03; source]: The OTA/comparator cases show circumstances in which // clustered/interdigitated patterns outperform some CC structures. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and // Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. §V, Figs.9–10, printed pp.119–120. Physical PDF // pp. 6–7. // Applies when: Reported device/circuit/process assumptions; not a universal CC prohibition or a // portable size threshold. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q03","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q03"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q04 · MOS internal topology and source paths","text":"Q04 · MOS internal topology and source paths Source result. Half-diffusion graphs/Euler paths express compatible sharing; source-current distribution and route resistance affect array transconductance. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead . §IV; §V-A Eq.(25)/Fig.9, printed pp.118–120. PDF pp. 5–7 . Assumptions and limits: Keep actual net labels and unit paths; generalized routing acceptance is a handbook adaptation. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q04; source]: Half-diffusion graphs/Euler paths express compatible sharing; // source-current distribution and route resistance affect array transconductance. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and // Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. §IV; §V-A Eq.(25)/Fig.9, printed pp.118–120. // Physical PDF pp. 5–7. // Applies when: Keep actual net labels and unit paths; generalized routing acceptance is a // handbook adaptation. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q04","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q04"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q05 · MOM unit construction exposes different parasitics","text":"Q05 · MOM unit construction exposes different parasitics Source result. MOM finger/sandwich/pillar/mortise-tenon constructions trade density and terminal coupling. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead . §III-B, Fig.5, printed p.117. PDF p. 4 . Assumptions and limits: A source-specific advanced-node comparison; selecting a qualified PDK primitive still requires its own model. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q05; source]: MOM finger/sandwich/pillar/mortise-tenon constructions trade density and // terminal coupling. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and // Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. §III-B, Fig.5, printed p.117. Physical PDF pp. 4. // Applies when: A source-specific advanced-node comparison; selecting a qualified PDK primitive // still requires its own model. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q05","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q05"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q06 · Polynomial additive-unit model","text":"Q06 · Polynomial additive-unit model Source result. Under an additive identical-unit parameter model, equal spatial moments through n cancel every polynomial component of degree at most n. Reference: Xin Dai, Chengming He, Hanqing Xing, Degang Chen and Randall Geiger · ISCAS 2005. An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation . §II Eqs.(4)–(6); §III Eqs.(15)–(23), printed pp.4836–4837. PDF pp. 2–3 . Assumptions and limits: Two identically designed devices; parameter additivity; unit center approximates area average, or finite-footprint equivalence is independently justified. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q06; source]: Under an additive identical-unit parameter model, equal spatial moments // through n cancel every polynomial component of degree at most n. // Source: Xin Dai, Chengming He, Hanqing Xing, Degang Chen and Randall Geiger · ISCAS 2005. An // Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation. §II // Eqs.(4)–(6); §III Eqs.(15)–(23), printed pp.4836–4837. Physical PDF pp. 2–3. // Applies when: Two identically designed devices; parameter additivity; unit center approximates // area average, or finite-footprint equivalence is independently justified. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q06","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q06"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q07 · Recursive central-symmetry parity","text":"Q07 · Recursive central-symmetry parity Source result. Reflect the prior pattern, retaining labels for odd target order and exchanging labels for even target order; the specified base leads to 2^n units per device. Reference: Xin Dai, Chengming He, Hanqing Xing, Degang Chen and Randall Geiger · ISCAS 2005. An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation . §III recursive construction and induction, printed pp.4836–4837. PDF pp. 2–3 . Assumptions and limits: First-order base has two units per device; legal disjoint placement; not a minimal-count or circuit-performance theorem. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q07; source]: Reflect the prior pattern, retaining labels for odd target order and // exchanging labels for even target order; the specified base leads to 2^n units per device. // Source: Xin Dai, Chengming He, Hanqing Xing, Degang Chen and Randall Geiger · ISCAS 2005. An // Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation. §III // recursive construction and induction, printed pp.4836–4837. Physical PDF pp. 2–3. // Applies when: First-order base has two units per device; legal disjoint placement; not a // minimal-count or circuit-performance theorem. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q07","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q07"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q08 · DAC plate identities and parasitic classes","text":"Q08 · DAC plate identities and parasitic classes Source result. Binary and split DAC networks respond differently to top/substrate, top/bottom and bottom/substrate parasitics; split banks retain two top-plate nodes. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . §II-A–B, Figs.1–2, printed pp.2783–2784. PDF pp. 2–3 . Assumptions and limits: Specified charge-scaling architecture and ideal static excitation conditions; dynamic reference/switch effects require extensions. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q08; source]: Binary and split DAC networks respond differently to top/substrate, // top/bottom and bottom/substrate parasitics; split banks retain two top-plate nodes. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §II-A–B, // Figs.1–2, printed pp.2783–2784. Physical PDF pp. 2–3. // Applies when: Specified charge-scaling architecture and ideal static excitation conditions; // dynamic reference/switch effects require extensions. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q08","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q08"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q09 · Constructive DAC families and odd counts","text":"Q09 · Constructive DAC families and odd counts Source result. Spiral/chessboard/block choices trade connected components against dispersion; the two one-unit members are deliberately only near the common center. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . §IV-A and §IV-C1, printed pp.2788–2790. PDF pp. 7–9 . Assumptions and limits: Identical nonoverlapping grid units; preserve exact electrical counts and quantify CC exceptions. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q09; source]: Spiral/chessboard/block choices trade connected components against // dispersion; the two one-unit members are deliberately only near the common center. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §IV-A and // §IV-C1, printed pp.2788–2790. Physical PDF pp. 7–9. // Applies when: Identical nonoverlapping grid units; preserve exact electrical counts and quantify // CC exceptions. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q09","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q09"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q10 · DAC routing construction","text":"Q10 · DAC routing construction Source result. Same-bit components are joined by branch/trunk/bridge routes; top plates use graph/MST routing, separately for split-bank nodes. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . §IV-B–C, printed pp.2789–2791. PDF pp. 8–10 . Assumptions and limits: Specified layer/pin/array model; MST wire cost does not prove minimum electrical delay. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q10; source]: Same-bit components are joined by branch/trunk/bridge routes; top plates // use graph/MST routing, separately for split-bank nodes. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §IV-B–C, printed // pp.2789–2791. Physical PDF pp. 8–10. // Applies when: Specified layer/pin/array model; MST wire cost does not prove minimum electrical // delay. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q10","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q10"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q11 · DAC INL and DNL definitions","text":"Q11 · DAC INL and DNL definitions Source result. DNL evaluates adjacent output steps relative to one ideal LSB; INL evaluates deviation from the declared ideal transfer. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . §III, Eqs.(17)–(18), printed p.2785. PDF p. 4 . Assumptions and limits: Record reference convention, code endpoints and normalization; endpoint or best-fit corrections change the metric. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q11; source]: DNL evaluates adjacent output steps relative to one ideal LSB; INL // evaluates deviation from the declared ideal transfer. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §III, // Eqs.(17)–(18), printed p.2785. Physical PDF pp. 4. // Applies when: Record reference convention, code endpoints and normalization; endpoint or // best-fit corrections change the metric. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q11","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q11"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q12 · Paper-specific DAC frequency","text":"Q12 · Paper-specific DAC frequency Source result. The paper’s Eq.(32) defines f=1/[2(N+2)ln(2)τ] from its charging/discharging model. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . §III-C, Eq.(32), printed p.2786. PDF p. 5 . Assumptions and limits: τ is the worst modeled charging-path delay. This N-dependent settling measure must not be equated to 1/(2πτ). Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q12; source]: The paper’s Eq.(32) defines f=1/[2(N+2)ln(2)τ] from its // charging/discharging model. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §III-C, Eq.(32), // printed p.2786. Physical PDF pp. 5. // Applies when: τ is the worst modeled charging-path delay. This N-dependent settling measure must // not be equated to 1/(2πτ). // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q12","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q12"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q13 · MOM bridge edge sensitivity","text":"Q13 · MOM bridge edge sensitivity Source result. For a common small edge displacement δ, the specified MOM geometry gives ΔC/C≈2δ(1/H+1/l), enabling joint nominal-C and fractional-sensitivity matching. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . §III-D, Eqs.(39)–(45), printed p.2787. PDF p. 6 . Assumptions and limits: H and l are in-plane dimensions; common small perturbation, continuous geometry, and simplified coupling model. Quantization/fringing require extraction. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q13; source]: For a common small edge displacement δ, the specified MOM geometry gives // ΔC/C≈2δ(1/H+1/l), enabling joint nominal-C and fractional-sensitivity matching. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §III-D, // Eqs.(39)–(45), printed p.2787. Physical PDF pp. 6. // Applies when: H and l are in-plane dimensions; common small perturbation, continuous geometry, // and simplified coupling model. Quantization/fringing require extraction. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q13","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q13"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q14 · Do not promote illustrative correlation to process data","text":"Q14 · Do not promote illustrative correlation to process data Source result. The DAC evaluation’s gradient/correlation constants are inherited assumptions, not a measured capacitor statistical characterization. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . §V setup and parameter discussion, printed pp.2791,2793. PDF p. 10 · PDF p. 12 . Assumptions and limits: Yield/correlation claims require qualified process data; parameter sweeps study conditional behavior only. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q14; source]: The DAC evaluation’s gradient/correlation constants are inherited // assumptions, not a measured capacitor statistical characterization. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §V setup and // parameter discussion, printed pp.2791,2793. Physical PDF pp. 10, 12. // Applies when: Yield/correlation claims require qualified process data; parameter sweeps study // conditional behavior only. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q14","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q14"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q15 · Widening shifts the critical bit","text":"Q15 · Widening shifts the critical bit Source result. Parallel critical-bit routes can improve delay but change the bottleneck and eventually have diminishing returns. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . Tables I–II; Fig.15/discussion, printed pp.2792–2793. PDF pp. 11–12 . Assumptions and limits: Re-evaluate all bits with changed capacitance, vias and routing capacity; numerical gains belong to that setup. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q15; source]: Parallel critical-bit routes can improve delay but change the bottleneck // and eventually have diminishing returns. // Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani // and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and // Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. Tables I–II; // Fig.15/discussion, printed pp.2792–2793. Physical PDF pp. 11–12. // Applies when: Re-evaluate all bits with changed capacitance, vias and routing capacity; // numerical gains belong to that setup. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q15","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q15"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q16 · Electrical feedback closes a proxy gap","text":"Q16 · Electrical feedback closes a proxy gap Source result. Geometric constraints and scalar physical objectives do not generally guarantee post-layout circuit performance. Reference: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions . §I–II, Fig.1, printed pp.679–681. PDF pp. 1–3 . Assumptions and limits: Contract and testbench determine required metrics. An extracted/simulated result is conditional on model coverage. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q16; source]: Geometric constraints and scalar physical objectives do not generally // guarantee post-layout circuit performance. // Source: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven // Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions. §I–II, Fig.1, printed // pp.679–681. Physical PDF pp. 1–3. // Applies when: Contract and testbench determine required metrics. An extracted/simulated result // is conditional on model coverage. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q16","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q16"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q17 · Model cost needs consistent denominators","text":"Q17 · Model cost needs consistent denominators Source result. The survey’s text and Fig.2 have inconsistent runtime fractions; only the qualitative data-acquisition-dominance lesson is safely reused. Reference: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions . §III-A; Fig.2, printed pp.681,683. PDF p. 3 · PDF p. 5 . Assumptions and limits: Apparent internal inconsistency, visually checked; not a publisher-verified erratum or an engine runtime budget. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q17; source]: The survey’s text and Fig.2 have inconsistent runtime fractions; only the // qualitative data-acquisition-dominance lesson is safely reused. // Source: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven // Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions. §III-A; Fig.2, printed // pp.681,683. Physical PDF pp. 3, 5. // Applies when: Apparent internal inconsistency, visually checked; not a publisher-verified // erratum or an engine runtime budget. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q17","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q17"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q18 · Transfer accuracy is metric/domain dependent","text":"Q18 · Transfer accuracy is metric/domain dependent Source result. The transfer example behaves differently for bandwidth, CMRR and noise across sizing/topology changes. Reference: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions . §III-B, Table I, printed p.682. PDF p. 4 . Assumptions and limits: Reported circuits and train/test domains; no generalization guarantee beyond them. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q18; source]: The transfer example behaves differently for bandwidth, CMRR and noise // across sizing/topology changes. // Source: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven // Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions. §III-B, Table I, printed p.682. // Physical PDF pp. 4. // Applies when: Reported circuits and train/test domains; no generalization guarantee beyond them. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q18","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q18"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q19 · Retain competing objectives","text":"Q19 · Retain competing objectives Source result. The MOBO/weighted-BO comparison motivates considering tradeoffs among circuit metrics. Reference: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions . §III-C and related figures, printed pp.682–683. PDF pp. 4–5 . Assumptions and limits: Reported case study. A feasible Pareto archive is our software policy and is not a global-frontier proof. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q19; source]: The MOBO/weighted-BO comparison motivates considering tradeoffs among // circuit metrics. // Source: Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024. Performance-Driven // Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions. §III-C and related figures, // printed pp.682–683. Physical PDF pp. 4–5. // Applies when: Reported case study. A feasible Pareto archive is our software policy and is not a // global-frontier proof. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q19","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q19"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q20 · Discrete per-net wire sizing","text":"Q20 · Discrete per-net wire sizing Source result. The manuscript assigns integer width choices per net after global routing; symmetric nets receive equal width choices. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . §§2–3, Eqs.(1)–(3), supplied manuscript pp.5–6. PDF pp. 5–6 . Assumptions and limits: Metrics in normalized ratio scores must have meaningful positive values; final feasibility remains separate from weighted FOM. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q20; source]: The manuscript assigns integer width choices per net after global routing; // symmetric nets receive equal width choices. // Source: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh // Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire // Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits. §§2–3, Eqs.(1)–(3), supplied manuscript pp.5–6. Physical // PDF pp. 5–6. // Applies when: Metrics in normalized ratio scores must have meaningful positive values; final // feasibility remains separate from weighted FOM. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q20","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q20"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q21 · WAGN physical graph features","text":"Q21 · WAGN physical graph features Source result. Pins and route Steiner nodes carry device/role/geometry features; edges include distance, via count, widths, pin dimensions and terminal types. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . §4.2, supplied manuscript p.8. PDF p. 8 . Assumptions and limits: These are the reported model inputs, not proof that any particular graph network is needed for this engine. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q21; source]: Pins and route Steiner nodes carry device/role/geometry features; edges // include distance, via count, widths, pin dimensions and terminal types. // Source: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh // Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire // Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits. §4.2, supplied manuscript p.8. Physical PDF pp. 8. // Applies when: These are the reported model inputs, not proof that any particular graph network // is needed for this engine. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q21","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q21"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q22 · WAGN output is a threshold classifier","text":"Q22 · WAGN output is a threshold classifier Source result. The model output is denoted P(FOM<T) at one specified threshold and trained with binary labels/cross entropy. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . §4.3–4.5, Eqs.(17)–(19), supplied manuscript pp.10–11. PDF pp. 10–11 . Assumptions and limits: Not a full CDF, metric regression or established calibration/yield model; observed classification accuracy does not supply those guarantees. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q22; source]: The model output is denoted P(FOM<T) at one specified threshold and // trained with binary labels/cross entropy. // Source: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh // Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire // Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits. §4.3–4.5, Eqs.(17)–(19), supplied manuscript pp.10–11. // Physical PDF pp. 10–11. // Applies when: Not a full CDF, metric regression or established calibration/yield model; observed // classification accuracy does not supply those guarantees. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q22","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q22"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q23 · Simulated shortlist after surrogate search","text":"Q23 · Simulated shortlist after surrogate search Source result. Relax/search widths through fixed WAGN, discretize, and simulate the top shortlist before final selection. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . §5, Eqs.(20)–(21), Algorithm1, supplied manuscript pp.12–14. PDF pp. 12–14 . Assumptions and limits: Multistart/local/BO methods are heuristic; finite penalty, rounding and route fallback require actual feasibility checks. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q23; source]: Relax/search widths through fixed WAGN, discretize, and simulate the top // shortlist before final selection. // Source: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh // Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire // Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits. §5, Eqs.(20)–(21), Algorithm1, supplied manuscript // pp.12–14. Physical PDF pp. 12–14. // Applies when: Multistart/local/BO methods are heuristic; finite penalty, rounding and route // fallback require actual feasibility checks. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q23","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q23"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q24 · Parallel routes implement width options","text":"Q24 · Parallel routes implement width options Source result. The experiments implement 1×, 2× and 3× width choices using parallel routes. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . §6, Fig.8, supplied manuscript pp.15–16. PDF pp. 15–16 . Assumptions and limits: The realized route graph may differ from a single wide conductor; routability and access remain conditional. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q24; source]: The experiments implement 1×, 2× and 3× width choices using parallel // routes. // Source: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh // Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire // Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits. §6, Fig.8, supplied manuscript pp.15–16. Physical PDF pp. // 15–16. // Applies when: The realized route graph may differ from a single wide conductor; routability and // access remain conditional. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q24","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q24"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q25 · Wider can improve one metric and hurt another","text":"Q25 · Wider can improve one metric and hurt another Source result. Table7’s CM-OTA 1×→3× change raises UGF 451.0→511.4 MHz while reducing gain 33.1→31.1 dB and FOM 0.92→0.90. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . Table7, supplied manuscript p.19. PDF p. 19 . Assumptions and limits: One reported circuit/setup; do not reuse the numbers as a target-process forecast. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q25; source]: Table7’s CM-OTA 1×→3× change raises UGF 451.0→511.4 MHz while reducing // gain 33.1→31.1 dB and FOM 0.92→0.90. // Source: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh // Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire // Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits. Table7, supplied manuscript p.19. Physical PDF pp. 19. // Applies when: One reported circuit/setup; do not reuse the numbers as a target-process forecast. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q25","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q25"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Q26 · WAGN evidence and stated limits","text":"Q26 · WAGN evidence and stated limits Source result. Runtime/gain claims compare specified BO budgets; the paper limits cross-family transfer, large circuits, process variation and classification-only prediction. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . §6.2/Table6; §7, supplied manuscript pp.17–19,23. PDF pp. 17–19 · PDF p. 23 . Assumptions and limits: Training-data cost, parallel hardware and circuit family must accompany comparisons; no experiments reproduced here. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [Q26; source]: Runtime/gain claims compare specified BO budgets; the paper limits // cross-family transfer, large circuits, process variation and classification-only prediction. // Source: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh // Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire // Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits. §6.2/Table6; §7, supplied manuscript pp.17–19,23. // Physical PDF pp. 17–19, 23. // Applies when: Training-data cost, parallel hardware and circuit family must accompany // comparisons; no experiments reproduced here. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q26","url":"theory-provenance.html#Q26"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Explicit derivations and foundational gaps","text":"Explicit derivations and foundational gaps D01 · Weighted and ratio centroid extension Derived result. For additive nominal contributions wi sharing the same fractional spatial field, normalized device response uses Σwi p(ri)/Σwi; matched normalized first moments cancel a linear field. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead . Derived extension of Eqs.(1)–(5), not a quoted weighted-ratio theorem. PDF p. 1 . Assumptions and limits: Additive electrical composition; weights defined for the actual absolute/fractional perturbation; sensitivities and bias compatible. Derivation: Expand p=p0+gx x+gy y in each normalized sum and subtract. Unequal counts are valid after normalization; arbitrary model sensitivities may not be simple positive area weights. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D01; derived]: For additive nominal contributions wi sharing the same fractional spatial // field, normalized device response uses Σwi p(ri)/Σwi; matched normalized first moments cancel a // linear field. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, // Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. // 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. // Derived extension of Eqs.(1)–(5), not a quoted weighted-ratio theorem. Physical PDF pp. 1. // Applies when: Additive electrical composition; weights defined for the actual // absolute/fractional perturbation; sensitivities and bias compatible. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D01 D02 · Finite-footprint polynomial certificate Derived result. Identical translated unit footprints preserve moment cancellation through n when their center moments match through n. Reference: Xin Dai, Chengming He, Hanqing Xing, Degang Chen and Randall Geiger · ISCAS 2005. An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation . Derived finite-footprint extension of §II/§III. PDF pp. 2–3 . Assumptions and limits: Identical footprint weighting kernels; linear/additive field response; same internal moments for all units. Derivation: Integrate (xi+u)^a(yi+v)^b over a common normalized footprint. Binomial expansion yields products of center moments of degree ≤a+b and common internal moments; all differential terms vanish when the corresponding center moments match. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D02; derived]: Identical translated unit footprints preserve moment cancellation through // n when their center moments match through n. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Xin Dai, Chengming He, Hanqing Xing, // Degang Chen and Randall Geiger · ISCAS 2005. An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for // nonlinear gradients cancellation. Derived finite-footprint extension of §II/§III. Physical PDF // pp. 2–3. // Applies when: Identical footprint weighting kernels; linear/additive field response; same // internal moments for all units. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D02 D03 · Single-branch source degeneration Derived result. gm,eff=gm/(1+gmRs) under a restricted one-branch small-signal model. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead . Derived from the mechanism in §V-A Eq.(25); the closed form is not printed there. PDF p. 6 . Assumptions and limits: Neglect gds/body effect; source-resistor current equals modeled branch current; gm is local and constant. Derivation: i=gm(vin−iRs) gives i(1+gmRs)=gm vin; divide by vin. A multi-unit array requires its actual current distribution. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D03; derived]: gm,eff=gm/(1+gmRs) under a restricted one-branch small-signal model. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, // Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. // 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. // Derived from the mechanism in §V-A Eq.(25); the closed form is not printed there. Physical PDF // pp. 6. // Applies when: Neglect gds/body effect; source-resistor current equals modeled branch current; gm // is local and constant. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D03 D04 · Linear RC nodal stamp Derived result. A physical capacitor c between i and j stamps +c diagonals and −c off-diagonals; KCL yields Gv+C dv/dt=i for the linear RC network. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . Circuit-law derivation; §2.5.3 provides RC modeling background, not this matrix formula. PDF pp. 46–51 . Assumptions and limits: Linear passive elements; identified reference/boundary nodes; capacitance convention fixed. Ideal voltage sources/inductors need additional treatment. Derivation: q_i=c(v_i−v_j), q_j=−q_i; differentiate and sum terminal currents. Resistor currents are (v_i−v_j)/R. This derivation supplies the stamp rather than a purported source quotation. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D04; derived]: A physical capacitor c between i and j stamps +c diagonals and −c // off-diagonals; KCL yields Gv+C dv/dt=i for the linear RC network. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and // Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability. Circuit-law // derivation; §2.5.3 provides RC modeling background, not this matrix formula. Physical PDF pp. // 46–51. // Applies when: Linear passive elements; identified reference/boundary nodes; capacitance // convention fixed. Ideal voltage sources/inductors need additional treatment. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D04 D05 · Fast capacitive charge sharing Derived result. For a floating victim with quiet-node Cv and aggressor coupling Cc, ΔVv=Cc/(Cv+Cc)ΔVa. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . Independent charge-conservation derivation; substrate electroquasistatics is background only. PDF pp. 45–46 . Assumptions and limits: Ideal fast imposed aggressor step; fixed quiet reference; no charge escape or other changing coupled nodes; linear C. Derivation: Conserve victim charge: CvΔVv+Cc(ΔVv−ΔVa)=0. Finite driver impedance or nonlinear clamps require transient analysis. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D05; derived]: For a floating victim with quiet-node Cv and aggressor coupling Cc, // ΔVv=Cc/(Cv+Cc)ΔVa. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, // Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. // Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design. Independent charge-conservation // derivation; substrate electroquasistatics is background only. Physical PDF pp. 45–46. // Applies when: Ideal fast imposed aggressor step; fixed quiet reference; no charge escape or // other changing coupled nodes; linear C. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D05 D06 · Toy finite optimal wire width Derived result. For τ(w)=(Rd+a/w)(CL+bw) with positive parameters, the continuous optimum is sqrt(aCL/(Rd b)), before legal bounds/discretization. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . Original illustrative model motivated by RC tradeoffs, not the manuscript objective. PDF pp. 2–3 . Assumptions and limits: Rd,b,a,CL>0; fixed topology; C varies linearly and R inversely with width; single time-constant objective. Derivation: dτ/dw=Rd b−aCL/w²; d²τ/dw²=2aCL/w³>0. With Rd=0 or b=0 and other terms positive there is no finite stationary optimum of this form. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D06; derived]: For τ(w)=(Rd+a/w)(CL+bw) with positive parameters, the continuous optimum // is sqrt(aCL/(Rd b)), before legal bounds/discretization. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna // Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied // manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated // Circuits. Original illustrative model motivated by RC tradeoffs, not the manuscript objective. // Physical PDF pp. 2–3. // Applies when: Rd,b,a,CL>0; fixed topology; C varies linearly and R inversely with width; single // time-constant objective. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D06 D07 · Johnson–Nyquist foundational citation gap Foundation with a reference gap. The glossary states classical one-sided resistor voltage PSD 4kBTR; no exact supporting equation was located in the supplied corpus. Origin: Foundational law with no verified corpus locator. No exact external source is attributed by this record. Assumptions and limits: Equilibrium passive resistor, classical frequency regime, one-sided PSD in V²/Hz. The formula itself is a background assumption in this handbook. Derivation: Conditional consistency check: filtering 4kBTR by 1/[1+(2πfRC)²] and integrating f≥0 gives kBT/C. This integration does not independently prove the underlying thermal-noise law. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D07; foundation]: The glossary states classical one-sided resistor voltage PSD 4kBTR; no // exact supporting equation was located in the supplied corpus. // Origin: Foundational law with no verified corpus locator. No external source attribution is // established by this record. // Applies when: Equilibrium passive resistor, classical frequency regime, one-sided PSD in V²/Hz. // The formula itself is a background assumption in this handbook. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D07 D08 · Equilibrium sampled-capacitor noise Derived result. One ideal equilibrated linear capacitor has voltage variance kBT/C. Origin: Independent equipartition derivation; no exact supplied-PDF kT/C passage located. No exact external source is attributed by this record. Assumptions and limits: One thermalized degree of freedom, classical equilibrium, no incomplete settling or correlated-sampling modification; a full DAC has other transfer factors. Derivation: Equipartition gives ½C Var(v)=½kBT, so Var(v)=kBT/C. Foundational equipartition premise is assumed; do not cite the DAC paper as the source of this formula. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D08; derived]: One ideal equilibrated linear capacitor has voltage variance kBT/C. // Origin: Independent equipartition derivation; no exact supplied-PDF kT/C passage located. No // external source attribution is established by this record. // Applies when: One thermalized degree of freedom, classical equilibrium, no incomplete settling // or correlated-sampling modification; a full DAC has other transfer factors. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D08 D09 · Zero failures do not prove zero failure rate Derived result. For n independent representative Bernoulli trials with zero failures, a one-sided 1−α upper bound is 1−α^(1/n), approximately −ln(α)/n. Origin: Original binomial inversion in topic-validation#statistics. No exact external source is attributed by this record. Assumptions and limits: Independent identically distributed outcomes and a fixed acceptance event; not weighted importance samples or adaptive/censored failures. Derivation: P(no failures|p)=(1−p)^n. Invert at α. At 95% confidence and n=1000 the upper bound is about 0.00299125 (0.2991%). Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D09; derived]: For n independent representative Bernoulli trials with zero failures, a // one-sided 1−α upper bound is 1−α^(1/n), approximately −ln(α)/n. // Origin: Original binomial inversion in topic-validation#statistics. No external source // attribution is established by this record. // Applies when: Independent identically distributed outcomes and a fixed acceptance event; not // weighted importance samples or adaptive/censored failures. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D09 D10 · Half-LSB single-pole settling Derived result. For a full-scale single-pole step and half an N-bit LSB residual, t≥(N+1)ln(2)τ. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . Original exponential-settling derivation; distinct from source Eq.(32). PDF p. 5 . Assumptions and limits: Single pole; unit full-scale initial error; half-LSB allocation; no slew/ringing or other error consuming the allocation. Derivation: Require exp(−t/τ)≤2^(−N−1), take logs. This is not a sample-rate certificate for an arbitrary switched array. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D10; derived]: For a full-scale single-pole step and half an N-bit LSB residual, // t≥(N+1)ln(2)τ. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, // Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), // 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in // Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. Original exponential-settling derivation; distinct from source // Eq.(32). Physical PDF pp. 5. // Applies when: Single pole; unit full-scale initial error; half-LSB allocation; no slew/ringing // or other error consuming the allocation. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D10","url":"theory-provenance.html#derived-records"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · D01 · Weighted and ratio centroid extension","text":"D01 · Weighted and ratio centroid extension Derived result. For additive nominal contributions wi sharing the same fractional spatial field, normalized device response uses Σwi p(ri)/Σwi; matched normalized first moments cancel a linear field. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead . Derived extension of Eqs.(1)–(5), not a quoted weighted-ratio theorem. PDF p. 1 . Assumptions and limits: Additive electrical composition; weights defined for the actual absolute/fractional perturbation; sensitivities and bias compatible. Derivation: Expand p=p0+gx x+gy y in each normalized sum and subtract. Unequal counts are valid after normalization; arbitrary model sensitivities may not be simple positive area weights. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D01; derived]: For additive nominal contributions wi sharing the same fractional spatial // field, normalized device response uses Σwi p(ri)/Σwi; matched normalized first moments cancel a // linear field. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, // Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. // 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. // Derived extension of Eqs.(1)–(5), not a quoted weighted-ratio theorem. Physical PDF pp. 1. // Applies when: Additive electrical composition; weights defined for the actual // absolute/fractional perturbation; sensitivities and bias compatible. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D01","url":"theory-provenance.html#D01"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · D02 · Finite-footprint polynomial certificate","text":"D02 · Finite-footprint polynomial certificate Derived result. Identical translated unit footprints preserve moment cancellation through n when their center moments match through n. Reference: Xin Dai, Chengming He, Hanqing Xing, Degang Chen and Randall Geiger · ISCAS 2005. An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation . Derived finite-footprint extension of §II/§III. PDF pp. 2–3 . Assumptions and limits: Identical footprint weighting kernels; linear/additive field response; same internal moments for all units. Derivation: Integrate (xi+u)^a(yi+v)^b over a common normalized footprint. Binomial expansion yields products of center moments of degree ≤a+b and common internal moments; all differential terms vanish when the corresponding center moments match. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D02; derived]: Identical translated unit footprints preserve moment cancellation through // n when their center moments match through n. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Xin Dai, Chengming He, Hanqing Xing, // Degang Chen and Randall Geiger · ISCAS 2005. An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for // nonlinear gradients cancellation. Derived finite-footprint extension of §II/§III. Physical PDF // pp. 2–3. // Applies when: Identical footprint weighting kernels; linear/additive field response; same // internal moments for all units. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D02","url":"theory-provenance.html#D02"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · D03 · Single-branch source degeneration","text":"D03 · Single-branch source degeneration Derived result. gm,eff=gm/(1+gmRs) under a restricted one-branch small-signal model. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead . Derived from the mechanism in §V-A Eq.(25); the closed form is not printed there. PDF p. 6 . Assumptions and limits: Neglect gds/body effect; source-resistor current equals modeled branch current; gm is local and constant. Derivation: i=gm(vin−iRs) gives i(1+gmRs)=gm vin; divide by vin. A multi-unit array requires its actual current distribution. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D03; derived]: gm,eff=gm/(1+gmRs) under a restricted one-branch small-signal model. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, // Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. // 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. // Derived from the mechanism in §V-A Eq.(25); the closed form is not printed there. Physical PDF // pp. 6. // Applies when: Neglect gds/body effect; source-resistor current equals modeled branch current; gm // is local and constant. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D03","url":"theory-provenance.html#D03"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · D04 · Linear RC nodal stamp","text":"D04 · Linear RC nodal stamp Derived result. A physical capacitor c between i and j stamps +c diagonals and −c off-diagonals; KCL yields Gv+C dv/dt=i for the linear RC network. Reference: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability . Circuit-law derivation; §2.5.3 provides RC modeling background, not this matrix formula. PDF pp. 46–51 . Assumptions and limits: Linear passive elements; identified reference/boundary nodes; capacitance convention fixed. Ideal voltage sources/inductors need additional treatment. Derivation: q_i=c(v_i−v_j), q_j=−q_i; differentiate and sum terminal currents. Resistor currents are (v_i−v_j)/R. This derivation supplies the stamp rather than a purported source quotation. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D04; derived]: A physical capacitor c between i and j stamps +c diagonals and −c // off-diagonals; KCL yields Gv+C dv/dt=i for the linear RC network. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and // Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability. Circuit-law // derivation; §2.5.3 provides RC modeling background, not this matrix formula. Physical PDF pp. // 46–51. // Applies when: Linear passive elements; identified reference/boundary nodes; capacitance // convention fixed. Ideal voltage sources/inductors need additional treatment. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D04","url":"theory-provenance.html#D04"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · D05 · Fast capacitive charge sharing","text":"D05 · Fast capacitive charge sharing Derived result. For a floating victim with quiet-node Cv and aggressor coupling Cc, ΔVv=Cc/(Cv+Cc)ΔVa. Reference: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design . Independent charge-conservation derivation; substrate electroquasistatics is background only. PDF pp. 45–46 . Assumptions and limits: Ideal fast imposed aggressor step; fixed quiet reference; no charge escape or other changing coupled nodes; linear C. Derivation: Conserve victim charge: CvΔVv+Cc(ΔVv−ΔVa)=0. Finite driver impedance or nonlinear clamps require transient analysis. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D05; derived]: For a floating victim with quiet-node Cv and aggressor coupling Cc, // ΔVv=Cc/(Cv+Cc)ΔVa. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, // Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. // Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design. Independent charge-conservation // derivation; substrate electroquasistatics is background only. Physical PDF pp. 45–46. // Applies when: Ideal fast imposed aggressor step; fixed quiet reference; no charge escape or // other changing coupled nodes; linear C. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D05","url":"theory-provenance.html#D05"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · D06 · Toy finite optimal wire width","text":"D06 · Toy finite optimal wire width Derived result. For τ(w)=(Rd+a/w)(CL+bw) with positive parameters, the continuous optimum is sqrt(aCL/(Rd b)), before legal bounds/discretization. Reference: Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits . Original illustrative model motivated by RC tradeoffs, not the manuscript objective. PDF pp. 2–3 . Assumptions and limits: Rd,b,a,CL>0; fixed topology; C varies linearly and R inversely with width; single time-constant objective. Derivation: dτ/dw=Rd b−aCL/w²; d²τ/dw²=2aCL/w³>0. With Rd=0 or b=0 and other terms positive there is no finite stationary optimum of this form. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D06; derived]: For τ(w)=(Rd+a/w)(CL+bw) with positive parameters, the continuous optimum // is sqrt(aCL/(Rd b)), before legal bounds/discretization. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna // Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied // manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022). Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated // Circuits. Original illustrative model motivated by RC tradeoffs, not the manuscript objective. // Physical PDF pp. 2–3. // Applies when: Rd,b,a,CL>0; fixed topology; C varies linearly and R inversely with width; single // time-constant objective. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D06","url":"theory-provenance.html#D06"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · D07 · Johnson–Nyquist foundational citation gap","text":"D07 · Johnson–Nyquist foundational citation gap Foundation with a reference gap. The glossary states classical one-sided resistor voltage PSD 4kBTR; no exact supporting equation was located in the supplied corpus. Origin: Foundational law with no verified corpus locator. No exact external source is attributed by this record. Assumptions and limits: Equilibrium passive resistor, classical frequency regime, one-sided PSD in V²/Hz. The formula itself is a background assumption in this handbook. Derivation: Conditional consistency check: filtering 4kBTR by 1/[1+(2πfRC)²] and integrating f≥0 gives kBT/C. This integration does not independently prove the underlying thermal-noise law. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D07; foundation]: The glossary states classical one-sided resistor voltage PSD 4kBTR; no // exact supporting equation was located in the supplied corpus. // Origin: Foundational law with no verified corpus locator. No external source attribution is // established by this record. // Applies when: Equilibrium passive resistor, classical frequency regime, one-sided PSD in V²/Hz. // The formula itself is a background assumption in this handbook. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D07","url":"theory-provenance.html#D07"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · D08 · Equilibrium sampled-capacitor noise","text":"D08 · Equilibrium sampled-capacitor noise Derived result. One ideal equilibrated linear capacitor has voltage variance kBT/C. Origin: Independent equipartition derivation; no exact supplied-PDF kT/C passage located. No exact external source is attributed by this record. Assumptions and limits: One thermalized degree of freedom, classical equilibrium, no incomplete settling or correlated-sampling modification; a full DAC has other transfer factors. Derivation: Equipartition gives ½C Var(v)=½kBT, so Var(v)=kBT/C. Foundational equipartition premise is assumed; do not cite the DAC paper as the source of this formula. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D08; derived]: One ideal equilibrated linear capacitor has voltage variance kBT/C. // Origin: Independent equipartition derivation; no exact supplied-PDF kT/C passage located. No // external source attribution is established by this record. // Applies when: One thermalized degree of freedom, classical equilibrium, no incomplete settling // or correlated-sampling modification; a full DAC has other transfer factors. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D08","url":"theory-provenance.html#D08"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · D09 · Zero failures do not prove zero failure rate","text":"D09 · Zero failures do not prove zero failure rate Derived result. For n independent representative Bernoulli trials with zero failures, a one-sided 1−α upper bound is 1−α^(1/n), approximately −ln(α)/n. Origin: Original binomial inversion in topic-validation#statistics. No exact external source is attributed by this record. Assumptions and limits: Independent identically distributed outcomes and a fixed acceptance event; not weighted importance samples or adaptive/censored failures. Derivation: P(no failures|p)=(1−p)^n. Invert at α. At 95% confidence and n=1000 the upper bound is about 0.00299125 (0.2991%). Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D09; derived]: For n independent representative Bernoulli trials with zero failures, a // one-sided 1−α upper bound is 1−α^(1/n), approximately −ln(α)/n. // Origin: Original binomial inversion in topic-validation#statistics. No external source // attribution is established by this record. // Applies when: Independent identically distributed outcomes and a fixed acceptance event; not // weighted importance samples or adaptive/censored failures. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D09","url":"theory-provenance.html#D09"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · D10 · Half-LSB single-pole settling","text":"D10 · Half-LSB single-pole settling Derived result. For a full-scale single-pole step and half an N-bit LSB residual, t≥(N+1)ln(2)τ. Reference: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs . Original exponential-settling derivation; distinct from source Eq.(32). PDF p. 5 . Assumptions and limits: Single pole; unit full-scale initial error; half-LSB allocation; no slew/ringing or other error consuming the allocation. Derivation: Require exp(−t/τ)≤2^(−N−1), take logs. This is not a sample-rate certificate for an arbitrary switched array. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [D10; derived]: For a full-scale single-pole step and half an N-bit LSB residual, // t≥(N+1)ln(2)τ. // Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, // Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), // 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in // Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. Original exponential-settling derivation; distinct from source // Eq.(32). Physical PDF pp. 5. // Applies when: Single pole; unit full-scale initial error; half-LSB allocation; no slew/ringing // or other error consuming the allocation. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#D10","url":"theory-provenance.html#D10"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · Original engineering policies","text":"Original engineering policies S01 · Constraint instance and provenance contract Proposed handbook policy. Store the physical mechanism, targets, units, scope, parent requirement, model provenance, enforcement and evidence for each proposed rule. Origin: Original handbook engineering policy, constraints.html#catalog-semantics. No exact external source is attributed by this record. Assumptions and limits: The 84 rule families are proposals to instantiate, not author-numbered textbook rules or universal foundry limits. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [S01; proposal]: Store the physical mechanism, targets, units, scope, parent requirement, // model provenance, enforcement and evidence for each proposed rule. // Origin: Original handbook engineering policy, constraints.html#catalog-semantics. No external // source attribution is established by this record. // Applies when: The 84 rule families are proposals to instantiate, not author-numbered textbook // rules or universal foundry limits. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#S01 S02 · Coverage and evidence freshness Proposed handbook policy. Distinguish pass/fail/unknown/not-applicable and invalidate checks when their geometry/model/scenario inputs change. Origin: Original handbook verification policy, topic-verification.html#coverage. No exact external source is attributed by this record. Assumptions and limits: Acceptance requires every selected mandatory evaluator to run successfully within its domain; no claim that a cited book prescribes this exact software schema. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [S02; proposal]: Distinguish pass/fail/unknown/not-applicable and invalidate checks when // their geometry/model/scenario inputs change. // Origin: Original handbook verification policy, topic-verification.html#coverage. No external // source attribution is established by this record. // Applies when: Acceptance requires every selected mandatory evaluator to run successfully within // its domain; no claim that a cited book prescribes this exact software schema. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#S02 S03 · Search guarantee and selection policy Proposed handbook policy. Distinguish best observed, nondominated among evaluated, and proven optimum within a restricted representation. Origin: Original methodological contract, topic-optimality.html#claims. No exact external source is attributed by this record. Assumptions and limits: Proof scope is limited by the represented feasible set, objective, model and solver evidence; a failed heuristic is not infeasibility proof. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [S03; proposal]: Distinguish best observed, nondominated among evaluated, and proven // optimum within a restricted representation. // Origin: Original methodological contract, topic-optimality.html#claims. No external source // attribution is established by this record. // Applies when: Proof scope is limited by the represented feasible set, objective, model and // solver evidence; a failed heuristic is not infeasibility proof. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#S03","url":"theory-provenance.html#policy-records"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · S01 · Constraint instance and provenance contract","text":"S01 · Constraint instance and provenance contract Proposed handbook policy. Store the physical mechanism, targets, units, scope, parent requirement, model provenance, enforcement and evidence for each proposed rule. Origin: Original handbook engineering policy, constraints.html#catalog-semantics. No exact external source is attributed by this record. Assumptions and limits: The 84 rule families are proposals to instantiate, not author-numbered textbook rules or universal foundry limits. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [S01; proposal]: Store the physical mechanism, targets, units, scope, parent requirement, // model provenance, enforcement and evidence for each proposed rule. // Origin: Original handbook engineering policy, constraints.html#catalog-semantics. No external // source attribution is established by this record. // Applies when: The 84 rule families are proposals to instantiate, not author-numbered textbook // rules or universal foundry limits. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#S01","url":"theory-provenance.html#S01"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · S02 · Coverage and evidence freshness","text":"S02 · Coverage and evidence freshness Proposed handbook policy. Distinguish pass/fail/unknown/not-applicable and invalidate checks when their geometry/model/scenario inputs change. Origin: Original handbook verification policy, topic-verification.html#coverage. No exact external source is attributed by this record. Assumptions and limits: Acceptance requires every selected mandatory evaluator to run successfully within its domain; no claim that a cited book prescribes this exact software schema. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [S02; proposal]: Distinguish pass/fail/unknown/not-applicable and invalidate checks when // their geometry/model/scenario inputs change. // Origin: Original handbook verification policy, topic-verification.html#coverage. No external // source attribution is established by this record. // Applies when: Acceptance requires every selected mandatory evaluator to run successfully within // its domain; no claim that a cited book prescribes this exact software schema. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#S02","url":"theory-provenance.html#S02"},{"title":"Theory Provenance · S03 · Search guarantee and selection policy","text":"S03 · Search guarantee and selection policy Proposed handbook policy. Distinguish best observed, nondominated among evaluated, and proven optimum within a restricted representation. Origin: Original methodological contract, topic-optimality.html#claims. No exact external source is attributed by this record. Assumptions and limits: Proof scope is limited by the represented feasible set, objective, model and solver evidence; a failed heuristic is not infeasibility proof. Comment template — retain the assumptions // Theory [S03; proposal]: Distinguish best observed, nondominated among evaluated, and proven // optimum within a restricted representation. // Origin: Original methodological contract, topic-optimality.html#claims. No external source // attribution is established by this record. // Applies when: Proof scope is limited by the represented feasible set, objective, model and // solver evidence; a failed heuristic is not infeasibility proof. // Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#S03","url":"theory-provenance.html#S03"},{"title":"Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits · Where the teaching treatment lives","text":"Where the teaching treatment lives Read wire sizing for the RC tradeoff and implementation procedure, performance feedback for surrogate validation, and electromigration for reliability limits that width optimization must respect. This source is a 27-page supplied manuscript; the file does not justify inventing a final journal DOI, volume, or issue.","url":"todaes.html#guide"},{"title":"Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits · Why width is a performance variable","text":"Why width is a performance variable Changing width changes resistance and capacitance differently. Series source resistance can reduce effective transconductance, while added capacitance at a sensitive node can reduce bandwidth or phase margin. Thus “wider everywhere” and “minimum total C” are both incomplete policies. The paper demonstrates this using OTAs, comparators, VCOs, and switched-capacitor filters in advanced processes. Motivation and examples, PDF pp. 2–4 Table 7 provides a useful counterexample: uniformly increasing wire width from 1× to 3× improves the example current-mirror OTA's UGF from 451.0 to 511.4 MHz while its gain falls from 33.1 to 31.1 dB and its aggregate FOM falls from 0.92 to 0.90. The lesson is competing sensitivities, not a SKY130 prediction. Table 7, PDF p. 19","url":"todaes.html#physical"},{"title":"Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits · Decision variables and objective","text":"Decision variables and objective The paper assigns discrete width choices per net after global routing and before detailed routing. Symmetric nets receive the same width. Its FOM combines bounded, normalized performance scores: for a positive larger-is-better metric z with target φ, use min(z/φ,1) ; for a positive smaller-is-better metric, use min(φ/z,1) . Weighted averaging then creates a maximization objective. §2–3, PDF pp. 5–6 Limit. These ratios assume meaningful positive quantities and do not handle zero, sign changes, or logarithmic metrics generically. A high aggregate FOM can still hide an unmet specification. Even a printed FOM of 1.00 should not be treated as a proof: rounding, normalization, and metric selection matter. Philis should retain the individual values and explicit pass/fail margins.","url":"todaes.html#formulation"},{"title":"Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits · What the WAGN model actually sees","text":"What the WAGN model actually sees The graph represents device pins, I/O pins, and routing Steiner points. Node features include device type, functional role, dimensions, and location. Edge features include horizontal/vertical span, via count, width choices, pin dimensions, and terminal types. This is substantially richer than a netlist-only or wirelength-only feature set. §4.2, PDF p. 8 The Wire Attention Graph Network combines node and edge information through attention and pooling. Its distinguishing attention kernels capture feature distance, similarity, and additional learned relations. The manuscript denotes the final soft classification output P(FOM<T) for one user-specified threshold. It estimates class membership; the reported classification experiments do not establish probability calibration, a full performance distribution or manufacturing yield. It is not a prediction of gain in dB or delay in seconds. The classifier's useful ranking range can saturate far from its threshold. §4.3–4.5, PDF pp. 9–11 Adoption reason. The feature design teaches Philis what information a physically relevant fast evaluator may need. It does not establish that a GNN should be the first implementation. Extracted terminal RC and finite-difference sensitivities provide an interpretable baseline against which a learned model must earn its complexity.","url":"todaes.html#model"},{"title":"Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits · The optimization procedure, with the safeguards preserved","text":"The optimization procedure, with the safeguards preserved Train the classifier on extracted, simulated layouts, including width changes. Reason: schematic-only labels omit the mechanism being optimized. Relax the width variables temporarily and search cheaply using the fixed learned model. One method uses Bayesian optimization; another differentiates through the model while treating widths as the optimized parameters. Use multiple starts to reduce sensitivity to local optima. This improves exploration but does not certify a global optimum. Discretize candidate widths. The paper rounds near-integer values and partly enumerates floor/ceiling combinations for ambiguous values. Enumeration can grow exponentially; a production implementation needs an explicit limit. Realize the candidate in detailed routing, preserving legal dimensions and symmetric width assignments. The physical result, including fallbacks, is the candidate that must be evaluated. Simulate a shortlist (ten by default in the experiments), then choose using simulated performance. This last step prevents the surrogate's favorite false positive from becoming the final answer. §5 and Algorithm 1, PDF pp. 12–14 The paper reports widths realized as multiple parallel routes on its gridded technology. Those are not electrically identical to replacing them with one continuous wide rectangle: access vias, current sharing, lengths, coupling, and neighboring clearances differ. Keep the realized topology in Philis's route model. Width realization, PDF pp. 15–16","url":"todaes.html#algorithm"},{"title":"Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits · Evidence, baselines, and limits","text":"Evidence, baselines, and limits The experiments use 12 circuit cases, ASAP7 and GF12, 2,000 generated layouts per schematic, and held-out test samples. They compare uniform widths, conventional simulation-guided BO, WAGN-guided BO, TensorFlow-based optimization, and transfer variants. Transfer is examined between topologies within the same circuit family; the authors explicitly identify transfer across families, regression, large circuits, and process variation as unresolved limitations. §6 setup, PDF pp. 14–17 Limitations, PDF p. 23 The headline 11% improvement or 8.7× speedup is relative to particular BO budgets and hardware. The timings include a distinction between training cost included and excluded; parallel label generation and sequential BO also use resources differently. Count data generation, training, detailed-route retries, extraction, and simulation in a Philis comparison. Runtime discussion and Table 6, PDF pp. 18–19 Reported 99% routability and limited die-area increase describe the tested layouts. Congested analog blocks, guard-ring obstacles, wide spacing rules, or pin access can make width changes much harder. A fallback to the original width must trigger reevaluation; otherwise the score describes geometry that was never built.","url":"todaes.html#experiments"},{"title":"Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits · Concrete Philis integration","text":"Concrete Philis integration DetailedCfg currently has base widths, signal/supply fattening, width-dependent spacing, and current-based trunk widening. Add legal per-net or per-segment width options as candidates, with corresponding contact/via strategies. Keep EM minima as feasibility conditions and circuit performance as the separate optimization target. Share width choices across electrically matched routes where justified. The current total-C winner selection can favor thinner wires even where source resistance dominates. First establish an RC-aware circuit evaluator; then run a small discrete search on critical nets using real extraction/simulation. Only after this baseline exists should model-guided search be compared at equal total cost. Log which net or segment changed, predicted metric effect, realized geometry, measured effect, and any model error.","url":"todaes.html#engine"},{"title":"Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits · Reading coverage and limits","text":"Reading coverage and limits Read PDF pp. 1–27: motivation, formulation, graph construction, attention/pooling, training, both optimization algorithms, experiment setup and results, parameter analysis, manual annotation comparison, limitations, references, and detailed appendix tables. Visually checked PDF p. 19 for runtime and performance table interpretation. These are source results; no WAGN model was trained and no circuit speedup was measured in this documentation session.","url":"todaes.html#coverage"},{"title":"Bipolar devices, diodes and precision structures · Choose the electrical quantity before choosing the pattern","text":"Choose the electrical quantity before choosing the pattern A bipolar transistor is an injection device. Collector current depends exponentially on base-emitter voltage in its useful forward-active regime, while base resistance, collector voltage, recombination, leakage and high-level injection modify the relationship. A diode-connected bipolar device adds the question of whether the external circuit controls collector current or emitter current. The optimum layout therefore depends on whether the specification is an absolute forward drop, a difference in base-emitter voltages, a current ratio, a temperature coefficient, power handling or protection behavior. This distinction is operational. A current mirror can need equal collector voltages and low base-access resistance; a bandgap pair deliberately uses different current densities while requiring very close temperature tracking; a power output transistor needs distributed current collection and safe operating area. They cannot share a single “matched BJT” penalty based only on geometric area. PDF pp. 429–483 , 506–529 First input contract. Record device family and qualified variant, temperature and current-density range, which current is forced, which terminal voltage is sensed, permitted saturation and reverse-bias events, and what quantity must match. Add the circuit's allowable error over those conditions. The layout optimizer may choose unit arrangement and access geometry inside that contract; changing emitter degeneration or current bias belongs to circuit design.","url":"topic-bipolar-diodes.html#bjt-objective"},{"title":"Bipolar devices, diodes and precision structures · Bipolar transistors and diodes need their own matching and interaction rules","text":"Bipolar transistors and diodes need their own matching and interaction rules Vertical bipolar devices. Match emitter geometry and use identical unit emitters for ratios. Active emitter area and perimeter determine injection, while base resistance, base/emitter contacts, collector resistance, Early effect and saturation alter the terminal currents. Compact clusters and common-centroid arrays help with temperature and stress fields, but contact and base distributions also need balancing. Placing collector contacts outside an array can bring its critical emitters closer together. When emitters share a base, do not blindly exploit the smaller “connected-emitter” spacing: overlapping depletion regions can alter carrier flow even when the terminals share a net. PDF pp. 506–526 Lateral bipolar devices. Both emitter and collector geometry matter because current flows laterally between them. A larger single emitter can reduce current gain, so the source often favors arrays of small emitters rather than simply enlarging one device. Match the collector's inner perimeter, base/contact environment and applicable field plates. A saturated collector can reinject carriers into neighboring collectors; equal nominal base nets do not make shared tanks harmless. Separate isolation regions can be worth their area where collector efficiency or saturation threatens matching. PDF pp. 527–529 Operating-point limits. Keep precision bipolar devices in their characterized current-density range, below problematic high-level injection and away from low-current leakage/recombination dominance. Equalize collector voltages when the circuit requires suppression of Early-effect error. Emitter degeneration transfers sensitivity from a transistor to a resistor and costs voltage headroom; it is an architectural option, not something the placer can add. Guard against reverse emitter-base stress, which can degrade current gain before an obvious catastrophic failure. A ratioed bandgap pair also relies on accurate temperature tracking; a common centroid does not remove all nonlinear thermal behavior. PDF pp. 509–523 , 526 PN diodes. Diode-connected transistors inherit their parent family’s matching limitations. Control the actual regulated current: emitter-current regulation includes base current, so it is not identical to collector-current regulation. Match junction shape, contact arrangement, guard structures, material and surrounding diffusions. Distinguish series resistance from junction-voltage error and include leakage over temperature. PDF pp. 565–571 Zeners. Localized avalanche can let one region with the lowest breakdown voltage dominate the entire junction. Consequently, increasing area does not generally deliver inverse-square-root matching at low current. Surface walkout/walkback and unequal accumulated charge can produce later divergence. Use a characterized structure and lifecycle model; neither a circular outline nor CC placement can promise precise Zener matching. PDF pp. 566–567 Schottky diodes. Barrier material, inhomogeneity, contact perimeter, field relief and series resistance matter. A field-relief guard junction can improve field uniformity but can also inject minority carriers when it forward biases ( §11.2, PDF p. 563 ). The source's comparison of aluminum and noble-silicide Schottkies demonstrates why material-specific primitives are necessary; it is not a license to synthesize an unqualified barrier. PDF pp. 568–571 For Philis, a bipolar/diode matching rule should name the matched output—V BE , current ratio, forward drop or reverse breakdown—and the controlled terminal conditions. Reject the tempting abstraction “all matched devices get the MOS area formula.” Validate geometry through extraction and the circuit's operating scenarios, including potential saturation and transient injection.","url":"topic-bipolar-diodes.html#source-bipolar-diodes"},{"title":"Bipolar devices, diodes and precision structures · Worked examples: area ratio, base current and temperature","text":"Worked examples: area ratio, base current and temperature Under the simplified matched forward-active model I C = A J S exp(V BE /V T ), two equal collector currents and an emitter-area ratio N produce a base-emitter-voltage difference with magnitude V T ln N. This equation assumes equal temperature and saturation-current density, neglects series resistance and Early effect, and is used here to expose sensitivities rather than replace a compact model. Area-ratio example. Let N = 8 and assume V T = 25.9 mV. The intended magnitude is approximately 53.9 mV. If the realized ratio is larger by 1%, the resulting change is 25.9 mV × ln(1.01), approximately 258 μV. This is why drawing one emitter eight times larger can be inferior to constructing a ratio from identical emitter units: changes in perimeter, contact arrangement and dimensional bias may fail to track. Identical units do not eliminate random variation, but they make more systematic error mechanisms common. Current-definition example. Suppose equal emitter currents are applied to two transistors with β values 100 and 80. Because I C = I E β/(β+1), their collector-current ratio becomes (100/101)/(80/81) ≈ 1.00248. In the same simplified exponential model this alone gives about 64 μV of V BE difference. The example does not predict a process's β distribution; it demonstrates why an emitter-current constraint is not equivalent to a collector-current constraint. Layout can improve matching of the relevant structures but cannot erase a circuit's sensitivity to uncontrolled base current. Thermal example. Suppose characterization of the chosen circuit gives a differential forward-voltage sensitivity of −1.8 mV/K at its operating point. A 0.05 K differential temperature then contributes about 90 μV. Those coefficient and temperature values are hypothetical design inputs, not universal constants. They show why a visually compact pair near one hot transistor may still miss an accurate reference's budget. Evaluate each device's electrically weighted temperature under all active power modes; distance to a heater is only a screening proxy. PDF pp. 509–523 The three examples belong in separate budget terms. Area/perimeter bias, β-dependent current error and differential temperature do not necessarily share a statistical distribution or cancel each other. Report their assumptions and correlations. Summing all worst cases is conservative; root-sum-square is justified only for the terms modeled as independent random contributions.","url":"topic-bipolar-diodes.html#bjt-example"},{"title":"Bipolar devices, diodes and precision structures · Diode matching and isolation must follow the operating mode","text":"Diode matching and isolation must follow the operating mode A PN diode, a Schottky junction and a Zener are not alternate symbols for the same physical primitive. In forward-biased PN operation, minority carriers can enter surrounding material; a Schottky barrier has different material and perimeter sensitivities; an avalanche device may conduct primarily through a small local region. Consequently, the engine must select matching and protection rules by device construction and operating mode. For a forward-biased PN reference, use identical junction construction, contact geometry and guard surroundings, then separate junction-voltage error from current-dependent series-resistance error. For a reverse-biased sensing diode, leakage, collection area, perimeter and coupling may dominate. For a Zener reference, use characterized breakdown and accumulated-stress behavior; increasing total area does not establish an inverse-square-root mismatch law when the weakest breakdown region dominates. For a Schottky rectifier, check barrier qualification, current crowding, series resistance and the possibility that its guard junction injects when forward biased. PDF pp. 534–573 The collecting-guard figure illustrates a second distinction: electrical isolation depends on carrier paths in the substrate, not only on a drawn surrounding rectangle. Identify the injected carrier, the region it traverses, the junction that should collect it, the applied bias and the return impedance. A shallow ring can leave a deep path; an uncontacted or debiased ring can lose collection efficiency. A majority-carrier substrate tap and a minority-carrier collecting ring can serve different purposes even when their top views appear similar. PDF pp. 256–270 Sharing a well or tank also changes these paths. A saturated lateral collector can reinject carriers that another collector receives. Net equivalence and passing nominal LVS do not prove that merging is benign across switching and overload scenarios. Permit a merger only through a qualified template whose current-density, bias and injection restrictions are retained in the layout record.","url":"topic-bipolar-diodes.html#bjt-guards"},{"title":"Bipolar devices, diodes and precision structures · Generation and validation procedure for Philis","text":"Generation and validation procedure for Philis Select a qualified primitive. Preserve its exact vertical or lateral family, recognition layers, voltage limits and allowed transformations. The reason is that collector geometry and isolation cannot be inferred from an abstract BJT symbol. Enumerate unitizations. Keep identical emitter units for ratio-sensitive structures, while respecting current-density and base-resistance limits. Larger units can reduce some random effects but worsen access resistance or lateral-transistor gain. Assign physical units to electrical devices. Balance active emitter locations and, where relevant, base and collector access. A ratioed array's connectivity determines the correct weights; a bounding-box centroid is insufficient. Construct the complete environment. Add contacts, collector access, guards, dummies if qualified, wells and local supply returns. Reject contact arrangements that introduce unequal terminal drops or violate precision spacing despite being permitted for ordinary connected emitters. Route the actual sensed quantities. Preserve Kelvin joins where the circuit requires them. Match finite base-current paths when they contribute error, and include collector-voltage differences in the Early-effect calculation. Extract and evaluate scenarios. Check DC matching, leakage at temperature extremes, transient saturation, reverse emitter-base stress, substrate injection and relevant thermal distributions. A power transistor additionally needs a qualified forward/reverse safe-operating-area check. Retain a reasoned result. Report the modeled quantity, every constraint's source, extracted values and unsupported effects. A geometry that passes a transistor-ratio test at one bias has not thereby proven a bandgap's temperature curvature or a power cell's turnoff survival. Required PDK information. Obtain recognized cell variants, emitter/perimeter models, β and Early-effect distributions or sensitivity models, permitted current densities, saturation/injection restrictions, reverse-stress limits, guard recipes and substrate stack, thermal/package assumptions and any power-device SOA data. These should be versioned facts, not coefficients inferred from a single textbook figure. Focused regression examples. Compare identical emitters with unequal base-contact distances; equal emitter currents with unequal β; a matched pair exposed to a controlled thermal gradient; a lateral pair with one saturated collector; and a PN diode whose reverse leakage matters more than its nominal forward voltage. The expected outcome is that the checker identifies the correct mechanism, not merely that the common-centroid metric is zero.","url":"topic-bipolar-diodes.html#bjt-procedure"},{"title":"Capacitor units and parasitic environments · Define what capacitance the circuit actually uses","text":"Define what capacitance the circuit actually uses A capacitor can set a time constant, integrate current, divide charge, sample a voltage, filter noise or resonate with an inductor. Each function responds differently to plate parasitics and losses. For a ratioed charge-redistribution array, the intended signal is a relation among charges in a network of switches and capacitors. The relevant object is therefore the extracted capacitance matrix and its switching states, not only the sum of PCell values. Hastings' capacitor chapter develops intended capacitance, fringing fields, nonlinear junction and MOS behavior, series resistance and different plate-to-surrounding parasitics. Those mechanisms explain why top/bottom plate identity and the electrical role of a shield should be part of circuit intent. A generic two-pin rectangle loses that information. PDF pp. 327–354","url":"topic-capacitor-devices.html#cap-function"},{"title":"Capacitor units and parasitic environments · Capacitors: match the whole charge-transfer network","text":"Capacitors: match the whole charge-transfer network The book distinguishes junction, MOS, poly-poly, metal-metal, stacked, lateral-flux and trench capacitors. Their voltage dependence, leakage, plate resistance, substrate parasitics, dielectric absorption, breakdown and density differ. Two terminals on a schematic do not imply interchangeable plates in layout. Even when the intended capacitance matches perfectly, bottom-plate or lead coupling can change charge redistribution. PDF pp. 327–354 Action Why it improves the relevant behavior Limit or validation Use identical unit structures for integer ratios, with a consistent plate assignment. A common dimensional bias and peripheral capacitance then scale with unit count rather than creating a different fractional error for each capacitor. Prefer parallel composition where consistent with the schematic. Series combinations expose asymmetric plate parasitics and require circuit-level analysis. For noninteger ratios, preserve the relevant area/perimeter relationship and verify corners. Matching area alone does not match fringe contributions; complex outlines introduce additional OPC and corner effects. Hastings discusses a nonunit capacitor supplement. Use field extraction to validate it instead of assuming equal A/P is exact. Prefer compact square or nearly square units and search unit size. A compact unit lowers perimeter per area; subdividing a large capacitor can reduce gradient error. Excessively small units make edge error and wiring dominant. Choose from PDK-supported cells; measure the ratio of random, peripheral, gradient and routing contributions over candidate sizes. Place matched units adjacently with equal row and column pitch, using compact CC arrangements when justified. Dielectric thickness and other fields vary spatially. Matched first moments suppress their first-order contribution. Row pitch need not equal column pitch. Include access tracks, dummies and routing before evaluating compactness. Preserve the same structure beneath every active unit. Different underlying oxide, moat edges, lower-metal patterns or topography change capacitance and local strain. Reserve controlled under-array regions. Either a qualified continuous plate or a qualified absence of lower material is preferable to accidental asymmetric routing. Give all array edges a characterized dummy environment. Dummies reduce edge etch differences and uncontrolled coupling from neighboring circuitry. Hastings recommends surrounding capacitor arrays on all four sides. Tie dummy electrodes to an intentional node; floating dummies can acquire charge or unpredictable coupling. Shield against external electric fields where the added load is acceptable. A low-impedance shield makes the electrostatic boundary more predictable and can contain fringe fields. Extract shield resistance and capacitance. Do not permit a noisy line over an array merely because a shield exists; attenuation is finite. Match the leads by capacitance, including their surroundings. Lead width, layer, crossings, neighbors and fill alter vertical and lateral coupling. Equal length alone is not equal capacitance. Extract a capacitance matrix. Dummy extensions or jogs are justified only if they reduce the circuit's ratio error without violating total-load, settling or antenna budgets. Assign the more substrate-coupled plate using circuit impedance and noise context. A low-impedance terminal is less disturbed by injected substrate charge than a high-impedance sensitive node. Preserve top/bottom plate intent from the circuit designer. A well shield or isolated structure brings additional junctions, bias constraints and area. Choose dielectric and electrode options using measured mismatch and voltage behavior. Electrode depletion, dielectric variability, dielectric relaxation and stress sensitivity differ strongly among capacitor types. The text favors certain thick, homogeneous dielectrics and metallic electrodes for precision; the actual PDK's characterization decides availability and merit. Separate extreme-accuracy arrays from strong temperature/stress gradients. Even comparatively stable capacitors have residual thermal and mechanical sensitivity; high-permittivity materials can be especially stress-sensitive. Package, temperature and actual capacitor family determine the needed precaution. “All capacitors are temperature-insensitive” is not a safe simplification. These actions synthesize the thirteen matching recommendations and their antecedents. PDF pp. 423–425 , 380–382 Philis acceptance criterion. For a switched-capacitor network, assess the extracted network in each switch phase and under the intended charge-transfer operation. An absolute capacitance error, a ratio error, a parasitic to a quiet rail and a parasitic to a switching aggressor have different consequences. The physical unit pattern should therefore be evaluated together with its route topology and phase-dependent connections.","url":"topic-capacitor-devices.html#source-capacitors"},{"title":"Capacitor units and parasitic environments · Worked example: a perfect unit ratio with a wrong total ratio","text":"Worked example: a perfect unit ratio with a wrong total ratio Suppose A uses one 100 fF unit and B uses two identical 100 fF units. Their drawn ratio is exactly 2:1. To isolate the arithmetic, assume the connecting leads add capacitance directly between the same two electrical terminals as each intended capacitor: 2 fF for A and 3 fF for B. The effective ratio is then 203/102 ≈ 1.9902 rather than 2. Its fractional error is about −0.49%. This example deliberately makes the lead parasitic act in parallel with the intended device. Real arrays are more complicated: a parasitic to ground, a switching bottom plate or a neighboring bit does not always add in that way. The appropriate procedure is to extract the matrix, apply the switching phases and compute the actual transfer or settling error. The simple example establishes why “identical units” is a starting condition, not a final matching result. Equal lead length is insufficient. One line may lie on a different layer, cross a ground strap, pass a dummy electrode, or run beside a switching signal. The same physical length then has a different electrostatic environment. The source therefore emphasizes lead capacitance, widths, crossings, shielding and surrounding material rather than length alone. PDF pp. 380–382 , 425","url":"topic-capacitor-devices.html#cap-worked"},{"title":"Capacitor units and parasitic environments · Why the unit-size choice has an optimum","text":"Why the unit-size choice has an optimum Increasing unit dimensions reduces edge/perimeter contribution relative to area and can improve local random matching. But a larger unit samples more spatial variation, makes a larger array and may complicate routing. Subdivision improves the ability to balance spatial moments while adding dummies, contacts and lead capacitance. The optimum depends on which term currently dominates the circuit error. A practical search. Start with qualified unit cells across a small size range. For each, generate a compact balanced pattern with its final dummy boundary and legal access. Estimate random/peripheral mismatch using the characterized model, assess first and second moments for known or bounded fields, route the array, and extract the total matrix. Reject candidates that miss settling, voltage, leakage, density or area requirements. Rank the remaining candidates by the specified transfer-error or yield objective. Do not maximize dispersion without a stopping criterion. If every improvement in device-gradient balance adds a disproportionately long top-plate route, the final conversion accuracy can get worse. Conversely, a slightly larger routing envelope may be worthwhile if it permits well-controlled, symmetric shielded connections. The correct comparison includes the entire array and the switches or reference connections that determine its operation. For noninteger ratios, a special extra unit can compensate nominal area and fringe terms, but sharp corners, added holes or narrow features can create new manufacturing sensitivity. Characterize the actual geometry. A matched area/perimeter ratio is a first-order construction aid, not proof of identical field solutions at every bias. PDF pp. 374–378 , 423–425","url":"topic-capacitor-devices.html#cap-size"},{"title":"Capacitor units and parasitic environments · Choose shielding and isolation for a specific disturbance","text":"Choose shielding and isolation for a specific disturbance An electrostatic shield works by providing a controlled boundary whose potential changes little under injected charge. That requires an appropriate connection and sufficiently low impedance over the relevant frequency range. A thin long ground lead can let a nominal shield move; a shared noisy ground can inject disturbances rather than suppress them. The shield also adds capacitance, changes routing access and may affect density or nearby devices. A well underneath a capacitor can isolate a plate from the bulk substrate, but introduces a junction and requires a valid bias. If that junction forward biases during a switching excursion, the circuit can inject minority carriers. Dummy electrodes also need intentional connections: they are not electrically absent merely because the netlist calls them dummy. The ideal tie depends on plate configuration, noise and the permitted voltage range. Boundary test. Compare the same array with a quiet shield, a resistive shield return and a shield tied to a switching reference. Extract the changes and simulate the actual measurement phase. This discriminates physical attenuation from a geometric “shield present” flag. Repeat after final fill because nearby floating or tied metal changes fringe coupling.","url":"topic-capacitor-devices.html#cap-shield"},{"title":"Capacitor units and parasitic environments · PDK inputs and signoff questions","text":"PDK inputs and signoff questions Obtain nominal and nonlinear capacitance models, local mismatch coefficients and their geometry domain, top/bottom plate parasitics, dielectric-loss/leakage behavior, voltage and lifetime limits, supported electrode/dielectric stacks, dummy/fill rules and extraction technology. Where junction or MOS capacitors are used, include bias conditions and temperature dependence. Where trench or lateral-flux structures are available, use their specific characterization rather than forcing a parallel-plate approximation. The final report should name the circuit metric, unit geometry, unit counts, moment balance, route and shield topology, extracted matrix, evaluated switch states, mismatch/yield assumptions and remaining uncertainty. A valid teaching conclusion is “this layout removes first-order spatial error under the assumed field and meets the extracted settling budget.” A claim of universal optimality or guaranteed converter accuracy needs substantially more evidence.","url":"topic-capacitor-devices.html#cap-evidence"},{"title":"Constraint architecture and consistency · A constraint system is the executable record of design intent","text":"A constraint system is the executable record of design intent The engine needs to know not only what a rule checks but why the rule exists, which objects it applies to, how it was derived, and what would invalidate that derivation. A flat list of penalty functions cannot answer those questions. A constraint system should connect high-level electrical specifications to physical choices and verification evidence while preserving the distinction between mandatory requirements, limited budgets and optional preferences. The constraint-engineering chapter of Analog Layout Synthesis treats generation, transformation, management and verification as separate activities. This is a useful architectural foundation: recognize an electrical relationship, express its intent, choose a valid physical implementation, and verify the result. Conflating these stages can make one convenient implementation, such as geometric symmetry, appear to be the original electrical specification. Topological Approaches, PDF pp. 281–291 . Proposed engine principle: every imposed action should carry a short causal explanation. “These devices move together because they share a required symmetry axis” is different from “these devices are pulled together because a calibrated spatial mismatch model predicts lower offset variance.” The former is an exact relation once selected; the latter is a model-based improvement with tradeoffs and a domain of validity.","url":"topic-constraint-systems.html#constraints-purpose"},{"title":"Constraint architecture and consistency · Give each requirement a complete semantic record","text":"Give each requirement a complete semantic record A useful record contains a stable identity; semantic kind; target objects; physical units; predicate or objective; enforcement mode; numerical tolerance; applicable scenarios; provenance; parent requirements; model revision; and evidence status. A separate expression captures alternative implementations or disjunctions. Keep these fields explicit because their meanings affect optimization and reporting, not merely logging. Field Why it matters Example Target identity Survives hierarchy changes and generated variants Electrical transistor pair plus all physical units realizing each member Scope Prevents a local check from claiming a global guarantee A routed branch, a whole net, a matched array or a power domain Mode Separates required feasibility from useful preference Exact symmetry equality versus shorter-wire objective Units and normalization Prevent unit changes from changing priorities Millivolts of allowed drop, then a dimensionless residual Scenario and validity Make model assumptions inspectable Specified bias, temperature, layer and width range Provenance and parents Explain which obligation a child protects Resistance allowance derived from a voltage-drop limit Evidence status Distinguish absent information from success Passed, failed, unknown or genuinely not applicable Philis already has an important separation in Requirements<On> (line 18): hard, budget and cost collections. Rule (line 9) exposes satisfaction, residual, usage, applicability, projection, touched objects and retargeting. Preserve that useful evaluation interface while adding stable semantic records around it. The hot scoring path need not parse a large provenance graph on every move; compiled evaluators can reference immutable records by ID. Default objective-only rules should remain clearly objective-only. If a rule's default satisfaction predicate always returns true, registering it as a hard requirement without a real predicate does not make the physics checked. Add a construction-time capability check: each enforcement mode requires the evaluator methods needed to support its claim.","url":"topic-constraint-systems.html#semantic-record"},{"title":"Constraint architecture and consistency · Preserve the chain from specification to physical action","text":"Preserve the chain from specification to physical action Suppose a sensitive supply branch allows at most 2 mV of DC drop and carries a verified 100 µA worst-case current. The parent electrical condition implies total branch resistance no greater than 20 Ω. If a chosen via structure consumes 4 Ω, the wire has 16 Ω left. On a layer with R□ = 0.08 Ω/square and a selected width of 0.4 µm, a uniform-wire approximation gives L ≤ 16 × 0.4 / 0.08 = 80 µm . This is an invented worked example, but every derivation step is dimensionally explicit. Traceable lowering of an electrical requirement A voltage-drop specification and branch-current model derive a resistance allowance. Via resistance and a sheet-resistance model derive a wire-length screening cap. Extraction returns evidence to the original requirement. Drop ≤ 2 mV at specified branch current I = 100 µA scenario + analysis revision Rtotal ≤ 20 Ω 4 Ω via → 16 Ω wire Lwire ≤ 80 µm only for chosen layer and width Extracted branch → verify actual drop evidence Download this SVG Original provenance diagram with illustrative numbers. The geometric cap is a conditional screening rule; extracted electrical evidence closes the original requirement. If the router changes width, layer, via count or branch topology, the 80 µm child constraint becomes stale. The voltage-drop requirement still stands. Recompute the child or evaluate the parent directly. If branch current changes after resizing, invalidate the entire dependent chain. This is the practical reason to retain parent-child links rather than copying 80 into a field named “maximum length.” The generic ParasiticBudget::extract helper documents placeholder length budgets, but the active annotator routing path constructs per-net limits from class/load-derived capacitance allowances divided by the process wire capacitance per unit length. Preserve that existing derivation and expose its provenance and assumptions. Extend it with circuit-performance sensitivities where supported; otherwise retain its heuristic status. A geometric shortcut and an electrical certificate can coexist, but their evidence levels must remain distinct.","url":"topic-constraint-systems.html#provenance"},{"title":"Constraint architecture and consistency · Generate intent first; choose the implementation afterward","text":"Generate intent first; choose the implementation afterward Pattern recognition can identify a differential pair, current mirror or stack. That is evidence of a functional relationship, not proof that every member must use one particular physical pattern. A differential pair may need matching, equivalent electrical environment and suitable isolation; an ABBA arrangement, a two-dimensional common centroid or another validated template may implement those needs with different routing costs. Retain alternatives until the implementation choice is justified by process, ratios, geometry and electrical priorities. Topological Approaches, PDF pp. 288–291 . The annotator's placement (line 53) already translates recognized blocks into matching, proximity, thermal, centroid, symmetry and DTI rules. Extend each emitted requirement with its origin: recognition rule, circuit members, confidence or checked preconditions, and a numerical parameter source. For example, the thermal difference and proximity constants near lines 37–43 are tuning defaults rather than PDK-derived physical limits. Preserving that distinction makes later calibration possible. Constraint transformation needs a stated logical relationship. A conservative child rule should imply its parent within declared assumptions; a heuristic proxy merely correlates with parent improvement; an exact reformulation is equivalent. These are not interchangeable. An independently allocated capacitance box may imply a coupled performance bound but exclude feasible candidates outside the box. That is a deliberate conservative transformation that can be relaxed or reallocated while preserving the parent. Meta-rules can combine measured quantities, layout geometry and simulator results. The source discusses this broader verification model because DRC and LVS alone do not establish all analog intent. Implement meta-rules with explicit dependencies and deterministic evaluation order; a rule depending on extraction cannot be marked passed using a placement-only snapshot. PDF pp. 291–298 .","url":"topic-constraint-systems.html#generation-transformation"},{"title":"Constraint architecture and consistency · Keep identities and weights through merging, splitting and hierarchy","text":"Keep identities and weights through merging, splitting and hierarchy Physical transformations are often many-to-many. One schematic device can become several fingers; several devices can share a macro; a shield requirement can apply across several connected shapes; a matched array may span hierarchy boundaries. A one-to-one device-index remap is insufficient to preserve every semantic relation. Maintain a realization map from electrical entities to weighted physical units and terminals. Consider an electrical common centroid with weights wi. Its x condition is ΣA wi xi / ΣA wi = ΣB wi xi / ΣB wi , with a corresponding y condition. The weights should represent the intended matched quantity, commonly equal unit counts or calibrated electrical contribution. A macro's bounding-box area can change when a halo or pin-access region changes while its electrical contribution remains identical. Replacing wi by that area then changes the meaning of the constraint. Philis's CentroidGroup::centroid (line 32) uses drawn bounding-box area, and its retargeting repeats IDs when members share a cell. Treat that as a geometric approximation unless the generator guarantees proportionality to intended electrical weights. A stronger interface preserves unit weights and first moments through collapse. Verify that changing a non-electrical halo does not change the target electrical centroid. Stable IDs also matter to optimizer state. The current order contract at lines 15–17 states that prices are matched by kind and ordinal among that kind; reordering silently moves prices. Prices::bind (line 70) and keys implement this mapping. Assign persistent semantic IDs and explicit model revisions so insertion of a new thermal pair cannot transfer another pair's learned price. Carry branch identity by the same principle.","url":"topic-constraint-systems.html#identity-hierarchy"},{"title":"Constraint architecture and consistency · Distinguish inconsistent intent from a failed search","text":"Distinguish inconsistent intent from a failed search Take a mirror pair with xA + xB = 2a . Suppose fixed placements require xA = 10 and xB = 170, while a fixed axis requires a = 100. The first two imply a = 90; all three together are inconsistent. Moving the axis would resolve the conflict only if the axis is genuinely free. A solver should return the conflicting assumptions, not endlessly increase a symmetry penalty or move a fixed block silently. A small inconsistent constraint set Fixed coordinates imply a symmetry axis at ninety, conflicting with a required axis at one hundred. The diagram identifies the contributing requirements. Fixed A: xA = 10 Fixed B: xB = 170 xA + xB = 2a therefore a = 90 Required a = 100 Contradiction Report the core; change only an authorized free choice. Download this SVG Original conflict example in abstract coordinates. Removing an optional axis preference differs from overriding a required fixed axis. Check easy contradictions before expensive search: impossible fixed equalities, empty variant domains, inconsistent ratio requirements, impossible die bounds, incompatible orientations and units. Check integer-grid compatibility explicitly. Mirror and centroid equations can require half-grid coordinates; a doubled-coordinate representation can express them exactly where the permitted physical grid allows it. Rounding each partner independently can turn a solvable continuous relation into an unacknowledged mismatch. For a linear or finite constraint subproblem, obtain an infeasible core or solver certificate when available. For a heuristic placer, distinguish “no solution found within this runtime and representation” from proven infeasibility. If a restricted hierarchy fails, widening the representation is different from relaxing the electrical requirement. The source's constraint-management discussion treats overconstraint and gradual removal of degrees of freedom as central design issues. PDF pp. 299–302 .","url":"topic-constraint-systems.html#conflicts"},{"title":"Constraint architecture and consistency · Use four outcomes: passed, failed, unknown and not applicable","text":"Use four outcomes: passed, failed, unknown and not applicable A thermal constraint with missing dissipation data is unknown unless a separate model proves the absence of relevant heating. A thermal constraint on a domain intentionally outside the thermal analysis scope may be not applicable for that stage. A routing capacitance check on an unconnected net is not evidence of complete electrical success even if its zero drawn length satisfies a cap. These distinctions prevent missing work from improving the score. The Rule::applicable interface (line 51) documents vacuous satisfaction for inapplicable rules. Preserve a separate completeness layer that distinguishes genuine inapplicability from absent model inputs. Each mandatory requirement needs an accepted outcome before final completion. Optimizers may temporarily use estimates, but final reporting should show which obligations remain unresolved. Verification evidence should identify candidate geometry hash, electrical mapping, PDK/rule-deck revision, extraction or simulation settings, scenario, evaluator version, numerical tolerance and outcome. A later reshape invalidates geometric and parasitic evidence touching that shape. A bias change invalidates current-dependent reliability evidence even if geometry is unchanged. Dependency-driven invalidation makes those effects explicit and avoids rerunning unrelated checks. Use cheap checks first when they can safely reject a candidate. Geometry legality and connectivity may eliminate a candidate before expensive circuit simulation. However, a cheap check cannot certify a stronger property than its model supports. The source's static versus dynamic checking distinction supports an incremental evaluator with snapshots rather than an undifferentiated final checklist. PDF pp. 291–298 .","url":"topic-constraint-systems.html#verification-status"},{"title":"Constraint architecture and consistency · Implementation sequence justified by failure prevention","text":"Implementation sequence justified by failure prevention Add immutable IDs, units and provenance around existing rule batches. This fixes price and diagnostic identity without replacing working evaluators. Represent generated, transformed and verified versions separately. A derived length cap can then be invalidated while the parent voltage-drop obligation survives. Preserve realization maps through cell generation and hierarchy. This prevents merged devices, finger multiplicities and terminal changes from corrupting matching semantics. Add pre-search consistency checks and explicit unknown outcomes. Early contradictions become actionable explanations instead of wasted search; missing analysis no longer appears as success. Attach candidate-specific certificates and dependency invalidation. This makes reported evidence correspond to the layout actually delivered. Verify identity preservation by reordering and inserting same-kind constraints while confirming prices and diagnostics stay attached to their original semantic targets. Verify transformation soundness on tiny analytic electrical examples. Verify conflict explanations with the three-equation mirror example and a grid-incompatible variant. Verify evidence invalidation by changing only width, current or PDK revision and checking exactly the affected records. These tests exercise the promised semantics rather than mirroring individual methods. A complete constraint system does not mean inventing every conceivable rule. It means that every requirement actually in the design contract has a defined meaning, an implementation path, and evidence tied to the delivered state. That is the infrastructure needed to justify an “optimal layout” claim at any stated level of model fidelity.","url":"topic-constraint-systems.html#constraints-implementation"},{"title":"Constructive capacitor-DAC arrays · Define the electrical contract before filling a grid","text":"Define the electrical contract before filling a grid Start with the actual switch topology and reference scheme. For a conventional N-bit binary charge-scaling bank, the bit capacitors have 1, 2, 4, …, 2 N−1 unit weights, and a grounded dummy contributes one more unit, giving 2 N units in the bank. The name “dummy” here means an electrically intentional capacitor in the DAC denominator. It is different from perimeter dummy geometry used to regularize processing. Confusing these two kinds of dummy changes the transfer function. A split bank has separate LSB and MSB top-plate nodes connected through an attenuation capacitor. Record the bridge's two terminals, the individual bank totals, and the intended attenuation ratio. Do not merge all top plates because a binary-array routing template did so. Nor should P&R silently replace a binary DAC with a split DAC: that changes the circuit architecture, reference loading and error sensitivities. Binary and split circuit models, PDF pp. 2–4 Required constraint Reason Evidence needed Exact terminal graph and unit multiplicities A single short, swapped plate, or missing unit can cause a large code error. Connectivity/LVS that recognizes the generated capacitor family and bridge. Worst-code INL and worst-transition DNL Correct mean capacitance does not guarantee correct binary carries. Extracted effective capacitances, switch states and an explicit INL reference convention. Settling within an LSB fraction by the sample time A ratio-correct array can still be dynamically wrong. Terminal RC, switch/driver impedance and relevant transition patterns. Noise and reference disturbance budgets Small units increase kT/C effects; routing and switching disturb references. System noise allocation, bandwidth, reference impedance and transient simulation. Gradient, random mismatch and manufacturing margin Code errors vary with local fields and device statistics. Characterized covariance, edge sensitivity, density and legal unit structures. The paper focuses on construction and analytical linearity/settling models. The complete testbench above is a proposed engine contract; switch, amplifier and reference nonidealities require more than the paper's idealized output model.","url":"topic-dac-arrays.html#contract"},{"title":"Constructive capacitor-DAC arrays · Why high-weight bits and carry transitions deserve special attention","text":"Why high-weight bits and carry transitions deserve special attention Let T(k) be the normalized settled output for code k, after applying a stated offset/gain reference. With ideal step q = 1/2 N , define DNL(k) = [T(k+1)−T(k)]/q−1. Define INL(k) = [T(k)−T ideal (k)]/q. These are definitions of measured transfer error, not a promise that a particular geometric score predicts it. Endpoint and best-fit references produce different INL values; store the reference with the result. Linearity definitions, PDF p. 4 Consider an illustrative 3-bit binary bank using the nominal full-scale reference, without gain removal: bit weights 1, 2, 4 plus a unit grounded dummy. Suppose only the MSB has an excess δ = 0.04 unit, with all other intended values exact. The total is 8.04. The 011→100 carry produces a step (4.04−3)/8.04 = 1.04/8.04 ≈ 0.12935 of full scale. Relative to a nominal 1/8 step, DNL ≈ +0.0348 LSB. The arithmetic depends on this normalization and simplified model, but it reveals why an aggregate RMS capacitor error can hide the important failure: multiple smaller bits switch against the large bit. Action. Store the bit identity of every physical unit through placement, routing and extraction. Evaluate the transfer across all codes when tractable, or use a justified screening strategy followed by the critical transitions. Reject a candidate on its worst required error, not the average error across capacitors. For dynamic tests, also include transitions that produce large reference steps, simultaneous switching or asymmetric initial conditions; the static worst carry need not be the dynamic worst case. Thermal noise sets another conditional lower bound on the effective sampling capacitance; it becomes a unit-size constraint only after the topology and noise allocation are fixed. Independent derivation, with a source gap: for one ideal linear capacitor fully equilibrated through resistance at temperature T, classical equipartition assigns its fluctuating voltage energy ½C·Var(v)=½kBT , giving Var(v)=kBT/C . The assumptions are thermal equilibrium, a classical frequency range and an ideal isolated sample after equilibration; finite settling, correlated sampling and complete converter topology change the result. No exact kT/C passage was located in the supplied PDFs, so do not attribute this equation to the DAC paper. A real charge-redistribution network has several noise sources and transfer factors. Derive the allowed unit capacitance from the system noise allocation before asking the placer to minimize area. Increasing unit C may reduce relative mismatch/noise while increasing reference energy, switch loading and settling time.","url":"topic-dac-arrays.html#accuracy"},{"title":"Constructive capacitor-DAC arrays · Unit geometry, odd counts, and what “common centroid” can actually certify","text":"Unit geometry, odd counts, and what “common centroid” can actually certify Use a qualified unit cell with explicit plate pins, layer occupancy, legal transforms, dummy requirements and capacitance-model identity. Identical drawn area alone is insufficient: perimeter fraction, fringe environment, plate orientation, vias and overlying fill may differ. Every unit should carry its electrical weight and local active geometry; an array's outer bounding box is not its capacitance-weighted centroid. Even-count groups can be placed as centrally reflected pairs so their equal-weight first moments vanish about the array center. The bank's two one-unit members cannot both occupy the unique center of a nonoverlapping grid. The paper places these members close to the center. Report their nonzero moment residual rather than certifying exact centroid coincidence for the entire bank. Odd unit counts in more general ratios need the same honest treatment. Construction and one-unit exception, PDF p. 7 A larger bank does not guarantee better matching. A compact contiguous bit has short routes but samples a restricted region; a highly dispersed bit samples more of the spatial field but uses longer wiring and more vias. If the process variation is uncorrelated, average unit count dominates variance. If it is spatially correlated, the signed covariance expression dᵀΣd determines ratio variation. If it is systematic, moments or a characterized spatial field determine error. Keep those mechanisms separate, as explained in gradient matching and random mismatch .","url":"topic-dac-arrays.html#unitization"},{"title":"Constructive capacitor-DAC arrays · Construct several pattern families because the objectives conflict","text":"Construct several pattern families because the objectives conflict Spiral construction. Place low-count capacitors near the center and grow outward, adding units in centrally reflected pairs. Favor adjacency within the same bit so bottom-plate routes form relatively few components. The reason is electrical: high wire and via resistance can dominate array speed. The cost is weaker spatial dispersion and potentially larger nonlinear-gradient or correlated-variation error. Chessboard construction. Disperse the largest bit across one alternating color and recursively allocate the other positions to smaller bits. This spreads electrical weight across the bank and exposes different members to more similar fields. The price is a fragmented bottom-plate graph: many pieces need branches, trunks and vias. A geometric improvement can therefore reduce settling quality. Block chessboard construction. Group some same-bit units into connected blocks while retaining a broader alternating structure. Vary block size and assignment. This provides intermediate candidates between maximum dispersion and minimum internal routing. The useful optimization variable is a family of legal connected patterns, not an arbitrary label permutation that later proves unroutable. Spiral, chessboard and block variants, PDF pp. 7–8 Proposed Philis enumeration. For each allowed outline/aspect ratio, generate a bounded set of these families. Attach first/higher moments, bit adjacency counts, perimeter exposure, route channel demand and estimated terminal RC. Discard exact-topology or manufacturing failures. Keep nondominated estimates for detailed internal routing and electrical evaluation. Record rejected patterns and reasons so a failed array can be diagnosed as a missing legal representation, channel shortage or performance conflict. Do not bake the paper's correlation coefficient or length into the generator. The authors explicitly describe these commonly reused parameters as inherited assumptions rather than measured capacitor statistics. Parameter sweeps can compare robustness to uncertainty, but only qualified data support a manufacturing-yield claim. Limits of the statistical parameters, PDF p. 12","url":"topic-dac-arrays.html#families"},{"title":"Constructive capacitor-DAC arrays · Route the two plate networks with different objectives","text":"Route the two plate networks with different objectives On each bottom-plate bit graph, find connected components of neighboring same-bit units, for example with breadth-first search. Connect nearby units with branches, connect components with trunks, then join trunks through peripheral bridges as needed. Mirror the required connections for symmetry while preserving legal pin access. This reduces arbitrary routing decisions and makes the cost of fragmentation explicit. Top plates share a node in a binary bank, so a tree can connect them while controlling length and top-to-substrate loading. In a split bank, construct separate top-plate trees for the two nodes. An MST is a useful low-cost starting topology under its edge model; it is not necessarily the minimum-delay or minimum-error tree once terminal loading, layer choices and extraction enter. Bottom- and top-plate routing, PDF pp. 8–10 Extract a terminal network that distinguishes intended plate-to-plate C, unwanted coupling between a bit route and a top plate, coupling to substrate/rails, and route resistance. Added coupling between two nodes contributes differently depending on their switching state. “These two nodes move together” is a scenario-specific simplification, not grounds to erase their physical capacitance from every simulation. Widening the currently slowest bit or adding a qualified parallel track can improve delay. Recompute every bit after each change: the critical bit may move. Include the added capacitance and routing congestion, and verify legal via access at both ends. A parallel line that is only well connected at one end is not the assumed parallel resistance. This connects directly to wire sizing .","url":"topic-dac-arrays.html#routing"},{"title":"Constructive capacitor-DAC arrays · Derive the settling requirement instead of copying a frequency label","text":"Derive the settling requirement instead of copying a frequency label For a single-pole full-scale step with residual error exp(−t/τ), reaching half an N-bit LSB requires exp(−t/τ) ≤ 2 −(N+1) , hence t ≥ (N+1)ln(2)τ. This derivation assumes a single pole, full-scale initial error and that half-LSB allocation. If other errors consume part of the budget, the allowed residual is smaller. A distributed RC network or nonlinear switch needs its actual transient response. The paper's Eq. (32) reports f = 1/[2(N+2)ln(2)τ] under its charging/discharging assumptions and labels it a 3-dB frequency. Preserve that model as a distinct paper metric. It is neither the generic first-order pole 1/(2πτ) nor a universal sample-rate limit. The handbook deliberately separates these definitions so an implementation does not optimize the wrong specification. Delay and frequency model, PDF pp. 5–6 A practical evaluator records the switch sequence, available settling interval, output load and measurement time. It reports the maximum residual over required codes, process/voltage/temperature and mismatch scenarios, plus reference disturbance. Use an efficient RC estimate to screen many layouts, then transient-simulate the candidates near the accuracy or speed limit.","url":"topic-dac-arrays.html#settling"},{"title":"Constructive capacitor-DAC arrays · A split-DAC bridge needs its own geometry and sensitivity model","text":"A split-DAC bridge needs its own geometry and sensitivity model The attenuation capacitor is often a noninteger multiple of the unit. Simply shrinking one dimension to obtain the desired nominal C may change its fractional edge sensitivity relative to the units. Under the paper's simplified MOM geometry, a common small edge shift δ gives approximately ΔC/C = 2δ(1/H + 1/l), where H and l describe its effective coupling geometry. The construction solves both the target capacitance and an equal effective perimeter-to-area sensitivity condition. Nonunit attenuation capacitor, PDF p. 6 The reason is ratio stability: the bridge and unit bank should respond similarly to common geometric errors. For Philis, enumerate legal quantized candidates near the analytic solution, extract them with the same neighboring environment, and compare their effective attenuation sensitivities. The analytic equations are a starting point for the stated MOM structure, not a general MIM or fringe-capacitor rule. Include bridge parasitics in the complete split-network transfer; its isolated nominal capacitance does not certify correct attenuation.","url":"topic-dac-arrays.html#bridge"},{"title":"Constructive capacitor-DAC arrays · Where this fits in Philis and how to prove the improvement","text":"Where this fits in Philis and how to prove the improvement The current capacitor generator constructs merged plate/comb geometries with a bounded set of aspect variants. A dedicated array generator should retain per-unit bit identity, internal route graphs and separate plate terminals, then expose a macro interface to global P&R. Do not claim that the existing per-device primitive implements the paper's routed array algorithms. Connectivity proof: compare generated and extracted plate/bridge graphs, including the electrical dummy and every bit count. Reason: a good matching score is meaningless on the wrong circuit. Spatial proof: compute actual weighted moments and a stated covariance model on placed units. Reason: a named pattern does not prove its instantiated weights or odd-count exceptions. Route proof: extract branch/trunk/via RC and check every bit's charging path. Reason: high dispersion can lose on electrical delay. Transfer proof: evaluate code INL/DNL and settling with explicit references and operating scenarios. Reason: these are the actual converter constraints. Comparative proof: compare spiral, chessboard, block and a simple baseline at equal unit size, testbench, PDK, legal outline policy and compute budget. Reason: an improvement must not come from an unreported size or simulation advantage. The acceptance result should show feasible alternatives and their margins: one design may offer better matching at a slower rate, another lower area at higher reference energy. This is the applicable meaning of “optimal” for a constrained converter, rather than a universal pattern ranking.","url":"topic-dac-arrays.html#implementation"},{"title":"Circuit intent and physical identity · Recognize a circuit role, then explain its consequence","text":"Recognize a circuit role, then explain its consequence A differential pair is valuable to recognize because differential behavior depends on balanced devices and paths. A current mirror is valuable to recognize because current ratios depend on device parameters, bias, and connection resistance. A series stack suggests proximity and an ordered connection path, but it does not automatically imply that its devices should occupy a common centroid. A clock net suggests switching activity and aggressor potential, but only the waveform and victim transfer path determine the resulting noise. Technique. Use terminal-labeled connectivity and device types to identify candidate motifs. Validate geometry, bias domains, body connections, and the surrounding circuit before turning those motifs into physical instructions. Hierarchical recognition reduces ambiguity by using both local patterns and the parent function. See the constraint-system chapter and the topological survey for structural constraints and their transformations. Preserve identity from schematic devices to physical units Two device owners map into an ABBA macro. Internal unit positions, terminals and weights remain available after merging. Preserve identity from schematic devices to physical units Device A G, D, S, B Device B G, D, S, B Macro outline ≠ electrical member geometry A B B A Each unit keeps owner + terminals + weight A halo or outline change must not move the active-area centroid. Sharing diffusion is legal only when the actual terminal nets agree. Download this SVG Original illustrative diagram; not PDK-valid or to scale. Macro placement still needs an internal electrical and geometric certificate. The diagram deliberately separates a recognized motif from a layout action. The same motif can justify multiple constraints, and some of those constraints may be alternatives. Keep the derivation instead of storing an unexplained boolean “matched.”","url":"topic-design-intent.html#recognition"},{"title":"Circuit intent and physical identity · Keep three identities: electrical device, unit, and macro","text":"Keep three identities: electrical device, unit, and macro An electrical transistor may be implemented as many fingers, a resistor as many series segments, and a capacitor as many parallel units. Several electrical devices can then be combined in one physical macro. The identities are related, but they are not interchangeable. Identity Information it must retain Failure if discarded Schematic device Device model, parameters, terminal names/nets, ratio and circuit role. A physically valid array may realize the wrong W/L, multiplicity, series/parallel connection, or bias domain. Physical unit Parent device, active footprint, local coordinates, orientation, neighbors, unit terminals and internal route. Collapsing to a macro center can erase real gradient, thermal, or routing mismatch. Macro candidate External pins, obstacles, legal orientations, variants, certified internal invariants, extraction model. The global placer may choose an orientation or route access that invalidates the macro's assumptions. Reason for this representation. A global coordinate transformation should move all of a macro's units while preserving their relative geometry. A change of internal pattern, however, changes the matching certificate and route graph. The engine needs to know which kind of transformation happened.","url":"topic-design-intent.html#identities"},{"title":"Circuit intent and physical identity · Derive units from circuit quantities","text":"Derive units from circuit quantities Suppose a current mirror requires 1:4 nominal strength using identical gate length and unit width. Implementing it with one versus four equal units can preserve the intended geometrical ratio, but the bias and connection resistance still need evaluation. If the source regions carry different nets, sharing diffusion is electrically illegal even if the DRC checker sees a perfectly legal polygon. If the transistor model is asymmetric, reversing source and drain may change behavior. For a resistor ladder, a 1:4 ratio built from identical series segments has a different electrical composition from parallel resistor segments. For capacitors, parallel units add capacitance; series units add reciprocally and change internal node behavior. Unit counts are therefore a typed electrical construction, not a generic multiplier. Action. Give each unitization a declared composition law, terminal map, exact integer ratio when applicable, and a method to reconstruct the schematic equivalent. Test. Flatten the generated macro and compare it to the intended device relation, including dimensions and intermediate nets. Do this before using matching or area estimates to select a variant.","url":"topic-design-intent.html#unitization"},{"title":"Circuit intent and physical identity · Specify what kind of symmetry is intended","text":"Specify what kind of symmetry is intended Distinguish geometric mirror symmetry, common-centroid moment equality, orientation balance, matched route impedance, and equal environment. They often support each other, but none implies all the others. A rectangular mirror of a transistor may swap source/drain orientation. A CC ABBA array can have unequal diffusion-edge distances. Two mirrored routes can see different neighboring aggressors. Technique. State symmetry constraints at the level of their purpose: which objects correspond, what transformation maps them, which terminals must map, and which exceptions are permitted. A symmetry group should be local to its relevant circuit structure; forcing an unrelated entire chip onto one axis can create long or crossed routes without an electrical benefit. The CC review's small-device examples demonstrate why a recognized matched pair should not automatically force a single pattern. Generate candidate constructions, then evaluate their LDE, parasitic, and variation effects. §V, PDF pp. 6–7","url":"topic-design-intent.html#symmetry"},{"title":"Circuit intent and physical identity · A constraint needs a reason and provenance","text":"A constraint needs a reason and provenance A useful derived constraint answers seven questions: what requirement it protects; what physical mechanism links the two; which objects and terminals it applies to; what process or simulation evidence sets its limit; how it is enforced; when it is inapplicable; and how it is validated. Without those answers, adding more constraints can make search less successful while giving no better circuit. Worked example. “Keep net X under 10 fF” is incomplete. A justified version might say: in the specified closed-loop OTA testbench, a local perturbation around the present extracted circuit gives a settling-time sensitivity of a particular value; the remaining settling margin allocates an estimated capacitance allowance to X; the allowance applies only in that model's trust region and must be refreshed when the route topology changes. The actual numerical value must come from the design, not this example. Reason. This records the connection between a proxy and a circuit requirement, permits conflict resolution, and prevents a stale derived limit from surviving a topology or bias change. Performance budgets develops the mathematics.","url":"topic-design-intent.html#constraints"},{"title":"Circuit intent and physical identity · Handle recognition uncertainty and explicit intent","text":"Handle recognition uncertainty and explicit intent Structural recognition can misclassify a device pair, and net names can be misleading. A signal named “vdd_sense” may be a sensitive measurement rather than a power trunk. A shared source is evidence for a differential pair, but the surrounding switching phases can matter. Treat inferred role, explicitly declared role, and measured behavior as distinct evidence. Procedure. Preserve user overrides; record each motif match and its confidence/reason; provide an explanation for every emitted rule; and reject contradictory hard instructions with a localized conflict report. Do not silently drop one of two constraints because it was encountered later. Where intent remains uncertain, use soft guidance or multiple candidates until electrical validation resolves the choice. Philis already has recognition overrides in AnnotationConfig . The improvement is to expose and propagate why a classification or constraint was produced, so later validation can refer back to it.","url":"topic-design-intent.html#override"},{"title":"Circuit intent and physical identity · Current Philis behavior to preserve and extend","text":"Current Philis behavior to preserve and extend annotate builds recognized blocks, recovers primitive children, derives net classes, and assembles placement, routing, and cell constraints. placement distinguishes paired primitives from stacks, and puts matching/centroid guidance in the cost arm. enumerate filters merged candidates with shared-pad or gate-strap shorts. These are useful existing foundations. Preserve the original device→unit→macro mapping through retargeting. Add structured reasons when a merged pattern is rejected and a device is emitted separately. Recompute or invalidate certificates when variants, dimensions, orientations, or internal connections change. Otherwise a constraint can remain syntactically present while no longer describing the geometry being optimized.","url":"topic-design-intent.html#current"},{"title":"Circuit intent and physical identity · Validation cases that teach the contract","text":"Validation cases that teach the contract Permute internal terminal storage while preserving explicit terminal labels and connectivity: role recognition and antenna attribution should remain correct or reject the unsupported representation explicitly. Do not permute positional SPICE terminals and assume the circuit is unchanged. Make a same-size transistor pair with different body domains: the generator must not merge wells or taps illegally. Give two fingers different source nets: diffusion sharing must be rejected even if the resulting polygon would pass spacing rules. Collapse an ABBA group into a macro, rotate it, and verify its internal moments and terminal map still evaluate correctly. Use a ratioed resistor/capacitor example with series and parallel alternatives: only the electrically correct composition may satisfy the reference netlist. Provide an explicit do-not-identify override: no downstream stage should resurrect the forbidden inferred grouping. These cases are discriminating because they target information loss at stage boundaries. They do not merely test whether a known pattern name produces a known rectangle.","url":"topic-design-intent.html#tests"},{"title":"Electromigration and interconnect lifetime · Evidence and limits","text":"Evidence and limits This chapter synthesizes all five chapters of the supplied EM book. Equations and via diagrams were checked in rendered pages 30, 83, 101, 118, 127, 138, and 139. The drawings, pulse calculation, temperature example, and engine architecture are instructional synthesis. Historical roadmaps, example material data, and proposed carbon-based routing technologies are not treated as current foundry capabilities. A final reliability claim requires characterized process rules and an explicit application profile; the chapter defines what must be supplied and verified to make that claim meaningful.","url":"topic-electromigration.html#coverage"},{"title":"Fabrication mechanisms and geometry · From masks to an electrical object","text":"From masks to an electrical object The first task in analog placement and routing is to know what a shape means physically. A layer name and a rectangle are insufficient: the same N-type region can be an ohmic contact, a collector, a diode cathode or a collecting guard, depending on its depth, enclosing material and voltage. A process stack determines which regions connect, which form junctions, and which are separated only while a junction remains reverse biased. Those distinctions explain why a layout can be DRC-clean and nominally LVS-correct yet inject current into a neighbor during a transient. Hastings develops this argument through device physics, fabrication and three representative processes. The engine should use those chapters to understand mechanisms, then obtain actual dimensions and legal constructions from its PDK. Do not convert an old process example into a modern generic cell. The representative-process chapter uses standard bipolar, polysilicon-gate CMOS and analog BiCMOS as archetypes. Appendix C explicitly says its numerical example rules represent older process classes and do not correspond to any single real process. PDF pp. 63–64 , 154–210 , Appendix C, PDF p. 836","url":"topic-fabrication.html#fab-purpose"},{"title":"Fabrication mechanisms and geometry · Fabrication knowledge must travel with the geometry","text":"Fabrication knowledge must travel with the geometry Chapters 1–4 explain why shapes on different layers are not interchangeable obstacles. Diffusion extends beyond the drawn opening; depletion extends beyond the metallurgical junction; implanted dopants have tails; oxide steps alter topography; local pattern density alters printing, etching, or polishing; contacts and vias depend on the actual metal process. Minimum dimensions include manufacturing tolerances and electrical requirements. Consequently, a device footprint alone cannot express all interactions. §§2.3–2.6, PDF pp. 79–95 , §2.7, PDF pp. 99–110 , §3.2.3, PDF pp. 138–144 Engine action Physical justification Scope and required evidence Store device variant, well/tank, implant, isolation and terminal semantics alongside polygons. The same drawn diffusion can serve as emitter, collector, resistor body, guard ring or unwanted junction depending on surrounding layers and bias. Use foundry-qualified primitives and extraction definitions. A DeviceKind label alone is insufficient. Distinguish drawn geometry from effective electrical geometry. Etch bias, diffusion and depletion change active dimensions; contacts and heads can change the effective length of a resistor. Use PCell/model parameters and fitted bias terms within their characterized geometry range. Attach an allowed-transform set to each primitive and matched group. Rotating a subcell can break relative device orientation. Directional implants and non-self-aligned dimensions can invalidate apparently harmless reflections. Transform terminal directions, active centroids, well-edge distances and environmental descriptors with the geometry. Treat fill, exclusions, slots and protective layers as part of the final candidate. CMP topography, hydrogen diffusion, mechanical stress, and capacitive coupling depend on nearby material, including electrically inactive shapes. Rerun critical checks after fill insertion, slotting and final mask operations, not only before them. Use technology-specific contact/via templates and actual polygon grids. Old aluminum openings and planarized plug vias have different stacking and enclosure constraints; legal path centerlines can still produce off-grid edges. Validate the emitted shapes against the actual rule deck. Do not infer stackability or enclosure symmetry from layer count. Separate process features that exist from fallbacks that merely produce geometry. A deep well, buried layer or trench cannot provide isolation if the process does not fabricate the required continuous structure. Missing characterization should produce a capability limitation or conservative alternative, never a invented default guarantee. Chapter 4's standard bipolar, polysilicon-gate CMOS and analog BiCMOS examples are valuable as contrasting process stacks: their substrate/well topology, available devices and parasitics differ. They argue for a process capability model rather than a single universal rulebook. Appendix C's example rules are teaching data, useful for synthetic generator exercises; they must not override installed PDK rules. PDF pp. 154–210 , 836–844","url":"topic-fabrication.html#source-process"},{"title":"Fabrication mechanisms and geometry · Drawn dimensions, effective dimensions and environmental effects","text":"Drawn dimensions, effective dimensions and environmental effects A drawn rectangle is the request sent to manufacturing. Printing, etching and lateral diffusion change the dimensions that matter to conduction. Depletion then changes the conducting cross-section under bias. The PCell may already account for some of these effects; extraction and compact models may account for others. Applying a second ad hoc correction can double-count an effect, while ignoring all corrections treats nominal artwork as a measured electrical device. Worked example. Consider two nominally equal sheet-resistance materials patterned into strips. If both have width 2 μm and the same process widens each by 0.1 μm, the fractional width change is equal. If one strip instead has width 1 μm and the other 2 μm, the same absolute widening produces different fractional changes: 10% versus 5%. Their resistance ratio changes even when the drawn lengths were chosen to give the intended ratio. The numbers are an illustrative perturbation, not a claim about a specific process. The engineering response is to prefer equal-width units or explicitly optimize sensitivity to the dimensional bias. A similar argument applies to capacitors: area and perimeter do not scale in the same proportion when the dimensions change. Contact end effects introduce an approximately additive resistance that cannot be removed by scaling length alone. Source/drain extension, well-edge spacing and STI proximity can change transistor behavior without changing its drawn channel dimensions. Each mechanism needs its own state variables. Neighborhoods operate at several scales. Adjacent gates and dummy shapes affect local etch/printing; oxide steps and well boundaries affect topography and strain; metal-density windows affect polishing; package features act across much larger distances. One isotropic halo cannot faithfully represent all these effects. A halo is a conservative routing abstraction, useful when qualified, but should retain its underlying mechanism and layer set. PDF pp. 139–150 , 374–385 , 692–704","url":"topic-fabrication.html#fab-effective"},{"title":"Fabrication mechanisms and geometry · How to translate a process fact into an engine rule","text":"How to translate a process fact into an engine rule Identify the object and mechanism. For example, “well implant scattering near a MOS channel,” rather than the vague instruction “keep devices away from edges.” This determines which geometry and device variant are relevant. Identify the physical input. The model may require distance to an edge, orientation, well shape, neighboring diffusion, terminal voltage, temperature or fill density. Missing inputs should be explicit. Choose the representation. Some requirements belong in a qualified cell template; others belong in a placement constraint, route restriction, extracted device parameter or signoff check. Enforcing a cell-internal rule only after routing makes avoidable rework likely. Attach the validity domain. State process option, device family, temperature/bias range and geometry range. A model fitted to one long-channel device cannot establish a guarantee for a different oxide or pocket implant. State the consequence. Report the quantity affected, such as threshold shift, current-ratio error, junction leakage or capacitance. This lets circuit sensitivity determine priority. Validate after transformation and composition. Rotation, merging, abutment, fill and neighboring blocks can change the originally qualified environment. Verification must consume the assembled layout. Data model proposal. Give each primitive a stable PDK identity, device/extraction model, legal parameter and transform domains, active electrical moments, terminal polygons, well/body topology, local environment requirements and characterization provenance. Keep geometry rules and electrical models related but distinct. A database of scalar distances is useful for polygon legality, but cannot by itself represent a voltage-dependent junction or an aging-dependent mismatch constraint. Validation example. Generate a qualified MOS cell in two legal orientations, then put one copy next to a well edge and one in the well interior. DRC may accept both. The LDE-aware extractor should report the difference that the device model expects; the analog checker should decide whether it spends too much of the circuit's error budget. If the extractor has no LDE model, report that limitation rather than assigning the same certified current to both.","url":"topic-fabrication.html#fab-translation"},{"title":"Fabrication mechanisms and geometry · Process and package choices that placement cannot invent","text":"Process and package choices that placement cannot invent Fabrication choices include available resistor materials, capacitor dielectrics, gate oxides, isolation regions, thick metal and buried layers. They can change the best layout dramatically, but most are fixed before placement begins. The engine can compare permitted alternatives; it cannot assume an extra mask or unsupported material exists. Similarly, packaging sets thermal paths, stress distribution, bond rules and external inductance. A thermal isolation recommendation is meaningless unless the package model and power scenario are identified. Keep a distinction between geometrically possible , extractable , modeled , and qualified . A creative merged structure might meet dimensions and be recognized by a custom extractor while still lacking reliable characterization. Production automation should favor known templates, retain experimental alternatives separately, and state which evidence would be needed to promote them. The highest-value process questions to answer next are: which LDE parameters the compact models accept; how PCells emit those parameters; what transformations and abutments are qualified; which density/fill and antenna steps belong to the final flow; and whether the selected package has a usable thermal/stress abstraction. Those answers determine how far a physical “optimality” claim can go.","url":"topic-fabrication.html#fab-boundaries"},{"title":"Floorplanning, hierarchy and interfaces · Floorplanning chooses the physical environment of the circuit","text":"Floorplanning chooses the physical environment of the circuit Analog floorplanning is the decision about which circuit blocks share space, heat paths, substrate paths, routing resources and package surroundings. It is not finished when every rectangle fits. Devices that match inside a macro can experience a different external well boundary or fill environment after integration. A short net can be a poor route if it crosses a noisy return. A compact core can leave no channel for protection currents or a sensitive Kelvin connection. The source's die-assembly chapter connects hierarchy, die size, pads, floorplanning, metallization and final review. That ordering is useful for automation: establish the boundary and interfaces early, reserve the resources that cannot be recovered cheaply, then refine placement with extracted electrical feedback. Keep circuit roles, physical constraints and process/package requirements visible throughout the hierarchy. PDF pp. 781–828","url":"topic-floorplanning.html#floor-purpose"},{"title":"Floorplanning, hierarchy and interfaces · Hierarchy is an interface contract, not a wall around the physics","text":"Hierarchy is an interface contract, not a wall around the physics A macro interface should describe more than its outline and pin coordinates. It needs allowed transforms, routing access, supply and return requirements, voltage domains, well and guard interfaces, sensitive/noisy-net roles, thermal power scenarios, fill or RF exclusions and relationships to devices outside the macro. A pair that must match across hierarchy should remain a named electrical group after flattening or composition. Rotation and reflection require special care. Rotating an entire matched block can preserve the relative geometry of its internal units, but it may change crystallographic orientation, source/drain direction, proximity to a heater or the surrounding package stress. Rotating one of two related subcells independently can directly break the intended matching relation. Transform the device descriptors, not merely the rectangle, then re-evaluate interactions with the outside world. A library's allowed transform set is a physical qualification statement, not an arbitrary optimization convenience. §15.1.1, PDF p. 783 (grouping matched devices); §13.2.1, PDF p. 691 (relative orientation); §13.3, PDF p. 715, rules 13–15 (package and thermal context). When optimizing hierarchically, keep a record of which quantities are exact and which are estimates. An abstract macro pin may stand for multiple access shapes; its estimated thermal source may later split among devices. The placement stage should expose enough information to detect obvious conflicts without claiming it has already verified the flattened layout. Final extraction must use the concrete integrated geometry.","url":"topic-floorplanning.html#floor-hierarchy"},{"title":"Floorplanning, hierarchy and interfaces · Worked example: the core is not always the die-size constraint","text":"Worked example: the core is not always the die-size constraint Consider a deliberately simplified square wire-bond die. There are 40 pads, allocated ten to each side, with a hypothetical 100 μm effective pitch. Suppose corner and assembly reservations require a further 400 μm per side. The pad plan therefore needs a 1,400 μm side. Separately, a 900 μm square core plus two 100 μm boundary reservations needs 1,100 μm. The illustrative die side is bounded by the larger requirement: 1,400 μm. If placement reduces the core to 800 μm, its boundary requirement falls to 1,000 μm, but the pad requirement remains 1,400 μm. Core compaction has not reduced die area in this model. The freed region may instead improve channel access, guard separation, matching area, thermal separation or return resistance. An optimizer that scores every square micrometer of core reduction as a die-area saving would misvalue this candidate. These dimensions are a worked abstraction, not package design rules. Real pads have unequal sizes and ESD structures; corners, bond trajectories, seal rings, scribe interfaces, power-pad multiplicity and bump arrangements complicate the bound. The engine should derive the external boundary from actual package and PDK constraints and expose whether the current design is pad-, core-, routing-, thermal- or assembly-limited. Historical textbook cost heuristics should not become current manufacturing economics. PDF pp. 785–810","url":"topic-floorplanning.html#floor-die"},{"title":"Floorplanning, hierarchy and interfaces · Make routing feasibility part of placement","text":"Make routing feasibility part of placement Pin access should be evaluated at the shape level. A pin centered on a macro edge is not usable if a guard, well enclosure, neighboring cell or routing-direction restriction blocks every legal approach. Critical nets may need a pair of comparable routes, a shield, a fixed join point or a wide-current transition. Those requirements consume more resources than ordinary shortest-path estimates imply. A useful early channel model counts separate reserved resources. As an illustrative accounting exercise, a corridor with ten nominal track positions might devote three equivalent positions to a wide supply, two to a return/shield arrangement and two to pin escape and via access, leaving only three for freely assigned signals. The exact decomposition depends on geometry and layers; overlapping reservations must not be double-counted. The point is to avoid equating minimum wire pitch with available routable capacity. At a corner, two channels that each seem adequate can fail jointly because turns and layer transitions overlap. Contact cuts require enclosure; a via array can exceed the width of its approaching wire; a wide conductor can conflict with output-grid parity. Use concrete access templates or a routing feasibility estimate that includes these effects. Preserve a feedback path from routing to floorplanning so congestion can trigger a move or channel enlargement before a late patch destroys matching. Route priority should follow consequence. Reserve power and protection paths, critical local access and sensitive/matched connections while placement remains flexible. This does not imply one universal sequence for every design; priorities depend on which resources are scarce and which nets have hard electrical constraints. Document the reason for each reserved corridor. PDF pp. 811–817","url":"topic-floorplanning.html#floor-corridors"},{"title":"Floorplanning, hierarchy and interfaces · Routing and floorplanning finish the analog circuit","text":"Routing and floorplanning finish the analog circuit Kelvin and star connections. The physical point at which sense and force conductors join determines which voltage drop the circuit measures. If they merge before the intended device or pad, shared current creates an unwanted offset. Separate net names and schematic resistors can assist LVS but do not prove the junction is in the right physical location. Likewise, matched emitters or sources should join an intended star at the actual location where common-mode rather than differential IR drop is desired. Include finite sense current and lead resistance for high accuracy. PDF p. 818 Noise. Coupling depends on aggressor slew, coupling capacitance, victim impedance and timing, not merely the aggressor's average frequency. A configuration bit that switches infrequently can still upset a sensitive measurement. Use circuit roles to separate incompatible nets, controlled crossing geometry, and shields tied to the relevant quiet reference. Check the shield's impedance; a shared noisy return can defeat its purpose. Slowing a signal is a possible circuit-level remedy only when its timing permits it. PDF pp. 819–820 Voltage and current. High-voltage spacing depends on the maximum voltage difference between adjacent conductors under allowed scenarios, not just their voltage to ground. EM is a local bottleneck condition; resistance is an integrated path quantity. The source's via illustration shows why vias near the inside of a corner carry more current than nominally identical outer vias. Prefer appropriate straight transitions and sufficient distributed access, then check the actual current distribution. PDF pp. 821–822 Capacity and access. Route pitch must account for via footprint, enclosure and output-grid legality, not only minimum line spacing. Reserve space for wide supplies, shields, matched routes and local access before filling channels with ordinary signals. At intersecting channels, available tracks can disappear into turning and via demand; a channel-width scalar alone is an imperfect congestion model. Use blockage/access-aware estimation early enough to revise the floorplan. PDF pp. 811–817 Thermal and stress placement. Conventional molded-package stress often has low gradients near the die center despite large absolute stress there. Heat-source avoidance can favor an off-center location. Bumps, pillars, die attach, board loading and multiple changing power sources create different distributions. Optimize the mismatch-relevant field difference over the active region, not simply distance from a heater or absolute stress. Preserve useful symmetry under unequal power scenarios rather than assuming equal power forever. PDF pp. 399–411 , 525 , 715 Whole-die feasibility. Core area and pad-ring requirements can limit die size independently. Pads, ESD devices, guard rings, seal rings, scribe interfaces, bumps, bond trajectories and assembly exclusions all consume resources. Put these structures and the actual pin order into the early floorplan. A smaller core may produce no die-area saving on a pad-limited chip; it may instead free area for lower resistance, extra taps, shielding or matching improvements. PDF pp. 782–810 Finalization. The book ends with a checklist covering DRC/LVS, high-current and resistance checks, antenna, ESD, latchup, dummy exclusions, noisy/sensitive routing, pad requirements, Kelvin locations, corner/slot/seal requirements and archiving. Philis can translate this into a reproducible evidence bundle: final layout and libraries, exact process/rule/model versions, extraction, evaluated scenarios, pass/fail/unknown results and constraint provenance. This is stronger than retaining only a final objective score. PDF pp. 823–825","url":"topic-floorplanning.html#source-routing-floorplan"},{"title":"Floorplanning, hierarchy and interfaces · A practical floorplanning workflow with explicit reasons","text":"A practical floorplanning workflow with explicit reasons Compile operating scenarios and interfaces. Classify blocks by sensitivity, injection/noise, power, temperature and voltage domains. A digital label alone is too coarse: an infrequently toggling trim line can still disturb a conversion. Build the actual die boundary. Place package pins, pads or bumps, mandatory exclusions, seals and primary protection. The reason is that these structures constrain both available area and current entry/exit paths. Place high-consequence regions. Reserve RF exclusion areas, high-voltage isolation, large power structures and precision matching groups. Use their actual access and environmental requirements, not only rectangular bounding boxes. Plan power, return and substrate networks. Evaluate where current joins the shared reference. Prevent a noisy return from traversing a sensitive reference segment merely because the nets have the same logical name. Place quiet groups using relevant fields. Compare thermal and package-stress mismatch over the active devices. A center preference or heater distance can be a first filter, but it is not the final evidence. Reserve and test routing channels. Include shields, matched access, via transitions, Kelvin endpoints and protection currents. Move blocks when feasibility estimates reveal a bottleneck instead of demanding impossible downstream routing. Integrate wells, guards and fill policies. Check that new surroundings preserve the intended device environment. A subcell's local rule pass does not automatically include integration effects. Flatten the relevant interactions and evaluate. Extract the complete interconnect and environments for the design's scenarios, then compare circuit metrics and reliability constraints. Keep a set of feasible alternatives when one trades area against precision or power loss. The output should explain why each block is where it is: pin-access demand, a noise separation, a symmetry relationship, a thermal requirement or an available routing corridor. An explanation like “the objective improved” is insufficient if it does not name the underlying electrical consequence.","url":"topic-floorplanning.html#floor-workflow"},{"title":"Floorplanning, hierarchy and interfaces · Inputs and checks that prevent a convincing but unusable floorplan","text":"Inputs and checks that prevent a convincing but unusable floorplan Obtain package geometry and mechanical restrictions, allowed pad/bump cells, seal/scribe rules, PDK voltage-spacing and well/guard rules, available routing layers and via constructions, power/current scenarios, noise budgets, thermal/package models and pin-order flexibility. Mark each as fixed, selectable from qualified variants or estimated. The optimizer must not assume it can move a package pin or change protection topology simply because doing so lowers cost. Useful adversarial checks include a pad-limited core that becomes smaller without reducing the die, a narrow channel that fails only when via enclosures are included, an independently rotated cross-hierarchy matching pair, a sensitive sense lead accidentally joined to force current early, a trim signal that switches only once but injects excessive charge, and an RF macro whose exclusion is violated by final fill. For each, retain the geometric and electrical evidence that caused rejection. Finally, review the finalized geometry rather than a pre-fill or pre-pad snapshot. Archive the exact layout, rule decks, models, extracted networks, scenario results and remaining external qualifications. A reproducible floorplan is one whose decisions can be re-evaluated when the PDK, package or circuit changes. PDF pp. 823–825","url":"topic-floorplanning.html#floor-qualification"},{"title":"Gradients, common centroid and higher moments · Derive constraints from the difference or ratio that matters","text":"Derive constraints from the difference or ratio that matters Many analog circuits depend more strongly on differences and ratios than on absolute device values: input-pair offset, mirror current ratio, resistor-ladder ratios, and capacitor DAC weights. Matching techniques arrange units so a shared physical variation affects the compared quantities similarly. The initial constraint is therefore an electrical requirement, such as a maximum input offset or ratio error; geometric matching constraints are derived means of satisfying it. A common process shift cancels only when the compared quantities have an appropriate common response. Equal transistors at different drain bias can convert the same parameter perturbation into different current changes. Resistors of different materials need not track temperature. Record device family, composition, bias, and metric before enforcing a pattern. Parameter-to-performance sensitivity, PDF p. 1 The random-mismatch chapter develops the bias and geometry implications.","url":"topic-gradient-matching.html#purpose"},{"title":"Gradients, common centroid and higher moments · Derive the centroid equality","text":"Derive the centroid equality For an equal-unit first-order model, write p(x,y)=p₀+gₓx+gᵧy . A device with m units samples the average of this field. Subtract the normalized values of A and B: p̄A − p̄B = gₓ(x̄A − x̄B) + gᵧ(ȳA − ȳB). x̄A=x̄B and ȳA=ȳB therefore cancel every linear gradient direction. Our extension to unequal contributing units replaces the ordinary mean with an explicitly justified electrical weighting. For ratio matching, normalize each member by its nominal value; this presumes an additive response to the specified field. The nth-order paper itself proves its recursive construction for two identically designed devices, not arbitrary weighted ratios. Comparing unnormalized coordinate sums of a 1:4 bank is incorrect because the larger member has four times the nominal contribution. Centroid equations, PDF p. 1 Additive unit model, PDF pp. 2–3 Constraint. Equal normalized active-unit first moments. Inputs. Unit coordinates, parent device, relevant electrical weights, exact ratio. Enforcement. Constructive pattern or explicit equality in the array solver. Reason. It eliminates the unknown linear gradient coefficients from the differential quantity. Limit. Macro bounding-box centers and areas are not electrical weights: guards, whitespace, and pin extensions can change the box without changing the active device. Equal first moments do not imply equal higher moments Three equal-pitch patterns compare clustered AABB, ABBA and two rows ABBA/BAAB. Their first and second x moments differ as stated. Equal first moments do not imply equal higher moments A A B B AABB: ΔMx = −2 pitch units A and B sample different mean positions. A B B A ABBA: ΔMx = 0; ΔMx² = 2 Linear x cancels; quadratic x² remains. A B B A B A A B Two balanced rows: ΔMx = ΔMy = ΔMx² = ΔMxy = ΔMy² = 0 Equal weights; centered, equal-pitch grid. Download this SVG Original illustrative diagram; not PDK-valid or to scale. Moments are normalized per member. Coordinates and moments use pitch and powers of pitch, respectively.","url":"topic-gradient-matching.html#moments"},{"title":"Gradients, common centroid and higher moments · Choose among patterns using the mechanism","text":"Choose among patterns using the mechanism Pattern Linear-field behavior Reason to keep it as a candidate Clustered AABB Separated A/B centroids along x. Simple wiring can minimize some parasitics; gradient and short-range correlated mismatch can be worse. Interdigitated ABAB Spatial mixing, but unequal centroids on a finite equal-pitch line. Can support balanced connections and diffusion sharing. The label “interdigitated” is not a CC certificate. ABBA Equal first moments for equal units on one row. Linear gradient cancellation, with potential inner/outer differences in LOD, dummies, contacts, and routes. ABBA / BAAB Equal first and second moments on a uniform two-row grid. Balances additional spatial modes and edge roles, at the cost of row connections and routing resources. The table describes coordinate properties, not a fixed circuit-performance ranking. The review demonstrates small CC transistor structures that perform worse because their parasitic and LDE mismatch exceeds their gradient benefit. Allow these patterns to compete where the design intent permits; make exact CC mandatory only when the corresponding requirement or approved intent demands it. §V-A–B, PDF pp. 6–7","url":"topic-gradient-matching.html#styles"},{"title":"Gradients, common centroid and higher moments · Higher-order gradient constraints","text":"Higher-order gradient constraints For a polynomial field, define Mₐᵦ=Σwᵢxᵢᵃyᵢᵇ/Σwᵢ . To cancel arbitrary second-order gradients, match x², xy, and y² in addition to x and y. Equal radial moments x²+y² alone miss anisotropic x²−y² and mixed xy fields. Place A at x=±1.5 and B at x=±0.5. ABBA has zero first-moment difference but an x² difference of 2. A quadratic field coefficient q therefore causes normalized mismatch 2q. The interactive moment laboratory demonstrates the difference between first-, second-, and third-order patterns. The nth-order paper recursively reflects a lower-order pattern, retaining labels at odd target orders and exchanging A/B at even target orders. Starting with two units per device, it reaches 2 n units per device. Constraint policy. Certify every requested monomial, but choose the required order from characterized spatial fields and circuit sensitivity. Reason for restraint. More units introduce contacts, boundaries, routes, and parasitics; algebraic cancellation alone does not justify their cost. Recursive construction and proof, PDF pp. 2–3","url":"topic-gradient-matching.html#higher"},{"title":"Gradients, common centroid and higher moments · Finite areas and nonlinear device response","text":"Finite areas and nonlinear device response Real devices occupy area. Their moments are integrals over the region that contributes electrically. Identical translated unit shapes often make internal shape contributions cancel, but differently shaped units require explicit treatment. A shared geometric center does not establish equal second moments. The physical process field and the electrical parameter may also differ. If oxide spacing t varies linearly, capacitance proportional to 1/t contains higher-order terms. Matching the first moments of t therefore does not prove exact capacitance matching. The DAC paper discusses how this nonlinearity leaves residual error as the gradient grows. Thickness-to-capacitance nonlinearity, PDF p. 12 Required model metadata. State whether a certificate concerns active geometry, threshold voltage, sheet resistance, capacitance, or sensitivity-weighted circuit contribution. Validate the approximation by perturbing the actual modeled process quantity. This keeps a geometric proof from being overextended into an unsupported electrical guarantee.","url":"topic-gradient-matching.html#finite"},{"title":"Gradients, common centroid and higher moments · Separate environment constraints from centroid constraints","text":"Separate environment constraints from centroid constraints Compare the distributions of well-edge distance, diffusion-edge distance, orientation, dummy environment, neighboring geometry, metal coverage, contact geometry, and thermal exposure. Any can differ at coincident centroids. For MOS LOD, inner and outer ABBA units can have different stress even with identical W/L. Swapping A/B roles across rows can balance some contexts, but the resulting device orientations and routes still require checks. LDE mechanisms, PDF p. 2 ABBA counterexample, PDF p. 6 The same centroid can hide unequal edge environments Two mirrored arrays show unbalanced edge exposure and a regularized perimeter with dummies. Dummy geometry requires qualified electrical ties. The same centroid can hide unequal edge environments Unequal nearby environment Qualified balanced surroundings A B B A Well / STI edge near A Centroid equality still holds. d d d d d d d A B B A d Dummies + matched edge distances Check ties, stress and route effects. d = process-qualified dummy; same count alone is not an environment match. Download this SVG Original illustrative diagram; not PDK-valid or to scale. A practical array must satisfy environment and terminal constraints as well as moment constraints. The right schematic illustrates end and top dummy context, not a complete required perimeter recipe. Enforcement technique. Generate contextual device variants with known edge conditions; use dummies and common pitch where characterized; constrain environment descriptors or their predicted device shifts. Preserve legal current orientation separately from macro mirror symmetry. The MOS construction chapter supplies the device-specific rules. Reason for separate diagnostics. A single opaque matching score cannot tell whether a failure requires more active area, a different well boundary, a changed pattern, or a better source connection. Report moment, environment, and route mismatches independently, then translate them into a shared circuit error budget when calibrated sensitivities exist.","url":"topic-gradient-matching.html#environment"},{"title":"Gradients, common centroid and higher moments · Proximity and dispersion protect different terms","text":"Proximity and dispersion protect different terms Proximity keeps devices in a region where process and temperature fields may be similar. Dispersion makes multi-unit devices sample the same field across an array. Maximum mixing is not automatically optimal because it can increase array extent, wire length, and vias. For linearized mismatch e=dᵀδp: Var(e)=dᵀΣd. d holds signed normalized electrical contributions; Σ contains unit variances and pairwise covariances. Reordering independent identical units at fixed total active area does not change the independent random term. Rearranging correlated units changes off-diagonal covariance terms and may reduce differential variance. Calibrate the spatial covariance model and check that it is positive semidefinite. The DAC authors explicitly warn that inherited correlation constants are not measured capacitor-process truth. Variation classification and covariance, PDF pp. 2–3 Correlation-data limitations, PDF p. 12 P&R metric. Use calibrated predicted differential variance where available; otherwise expose dispersion and distance as proxies with uncertainty. Sweep plausible correlation lengths and gradients instead of using one assumed number as a manufacturing constraint.","url":"topic-gradient-matching.html#covariance"},{"title":"Gradients, common centroid and higher moments · Construct a usable matched array, step by step","text":"Construct a usable matched array, step by step Select legal units and their composition law. Verify terminals, active contribution, and permitted orientations; otherwise the array may realize the wrong circuit. Choose counts and identify impossible exact conditions. Distinct nonoverlapping identical single-site units on the chosen grid cannot share one physical location; report an approximation or subdivide only if permitted. Generate coordinates satisfying the chosen moments. Use central pairs, recursive patterns, or integer optimization. Constructive equalities reduce the burden on global search. Constrain adjacency and environment. Enforce diffusion-net compatibility, well/tap rules, dummies, and orientation. A short cannot be repaired by relaxing a matching score. Reserve internal route resources. Expose pin access, contact placement, and corresponding electrical paths before accepting the pattern. Certify the drawn result. Recompute counts, moments, environment descriptors, connectivity, and internal parasitics after every relevant transformation. Compare extracted circuit outcomes. Use common variation samples across candidates, keeping feasible alternatives with worthwhile tradeoffs. Every step protects an assumption used by the earlier derivation. A moment certificate created before arbitrary internal routing is only a partial certificate.","url":"topic-gradient-matching.html#construction"},{"title":"Gradients, common centroid and higher moments · Worked ratio and odd-count cases","text":"Worked ratio and odd-count cases Let A contain two units at (−1,0),(1,0), and B contain four at (−1,−1),(1,−1),(−1,1),(1,1). Both normalized centroids are (0,0), while their nominal values retain a 1:2 ratio. Their normalized y² moments differ by 1. This pattern matches linear fields but not every quadratic field. One A unit at the origin and two B units placed symmetrically can share a centroid if the footprints fit. Two different nonoverlapping identical single-site units on the chosen grid cannot both occupy that origin. This restriction concerns that unit-cell representation; arbitrarily nested or differently shaped devices may have coincident centroids. A solver should explain that conflict or attach a quantified residual to an approximation. The DAC paper places its odd-count members near the array center and acknowledges that they are not exact CC members. Odd-count placement, PDF p. 7 Technique selection. Keep the circuit ratio fixed. If subdivision is allowed, check whether it changes contact resistance, perimeter effects, random mismatch, or device-model interpretation. If it is not allowed, do not manufacture an extra electrical unit merely to improve the picture.","url":"topic-gradient-matching.html#ratio"},{"title":"Gradients, common centroid and higher moments · Implementable constraints and discriminating tests","text":"Implementable constraints and discriminating tests The current CentroidGroup uses bounding-box areas and macro centers. Add a unit-layout certificate to generated macros, transform it with each macro, and evaluate matching by electrical member. Preserve local coordinates through device-to-cell collapse . Keep the existing global cost as a clearly scoped search proxy. For integer-grid equal-unit patterns, compare normalized sums by cross multiplication and bound arithmetic overflow. For sensitivity-derived floating weights, attach tolerances and model uncertainty instead of describing numerical equality as exact. Tests. Include ABBA with zero first/nonzero second moments; a ratioed bank; an impossible odd-count case; a rotated macro; one unit displaced by one grid step; altered dummies at fixed centroids; and equal placements with unequal internal routes. Each check should detect its own mechanism. Circuit simulation should confirm the direction and importance of the predicted improvement before the constraint receives a strong optimization priority.","url":"topic-gradient-matching.html#engine"},{"title":"Layout-aware sizing and retargeting · Device sizing and layout must negotiate a realizable circuit","text":"Device sizing and layout must negotiate a realizable circuit Sizing chooses an electrical design, while placement and routing create the geometry that changes its behavior. Treating those activities as completely independent creates a predictable failure: the nominally optimized schematic has no feasible low-parasitic physical implementation, or the layout's smallest cell variants consume the margin that sizing assumed remained available. Layout-aware sizing closes this loop by evaluating meaningful physical alternatives during electrical optimization. Lampaert discusses device generation, simulation-model synchronization and the limitations of a separated placement/routing flow. The layout-aware sizing chapter in the topological volume combines fixed topology, geometric constraints, layout evaluation and parasitic models inside a simulation loop. Graeb supplies the multiobjective and robust optimization formulation. Together they support an iterative architecture, not the claim that minimizing transistor area automatically minimizes a useful analog layout. Lampaert, PDF pp. 66–82 , pp. 167–172 ; Topological Approaches, PDF pp. 259–275 ; Design Centering, PDF pp. 75–83 .","url":"topic-layout-sizing.html#sizing-loop-purpose"},{"title":"Layout-aware sizing and retargeting · Separate electrical variables from implementation variables","text":"Separate electrical variables from implementation variables Electrical variables include total device width and length, multiplicity conventions, resistor or capacitor values, bias current and allowed device flavors. Physical variables include unit count, row/column arrangement, permitted finger realization, diffusion sharing, orientation, contacts, terminal access, placement topology, wire layers and widths. Some physical choices preserve the intended ideal netlist; others change the circuit or its model parameters and require an updated electrical reference. For a hypothetical legal refolding operation, total width may satisfy Wtotal = Nf × Wfinger with unchanged L. This identity alone does not prove electrical equivalence. Diffusion area and perimeter, gate resistance, contact distribution, source/drain assignment, local environment and model interpretation can change. A simulator's multiplicity parameter may not have the same statistical meaning as independent physical fingers. State the intended equivalence explicitly, then verify generated connectivity and extracted device parameters. Philis already preserves an important boundary. In Mosfet::enumerate (line 26), the generator keeps schematic finger count and per-finger width; the comments explain that an arbitrary refold would change the drawn device and LVS reference. It also withholds the ABAB interdigitation option because the current shared-diffusion construction would short drains at inter-device boundaries. These are physical/electrical constraints on the available variant space, not missing aesthetic patterns. Proposed action: classify variants into electrically equivalent implementations and intentional sizing changes. The first class can be searched within a fixed electrical design; the second invokes a sizing transaction that updates the schematic reference, unitization, statistical interpretation and parasitic model. This avoids using a convenient rectangle reshape to silently change the circuit.","url":"topic-layout-sizing.html#decision-spaces"},{"title":"Layout-aware sizing and retargeting · Why a larger device or smaller cell can be the wrong choice","text":"Why a larger device or smaller cell can be the wrong choice Larger matched area can reduce a calibrated random mismatch term approximately as σ ∝ 1/√(WL) . It also increases intrinsic capacitance and physical footprint. Derived scaling example: under an ideal long-channel strong-inversion square-law model in saturation at fixed drain current, gm ≈ √(2μCox(W/L)ID) , whereas a gate-capacitance component scales approximately with WL. Combining those scalings at fixed L gives gm/C ∝ 1/√W when that capacitance dominates. This algebra assumes the same mobility and oxide capacitance, neglects short-channel effects, and does not apply across a change of inversion regime. The capacitance scaling is an ideal gate-oxide area approximation at a comparable bias, ignoring overlap, fringe and depletion; actual Cgs and Cgd depend on bias. The model is illustrative and not a replacement for the PDK. Its source foundations are Hastings §12.1, Eqs. (12.3)–(12.4), PDF p. 576 , §12.1.1, Eqs. (12.6) and (12.9), PDF p. 577 , and the ideal planar capacitance relation in §7.1, Eq. (7.2), PDF p. 327 ; the gm/C combination above is our derivation. More fingers can reduce a long gate's resistance and change diffusion sharing, but add gate straps, contacts, local interconnect and access congestion. A compact common-centroid pattern can shorten some connections and lengthen others. A dummy or well extension increases bounding area while preserving a valuable local environment. Evaluate each implementation with the physical effects it changes, rather than assigning a universal cost to finger count or dummy count. Lampaert, PDF pp. 66–74 . A generated cell should therefore expose more than width and height: terminal shapes and access alternatives, unit membership, electrical mapping, occupied and reserved regions, diffusion geometry, relevant device-environment parameters, internal connectivity and model revision. A fast interface-only generator may support early search, followed by full geometry for selected variants; their agreement must be checked because the fast view guides the optimization. PDF pp. 73–74 .","url":"topic-layout-sizing.html#physical-tradeoffs"},{"title":"Layout-aware sizing and retargeting · Worked example: local area dominance can fail at the parent level","text":"Worked example: local area dominance can fail at the parent level Suppose two electrically equivalent cell templates have the same device and external load capacitance of 50 fF and drive through an illustrative 20 kΩ output resistance. Template A occupies 12×6 µm² = 72 µm², but its output access forces 95 fF of routed capacitance. Template B occupies 14×6 µm² = 84 µm² and exposes the output near its destination, producing 40 fF of routed capacitance. In a one-pole screening model fp = 1/(2πRoutCtotal) , A gives about 54.9 MHz and B about 88.4 MHz. If the requirement is at least 70 MHz, only B passes this model. A larger template can produce a better routed circuit The smaller cell has an output pin on the far side and a long route to the load. A slightly wider cell exposes its output near the load and uses a shorter route. Numerical capacitance and pole results are shown. A: compact cell, costly output access 72 µm² load Croute = 95 fF fp ≈ 54.9 MHz Fails 70 MHz screening target B: larger cell, useful output access 84 µm² load Croute = 40 fF fp ≈ 88.4 MHz Passes this local model Download this SVG Original illustrative comparison. Capacitances are assigned example values, not inferred solely from the drawn line lengths; a real candidate requires extraction and circuit verification. Pruning B solely because A is narrower and equal-height loses the only feasible parent-level result in this example. Shape-curve dominance is safe only with respect to the attributes and parent contexts represented in the comparison. The hierarchical chapter retains width/height alternatives and uses additional criteria, but a modern electrical interface may need terminal-access and parasitic classes as well. Topological Approaches, PDF pp. 137–147 . Action: retain a bounded Pareto set of implementation variants across footprint, sensitive-pin access, internal parasitics and required environment. A variant should be removed only when another is no worse for the relevant context or when the loss of search coverage is explicitly accepted. This keeps enumeration tractable without treating local geometry as the complete circuit objective.","url":"topic-layout-sizing.html#worked-interface-example"},{"title":"Layout-aware sizing and retargeting · Nested and joint optimization make different cost/coverage tradeoffs","text":"Nested and joint optimization make different cost/coverage tradeoffs A nested flow chooses an electrical sizing vector, generates legal physical variants, runs placement and routing, extracts parasitics and evaluates specifications. The outer optimizer changes sizing using those results. This cleanly separates tools but makes every outer evaluation expensive. A joint flow changes sizing and layout variables together, potentially discovering interactions sooner, but faces a larger mixed discrete/continuous search and more frequent model invalidation. Nested sizing and layout loop The outer loop proposes sizes. The inner loop generates variants, places and routes them, and extracts geometry. Electrical and robustness evaluation returns measured margins to the sizing optimizer and local feedback to layout. Sizing proposal W, L, bias, ratios Legal variants geometry + identity Place + route physical choices Extraction R, C, devices Circuit + robust performance checks margins, failure modes, model error local repair feedback Cache and warm-start only when model, topology and scenario identities remain valid. Download this SVG Original block diagram. The loop distinguishes physical feasibility, model prediction and actual electrical evaluation. A practical hybrid begins with a fixed electrical design and a small set of validated templates, improves their physical implementation, then allows controlled sizing changes when no physical alternative supplies enough margin. Local sensitivities suggest which sizes are worth changing. Regenerate geometry and re-extract after changes, because a new size can cross a contact-count, row-count or routing-topology boundary. A smooth surrogate through such a discontinuity must not be treated as exact. Warm-starting from the previous layout reduces cost, but can bias every new sizing toward the same local topology. Occasionally compare alternative initializations or templates, especially after large sizing changes. A stochastic inner placer also makes the outer objective noisy: distinguish a genuinely better sizing from one lucky inner seed. Use controlled seeds for comparisons and repeated evaluations where differences are comparable to search variability.","url":"topic-layout-sizing.html#nested-joint"},{"title":"Layout-aware sizing and retargeting · Templates and parasitic models need a declared domain","text":"Templates and parasitic models need a declared domain A template preserves known-good relationships: transistor grouping, symmetry, routing topology, local environment and critical connections. It can make sizing evaluation fast and predictable. The price is limited coverage. A topology valid for one width ratio may become congested or geometrically impossible for another. Independently preserving horizontal and vertical relations may still allow overlap after resizing unless the combined constraints guarantee separation throughout the supported dimension range. Topological Approaches, PDF pp. 196–203 . Use a template contract: legal device-size ranges, allowed ratios, orientation and unitization choices, terminal-access constraints, complete separation relations and model validity conditions. Test boundary sizes and aspect ratios, not only the nominal example. If a parameter leaves the domain, regenerate using another template or report that the current template cannot realize it. The layout-aware sizing chapter contrasts direct extraction with analytical device-parasitic estimates and lookup models of routing. A table with m samples on each of n dimensions grows as mⁿ, so blindly sampling every design parameter becomes infeasible. First identify sensitive parasitic/output relationships, then model their relevant dimensions, retaining independent validation points. A model fitted to one topology and PDK must not be reused after a different routing structure or device environment appears. PDF pp. 272–275 . Action: maintain a staged model hierarchy. Early search uses cheap footprint and access estimates; shortlisted candidates get route-aware RC estimates; finalist sizing/layout pairs receive full extraction and circuit analysis. Record prediction error against each higher-fidelity stage. Increase detail where error can change feasibility or ranking, rather than spending equal computation on every insensitive net.","url":"topic-layout-sizing.html#templates-models"},{"title":"Layout-aware sizing and retargeting · Process retargeting preserves intent, not dimensions","text":"Process retargeting preserves intent, not dimensions A source layout contains valuable design decisions: matched groups, signal flow, critical pin placement, shielding topology, well relationships and environment equivalence. A new process changes spacing, contact structures, layer resistances, device models and sometimes the valid device architecture. Uniform geometric scaling therefore does not establish electrical or manufacturing equivalence. Retargeting must preserve the intended relationships while regenerating process-specific implementation choices. Topological Approaches, PDF pp. 215–221 . The retargeting chapter develops generalized graph formulations for linear constraints and discusses contact-array reconstruction, conditional rules, changes in orthogonal ordering and layout-dependent effects. These details explain why simple compaction is insufficient. A narrower target wire may require more vias for current reliability; a compacted well edge may alter device behavior; the ordering assumptions used in the next compaction direction can become stale after the first. PDF pp. 238–247 . Proposed sequence: recover semantic electrical/physical intent from the source; map device and layer roles to the target process; generate target-compatible device variants; solve target constraints; reroute critical connections; extract and re-evaluate the target circuit. Keep source-layout evidence as provenance, not as a target-process certificate. A topology can be a useful initialization while still requiring new sizing and robustness analysis. When preserving surroundings, identify the physical mechanism. Copying a numerical well margin from one process can be meaningless in another; preserving equivalent response requires the target PDK's supported layout-dependent-effect model or a calibrated conservative rule. If that model is unavailable, report the inherited heuristic and its limitation rather than claiming electrical equivalence.","url":"topic-layout-sizing.html#retargeting"},{"title":"Layout-aware sizing and retargeting · Map the loop onto the existing Philis interfaces","text":"Map the loop onto the existing Philis interfaces Cell (line 39) already separates deterministic variant enumeration from drawing. sizing (line 148) obtains per-member finger counts and unit dimensions from unitization. VariantSpace (line 20) stores pre-drawn alternatives; choose_variants (line 311) selects the actual macros; try_reshape (line 460) changes a candidate's variant and pin offsets. These are concrete extension points for richer variant descriptors and electrical equivalence metadata. Actual area must include required surrounding structures and routing reservations. guard_rings (line 18) constructs rings after placement using reserved halos. Any sizing objective that counts only active transistor WL misses this area and its placement interactions. Track drawn cell area, reserved footprint and final routed block area separately, then optimize the measure relevant to the floorplan contract. Before allowing sizing as an automated move, create an explicit transaction that regenerates unitization, all affected cell variants, electrical reference data, parasitic baselines, statistical models, current estimates and dependent constraints. The reason is consistency: a wider input device changes more than its rectangle. Apply the provenance and invalidation model in constraint systems so stale analysis cannot certify the new design.","url":"topic-layout-sizing.html#sizing-engine"},{"title":"Layout-aware sizing and retargeting · Evidence needed before trusting layout-aware sizing","text":"Evidence needed before trusting layout-aware sizing Check every generated variant for intended connectivity and extracted device parameters across its declared size domain. Compare equivalent variants using the same ideal circuit reference, then quantify their parasitic differences. Verify that illegal sharing patterns are excluded by generator preconditions, not merely penalized after drawing. Test changes at discrete thresholds: a new finger, contact row, dummy choice or routing layer can reveal failures invisible to small continuous perturbations. For the optimization loop, compare three baselines on the same circuit and process: nominal sizing followed by layout; sizing with estimated parasitics; and sizing with validated layout feedback. Report final extracted specifications, robustness estimates, area, runtime and unresolved analyses. The historical source examples motivate the comparison but do not prove that a particular surrogate or template will help this engine. Keep a Pareto archive of verified sizing/layout pairs and their model context. A single weighted scalar can hide a meaningful area-versus-matching or speed-versus-power tradeoff; the archive makes those choices reviewable. Completion means the delivered physical realization satisfies its selected electrical and manufacturing contract with stated evidence, not that the outer sizing optimizer stopped changing W and L.","url":"topic-layout-sizing.html#sizing-validation"},{"title":"Manufacturability, density and final geometry · Manufacturable is a necessary condition, not a performance score","text":"Manufacturable is a necessary condition, not a performance score Manufacturability begins with a complete legal structure: correct device layers, widths, spaces, overlaps, enclosures, connectivity, pattern density and process options. These constraints are not rewards that may be traded away for lower area. A minimum-space violation does not become acceptable because a common-centroid score improved. However, passing the geometric checks only establishes the checks that were actually run, using their actual coverage and rule-deck version. Hastings explains design rules in terms of process bias, linewidth control, mask alignment, outdiffusion and depletion. These ingredients justify why rules vary by layer pair, device type and voltage. Modern rule decks encode much richer interactions than the simplified teaching examples. The automation should consume the authoritative deck and preserve its semantics rather than infer an all-purpose spacing from feature size. PDF pp. 130–150","url":"topic-manufacturability.html#mfg-feasible"},{"title":"Manufacturability, density and final geometry · Geometric primitives that deserve independent checks","text":"Geometric primitives that deserve independent checks Check Why it exists Typical algorithmic trap Width and local neck width Printing/etch control, physical continuity and sometimes current capability. A bounding box is wide enough while a jog, notch or corner creates a narrow region. Same/different-net spacing Avoid shorts, excessive fields and unwanted process interactions. Using center-to-center distance or ignoring a via's larger landing rectangle. Overlap and enclosure Maintain connection or material containment under alignment and dimension variation. Assuming that any nonzero intersection is an adequate contact. Notch, end-of-line and corner conditions Local patterns can print differently from long parallel lines. Checking only axis-aligned rectangle pairs and missing a notch formed by a polygon union. Grid and legal angles Ensure final mask coordinates can represent the intended shape reliably. Snapping a path centerline but emitting half-width edges or diagonal vertices off grid. Density, slotting and fill Control polishing, stress and manufacturable wide conductors. Checking only the pre-fill placement or treating a slot as irrelevant to resistance. Device recognition The intended model must match the actual assembled layers. A legal-looking shape extracts as a different device, extra transistor or unintended diode. Voltage-dependent geometry Electrical field and depletion depend on the voltage between regions. Using only supply names or voltage to ground rather than the possible pairwise voltage difference. The table is an implementation taxonomy, not an exhaustive reproduction of a particular foundry deck. Its purpose is to force a checker to state what it does and does not prove. Where the engine supports only rectangles, document which polygon operations, angles and derived layers are represented and delegate the remainder to signoff.","url":"topic-manufacturability.html#mfg-primitives"},{"title":"Manufacturability, density and final geometry · Worked example: a legal line that cannot accept a via","text":"Worked example: a legal line that cannot accept a via Suppose a hypothetical metal layer permits 100 nm line width and 100 nm spacing. A via cut is 80 nm wide, and the connected metal must extend 30 nm beyond it on the two relevant sides. The minimum landing width is therefore 80 + 2×30 = 140 nm. A router using a 200 nm pitch from line width plus spacing will discover that a 140 nm landing beside an unchanged neighboring wire violates the original spacing reservation. The necessary response is not a late arbitrary jog. First compute legal landing templates, including asymmetric enclosures if the PDK permits them. Next choose track/via sites that allow those templates. Reserve any enlarged footprints in congestion estimation. Finally check actual emitted polygons and via connectivity. These numbers are illustrative; the book's argument about lead-to-lead, via-to-via and via-to-lead pitches establishes the mechanism. PDF pp. 812–815 A manufacturing grid adds another condition. A path of odd width centered on an integer grid can put its edges on half-grid positions. Either choose a supported centerline grid, emit legal rectangles directly, or select a width whose edges are representable. Do not silently round each edge independently: that can shrink enclosure or break the equality of supposedly matched wires. Validate this example with the exact shape generator, not with a test of the algebra used to choose the pitch. Include a neighboring via, a channel intersection and both legal orientations of an asymmetric landing. Those cases expose errors a straight isolated route cannot reveal.","url":"topic-manufacturability.html#mfg-route"},{"title":"Manufacturability, density and final geometry · Final material changes the device and the verification result","text":"Final material changes the device and the verification result Fill insertion is often deferred until late in a flow, but it adds material near devices and wires. That material affects capacitance, stress, hydrogen access and RF losses. A successful pre-fill mismatch estimate therefore cannot automatically be promoted to a final post-fill guarantee. Hastings discusses metal-fill exclusions for matched MOS devices, controlled surroundings for passive arrays and removal or careful design of dummy material near inductors. PDF pp. 703–704 , 716 , 363 Worked tradeoff. A capacitor array may benefit from an electrostatic shield that fixes its field boundary. That shield increases local metal density and may need slotting; slots alter the shield and its resistance. Meanwhile, a nearby critical MOS array may prefer an over-gate metal exclusion to preserve passivation uniformity. A universal instruction to “fill empty area” or “shield all precision devices” cannot satisfy both. The floorplanner should reserve separate environmental regions and the finalizer should solve density with those constraints present. Use an iterative finalization loop: generate candidate material; run density and geometry checks; extract or update the relevant parasitics; evaluate device environment and circuit budgets; revise the fill or the macro plan when a budget fails. Cache checks whose dependency regions did not change, but invalidate a result whenever its actual physical inputs change. A final layout checksum should identify the geometry to which the evidence applies.","url":"topic-manufacturability.html#mfg-final"},{"title":"Manufacturability, density and final geometry · A manufacturability evidence bundle","text":"A manufacturability evidence bundle Freeze the inputs. Retain PDK/deck/model versions, device options, package choice, layer mapping, grid, netlist and scenario specifications. This makes later comparison meaningful. Validate primitives before composition. Qualified device, dummy, tap, guard, pad and via templates reduce the chance that every optimization move invents a new structure. Check assembled geometry and extraction. Abutment, rotation, routing and merging can invalidate a previously legal primitive or change the extracted device network. Run electrical and reliability checks with their required inputs. DRC does not substitute for antenna history, EM current distribution, voltage-aware checks, ESD path verification or latchup qualification. Finalize fill, slots, seals and pad structures. The manufactured database is the object that must pass; do not attach a pre-finalization certificate to it. Record exceptions and unknowns explicitly. A foundry-approved waiver has different status from an engine feature that has not been implemented. Archive the actual deliverables and reports. Include libraries and layer maps needed to reconstruct the design, not merely a screenshot or optimizer seed. The source's final checklist supplies the basis for this evidence-oriented completion criterion. It includes pad/bonding requirements, Kelvin locations, high-current paths, antenna, noise separation, latchup precautions, fill exclusions and archive completeness. PDF pp. 823–825 Philis priority. Preserve the distinction between a cheap optimization surrogate and the authoritative acceptance check. A legal bbox placement is useful progress; a post-route, post-fill checked design is a stronger result. Surface these distinctions in reports so that the engine can improve incrementally without overstating what any stage guarantees.","url":"topic-manufacturability.html#mfg-evidence"},{"title":"Manufacturability, density and final geometry · Voltage-aware legality and the limits of a waiver","text":"Voltage-aware legality and the limits of a waiver A voltage-aware spacing rule needs the maximum differential voltage that can occur between the relevant neighboring shapes. Merely recording that one belongs to a “high-voltage net” loses information. Consider hypothetical nets A and B that are both 5 V during steady operation. If startup allows A = 5 V while B remains at 0 V, their spacing cannot be justified by the steady-state zero difference. Conversely, two nets that track by an explicit circuit guarantee may have a smaller differential than a naive combination of unrelated extremes. The checker must evaluate allowed joint scenarios, retain the guarantee and include its failure conditions. PDF p. 821 The same discipline applies to device recognition and exceptions. A DRC exclusion marker does not physically repair a violation, and a waived check is not an ordinary pass. Record the exact geometry, violated rule, justification, approving qualification and scope. A foundry-qualified special device may legitimately rely on a dedicated recognition layer and special rules; a generic rectangle copied from a drawing does not inherit that qualification. When the geometry or device version changes, re-evaluate whether the exception still applies. For an automated engine, distinguish hard failure, verified pass, qualified exception and missing evidence. These states prevent an optimizer from exploiting a missing model as though it were permission. They also make development practical: an early candidate can be useful while still reporting that final density, antenna or special-device recognition awaits the designated signoff check. The final “manufacturable” claim should refer to the exact final geometry and declared rule domain, not to a list of checks performed on earlier versions.","url":"topic-manufacturability.html#manuf-scenarios"},{"title":"MOS layout, orientation and local effects · Start from the circuit’s matching target","text":"Start from the circuit’s matching target A differential input pair is often judged by input-referred voltage offset at a specified current, whereas a current mirror is judged by current-ratio error at specified terminal voltages. The two use the same transistors but different sensitivity functions. The first decision is therefore to identify the matched electrical quantity, allowed bias range and importance of local versus global variation. For intuition, in a strong-inversion square-law model I = β(V GS − V TH )²/2. A small threshold perturbation gives a relative current change approximately −2δV TH /V OV at fixed gate voltage. The same threshold perturbation contributes nearly directly to the voltage correction needed to restore a fixed current. This local derivation explains why overdrive and transconductance belong in a mismatch budget. It is an illustrative model, not a substitute for the compact model in short-channel or weak-inversion operation. Hastings explicitly discusses the area rule's limitations, pocket-implant behavior and body-bias dependence. Philis should propagate characterized variation through actual operating-point derivatives and compare candidates over the circuit's scenarios. A distance penalty can encourage proximity while still being insufficient to predict offset. PDF pp. 686–690","url":"topic-mos-layout.html#mos-objective"},{"title":"MOS layout, orientation and local effects · MOS: distinguish voltage matching, current matching, orientation and LDE","text":"MOS: distinguish voltage matching, current matching, orientation and LDE Hastings begins MOS matching by distinguishing equal-current voltage matching from equal-gate-voltage current matching. Threshold and current-factor variation enter these cases differently. In the long-channel, strong-inversion, saturation approximation at fixed drain current, increasing width both averages threshold variation and raises transconductance; these effects can cancel in the relative current-error expression. Increasing length can still help. This is the basis of the source's current-matching versus voltage-matching sizing guidance—not a universal theorem for arbitrary compact models, inversion levels or pocket-implanted devices. PDF pp. 686–690 , 712–714 Propagate mismatch through the actual operating-point sensitivities. A useful first-order decomposition is δI ≈ (∂I/∂V TH )δV TH + (∂I/∂β)δβ + (∂I/∂V DS )δV DS + (∂I/∂V BS )δV BS , including covariance where characterized. This is an implementation proposal, not a replacement compact model. Use derivatives from supported device models and simulation; distinguish fixed-current, fixed-voltage and ratioed operation. Orientation is a terminal-direction property Equation 13.61 defines Φ = (1/N)ΣΦ i for identical units, with Φ i = +1 or −1 according to drain-current direction. Equal signed orientation is required; equal absolute magnitude is insufficient. Three right-oriented and one left-oriented unit give +1/2, as do nine right-oriented and three left-oriented units. Zero is convenient but is not compulsory. In two dimensions, track both directional components. The formula and tilted-implant diagram were visually checked. PDF pp. 708–711 During pattern construction, retain source/drain labels and legal sharing boundaries. During hierarchy transforms, rotate or reflect the orientation descriptor consistently. Add a test in which two macros have identical outlines and coincident centroids but opposite normalized source/drain directions: the engine should detect the mismatch. For asymmetric drain-extended devices, use explicitly qualified transformations; apparent geometric mirroring can change a non-self-aligned channel length under mask misalignment. PDF p. 691 Action Physical reason Scope or tradeoff Use identical unit dimensions and variants; preserve intended series/parallel topology. Unequal W/L, oxide options and edge proportions create systematic and random differences. Series-stacked units do not exactly reproduce an arbitrary long device. Use circuit-authorized unitization; validate effective dimensions, terminal voltages and body connections after extraction. Measure equal distances and environments around wells, active edges, implants and STI. Well implant scattering and length-of-diffusion stress change local threshold/current factor. Outer fingers can differ from interior fingers even at equal nominal W/L. Use PDK LDE parameters and known geometry domains. Simple pair spacing cannot encode the full environment. Add end dummies and active extensions appropriate to the technology. They regularize poly etch loading and distance to STI, helping both dimensional and stress matching. Half dummies can save area but may be unrecognized by DRC/LVS or bring a well/moat boundary too close. Full, qualified dummies are often safer to generate. Tie dummy gates to suppress unwanted channels. An electrically active dummy adds leakage and parasitic capacitance; its bias can also change the local electrical environment. Use a valid tie for the device polarity, well and available rails, not an unconditional ground connection. Keep gate overhang, nearby poly and gate-connection patterns comparable. Poly corners and neighboring shapes alter etch behavior. Metal straps can connect gates without creating unequal poly environments beside active channels. Gate-contact placement and strap geometry still require resistance, antenna and matching checks. Avoid contacts over critical active gate regions and control over-gate metal/fill. The source associates local processing and hydrogen passivation differences with increased mismatch. Uniform-looking metal coverage is not necessarily electrically neutral. Hastings discourages over-gate plates for its moderate/exceptional MOS matching classes; many passive devices instead benefit from shielding. Treat this as a family/process-specific rule. Use compact CC subarrays where gradient sensitivity warrants them. Coincidence handles first order; compactness and smaller balanced subarrays reduce exposure to nonlinear fields. Two-dimensional layouts help when they actually become more compact. Complex patterns can impose disproportionate gate-routing and parasitic costs. Use measured bias dependence, including body and drain conditions. Body effect, channel-length modulation and impact ionization create current errors independent of centroid equality. Layout can reduce unequal IR drops and body resistance. It cannot insert a cascode or change device bias without circuit authorization. Account for the planned stress history. Different V DS , V GS , duty cycles and temperatures can produce unequal hot-carrier and bias-temperature aging. Evaluate time-dependent circuit error with the qualified aging model. Initial matching is not a lifetime guarantee. Respect pocket-implant and subthreshold exceptions. Pocket implants can make mismatch stop improving with channel length; edge/stringer conduction can dominate at low currents. Do not infer improvement from a larger W×L alone. Special annular or fin-like layouts require qualified generators and models. Select low-stress, thermally suitable locations using the actual package. Global package stress, bumps/pillars and power devices generate different spatial fields. The preferred central region of a leaded molded package is not a universal rule for a bumped die. Use device-type comparisons only where characterized. The book discusses studies in which NMOS matched better than PMOS and others in which the reverse occurred. Never choose transistor polarity, oxide thickness or diagonal orientation from a universal ranking. Voltage ratings, noise and circuit function also constrain those choices. The detailed mechanism discussions are at PDF pp. 692–706 , and the twenty-four-item matching catalog is at 712–717 . The LOD discussion is on PDF page 697; Figure 13.52 on PDF page 698 was visually inspected: its proposed active extensions and dummies address distance to STI, not an abstract “dummy count.” That distinction matters when comparing two generated arrays with the same number of dummy fingers but different active envelopes.","url":"topic-mos-layout.html#source-mos"},{"title":"MOS layout, orientation and local effects · Worked construction: a matched pair with real terminals","text":"Worked construction: a matched pair with real terminals Choose a valid unit. Begin with a PDK-approved device variant and identical unit W/L. Verify that decomposing the original devices into these units preserves the authorized total dimensions and terminal topology. Assign units to devices. For two equal devices with two units each, ABBA or a two-dimensional AB/BA pattern provides coincident unit centroids under equal weights. This is the placement skeleton. Assign source and drain sides. Mark each unit's terminal direction before merging diffusion. An ABBA label sequence without terminal labels can accidentally give A and B different orientation averages. Check allowed sharing. Adjacent source/drain regions can merge only when the same electrical node and the qualified device structure permit it. Otherwise insert separation and recompute active moments and compactness. Complete the environment. Add end dummies, appropriate body contacts, well enclosure and active extension. Verify that edge fingers see comparable LDE conditions. Route gates and drains. Compare resistance, capacitance, coupling and access symmetry. Preserve the gate environment rather than adding arbitrary poly straps across one side. Re-extract and simulate. Confirm device identity, effective geometry, terminal correspondence, LDE parameters and final electrical error. Check again after fill. The order matters because late terminal assignment can invalidate the assumed pattern, and late dummies can move the macro boundaries without fixing active geometry. The pattern generator should produce a complete electrical unit arrangement, not just an A/B coloring for a later stage to interpret.","url":"topic-mos-layout.html#mos-worked"},{"title":"MOS layout, orientation and local effects · Why zero centroid error does not finish the job","text":"Why zero centroid error does not finish the job Imagine four equal active rectangles in an ABBA row. A linear threshold field across the row cancels in the average difference. A nearby well edge can still change just the leftmost finger; a source contact with extra resistance can shift one device's source voltage; a metal plate can change one finger's process environment; and a quadratic temperature field can have different averages over A and B. None of those failures is repaired by reporting a zero first-moment residual. Use a staged model: an exact structural check for identical variants and legal terminal topology; active-moment checks for smooth fields; neighborhood checks for LDE and dummies; extracted RC and bias checks for wiring; and scenario/aging evaluation for circuit accuracy. These layers explain which technique addresses which mechanism without promising that one scalar score encompasses all of them. Compactness and repeated small CC subarrays are useful when nonlinear fields are uncertain, but more subdivision consumes contact and route area. In a fixed footprint, that may reduce active area and worsen random mismatch. A route-intensive pattern can also add enough capacitance to slow a critical node. Compare alternatives at the circuit level and retain the reason for choosing a simpler pattern where it wins. PDF pp. 393–394 , 710–711","url":"topic-mos-layout.html#mos-gradient"},{"title":"MOS layout, orientation and local effects · Meaningful validation and required characterization","text":"Meaningful validation and required characterization Build a test family that holds nominal W/L and connectivity constant while independently changing source/drain orientation, well-edge distance, active extension, dummy topology, metal coverage and body-contact distance. Where the PDK provides LDE or statistical models, compare the predicted current or offset; where it does not, distinguish a controlled-environment preference from a calibrated guarantee. Test hierarchy transforms explicitly. A matched group entirely inside a macro can preserve its internal relations after rotation, yet the external well, fill or package environment can change. Two matching devices in independently rotated subcells can lose their relative orientation. A correct implementation transforms electrical descriptors and then recomputes external interactions. For long-term matching, compare stress scenarios as well as initial nominal bias. Hot-carrier and bias-temperature effects depend on voltage, duty and temperature history. Equal initial geometry is not evidence that two differently stressed devices age identically. Finally, do not automatically swap source and drain for an asymmetric or high-voltage device just because an ideal symmetric MOS symbol would allow it. PDF pp. 691–706","url":"topic-mos-layout.html#mos-validate"},{"title":"What makes a layout optimal? · What you should be able to decide","text":"What you should be able to decide After this chapter, you should be able to define what “better” means for a layout, distinguish a physical requirement from an optimization preference, explain why apparently good geometry can produce a bad circuit, and state what evidence supports an optimality claim. The rest of this handbook develops the mechanisms and techniques behind each decision. The corpus does not establish one universally best shape or one fixed checklist sufficient for every analog circuit. Hastings gives construction rules tied to physical mechanisms; Lampaert connects parasitics to circuit specifications; Graeb connects specifications to statistical robustness; the newer CC, DAC, and wire-sizing papers demonstrate conflicts between otherwise sensible layout choices. The definition above is a synthesis of those sources, not a quotation or a claimed consensus theorem. Performance-driven synthesis objective, PDF p. 20 Common-centroid limitations, PDF pp. 6–7 Competing width effects, PDF p. 19","url":"topic-optimality.html#learning"},{"title":"What makes a layout optimal? · The layout is an electrical object","text":"The layout is an electrical object A schematic specifies intended devices and connectivity. The layout adds device environment, contact resistance, distributed interconnect resistance, capacitance between many pairs of conductors, substrate paths, thermal coupling, package interaction, and manufacturing exposure. Some of these change nominal behavior. Others change variation, noise, or aging. A short connection can be worse than a longer one if it passes over a sensitive device, couples to a clock, constricts a current path, or disturbs a matched environment. Consequently, the object to optimize is more than rectangles and center coordinates. It is a coupled model of device geometry, internal macro wiring, external routes, wells, taps, fill, protective structures, and their association with circuit terminals. Once a stage collapses two devices into one macro, their local unit coordinates and terminals still matter. Feasibility first; compare quality among verified candidates Five rectangles show intent, generated geometry, physical checks, electrical evaluation and selection. Missing evidence prevents final certification. Feasibility first; compare quality among verified candidates Intent + operating modes PDK + required metrics Generate candidates place + route + repair Physical checks connectivity + coverage Extract + simulate corners / variation Feasible alternatives area / power / margins Selected layout with evidence A low proxy cost does not establish circuit feasibility. Unknown coverage or missing models remain visible in the result. Download this SVG Original illustrative diagram; not PDK-valid or to scale. Optimization is conditional on a declared contract and on the scope of the checks that actually ran. Why this changes the algorithm. A placer cannot optimize route resistance that its representation cannot estimate, and an extractor cannot check an omitted internal connection. Preserve the information required by downstream verification at each boundary. Use fast geometry models to guide search, but trace every claimed improvement to the physical quantity and circuit behavior it affects.","url":"topic-optimality.html#electrical-object"},{"title":"What makes a layout optimal? · Start by writing the design contract","text":"Start by writing the design contract Before choosing a pattern, specify the operating conditions and acceptable behavior. An OTA needs a supply range, input common-mode range, load, bias, feedback configuration, gain, stability, settling, noise, offset, and power requirements as applicable. A DAC needs reference behavior, switching scheme, output load, code range, static linearity convention, settling error and time, noise, and energy limits. A nominal schematic without these conditions does not define a unique optimization problem. Contract component What to record Why it matters Electrical intent Terminal meanings, exact ratios, matching relationships, bias domains, clocks, sensitive nodes, external interfaces. A visually symmetric connection can join the wrong terminals or match quantities the circuit does not require. Operating domain Supply, temperature, load, input range, frequency, modes, startup, transitions, package conditions. The same parasitic changes can be harmless in one mode and dominant in another. Manufacturing model PDK revision, device options, masks, geometry rules, extraction and LDE models, variation distributions. Legal geometry and matching mechanisms depend on the process; a numerical rule from a textbook is not a foundry limit. Reliability domain Lifetime, mission profile, current waveforms, voltage stress, junction temperature, permitted failure criteria. Reliability depends on exposure over time, rather than one nominal operating point. Optimization policy Which specifications are mandatory, which margins matter, and how area/power/speed/runtime tradeoffs are selected. Otherwise a scalar score quietly decides priorities the designer never specified. Action. Store this contract next to each run, with units and source of every numerical limit. Reason. A comparison is meaningful only when both candidates were evaluated against the same requirements and models.","url":"topic-optimality.html#requirements"},{"title":"What makes a layout optimal? · A mathematical definition, with the model boundary visible","text":"A mathematical definition, with the model boundary visible Let L include all geometric and discrete layout choices. Let u denote operating conditions, ξ manufacturing variation (ξ = 0 is the reference process point), and θ the process/device/interconnect models. Let F(L,u,ξ;θ) be the vector of circuit metrics. Let gⱼ express limits on those metrics, and let D(L;θ) express physical legality and connectivity. Reference-process feasibility over the required operating conditions: D(L;θ) = pass gⱼ(F(L,u,0;θ)) ≤ 0 for every required j and u For deterministic process robustness, also check the specified process scenarios. Statistical requirement, when specified: Y(L) = Prξ[all required metrics pass for all required u] ≥ Yrequired Among feasible layouts: minimize a chosen vector such as (area, power, worst settling time, noise, −yield margin). This separates the deterministic checks, stochastic claim, and design preferences. Operating conditions are not automatically random variables; a requirement to work over a temperature range cannot be replaced by giving unlikely temperatures little probability. Conversely, a process corner set does not capture random local mismatch. The robust-design chapter explains those distinctions and their computational cost. The function F is only as credible as its extraction, compact models, testbenches, and numerical convergence. If the available extractor omits substrate coupling, a pass cannot prove substrate immunity. Report that evaluation as outside coverage, rather than silently treating the missing term as zero.","url":"topic-optimality.html#mathematical"},{"title":"What makes a layout optimal? · Hard requirements, budgets, objectives, and unknowns","text":"Hard requirements, budgets, objectives, and unknowns A useful engine separates four concepts. Physical legality includes manufacturing geometry and correct connectivity. Design requirements include performance and reliability bounds chosen for this circuit. Search budgets are surrogate limits, such as a maximum sensitive-net capacitance, that help find a solution. Preferences choose among acceptable alternatives. Unknown or uncomputed quantities belong to none of these passing categories. Philis already has hard, budget, and cost arms in Requirements , and lexicographic ordering in Report::lex . Preserve the distinction between an optimization tier and a physical truth: an inferred symmetry rule can be represented as hard during search yet remain a design heuristic that needs an override; a current-density lifetime limit is physically mandatory even if an implementation currently reports it in a budget tier. Normalize residuals for a reason. To compare excess capacitance and thermal mismatch, use each rule's own allowance: for an upper bound, max(0,(measured−limit)/scale) , with an explicit positive physical scale. A zero limit or signed metric needs a separately defined scale. This avoids making nanometers numerically more important than volts just because their unit representation is larger. It does not make different risks physically or electrically interchangeable. Keep uncertainty separate. A 20% routing reserve is a design margin, not automatically a 95% confidence bound. A “3σ” label needs a distribution, model, and joint-event definition. Combining model uncertainty with process variance requires an explicit assumption; adding every uncertainty in quadrature by habit is unjustified.","url":"topic-optimality.html#tiers"},{"title":"What makes a layout optimal? · Worked example: why the lowest capacitance can lose","text":"Worked example: why the lowest capacitance can lose Consider three otherwise legal OTA layouts with illustrative simulated values. The contract requires phase margin ≥60° and input offset ≤1 mV. The units and numbers below teach selection logic; they are not measured Philis results. Candidate Extracted total C Phase margin Offset Area Decision A: shortest routes 80 fF 54° 0.7 mV 100 units Fails stability requirement; its low C cannot buy acceptance. B: balanced source route 90 fF 65° 0.6 mV 110 units Feasible nominally, with useful margin. C: more compact array 95 fF 62° 0.4 mV 103 units Also feasible; better area/offset but less stability margin than B. B and C may both be nondominated. Choosing one requires a stated preference or additional robustness information. The total-C ranking would choose A, demonstrating that even an extracted physical proxy is not the circuit specification. The wire-sizing paper supplies real cases of competing performance changes with geometry; this table isolates the decision principle. Table 7, PDF p. 19 If B has no local-mismatch model, label it “nominally feasible, mismatch yield unknown.” Do not compare that status as equivalent to C's complete statistical evaluation. Evidence completeness is part of the result.","url":"topic-optimality.html#worked"},{"title":"What makes a layout optimal? · What can reasonably be called optimal?","text":"What can reasonably be called optimal? Feasible under the checked model: all specified checks ran and passed within their stated domain. This is the first useful accomplishment. Best observed candidate: the selected result is best among evaluated alternatives under an explicit policy. Report the search budget and random seeds. Nondominated among evaluated candidates: no retained candidate is no worse in every chosen objective and strictly better in at least one. This is a finite archive property. Optimal within a restricted formulation: an exact solver proves the best solution for a fixed representation, discretization, and objective. The proof does not automatically apply outside that representation. Global physical optimum: would require complete models and search over every admissible physical implementation. The corpus does not offer such a general certificate for analog P&R. This vocabulary keeps heuristic search useful without overclaiming. The topological survey contains encodings that make certain symmetry constraints easy by restricting the search space. The performance literature contains surrogate-guided searches whose quality depends on the model. Both are valuable; their guarantees must retain their assumptions. See representations and search .","url":"topic-optimality.html#claims"},{"title":"What makes a layout optimal? · How to work toward the optimum","text":"How to work toward the optimum Characterize the schematic and identify fragile quantities. Simulate the required modes and perturb devices/parasitics. This reveals which ratios, terminals, and paths need physical care. Choose legal, characterized device structures. Units, contacts, wells, dummies, shields, and protective structures establish what can be built. Device construction precedes trusting a placement score. Generate multiple plausible matching and floorplan structures. Use structural knowledge to reduce the search, but preserve alternatives when physics predicts tradeoffs. Reserve routing, return paths, isolation, and reliability resources early. Their space and connectivity can invalidate a compact floorplan if added late. Place and route with calibrated fast models. Use moment checks, lengths, estimated RC, current paths, and environment metrics for cheap guidance. Extract and evaluate the realized circuit. Include internal macro wiring, supplies, substrate or package models where required, and the relevant physical effects. Diagnose the limiting mechanism before modifying geometry. Increase width for a demonstrated resistance bottleneck; change a pattern for demonstrated mismatch; move a heater or return path for a demonstrated coupling problem. Recheck interacting requirements. A fix to one mechanism can worsen another: width adds C, shields load victims, isolation adds area, and dispersion adds route complexity. Record the selected tradeoff and remaining coverage limits. The final result should explain its construction, the reason for its constraints, and the tests that support acceptance. The following chapters supply the details needed at every step. They are intended to teach decisions rather than prescribe one frozen layout style.","url":"topic-optimality.html#workflow"},{"title":"Terminal parasitic networks · What the layout adds to a schematic","text":"What the layout adds to a schematic A drawn net is not equipotential at all frequencies or currents. Contacts and wires introduce resistance, so different points on the same named net can have different voltages. Each conductor also stores charge relative to surrounding conductors and substrate. Routing can therefore create source degeneration, shift poles and zeros, load references, inject clock edges, or unbalance differential paths. Derived constraint. Bound the electrical effect on the relevant terminals or performance metrics. A maximum route length is only a surrogate for that constraint under assumed width, layer, topology, and surroundings. Lampaert's performance-driven method relates parasitic changes to circuit specifications; the wire-sizing paper demonstrates that width changes can alter performance even at a fixed placement. Performance objective, PDF p. 20 Width sensitivity example, PDF p. 2 Equal total capacitance can produce unequal circuits Two rectangular device networks put the same 20 femtofarads on different nodes: high impedance versus low impedance. Resistance and terminal assignment determine performance. Equal total capacitance can produce unequal circuits Candidate A Driver Sensitive node Load R Candidate B Driver Sensitive node Load R C = 20 fF C = 20 fF High-Z node loading Driver-side loading Keep a terminal network, not only ΣC. Download this SVG Original illustrative diagram; not PDK-valid or to scale. The placement of capacitance in the circuit matters. This is a qualitative network schematic, not a complete transistor model.","url":"topic-parasitics.html#physical"},{"title":"Terminal parasitic networks · Resistance: material, shape, contacts, and topology","text":"Resistance: material, shape, contacts, and topology Uniform rectangular wire approximation: Rwire = R□ · L/W = ρL/(tW). Series path: Rpath = Σ Rwire + Σ Rvia + Σ Rcontact. R□ is sheet resistance, L and W must use the same length unit, ρ is resistivity, and t is effective conductive thickness. The formula assumes a reasonably uniform strip and material model. §2.5.3.2, Eq. (2.33), sheet-resistance law, PDF p. 51 / printed p. 38 Width-dependent resistivity, liners, corners, access spreading, contact crowding, and temperature can invalidate a constant-R□ approximation. Use characterized values when the PDK supplies them. A series path sum is valid for a single path. In a branched or meshed net, solve the conductance network: current can split, and resistance between two terminals depends on all available paths and boundary conditions. “Total resistance on the net” is not generally the resistance relevant to a differential source path, a supply sink, or a DAC charging branch. Constraints and enforcement. Preserve segment endpoints and layer/type; characterize contacts and vias separately; constrain terminal voltage drop or effective impedance where possible. Use layer-aware path estimates during routing, followed by network extraction. When a via array is added, include access resistance and unequal current sharing rather than assuming N perfectly equal parallel resistors. See EM for why the same topology also matters to reliability.","url":"topic-parasitics.html#resistance"},{"title":"Terminal parasitic networks · Capacitance is a relation between conductors","text":"Capacitance is a relation between conductors Distinguish intended device capacitance from interconnect parasitics. Ground/substrate capacitance, same-layer lateral coupling, cross-layer overlap/fringe coupling, and capacitor terminal parasitics have different circuit meanings. A parallel run's coupling depends on edge gap, overlap length, layer stack, dielectric geometry, and shields, not just whether the two net names differ. The following matrix stamp is our circuit-law derivation: a two-terminal linear capacitor obeys qᵢ=c(vᵢ−vⱼ) , qⱼ=−qᵢ , and terminal current is the time derivative of charge. For a positive physical capacitor c between nodes i and j, the nodal capacitance matrix receives +c on Cᵢᵢ and Cⱼⱼ, and −c on Cᵢⱼ and Cⱼᵢ. The off-diagonal matrix entry is therefore negative even though the physical coupling capacitor is positive. Store the convention explicitly; mixing these conventions can invert noise predictions or double-count coupling. Linear interconnect network: Gv(t) + C dv(t)/dt = i(t). Frequency domain: (G + jωC)v = i. These equations follow by summing the resistor and capacitor branch currents with Kirchhoff’s current law. They describe a linear RC network with defined reference/boundary nodes; ideal voltage sources or inductors require additional variables or boundary treatment. The circuit devices and their operating-point models connect to these terminals. Reason to keep terminal identities. Top-to-substrate capacitance in a DAC can reduce gain, while bottom-plate loading mainly burdens a reference/switch under the paper's ideal static assumptions. Merging both into one total-C penalty loses that distinction. Parasitic classes and their roles, PDF pp. 2–4","url":"topic-parasitics.html#capacitance"},{"title":"Terminal parasitic networks · Worked example: two widths, two different bottlenecks","text":"Worked example: two widths, two different bottlenecks Take an illustrative 100 µm route with R□=0.1 Ω/□. At W=0.5 µm, R=20 Ω; at W=1 µm, R=10 Ω. Suppose the corresponding shunt capacitances are 10 and 16 fF from an assumed extraction table. These are teaching numbers, not a PDK recommendation. If this route is a source lead for a device with gₘ=5 mS, our single-branch derivation i=gₘ(vin−iRₛ) gives gₘ,eff≈gₘ/(1+gₘRₛ) , neglecting body effect/output conductance and equating source-resistor current with the modeled branch current. It changes from about 4.55 mS to 4.76 mS as resistance halves. This suggests widening could help that branch, subject to the rest of the circuit. In a different circuit position, if the added capacitance loads a 100 kΩ node, the corresponding simple pole from this capacitance alone changes from about 159 MHz to 99.5 MHz. Widening now worsens that pole. These are separate illustrative models, not two independent predictions that can simply be added in a real OTA. Their purpose is to show why the constraint must identify the electrical path. P&R implication. Classify resistance-sensitive, capacitance-sensitive, and coupled RC-sensitive paths using simulation or a validated model. Optimize width and topology jointly with their actual effects. The wire-sizing paper's real circuit examples show the same competing tendency. Table 7, PDF p. 19","url":"topic-parasitics.html#worked"},{"title":"Terminal parasitic networks · Build the extracted object that the constraints need","text":"Build the extracted object that the constraints need Establish connectivity and device terminals first. An RC network extracted from mislabeled or shorted geometry cannot validate the intended circuit. Split conductors at contacts, junctions, branches, and changes of width/layer. This preserves where voltage drops and branch currents occur. Extract resistance and coupling using the appropriate process model. Include internal macro wiring and pin access; external route-only extraction misses hidden bottlenecks. Attach parasitics to terminal-resolved nodes. Keep matched sides and capacitor plates distinct, even if they eventually connect to one schematic net. Reduce the network only with a known error bound or validation. A reduction appropriate for DC IR drop may not preserve frequency-dependent coupling or settling. Simulate with the original devices and the extracted network. Re-evaluate bias when voltage drops or LDEs change it; do not freeze a schematic operating point beyond its justified range. Why reduction needs care. Eliminating internal nodes in a resistor network can preserve terminal DC behavior. Eliminating an RC network is frequency dependent; independently collapsing all R and C into a total often changes its poles and transfer functions. Validate the reduced model over the frequency and time domain that matters to the specification.","url":"topic-parasitics.html#extraction"},{"title":"Terminal parasitic networks · Use a hierarchy of models during optimization","text":"Use a hierarchy of models during optimization Stage Useful model Permitted interpretation Early floorplan Weighted pin distances, route-resource estimates, characterized macro interfaces. Ranks rough alternatives; does not prove routed RC. Global routing Layer-aware segments, expected vias, approximate coupling and width choices. Predicts likely bottlenecks and guides detailed routing. Detailed routing Drawn widths, cuts, local neighbors, terminal connection graph. Supports substantially better RC and reliability estimates. Final evaluation Validated extraction plus device/LDE models and required testbenches. Supports a performance claim within the model's declared coverage. A fast model should report uncertainty or its applicability domain. When a change crosses a layer boundary, adds a shield, or changes route topology, reevaluate rather than extrapolating an old length-to-C conversion. Calibrate model error against extracted results and use that error to choose meaningful reserves.","url":"topic-parasitics.html#hierarchy"},{"title":"Terminal parasitic networks · Electrical route matching as a multiport constraint","text":"Electrical route matching as a multiport constraint For a differential structure, relevant comparisons may include source resistance, drain load, gate connection resistance, coupling to common aggressors, and frequency-dependent input/output impedance. Equal total length and equal sets of layers are necessary only for some chosen construction methods; they are not a sufficient electrical matching condition. A useful intermediate signature includes per-layer lengths and widths, via types/counts, branch topology, corresponding terminal R, and selected C matrix entries. The final condition should be derived from the allowed differential error: |Rpos−Rneg|≤ΔRmax , |Cpos,k−Cneg,k|≤ΔCmax,k , or directly a maximum simulated offset/CMRR/settling imbalance. Derive the limits from circuit sensitivity rather than assigning one percentage to every path. Tradeoff. Adding length to the faster or lower-C side can improve one balance metric while worsening both paths' speed and noise. Prefer a more balanced topology, layer allocation, or access structure when it achieves matching with less common degradation.","url":"topic-parasitics.html#matching"},{"title":"Terminal parasitic networks · Current Philis models and the next constraints","text":"Current Philis models and the next constraints routing lowers a class-derived capacitance allowance to a drawn-length cap using one wire capacitance-per-length value. That is more specific than the fallback in ParasiticBudget::extract ; document the actual active path. Extend it with layer, width, via, and topology information, then retain the original electrical allowance for extracted validation. CouplingBudget usefully sums coupling across multiple aggressors, but its estimate is same-layer and uses one stack-wide coefficient. Cross-layer coupling, fringing, shielding, and excitation are outside that formula. Differential compares total length and a deduplicated layer set; it does not perform RC matching. harvest currently assigns total extracted capacitance to the final cost. Preserve that diagnostic, but add terminal-resolved metrics and circuit performance. A larger total-C candidate may be better if it substantially reduces a critical resistance or balances a sensitive path.","url":"topic-parasitics.html#engine"},{"title":"Terminal parasitic networks · Validate the model before strengthening the constraint","text":"Validate the model before strengthening the constraint Use simple structures with independently calculable behavior: one rectangle, series widths, a T branch, a via stack, a via array, overlapping layers, adjacent parallel wires, a grounded shield, and a floating conductor. Check unit conversions, resistance monotonicity, reciprocity/passivity where applicable, connectivity, and geometric invariance under translation. Then compare representative generated layouts against an independent extractor using the same conductor/device interpretation. The existing PEX cross-check script provides a starting point; agreement in total C alone is not enough if the new constraints depend on individual terminal couplings. Record per-path errors and the circuit-metric error they cause. Increase optimization authority only after the model predicts the distinctions the engine will rely on.","url":"topic-parasitics.html#validation"},{"title":"From circuit specifications to physical budgets · A layout budget is a derived allowance, not an arbitrary weight","text":"A layout budget is a derived allowance, not an arbitrary weight An analog layout engine needs a reason for every requirement it imposes. “Keep this net short” is useful only after identifying what shortness protects: settling time, gain, stability, noise, offset, supply drop or another specification. Performance-driven layout translates those electrical obligations into quantities geometry can control. The translation is an approximation with a reference point, assumptions and validity range; it is not a permanent property of the net name. Lampaert's central observation is that one fixed allocation of independent parasitic limits can reject a layout whose combined electrical behavior is acceptable. Different nets consume the same performance margin in different proportions. The more faithful constraint is often a coupled region in parasitic space, not a box of unrelated maximum lengths. The topological survey compares bound-based routing with sensitivity-weighted performance-driven routing and explains the allocation problem. Lampaert, PDF pp. 41–45 ; Topological Approaches, PDF pp. 182–193 . This chapter develops the translation from specification to physical action. The numerical examples are invented teaching cases, not measured Philis circuits or process constants. They illustrate what information a real budget generator must obtain.","url":"topic-performance-budgets.html#budget-purpose"},{"title":"From circuit specifications to physical budgets · Begin with the performance margin that actually remains","text":"Begin with the performance margin that actually remains Suppose a circuit metric P must satisfy L ≤ P ≤ U . If pre-layout process analysis produces a range [Pproc,min, Pproc,max] and layout contributes the same additive shift ΔP across that range, the allowed layout shift is: L − Pproc,min ≤ ΔP ≤ U − Pproc,max. This follows by applying the lower bound to the smallest pre-layout value and the upper bound to the largest. It is the source's basic margin allocation. The same-shift assumption matters: a wire's effect on phase margin or settling can vary with transistor operating point, supply and temperature. For a modern engine, retain a separate bound and response model for each relevant scenario instead of relying on a single additive interval whenever that assumption fails. Lampaert, PDF pp. 34–37 . For example, phase margin must be at least 60°. Nominal simulation gives 70°, but a required process/operating scenario gives 66°. Only 6° remains in that scenario before additional modeled uncertainty is considered. Using the nominal 10° as the routing budget would spend margin already consumed by process behavior. Conversely, if the pre-layout circuit is already below 60°, placement should report that condition; no generic minimum-area policy can guarantee that parasitics will repair it. Action and reason: create a performance ledger with specification bounds, pre-layout values by scenario, reserved model error and current extracted values. This makes budget creation auditable and prevents separate subsystems from spending the same margin twice. A reserve should be tied to measured prediction error or a stated conservative policy, not silently blended into several independent “safety factors.”","url":"topic-performance-budgets.html#headroom"},{"title":"From circuit specifications to physical budgets · Translate parasitics with a local response model","text":"Translate parasitics with a local response model Let p contain extracted quantities such as series resistance, grounded capacitance, coupling capacitance and mismatch descriptors. Around a reference p0, linearize each performance metric: Pj(p) ≈ Pj(p0) + Σi Sji(pi − p0,i), where Sji = ∂Pj/∂pi. The original zero-parasitic form is a special case with p0 = 0. Once the reference circuit already includes estimated diffusion or interconnect parasitics, add only the change from that reference; adding the full extracted value again double-counts it. Every sensitivity must identify its units: degrees per femtofarad, seconds per ohm, volts per threshold perturbation, and so forth. Lampaert, PDF pp. 42–45 . In the phase-margin example, assume the calibrated local model is ΔPM ≈ −0.02 CA − 0.05 CB , with capacitances in fF and the reference increments nonnegative. The scenario requirement becomes 0.02 CA + 0.05 CB ≤ 6 . B is more sensitive per femtofarad; treating both capacitances identically wastes information. From circuit specification to a coupled wire budget A phase-margin requirement and worst pre-layout value leave six degrees. Two routed branches consume that same allowance according to different sensitivities. Required PM ≥ 60° Worst baseline PM = 66° 6° shared allowance before model reserve Node A Node B CA consumes 0.02°/fF CB consumes 0.05°/fF 0.02 CA + 0.05 CB ≤ 6 One electrical requirement Download this SVG Original block-and-wire schematic. The two paths illustrate shared budget accounting, not a literal circuit topology or PDK geometry. Allocate half of the allowance to each net and the independent caps become CA ≤ 150 fF and CB ≤ 60 fF. A candidate with CA = 200 fF and CB = 20 fF violates the first cap yet consumes only 4° + 1° = 5°, so it is feasible in the coupled model. This is why allocated caps should be revisable search aids when the true contract is the sum. It is also why raw total capacitance is inadequate: a 160 fF layout split as (100, 60) consumes 5°, whereas a 200 fF layout split as (200, 0) consumes 4°. With several metrics and scenarios, stack their sensitivity rows to obtain a system of inequalities. The feasible region is their intersection. One move can improve bandwidth while worsening offset or phase margin; retain the complete vector before selecting a weighted preference among feasible candidates.","url":"topic-performance-budgets.html#linear-response"},{"title":"From circuit specifications to physical budgets · Lower electrical allowances into geometry without losing the model","text":"Lower electrical allowances into geometry without losing the model For a uniform rectangular wire in a calibrated resistive model, R = R□ L/W , where sheet resistance R□ is in ohms per square and L/W is dimensionless. A resistance limit gives Lmax = Rmax W/R□ only if width and layer are fixed and contact/via resistance has been accounted for. If vias contribute Rvia, use the remaining allowance Rmax − Rvia ; a negative result means the chosen transition alone is infeasible. Lampaert §2.5.3.2, Eq. (2.33), PDF p. 51 . For a branch carrying known DC current I and permitted drop εV, Rmax ≤ εV/|I| . The current is branch current, not an arbitrary total net current: shared supply trunks carry sums of downstream currents. Current direction, simultaneous activity and bias matter. A low-resistance layer or added via may satisfy the drop while introducing capacitance or coupling elsewhere; rerun the coupled electrical check after selecting it. A simple capacitance approximation is Cwire ≈ ca WL + cf L + Ccoupling , where ca is area capacitance density and cf represents an effective fringe term for the assumed environment. Coupling depends on neighboring routes, not only the wire itself. Derived example: for a uniform RC line with distributed capacitance to an AC reference, driven through resistance Rs and terminated by a lumped load, an illustrative Elmore first-moment expression is τ ≈ Rs(Cwire + CL) + Rwire(CL + Cwire/2) . This expression follows by integrating each infinitesimal wire resistance against its downstream capacitance; the average wire-capacitance contribution is Cwire/2. It is our teaching derivation, not an equation quoted from the cited pages. Apply it to coupling only under a stated effective-load approximation. Increasing width reduces Rwire but increases the area-dependent capacitance seen through driver resistance Rs. This derivation explains why blindly maximizing wire width is not an electrical optimum. The model omits inductance, nonlinear driver behavior and detailed distributed coupling, so it is a screening model with a stated domain. Lampaert, PDF pp. 47–50 . Engine mapping: ParasiticBudget stores a drawn-length cap. Its generic extract helper contains a documented 1 mm fallback, but that helper is not the active per-net budget path: the annotator already converts class/load-derived capacitance allowances to length using the process wire capacitance per micrometer. Extend this path with a derivation record containing the parent electrical requirement, allowed R/C contribution, assumed layer, width, via allowance and reference geometry. Retain the fast length check when appropriate, but invalidate or recompute it when those assumptions change. One millimeter on different layers or widths does not imply equal R/C, and a class/load heuristic does not by itself prove a circuit specification.","url":"topic-performance-budgets.html#budget-geometry"},{"title":"From circuit specifications to physical budgets · Independent caps approximate a coupled feasible region","text":"Independent caps approximate a coupled feasible region A box allocation excludes electrically feasible candidates A triangular coupled capacitance allowance contains a point outside a smaller box of independent caps. A table verifies the example numerically. (200, 20) 0 150 300 60 120 CA (fF) CB (fF) Coupled check 0.02 × 200 + 0.05 × 20 = 5° 5° ≤ 6°: feasible Independent allocation 200 fF > 150 fF cap Reallocate; do not reject blindly. Download this SVG Original quantitative diagram for the invented linear example. Feasibility here means feasibility of this local model, which still needs electrical validation. One implementation can keep the coupled row as the actual requirement and derive net prices from its active multiplier. A marginal cost on pi is then proportional to the row sensitivity Sji and the scarcity of that requirement's remaining headroom. Multiple active rows contribute multiple prices. These prices indicate where a small improvement is valuable; they are not substitutes for evaluating the original coupled inequalities. A multiplier is interpretable only for the particular objective, scales and local optimum from which it was obtained. Another implementation can allocate conservative independent caps for a simpler router, then reallocate after routing exposes which nets are expensive. Store the allocation as a child of the shared parent requirement so that unused allowance can move without changing the specification. Freeze prices within one search epoch, because changing the energy during every move makes annealing comparisons inconsistent. The source surveys precisely the tension between simple per-net constraints and direct performance cost. Topological Approaches, PDF pp. 184–193 .","url":"topic-performance-budgets.html#coupled-region"},{"title":"From circuit specifications to physical budgets · Statistical variation and model error also consume margin","text":"Statistical variation and model error also consume margin If uncertain parasitic increments have covariance Σp and the performance sensitivity is the column vector s, then σP² ≈ sᵀΣp s . This follows from linear covariance propagation. For sensitivity magnitudes 0.02 and 0.05 degrees/fF, both standard deviations 10 fF and correlation 0.8, the variance is 0.2² + 0.5² + 2(0.2)(0.5)(0.8) = 0.45 degree² , giving σP ≈ 0.671°. Ignoring correlation gives √0.29 ≈ 0.539°, an optimistic margin here. If the sensitivities have opposite signs, the same correlation can instead reduce output variation. Preserve the signs and covariance. Design Centering, PDF p. 111 , p. 183 . A conservative single-output lower-bound screen can require Ppred − kσP − emodel ≥ L , where emodel is a defined bound or reserve for prediction error. It becomes a probabilistic statement only under the distribution and linearization assumptions used to interpret k. It is not automatically a joint yield claim over multiple outputs and operating conditions. Uncertain systematic model bias should not be merged into independent random mismatch merely to obtain a smaller root-sum-square number. Likewise, thermal gradient and random mismatch are different mechanisms. A deterministic temperature difference can shift the mean; process variation changes the distribution; nonlinear interactions may alter both. Record which terms are estimated as worst-case bounds, covariance contributions or unresolved modeling error. See robust design and yield for the complete acceptance event.","url":"topic-performance-budgets.html#uncertain-budgets"},{"title":"From circuit specifications to physical budgets · Refresh sensitivity models when the layout leaves their domain","text":"Refresh sensitivity models when the layout leaves their domain Compute sensitivities with an appropriate circuit method: perturbation, direct sensitivity or adjoint analysis. An adjoint can make many parameter derivatives efficient for one scalar response; it does not generally produce arbitrarily many independent response gradients for free. Choose perturbation sizes large enough to exceed solver noise but small enough to remain local, and compare forward and reverse estimates for critical quantities. Lampaert, PDF pp. 43–45 ; Design Centering, PDF pp. 72–75 . Normalize a trust region using physical scales D: ||D⁻¹(p − p0)|| ≤ Δ . This prevents a numerical unit change from changing the permitted physical step. After evaluating a candidate, compare actual and predicted changes in every critical metric. If a sensitivity predicts a 1° phase-margin loss and simulation finds 3°, shrink the domain, increase the error reserve, and refresh the model near the relevant candidate. A rejected prediction is valuable evidence, not noise to average away. Trigger refresh after a topology change, device resize, variant or finger change, operating-region change, altered aggressor neighborhood, or active worst-case scenario change. These can cause discontinuities that a small coordinate perturbation model cannot represent. Keep a cache key containing netlist, device models, PDK, extraction setup, scenario and geometry revision. This is why the performance loop needs explicit model identity rather than a global array of unnamed weights.","url":"topic-performance-budgets.html#model-refresh"},{"title":"From circuit specifications to physical budgets · A concrete implementation contract and checks","text":"A concrete implementation contract and checks for each specification and required scenario: establish baseline performance and available headroom define parasitic variables, units and ownership estimate sensitivities and verify perturbation consistency build coupled performance inequalities plus uncertainty reserve derive cheap geometric child constraints with explicit assumptions search with those children while retaining the electrical parent extract selected complete routes; compare prediction with simulation reallocate, refresh or report insufficient headroom as justified The existing Requirements tier split can hold these rules, but shared electrical constraints need identities and scopes that survive lowering. The current per-net length rule deliberately treats an unrouted net's zero length as passing; therefore connectivity must be checked separately before interpreting the parasitic result. A short or missing route cannot count as an electrically successful low-capacitance solution. Validate dimensions with a known sheet-resistance example; compare predicted with extracted R while changing width, layer and via count independently. Validate coupled allocation with the worked two-net example and a second metric that makes the first tradeoff infeasible. Check correlated propagation against sampled linear models. Finally compare candidate rankings against actual circuit responses across held-out geometries and scenarios. These checks establish whether the budget protects the intended performance, which is the reason to generate it.","url":"topic-performance-budgets.html#budget-implementation"},{"title":"The post-layout performance feedback loop · Make the circuit specification executable","text":"Make the circuit specification executable A schematic alone does not define the layout objective. Define the operating supplies, bias conditions, loads, modes, process/temperature range, testbenches and measurement definitions. “Bandwidth” can mean unity-gain frequency, a closed-loop −3 dB bandwidth, or a settling requirement. “Offset” can mean nominal systematic offset, a mismatch standard deviation or a tail quantile. The optimizer must know which quantity and which scenario are constrained. For each upper-bounded metric f i , store a residual r i (L,s) = [f i (L,s)−u i ]/q i , with a positive scale q i tied to the allocation or tolerance. For lower bounds reverse the sign. The deterministic constraint is max s∈S r i ≤ 0 over required scenarios S. A statistical specification adds a probability requirement over characterized random variables; a few deterministic corners are not a yield estimate. See robust design . A nominally attractive result with failed phase margin is infeasible if phase margin is a requirement. Keep feasibility separate from objective weighting. Once all required constraints pass, rank the remaining area, power, performance and runtime tradeoffs according to the declared design policy. This is the bridge between optimality and implementation. Performance-driven motivation, PDF pp. 1–3 Layout objective, PDF p. 20","url":"topic-performance-loop.html#specification"},{"title":"The post-layout performance feedback loop · Use several evaluation fidelities, with explicit promotion criteria","text":"Use several evaluation fidelities, with explicit promotion criteria Stage Question answered Why use it here? What it cannot certify Intent and topology Are device roles, terminal graph and allowed transformations valid? Rejects impossible candidates before geometric work. Actual manufacturability or circuit performance. Placement/route estimates Are there plausible pin access, matching, thermal and resource margins? Cheap evaluation guides thousands of moves. Final route parasitics or foundry DRC. Drawn geometry checks Did the generator produce connected, legal shapes within supported rules? Finds detailed failures hidden by bounding boxes. Rules/models absent from the checker. Extracted nominal simulation Does the realized circuit meet required nominal/corner behavior? Connects geometry to actual analog metrics. Unmodeled variation, mission history or manufacturing effects. Robust and independent verification Does the selected candidate retain margin under required uncertainty and qualified decks? Concentrates expensive evaluation on viable candidates. A global optimum among all possible layouts. A low-fidelity rejection must itself be justified. For a requirement f ≤ U, a proven upper bound at or below U certifies a pass, while a proven lower bound above U certifies a failure. An upper bound above U alone does not prove failure; an uncertain heuristic should prioritize evaluation, not silently prove infeasibility. Store model domain and uncertainty with each result. If two candidates are close relative to model error, promote both instead of assigning false precision to their ordering.","url":"topic-performance-loop.html#tiers"},{"title":"The post-layout performance feedback loop · Translate performance margin into useful physical budgets","text":"Translate performance margin into useful physical budgets Near a characterized point p₀, approximate a metric as f(p₀+Δp) ≈ f(p₀)+JΔp, where p includes selected parasitic R/C, device parameters or local effects. Estimate derivatives analytically or with controlled perturbation of the extracted/testbench model. Keep the signs: added source resistance may reduce a transconductance-related metric, whereas added compensation capacitance can improve one stability measure while hurting speed. Consider an illustrative requirement allowing at most 10 mV of extra error. Two layout quantities x and y contribute approximately 2x+y mV in the valid local domain. The shared constraint is 2x+y ≤ 10. Independent caps x≤2 and y≤4 only use 8 mV of that allocation at their corner, and reject x=3,y=2 even though its predicted error is also 8 mV. Conversely, assigning each variable the entire 10 mV budget admits combinations exceeding the total. This is why shared performance constraints are better than arbitrary independent limits when reliable sensitivities exist. The example is a derivation of budget coupling, not a calibrated Philis circuit. Performance budgets develops the full method from Lampaert. Use a trust region around the characterized operating point, then re-extract and re-simulate after meaningful geometry changes. Near a mode transition, saturation boundary, oscillation onset or disconnected route, a linear model may be invalid. Objective and sensitivity-derived bounds, PDF pp. 34–42 Make feedback actionable: report “output-node capacitance consumed 80% of the modeled bandwidth margin” with the model's scope, not just “cost rose.” Local sensitivity is still an attribution estimate. Validate an intervention by changing that feature and observing the full circuit; interactions may reverse a predicted benefit.","url":"topic-performance-loop.html#sensitivities"},{"title":"The post-layout performance feedback loop · Choose search variables that can reach the needed improvement","text":"Choose search variables that can reach the needed improvement A net-weight optimizer searches the layouts produced by its P&R generator. It cannot discover a missing capacitor-array topology or a device orientation that the generator excludes. A width optimizer cannot repair a floorplan that leaves no legal route corridor. Therefore expose several levels of decisions: macro pattern and aspect ratio, relative topology, allowed orientation, route topology/layer/width, and, only if circuit-authorized, sizing. Use a staged or hierarchical search because the Cartesian product is large. Retain several placement alternatives through routing; route-aware evaluation may reverse a placement-only ranking. Within a promising topology, optimize discrete widths or layer choices; if electrical limits remain unreachable, reopen placement or an authorized sizing level. Distinguish “no feasible candidate found within this search” from “the specified constraints are mathematically inconsistent.” Optimization and prediction approaches, PDF pp. 2–3 Chapter 3, §§3.4–3.5, hierarchical alternatives, PDF pp. 137–147 Chapter 4, §4.5.4, placement/global-route integration, PDF pp. 191–193 Every candidate needs a reproducible record: schematic and elaboration hashes, unit/macro choices, coordinates and transforms, route graph and geometry, rule/model versions, seed, testbench, simulation status and metric vector. This record lets the engine revisit a candidate after budgets or models change without confusing stale predictions with fresh measurements.","url":"topic-performance-loop.html#search"},{"title":"The post-layout performance feedback loop · Preserve feasible alternatives instead of hiding them in one scalar","text":"Preserve feasible alternatives instead of hiding them in one scalar Candidate A dominates B for minimization objectives if A is no worse in every required objective and strictly better in at least one. Apply that comparison among candidates passing the same required constraints and evaluated under compatible assumptions. A failed high-speed layout does not dominate a feasible slower one. Uncertain or missing metrics should prevent a confident dominance claim. Retain a bounded nondominated archive over the relevant metrics. Choose the final layout by an explicit policy, such as minimum area subject to bandwidth, noise, offset, reliability and yield margins. A weighted score is useful when weights have a design meaning, but it may miss alternatives on a nonconvex frontier and can conceal units or feasibility if used carelessly. The observed archive is not a proof of the global frontier. Multiobjective discussion, PDF pp. 4–5 Report measured margin to each required limit, not only a figure of merit. For example, a gain×bandwidth/power objective can rise while phase margin or output swing becomes unacceptable. A normalized objective with an undocumented denominator can also make results incomparable across different circuits. Use circuit-specific objectives and compare methods under identical definitions.","url":"topic-performance-loop.html#archive"},{"title":"The post-layout performance feedback loop · Use learned models as accountable evaluators","text":"Use learned models as accountable evaluators The wire-sizing manuscript demonstrates one useful pattern: learn a graph-based classifier at a chosen performance threshold, cheaply propose width assignments, then route/extract/simulate finalists. It retains connectivity, device and routing features because those affect behavior. The manuscript denotes its soft classification output P(FOM<T) for a user-specified threshold. A single-threshold score supplies neither a full cumulative distribution nor a calibrated manufacturing-failure probability. It also differs from FOM regression. Preserve these distinctions in the evaluator interface. §4.2, graph features, PDF p. 8 §4.3–4.5, Eqs. (17)–(19), classification output/training, PDF pp. 10–11 A surrogate needs a domain declaration: PDK, circuit topology/family, sizes, legal route widths/layers, bias modes and training distribution. Measure error separately for each required metric, especially false-feasible predictions near a limit. Held-out random variants of one circuit establish less than held-out topologies or processes. Estimate uncertainty and fall back to simulation when the candidate leaves the validated domain. Begin with simple sensitivity or regression baselines. Record the cost of obtaining labels as well as training and inference. Cached evaluations and active selection of informative candidates can matter more than a larger model. In the supplied survey the runtime chart and prose have inconsistent percentages; the defensible lesson is to measure Philis's own end-to-end cost, not import the reported fractions. Data and transfer costs, PDF pp. 3–5 An optimizer can exploit model errors, particularly when it makes many queries or proposes shapes unlike the training data. Validate the selected candidates with the actual extractor/testbench, and periodically evaluate candidates the model dislikes to detect blind spots. Keep final verification data separate from iterative fitting when estimating honest generalization.","url":"topic-performance-loop.html#surrogates"},{"title":"The post-layout performance feedback loop · Build on the checks Philis already performs","text":"Build on the checks Philis already performs The current library flow already runs drawn-geometry DRC/ERC/LVS/PEX scoring within its epoch loop, then selects through lex_key . This is valuable feedback; the missing extension is a complete circuit-performance result vector and its required scenarios. The current final scalar uses signoff's extracted total capacitance. Total capacitance cannot distinguish two equal-C layouts whose capacitance lies on different electrical nodes. Preserve the existing early checks while adding a performance-evaluation boundary after usable extraction. Do not call a failed or unsupported extraction a zero-parasitic result. Cache only when geometry, PDK/model, extraction options and testbench identities match. Add result states for failure, unknown applicability, insufficient coverage and successful evaluation. Missing operating-point current also must not certify EM or thermal feasibility. A practical first milestone is an OTA with fixed schematic and qualified testbenches. Compare simple generated alternatives using gain, UGF, phase margin, input noise, offset, power and area under stated conditions. Then introduce one new search dimension at a time and explain its causal result. The handbook's roadmap orders infrastructure ahead of more elaborate search because optimization only helps when its evaluator answers the right question.","url":"topic-performance-loop.html#philis"},{"title":"The post-layout performance feedback loop · State what completion and optimality mean","text":"State what completion and optimality mean A run can stop on a compute budget, lack of improvement, exhaustion of a finite candidate set or a proof-bound criterion. Record which occurred. “Best verified candidate after 200 evaluations” is an honest result. “Optimal layout” requires an explicit feasible set, objective and proof or stated approximation meaning. For practical analog P&R, the immediate target is a reproducibly feasible, high-quality layout with explainable margins and evidence that the search improved it. Acceptance experiments should include an equal-total-C but different-node-loading pair; a candidate that improves bandwidth while failing phase margin; a layout that defeats a local sensitivity model; a stale-cache identity change; and a surrogate that is uncertain outside its circuit family. These discriminate a true performance loop from a renamed geometric score.","url":"topic-performance-loop.html#stop"},{"title":"Placement representations and symmetry · Choose a representation for the constraints that must survive every move","text":"Choose a representation for the constraints that must survive every move A placement representation is more than a way to store coordinates. It determines which layouts can be reached, which constraints are true by construction, the cost of generating coordinates, and what a search move means. Coordinates offer freedom but admit overlap. A sequence pair encodes a relative ordering that can be packed without overlap. A symmetry-aware tree can make reflected pairs inseparable during search. A hierarchy can reduce the search dramatically while excluding useful global arrangements. The correct choice follows from required invariants and available computation, not from a universally superior tree. Separate three objects. Electrical devices belong to the netlist. Physical modules are generated shapes containing one device, several fingers, or multiple electrically related devices. Placement states select a module variant, orientation and location while preserving the device-to-terminal mapping. Confusing those objects makes a geometric swap change electrical identity or makes a common-centroid equation count a merged macro as one unit when it represents several. The distinction is explicit in the topological volume's hierarchical synthesis chapter. Topological Approaches, PDF pp. 111–115 . This chapter derives small examples and then proposes an incremental path for Philis. Geometry is illustrative and is not a PDK rule set. Before comparing algorithms, read the optimality contract : a representation cannot make an omitted electrical or manufacturing constraint true.","url":"topic-placement-representations.html#placement-purpose"},{"title":"Placement representations and symmetry · Coordinates: flexible state, explicit legality burden","text":"Coordinates: flexible state, explicit legality burden For each rectangle i, let its lower-left corner be (xi, yi), width wi and height hi. Two rectangles do not overlap if at least one relation holds: i left of j, j left of i, i below j, or j below i. With required horizontal and vertical clearances, these are inequalities such as xi + wi + sij ≤ xj . Their logical or is what makes unconstrained placement difficult: the optimizer must select a separating relation before ordinary continuous compaction can enforce it. Philis stores device centers and oriented half-extents in Layout (line 11), together with orientation, variant, group, symmetry-axis and discrete-branch state. The equivalent left edge is x − hw . This is a useful general state representation: topology packers can emit the same coordinates, so an experimental sequence-pair or hierarchical packer need not replace the physical database. However, a bounding box does not describe terminal access, well spacing, internal diffusion, or a nonrectangular halo. Action and reason: define a canonical placement candidate with stable physical-module identity, generated geometry revision, terminal map and exact coordinate transform. Let each search representation produce a candidate through an adapter. A single downstream legality evaluator then makes comparisons meaningful. Otherwise one algorithm can appear better because its packer ignored a halo or used device-center wirelength while another used actual pins. Maintain variant and orientation invariants atomically. A rotation that changes the footprint must update extents and pin transforms in the same transaction. This is already stated as an invariant in Layout . Add independent checks for transformed polygons and accesses, because equal rectangle dimensions do not imply equal terminal geometry.","url":"topic-placement-representations.html#coordinate-model"},{"title":"Placement representations and symmetry · Sequence pairs: decode orders into separation constraints","text":"Sequence pairs: decode orders into separation constraints A sequence pair consists of two permutations α and β of the same modules. Use the convention in the source: if i precedes j in both sequences, i is left of j. If i precedes j in α but follows j in β, i is above j. Every pair therefore receives one horizontal or vertical separation relation. Implementations using the opposite vertical convention are equally possible, but packer, moves and tests must share one definition. Topological Approaches, PDF pp. 48–51 . Take α = (A, B, C), β = (B, A, C), with A of size 4×2, B of size 3×3 and C of size 2×4. A is above B because their order reverses. A and B are both left of C because each precedes C in both sequences. Set the source coordinate to zero. Horizontal constraints give xA = xB = 0 and xC = max(4, 3) = 4. Vertical constraints give yB = yC = 0 and yA = 3. The resulting bounding rectangle is 6×5. This packing is minimal for those separation relations and dimensions; it is not a proof that 6×5 is globally optimal over all sequence pairs. Three blocks decoded from a sequence pair Two permutations give A above B and both left of C. A constraint graph and corresponding six by five packing are shown. α = A B C β = B A C Horizontal precedence A B C +4 +3 Vertical: yA ≥ yB + 3 Longest paths produce coordinates. A: 4 × 2 B: 3 × 3 C: 2 × 4 Packed width 6; height 5; zero gap example Download this SVG Original teaching schematic. Abstract units and zero clearance illustrate the representation; real packing adds technology-dependent clearances and access reservations. A straightforward implementation builds directed horizontal and vertical graphs and computes longest paths. Faster weighted longest-common-subsequence algorithms avoid explicitly storing every pairwise relation. The surveyed contour and priority structures improve decoding cost, but do not change the electrical meaning of a placement. Benchmark both decode time and feasible useful candidates per second. A faster decoder that generates mostly constraint violations can be slower at reaching a usable layout. PDF pp. 25–47 . decode(alpha, beta, dimensions, required_gaps): verify both permutations contain the same unique module IDs classify every pair using the documented order convention add directed separation edges with dimension + required gap check the resulting coordinate systems for consistency obtain x and y by longest paths or an equivalent decoder emit a candidate and verify the constraints outside the encoding Same-axis symmetry is not automatic. The source derives a sufficient ordering condition for symmetry-feasible sequence pairs involving each module's reflected partner. It explicitly states that the condition is sufficient, not necessary. Searching only this subset exchanges coverage for efficiency. Record that restriction in experiment reports; an excluded feasible layout is not evidence that the circuit itself is infeasible. Multiple symmetry groups also require care because interleaved repair passes can obstruct each other; nested embedding or controlled group ordering avoids that failure mode. PDF pp. 49–50 , pp. 60–64 .","url":"topic-placement-representations.html#sequence-pair"},{"title":"Placement representations and symmetry · B* trees and symmetry islands: encode constructive structure","text":"B* trees and symmetry islands: encode constructive structure In the conventional B* interpretation presented in the symmetry-island chapter, a left child is placed to the right of its parent and a right child has the parent's x coordinate. A contour records the lowest legal y position above previously packed blocks. The contour is essential: a tree edge alone does not completely determine y when intervening blocks occupy space. Check conventions when combining chapters, because the hierarchical LP chapter uses a related tree with its own construction details. PDF pp. 79–85 . An automatically symmetric representation stores one representative of each mirrored pair and reconstructs its partner. Self-symmetric blocks lie on the axis. The island requirement additionally keeps group members connected by abutment. This is useful when close matching and local routing are essential, but it is stronger than symmetry itself: a symmetric solution need not be one connected island. Later extensions permit nested groups and otherwise unused spaces. The restriction should be selected because it serves an identified design purpose, not applied to every recognized pair automatically. PDF pp. 100–106 . Symmetry island and hierarchical physical boundary Two pairs and one central module form a mirrored island. Its enclosing boundary includes an access and well margin. A parent-level box consumes that boundary rather than only active devices. A A′ B B′ self Include well / access / isolation boundary Parent-level module outline + pin access + inherited intent Conceptual mirrored group; adjacency and PDK details must be generated explicitly. Download this SVG Original schematic of representative reconstruction and the interface passed to a parent. The rectangles show intent, not an implementation-ready transistor arrangement. Action: add a rigid or parametrically deformable group move before replacing the whole placer. Move the shared axis and legal representatives, regenerate partners and then check external constraints. This preserves equalities by construction and reduces repeated repair. The cost is a restricted move family, so retain occasional group restructuring or alternative templates to avoid freezing an initially poor internal arrangement. The hierarchical B* work supports rectilinear island boundaries and contour-aware placement. A parent that sees only a bounding rectangle may waste reusable cavities; a parent that sees only active rectangles may illegally place another block in required well or guard-ring space. Offer a deliberate interface with occupied geometry, exclusive regions and permitted routing regions. Each region answers a different physical question.","url":"topic-placement-representations.html#bstar-islands"},{"title":"Placement representations and symmetry · Constraint graphs, linear equations and hierarchy","text":"Constraint graphs, linear equations and hierarchy Once topology selects a relation, many spacing requirements become difference constraints xj − xi ≥ dij . A directed acyclic graph can compact these by longest paths. General linear equalities such as xA + xB = 2a for a symmetry axis a, or weighted centroid relations, need a richer linear system. Pretending a four-variable relation is an ordinary two-node spacing edge loses information. The retargeting chapter develops generalized coefficient graphs for this reason. PDF pp. 215–232 . Use a constraint graph or LP as a legality and compaction stage after a topology move. The rationale is separation of duties: search chooses combinatorial ordering and variants; a solver obtains the best coordinates for those choices. A feasible LP certifies only its encoded linear constraints. It does not prove diffusion sharing, orientation-dependent effects or post-route performance unless those models are actually included and valid. Hierarchy should reflect electrical relationships while permitting overlap between relationship types. A device can belong to a matched pair, a current path and a proximity set simultaneously. The source's multi-level symmetry/matching/proximity graph gives one priority ordering, but also recognizes simulation-dependent priorities. Collapsing all intent into a single disjoint tree can erase those simultaneous requirements. Keep the semantic constraint graph even when a tree is chosen as the search decomposition. PDF pp. 119–129 . Bottom-up shape curves enumerate several child arrangements and retain width/height alternatives. For an ordinary horizontal composition, width is the sum of child widths and gaps while height is their maximum; vertical composition exchanges those operations. Enhanced shapes can exploit contours. Pruning a shape because another is narrower and shorter is safe only for the objectives and contexts represented by that dominance relation. A slightly larger child may expose the sensitive output on the correct side and reduce parent-level parasitics. Retain pin-interface or electrical equivalence classes when pruning. PDF pp. 137–147 .","url":"topic-placement-representations.html#graphs-hierarchy"},{"title":"Placement representations and symmetry · Choose and verify the hybrid that fits the engine","text":"Choose and verify the hybrid that fits the engine Representation Useful guarantee Remaining burden Coordinates with repair Flexible placement and local geometry updates Overlap, coupled repairs and reachability between feasible regions Sequence pair Pairwise nonoverlap after valid decoding Symmetry, shape changes, layer rules and electrical costs Symmetric B* island Constructive mirrored structure within its represented family Coverage loss, external access and non-island alternatives Constraint graph / LP Feasible coordinates and optimum for a fixed modeled subproblem Combinatorial topology selection and nonlinear physics Hierarchical shape alternatives Reuse and efficient assembly Parent context, overlapping intent and unsafe local pruning Philis already recognizes blocks in recognize (line 175), emits stage axes in placement (line 53), and applies projected moves in Sa::trial (line 140). Build a comparison around that boundary: current coordinate search; group-preserving coordinate moves; then an optional topological initializer or packer. This sequence lets each added representation justify itself through better feasible search rather than architectural fashion. Verify a tiny sequence-pair case by enumerating both permutations and comparing decoded nonoverlap with a direct rectangle check. Verify group moves with unequal widths and self-symmetric blocks, then with nested axes and fixed devices. Check that round-tripping a topology through coordinates preserves electrical identity, orientation and pin geometry. Finally compare complete routed results with identical model fidelity, random-seed budgets and hardware time. Area alone cannot tell whether a representation helped analog layout. A useful report records both solution quality and representation coverage : fixed hierarchy, permitted variants, symmetry restrictions, ordering convention and unresolved failures. Search failure inside a restricted family is evidence about that family and runtime budget. It should trigger a broader representation or a clear incomplete result, never an unsupported statement that no valid layout exists.","url":"topic-placement-representations.html#representation-selection"},{"title":"Power, ground, IR drop and current paths · Validation and limits","text":"Validation and limits Validate a three-load tree with analytically known branch currents and drops; a mesh whose currents change after one branch is widened; simultaneous versus staggered pulses; equal device geometry with unequal source resistance; a wide trunk with a narrow access throat; a force/sense landing before versus after a resistive contact; and a quiet body ring sharing a switching return. Compare DC network results with an independent solver and AC/transient results with extracted circuit analysis. Check current conservation and explicit units, and inspect both forward and return contributions. This chapter derives a power-distribution workflow from the supplied EM and substrate sources plus elementary circuit relations; it does not claim that a single textbook provides a universal analog power-grid template.","url":"topic-power-distribution.html#coverage"},{"title":"Antenna, ESD, latch-up and operating stress · Reliability is a set of conditional physical claims","text":"Reliability is a set of conditional physical claims A manufacturable layout must continue meeting its specification under the required voltage, current, temperature, handling and lifetime conditions. These are different questions. An ordinary operating current can cause long-term electromigration; a brief discharge can rupture an oxide; a transient pad excursion can inject minority carriers and trigger latchup. A circuit may remain functional after stress while its input bias or matching has degraded beyond the allowed error. Reliability therefore belongs in the engine's acceptance conditions, not in a single optional aesthetic penalty. The source supplies mechanisms and examples, not the qualification limits for the target technology. Every numerical protection threshold must come from the appropriate process/device/package characterization and product specification. Keep separate records for normal operation, startup/shutdown, permitted fault conditions, manufacturing exposure, ESD qualification and aging. Passing one category cannot be substituted for passing another. PDF pp. 211–274 , 748–776","url":"topic-protection.html#pro-purpose"},{"title":"Antenna, ESD, latch-up and operating stress · Reliability and protection are constraints on paths, scenarios and time","text":"Reliability and protection are constraints on paths, scenarios and time Mechanism Required layout action and reason Evidence to retain Self-heating Size resistors, contacts, wires and power cells for their continuous and transient energy dissipation. Deposited material over oxide and short adiabatic pulses require different thermal reasoning. Current waveform, thermal boundary conditions, temperature rise and qualified material/device limits. Filamentation/current focusing Use qualified distributed contacts, ballasting and device shapes so a small conducting region does not carry destructive current. SOA/TLP or other relevant characterization of the actual structure; total drawn width alone is not proof. Electromigration Check every segment, neck, bend and via transition, accounting for current crowding and temperature. Low total resistance can coexist with one failing bottleneck. Foundry current/lifetime model, material, temperature, waveform and local current distribution. Dielectric breakdown Respect electric-field and lifetime limits, including voltage differences across adjacent shapes and gate/plate dielectrics. PDK voltage-dependent rules and qualified voltage, duty-cycle, area and temperature domain. Antenna damage Check connected conductors as they exist at each process stage, with the required area and perimeter measures. Use higher-layer jumpers or qualified discharge structures where indicated. Stage/layer connectivity, gate oxide family, exposed area/perimeter, diode credit and final foundry antenna results. ESD Place primary devices near pad entry and secondary protection near vulnerable terminals. Route complete low-impedance discharge paths and protect crossings between domains. Qualified clamp behavior, relevant stress model, path R/L, current capability and protected-terminal transient voltage. Latchup/debiasing Control minority injection and well/substrate potential using suitable isolation, contacts and carrier-specific guards. Cross-section connectivity, bias range, contact resistance, injector scenarios and foundry latchup qualification. Hot-carrier/BTI and long-term drift Avoid unequal stress histories where matching matters and preserve qualified bias limits. Include drift in the final error budget. Operating scenarios, duty cycles, aging models and matched differential degradation. Surface charge, contamination and package damage Use appropriate field plates, channel stops, seals, passivation openings and package exclusions. Actual process/assembly rules and supported device structures, rather than historical textbook distances. The physical mechanisms and summary are in PDF pp. 212–274 ; detailed ESD structures and networks are in 748–776 . Antenna is fabrication-history dependent. A long conductor may not yet connect to a gate when its layer is etched. Conversely, a small final connected component can have a problematic exposure history. The book distinguishes areal and peripheral ratios and discusses latent connections during incomplete etching. A higher-layer jumper changes the earlier connected component; it is not merely a change in final total wire area. A top-layer violation cannot be repaired by jumping to a nonexistent higher layer. A diode brings junction capacitance, leakage, placement and sometimes illumination requirements, all of which must be checked. PDF pp. 228–230 ESD protection must remain benign during operation. A snapback clamp has trigger and holding behavior, while a rate-triggered device responds to slew. A normal power-up ramp, a failsafe input above an unpowered supply, or an external source able to sustain holding current can therefore alter device choice. Extra rail width alone cannot repair a clamp that is inappropriate for the application. Primary pad protection also does not ensure a nearby thin gate survives fast local discharge; return points and local secondary clamps matter. The book's illustrated HBM/CDM values are examples from specific structures and eras, not current universal qualification requirements. PDF pp. 749–752 , 766–776","url":"topic-protection.html#source-protection"},{"title":"Antenna, ESD, latch-up and operating stress · Antenna checking as a process-stage graph problem","text":"Antenna checking as a process-stage graph problem Plasma processing can deposit charge on a conductor before the final interconnect stack exists. The vulnerable gate oxide is the discharge path. During late etching, exposed perimeters can matter; during ashing, exposed surface area can matter. The source also describes temporary connections that disappear only after narrow gaps finish clearing. Final-net area alone is consequently an incomplete physical model. The exact foundry deck determines which effects are represented and how they are accumulated. Proposed algorithm. For each relevant manufacturing stage, build the connected-component graph using only connections that exist at that stage. Associate each component with the vulnerable gate areas and oxide families it reaches. Compute the stage's defined exposed area, perimeter or cumulative quantity, then apply qualified diode credits and rule limits. Retain the offending stage and component as the diagnostic. If the process includes latent-connection rules, apply the foundry-defined geometric grouping; do not invent an oversize radius from a generic example. PDF pp. 228–230 Worked example. Assume a hypothetical M1 rule limits its exposed-area-to-gate-area ratio to 100. A gate of 1 μm² connected to 180 μm² of M1 fails that rule. A qualified M2 bridge can divide the early M1 into a 20 μm² gate-connected island and a remote conductor. At the M1 stage the gate now sees a ratio of 20. This calculation says nothing about the M2 rule, which must still be evaluated after its connections exist; it also ignores perimeter and cumulative rules unless the hypothetical deck includes them. The example numbers are deliberately illustrative, not usable PDK defaults. Repair selection is itself an analog optimization. A bridge adds vias, changes resistance and parasitics, consumes upper-layer access and may disturb route symmetry. A discharge diode adds leakage and capacitance, has a process-qualified bias construction and may need a fill exclusion. The source's illuminated-junction mechanism for one antenna-diode type is a particularly useful reminder that an ordinary-looking diode footprint may carry non-obvious surrounding-metal restrictions. A top-layer violation cannot be fixed by adding an unavailable higher layer. After repair, repeat antenna and circuit checks on the resulting geometry. Philis currently has a useful seed for an antenna metric, but the inspected kernel/analog/src/routing/antenna.rs sums routed area and divides by gate area. Treat that result as a screening estimate unless the target process explicitly validates that simplification. A stage-aware rule should name the graph, measure, gate family, diode credit and deck version. This recommendation concerns the inspected implementation, not every possible downstream signoff tool. Confirmed integration issue in the inspected working tree. The SPICE parser emits MOS terminals in G, D, S, B order, and the hypergraph preserves that order. However, the antenna requirement annotator adds gate area to nets[1] , which denotes the drain in this representation. That can attach a gate-area requirement to the wrong net before the geometric antenna estimate even runs. Resolve the gate by its named terminal or a shared typed terminal accessor, and add a regression with distinct gate and drain nets. A diode-connected device, where those nets coincide, can conceal the mistake. This is a code observation and proposed fix; no engine code was changed for these notes.","url":"topic-protection.html#pro-antenna"},{"title":"Antenna, ESD, latch-up and operating stress · ESD architecture, local protection and complete discharge paths","text":"ESD architecture, local protection and complete discharge paths ESD planning begins at the pins and domain boundaries. For each required stress polarity and pin-to-pin combination, identify how current enters, which qualified devices conduct and where it exits. Include internal supply, ground and substrate-return segments. A clamp with an acceptable standalone characteristic can fail to protect a gate if the path adds excessive voltage drop or if another device triggers first and absorbs the current. The book distinguishes clipping, snapback and rate-fired protectors. A clipping device has a leakage-versus-clamp-voltage tradeoff. A snapback device must not remain on under an allowed supply or externally driven condition. A rate-fired protector must distinguish a qualifying discharge from the allowed supply/input slew or provide qualified control appropriate to the application. A failsafe input above an unpowered supply adds a further constraint. These are circuit and library decisions; an automatic router should preserve the selected protection architecture rather than silently synthesize a different one. §14.4.1, PDF pp. 750–752 ; §14.4.2, PDF p. 769, failsafe CDM clamps Why secondary protection is local. The protected quantity is usually a voltage across an oxide or junction, not voltage at the package pad relative to an abstract ideal ground. A remote ground line can rise while a local source remains at another potential. Put qualified secondary structures near the vulnerable terminals and join their return to the same local reference. Preserve the specified limiting resistance or filter topology. Lower routing resistance is not universally beneficial if a deliberately inserted resistance coordinates protection. Illustrative path budget. Suppose a qualified clamp develops 7 V at a chosen 2 A transient operating point, the relevant path contributes 0.4 Ω, and a 1 nH loop segment experiences 0.5 A/ns. The lumped terms give 7 + 0.8 + 0.5 = 8.3 V. An 8 V allowed transient stress would therefore be exceeded in this simplified example. Actual qualification needs the nonlinear time-domain network and appropriate pulse characterization; the arithmetic shows why clamp voltage alone is not the protected-terminal voltage. Shortening a local loop can matter as much as increasing the number of remote clamps. Power handling cannot be inferred solely from total drawn transistor width. During snapback, one finger may turn on first and conduct a disproportionate fraction of current. The source discusses local ballasting and structure-dependent current spreading. Use qualified multifinger ESD cells with permitted routing and contact arrangements. Do not remove ballast or silicide-block geometry merely because a DC optimizer sees extra resistance. ESD damage can be parametric: reverse emitter-base avalanche may degrade β and input-bias accuracy without immediately destroying the transistor. PDF pp. 757–761 , 766–769","url":"topic-protection.html#pro-esd"},{"title":"Antenna, ESD, latch-up and operating stress · Current, heat, dielectric stress and aging","text":"Current, heat, dielectric stress and aging Electromigration is local. Resistance integrates along a path, but reliability can be set by one narrow neck or crowded via. The relevant current and temperature metrics depend on the foundry lifetime model and waveform. A via array's total cut count is not sufficient when geometry feeds mostly the inner corner. Route straight distributed transitions where useful, check every bottleneck and retain the current direction, waveform and local temperature assumption. Extra metal can reduce resistance while leaving the critical neck unchanged. PDF pp. 217–220 , 821–822 Heating depends on time and stack. A deposited resistor separated from silicon by oxide can have a different thermal path from a diffused resistor. A brief pulse can deposit energy before heat has spread through the same volume involved in steady state. Check continuous dissipation and transient energy using qualified models. A large total device area does not rule out current focusing within a smaller hot region. Electrothermal verification should include the feedback of temperature on conduction, not merely annotate a temperature after a fixed-current calculation. Dielectric stress is not just a spacing lookup. Gate and capacitor dielectrics, interlevel insulators and field regions have different qualified voltage, time and temperature domains. Nearby-net spacing must use the maximum allowed differential voltage, including startup and domain sequencing. For a lifetime requirement, retain the applicable dielectric-aging model or conservative qualified operating limit. Historical source examples demonstrate the mechanism but do not establish a modern allowable field. §5.1.4, PDF pp. 220–224 ; §15.4.4, PDF pp. 820–821, differential-voltage spacing Matching can age. Devices with equal initial geometry but unequal drain stress, gate bias, duty cycle or temperature can diverge. Model differential degradation where it affects the specification. A symmetric pattern cannot make different operating histories equal. This is especially relevant when the engine claims that a mirror or differential input remains accurate over lifetime rather than only at initial extraction. §5.3.1, PDF pp. 236–238 ; §5.3.4, PDF pp. 243–244","url":"topic-protection.html#pro-lifetime"},{"title":"Antenna, ESD, latch-up and operating stress · Guard rings and device sharing require a cross-sectional model","text":"Guard rings and device sharing require a cross-sectional model A substrate tap usually reduces majority-carrier voltage drop. A minority-carrier collecting ring intercepts carriers at a suitable reverse-biased junction. A blocking ring uses an effective high-low junction or dielectric barrier to obstruct their path. These are different mechanisms even when their top views look like similar rings. The relevant carrier type, depth, buried layer, bias and contact resistance determine whether carriers pass beneath a ring, over a weak junction, or through an opening. PDF pp. 256–270 , 735–742 Engine actions and reasons: identify potential injectors over transient as well as DC scenarios; choose a qualified guard architecture for the actual substrate; ensure its required three-dimensional continuity; connect it to an appropriate low-impedance node; and verify the return path and local debiasing. A nominal enclosure without a sound electrical connection can fail. More width eventually gives diminishing benefit, and a ring that works on a heavily doped substrate may be marginal on a lightly doped one. A blocking ring typically needs complete enclosure; the text also discusses qualified collecting-ring arrangements near die edges that are not closed rectangles. Do not collapse these distinctions into “all rings must look alike.” Sharing is an electrical transformation. Merging devices in a common well/tank saves repeated isolation area but introduces shared resistance, junction capacitance and minority-carrier paths. Net equivalence is necessary for many mergers but not sufficient for safe operation. Saturating lateral PNPs, noisy isolated MOS devices, shared contacts carrying large current, and resistors whose bodies become forward biased illustrate the failure modes. Whitelist qualified merger templates with terminal/bias and injection conditions, and otherwise retain separate regions. A merge should be re-extracted and rechecked under scenarios, not approved merely because DRC and nominal LVS pass. PDF pp. 723–734 , 600","url":"topic-protection.html#source-isolation"},{"title":"Antenna, ESD, latch-up and operating stress · What the engine must produce to justify “protected”","text":"What the engine must produce to justify “protected” Create a per-scenario evidence record containing the exact cell versions, current paths, terminal voltages, thermal assumptions, rule/model revisions, measured or simulated limits and remaining unknowns. Protection proof is a conjunction: the structure must be qualified; the integration must respect its restrictions; the evaluated paths must stay inside its domain; and the intended product qualification must be satisfied. A low routing cost establishes none of those facts by itself. Regression cases should exercise different failure mechanisms: an antenna route whose final connectivity is unchanged by a successful stage-specific repair; an ESD clamp with an inadequate return path; an off supply with a driven failsafe input; a many-via transition with current crowding; a substrate injector beneath a shallow collecting guard; and a matched pair with unequal aging duty. Each test should fail for its physical reason and pass only after the corresponding mechanism is resolved. Where the PDK supplies no model, expose an unsupported or externally qualified requirement rather than a fictional numerical margin.","url":"topic-protection.html#pro-evidence"},{"title":"Random mismatch and statistical matching · Acceptance evidence and chapter scope","text":"Acceptance evidence and chapter scope Validate with equal-area square and extreme-aspect-ratio devices; fixed-current width and length sweeps; single versus equal-total-area split devices; correlated versus independent unit errors; known linear gradients; unequal source access resistance; and thermal asymmetry. The expected answers should come from independent model calculations or measured data, not from reproducing the implementation’s own formula. For statistical targets report sample uncertainty, mean error, spread, and model range. This chapter synthesizes the complete supplied Pelgrom and Drennan papers, supplemented by the substrate source’s circuit-sensitivity reasoning. Its illustrative values and proposed Philis algorithm are explicitly distinct from characterized process rules.","url":"topic-random-mismatch.html#coverage"},{"title":"Resistor geometry and precision ratios · Start from the error that the resistor network can tolerate","text":"Start from the error that the resistor network can tolerate Resistance is often used as a ratio, a transconductance-setting element, a current sensor, a time constant or a degeneration element. Those applications care about different errors. A uniform 10% shift in two divider resistors can leave the ideal ratio unchanged, while 0.1% differential error changes its output. A current-sense resistor cares about absolute value and terminal definition; a high-value bias resistor may care more about leakage and voltage coefficient. State the function before selecting a layout technique. Hastings distinguishes random variability from systematic geometry and environment effects. Increasing area primarily attacks some random contributions; a larger wrong geometry remains wrong. The useful engine contract includes material/model, value and tolerance, required ratios, bias range, temperature range, power, terminal-current direction, parasitic limits and any trim/Kelvin intent. PDF pp. 284–295 , 368–385","url":"topic-resistors.html#res-target"},{"title":"Resistor geometry and precision ratios · Resistors: geometry, connectivity and electric fields are inseparable","text":"Resistors: geometry, connectivity and electric fields are inseparable Chapter 6 shows why a resistor is not just sheet resistance multiplied by drawn length/width. It has dimension biases, contact crowding, end effects, corner effects, nonlinear voltage and temperature dependence, distributed capacitance, and sometimes parasitic junctions. For a right-angle equal-width corner, the book gives 0.5587 squares rather than the approximate half-square mnemonic. This small difference matters if the algorithm uses many corners to obtain a precision ratio. Dogbone heads do not inherently make two devices match better: identical systematic head contributions can cancel, while unmatched heads cannot. PDF pp. 279–295 Recommended action Reason Qualification or verification Preserve material, implant, sheet-resistance option, width and unit shape across a matched group. Different materials have different process, temperature, stress and voltage responses. Unequal widths convert a common linewidth error into a ratio error; unequal heads/corners add non-ratiometric resistance. Use identical rectangular units where feasible. If a circuit requires unequal units, evaluate their bias sensitivity and end/contact terms explicitly. Select area and width from an allocated random-error budget. More area averages microscopic fluctuations; wider strips reduce edge and linewidth sensitivity. The smaller resistor can dominate a highly unequal ratio. Fit the material's area/periphery model. The textbook's width multipliers and matching grades are provisional guidance, not process-qualified constants. Keep electrically matched directions parallel. Silicon and poly respond differently to stress along and across current flow. A rotated rectangle can have the correct nominal resistance but a different package shift. Record wafer orientation and material dependence. Do not automatically rotate P-type silicon by 45° without a supported PCell and process permission. Use compact balanced banks with equal pitch, preserving symmetry within each bank. Close proximity reduces field differences; smaller CC subarrays limit nonlinear residues. A long single row can span a larger thermal/stress field. Include the area and routing cost of banks. Prioritize the electrically most critical ratios when several devices share an array. Provide appropriate edge dummies and comparable end extensions. Array boundaries experience different etch loading, diffusion tails and lithographic surroundings. A dummy equalizes the environment of an active edge strip. Match active-to-dummy pitch; use process-characterized dummy extent. Contact/tie dummies where necessary to avoid charge accumulation without disturbing sensitive nodes. Avoid excessively short segments and unnecessary bends. Heads, contacts, dopant encroachment and grain boundaries become a larger share of a short resistor. Serpentines compound corner and mask-alignment terms. Use bends when their area benefit justifies a modeled error. Moderate/exceptional matching in Hastings generally favors arrays of repeatable strips. Search segmentation choices for noninteger ratios. A fixed additive error per segment changes two resistors differently if their segment counts do not scale with their intended values. Evaluate the source's segmentation-sensitivity concept using actual head/contact models. Center partial sections within the surrounding geometry and preserve their local environment. Pair opposite electrical traversals of series sections. Contact potentials vary with contact temperature. A thermal gradient along every strip can make their thermoelectric voltages add even when array centroids coincide. Prefer an even number of series sections where compatible with the circuit; test endpoint temperatures and connection polarity, not only placement labels. Match the substrate/field-oxide context and keep disruptive process boundaries away. Underlying topography affects deposited film thickness and parasitics; oxide steps, STI stress, diffusion tails, doping-mask edges and an NBL shadow can create unequal local properties. Use capability flags and characterized exclusion distances. NBL-shadow precautions are relevant only to processes that actually retain that topography. Control nearby and overlying metal, including automatic fill. Metal changes hydrogen access and electric fields. Folded-in connections over only some strips create a different process environment even if nominal interconnect R is balanced. Compare matched coverage or a qualified field-plate strategy. Folded-out routing reduces coverage asymmetry but may require more vias and resistance. Account for tank/body and shield voltage along every segment. Depletion changes conducting cross-section; a common body tied to one rail can create unequal modulation across a divider. A shared shield can itself impose unequal fields. Use extracted body-connected device models. Separate bodies or segmented field plates may help, at added area and capacitance; do not silently alter circuit connections. Limit self-heating and separate sensitive arrays from external heaters. Deposited resistors sit above thermally resistive oxide; unequal power creates local gradients in addition to package-scale gradients. Compute P = I²R over scenarios and use a qualified thermal model. A common ambient temperature is insufficient. Budget metal, contacts, vias and sense-lead drops as part of the ratio. Equal resistor bodies do not guarantee equal terminal resistance. A 2:1 ratio can require each material's interconnect contribution to scale 2:1, because their temperature coefficients differ. Extract terminal-to-terminal resistance by material and topology; insert physical Kelvin/star connections when circuit intent requires them. Keep accurately matched poly away from process features that perturb coating/etching. The text describes large poly-poly capacitor structures affecting photoresist flow and therefore nearby resistor or gate patterning. Characterize the interaction range for the actual stack; a generic geometric nonoverlap test cannot detect it. The detailed source catalog contains 25 resistor recommendations and explains their matching-grade dependence. The table above groups them by mechanism so Philis can avoid encoding the same physical effect several times under different heuristic names. PDF pp. 418–422 Thermoelectrics are a connectivity test, not a centroid test Equation 8.34 is E T = S ΔT C , where S is the contact Seebeck coefficient and ΔT C is the temperature difference between a segment's contacts. Figure 8.22, visually checked here, contrasts connections that add these voltages with connections that largely cancel them. The engine should trace the ordered path through each series chain and accumulate signed thermoelectric sources. This is separate from matching the average resistance under a temperature field. Close placement of serpentine heads reduces endpoint temperature difference, but their orientation must also address mask-alignment sensitivity. PDF pp. 401–402 Material and trim decisions. Thin-film, poly, diffusion, well, metal and pinched resistors occupy different places in the accuracy/area/power/parasitic trade space. Diffused resistors can dissipate heat more effectively through silicon, while deposited resistors avoid the same junction parasitics. Trimming can compensate a value at its calibration condition, but cannot generally remove thermal gradients, material-dependent temperature behavior, hysteresis or later drift. Preserve trim accessibility, programming-current paths and exclusions when trim structures are present; component substitution or adding trim is a circuit-design choice, not a permissible placement repair. PDF pp. 295–323 , 418–422","url":"topic-resistors.html#source-resistors"},{"title":"Resistor geometry and precision ratios · Worked example: equal shapes can have unequal electrical weights","text":"Worked example: equal shapes can have unequal electrical weights Construct a 25 kΩ resistor from two 10 kΩ sections in series with a parallel pair of 10 kΩ sections. All four drawn strips can be identical. For either series section, ∂R/∂R i = 1. For either member of the equal parallel pair, ∂R/∂R i = 1/4. The sensitivity weights are therefore 1, 1, 1/4 and 1/4, not four equal weights. This is the kind of distinction behind the source's general centroid expression. PDF p. 390 Place them at x = −3, −1, +1, +3 arbitrary pitch units, with the two series sections on the left. Their ordinary geometric mean is zero, but their sensitivity-weighted mean is (−3 − 1 + 1/4 + 3/4)/2.5 = −1.2. The array will respond as though its effective resistance were displaced toward the series sections under a field producing equal local absolute resistance changes. Pair the two series sections symmetrically and the two parallel sections symmetrically to remove this first-order displacement. This calculation teaches two implementation requirements. First, the engine must know how the units compose electrically; a bag of rectangles cannot determine the weights. Second, the weights depend on the perturbation. If local changes are fractional and unequal nominal units are used, include each nominal resistance in the sensitivity. Document that distinction rather than choosing one universal centroid formula for all networks.","url":"topic-resistors.html#res-weighting"},{"title":"Resistor geometry and precision ratios · Worked example: segment-count sensitivity in a ratioed pair","text":"Worked example: segment-count sensitivity in a ratioed pair Consider a desired 10 kΩ : 15 kΩ pair. One construction uses two and three identical 5 kΩ units. Another uses three 3 kΩ units plus one 1 kΩ partial unit for the first resistor, and five 3 kΩ units for the second. Suppose, only to illustrate sensitivity, that each segment contributes an extra 10 Ω from its ends and contacts. The identical-unit option adds 20 Ω to 10 kΩ and 30 Ω to 15 kΩ. Those contributions scale with the intended ratio, so the ratio is unchanged in this simple model. The partial-unit option adds 40 Ω and 50 Ω. Its first-order fractional ratio change is 40/10000 − 50/15000 ≈ 0.000667, or 0.0667%. A nominally accurate drawn length did not protect the ratio against the same small end effect. In practice, head corrections need not be identical for short and long sections, and resistance has temperature and voltage dependence. Search candidate unit values and compositions; compute sensitivities to width, sheet resistance, heads, contacts and route contributions; then compare area, number of contacts, required dummies and CC quality. This is the practical use of the book's segmentation-sensitivity discussion. PDF pp. 394–399","url":"topic-resistors.html#res-segmentation"},{"title":"Resistor geometry and precision ratios · A generation and verification procedure","text":"A generation and verification procedure Choose only allowed materials and variants. Filter by voltage coefficient, power, parasitics and available characterization before optimizing footprint. Enumerate a modest set of widths and segment compositions. Preserve schematic value and topology; reject candidates outside model or PCell limits. Compute electrical sensitivities and moments. Include partial segments and series/parallel connections. Use these to form balanced local subarrays. Generate boundary conditions. Add qualified dummies, body contacts, field plates or exclusions and record their electrical ties. Match active environments, not merely labels. Route according to the network function. Preserve thermoelectric cancellation, ratio-scaled metal contributions and any Kelvin boundary. Avoid asymmetrical over-body coverage. Extract the complete network. Include bodies, shields, contact/via resistance and distributed capacitance. Verify value, ratio, voltage dependence, settling and power. Evaluate temperature and package scenarios. Compare uniform heating, a directed gradient and a nearby heater. Retain the best feasible tradeoffs rather than a single heuristic score. Discriminating validation. Keep the resistor bodies fixed while changing only interconnect polarity, one via stack, an overlying metal plate or the body bias. A good checker must show which change affects thermoelectric voltage, series resistance, passivation, capacitance or conductivity modulation. Conversely, adding decorative geometry outside all characterized interaction regions should not change an electrical centroid. These checks guard against confusing physical effects with arbitrary footprint measures. Uncertainty. When material coefficients or package fields are missing, report a relative geometric comparison with an explicit uncalibrated budget. Do not turn the textbook's qualitative “moderate” or “exceptional” grades into guaranteed ppm accuracy. The source itself warns that prediction is difficult without quantitative matching data. PDF p. 426","url":"topic-resistors.html#res-algorithm"},{"title":"RF, power and specialty-device boundaries · Frequency, power and process-specific devices need richer primitives","text":"Frequency, power and process-specific devices need richer primitives Some devices cannot be evaluated accurately as a compact rectangle plus a frequency-independent parasitic estimate. An inductor includes an electromagnetic field extending into nearby metal and silicon. A power transistor distributes current and heat along its fingers. A high-voltage transistor relies on carefully shaped depletion and electric fields. A JFET or embedded nonvolatile-memory element depends on process-specific structures and operating modes that an ordinary MOS generator may not support. The right abstraction is a qualified physical primitive with an explicit domain of validity: frequency, voltage, current, temperature, waveform, environment and permitted transformations. The engine can optimize placement and access around that primitive, or enumerate characterized variants. It should not silently substitute a generic device merely because its schematic terminal count matches.","url":"topic-rf.html#rf-contract"},{"title":"RF, power and specialty-device boundaries · Inductor optimization is a multi-parameter frequency problem","text":"Inductor optimization is a multi-parameter frequency problem A spiral obtains inductance from magnetic flux linkage between portions of its winding. Adding turns can increase inductance, but also lengthens the conductor, increases inter-turn and substrate capacitance and places more conductor in regions where magnetic coupling and loss differ. Wider traces reduce simple DC resistance while changing capacitance, proximity effects and usable winding area. Thin or nearby conductors can support eddy currents that dissipate energy. These mechanisms explain why neither maximum turn count nor minimum DC resistance defines an optimum RF inductor. Hastings recommends attention to upper low-resistance metal, short connections, the central opening, surrounding material and appropriate shielding. The process and operating band determine which option wins. A solid conductive shield can itself support loss-producing loops; a patterned shield is useful only if its geometry interrupts the relevant loops while its connection and capacitance remain acceptable. Parallel use of metal layers to reduce resistance is not the same topology as a multilayer series winding. PDF pp. 355–363 Worked screening example. For an illustrative 2 nH inductance at 2 GHz with 2 Ω series resistance, the low-frequency series-model approximation Q ≈ ωL/R gives about 12.6. Adding 1 Ω of lead resistance reduces it to about 8.4 even though the winding's nominal inductance is unchanged. This model deliberately omits substrate loss, capacitance and the frequency dependence of resistance; near self-resonance it is inadequate. Its purpose is to show that lead planning can materially change the circuit value of a supposedly fixed cell. A first-order isolated LC estimate places self-resonance near 1/(2π√(LC)), but a real distributed inductor needs an appropriate frequency-dependent port model. Define the extraction ports and reference/return conductors, include substrate and neighboring materials, and examine the required band rather than a single nominal L. Compare Q, loss, resonant behavior, differential balance and coupling into or from nearby circuits. The optimal geometry is the feasible candidate that best serves the actual RF circuit. Automatic fill is an integration operation on the electromagnetic device. Keep the cell's qualified exclusion region or regenerate its EM model with the intended fill. The same applies to a new supply strap, shield or overpassing signal. An identical nominal spiral with different surroundings is not automatically the same modeled component.","url":"topic-rf.html#rf-inductor"},{"title":"RF, power and specialty-device boundaries · Inductors, power devices, JFETs and high-voltage structures","text":"Inductors, power devices, JFETs and high-voltage structures Integrated inductors. More turns do not monotonically improve performance. Winding resistance, skin/proximity effects, substrate eddy currents and parasitic capacitance compete with inductance and mutual coupling. High frequency makes conductor shape, neighboring metal and return paths part of the device. The source favors high-level, low-resistance metal, short leads, a controlled empty center and limited unrelated material nearby. A solid shield can create eddy-current loss; a patterned shield must interrupt the relevant current loops. Stacking metal in parallel is different from adding serial winding layers, which can greatly increase interwinding capacitance. PDF pp. 355–363 Treat RF passives as characterized electromagnetic cells with frequency-dependent port models, transform restrictions and exclusion regions. Evaluate Q, self-resonant behavior, loss and coupling across the specified band. Preserve equal terminal environments for differential inductors. Automatic fill and routing must honor these regions or trigger fresh EM extraction. Minimized DC resistance and a correct nominal inductance are insufficient acceptance criteria. Power bipolar devices. Emitter debiasing, thermal runaway and turnoff current focusing distinguish continuous, switching and pulsed operation. Base contact placement and ballast must be designed for those conditions. A structure good for a linear pass element can have poor reverse-bias safe operating area during turnoff. Repeating a small-signal transistor until its total area is large enough does not establish power capability. PDF pp. 485–505 Power MOS devices. Include silicon, metal and package contributions to on-resistance. Limit distributed gate delay, keep source/body resistance under control, and check electrical as well as electrothermal safe operating area. A positive temperature coefficient in some operating regions does not imply universal immunity to current localization: the text discusses impact-ionization feedback and the Spirito effect. SenseFET units must sample comparable temperature, terminal voltage and edge environments; a small sense element at the edge can see first-finger effects absent from the average main transistor. PDF pp. 644–668 The rule of one-third: useful approximation, with a source check For a uniformly distributed, singly terminated metal finger, the source derives an effective added resistance approximately one third of its disconnected end-to-end resistance. That differs from a lead delivering all its current to a load at the far end: current is collected or delivered continuously along the finger. Unequal material coverage, tapering or strong debiasing requires a fuller distributed model. PDF pp. 650–664 , 870–876 Using the visually checked definitions on pages 871–872, let r = R sheet /W metal be resistance per length, g = 1/(L R Si ) be silicon conductance per length, and u = L√(rg). The exact one-finger model gives R total = R Si u coth(u), so R metal ≈ rL/3 for small u. This notation renames the source's finger length W to L to avoid confusion with metal width. Apparent source inconsistency. Equation E.14 as displayed omits the square root implied by E.1, E.2 and E.7 when expressing γW. In the same derivation, the displayed E.6 has a plus sign for the second exponential term although E.3 and E.5 require a minus, and E.8 writes the current prefactor γ/G although differentiation and units require G/γ. Equation E.9 gives the consistent total-resistance expression used above. The audit therefore checks the differential equations and boundary conditions, not an uncorrected transcription of every displayed intermediate equation. Its quoted approximation accuracy also needs care: accuracy of total resistance is not automatically the same as relative accuracy of the much smaller added metal resistance. Implement the exact dimensionless model or validate the approximation against it; do not copy an isolated penetration-distance threshold without reconciling the equations. This is our algebraic check of the supplied edition, not a publisher-confirmed erratum. High-voltage, annular and JFET structures. Drift regions, RESURF, field plates, oxide options, body ties and isolation define their operation. Annular transistors are not ordinary rectangles with an interchangeable source and drain. JFET pinch-off and parasitic junctions depend on a process-specific channel construction. Embedded NVM likewise needs programming/erase voltage, endurance and antenna/protection considerations beyond its static footprint. Restrict automatic transformations and mergers to the characterized variants; offer unsupported structures as explicit capability gaps. PDF pp. 616–639 , 669–685","url":"topic-rf.html#source-rf-power"},{"title":"RF, power and specialty-device boundaries · Derive the one-third approximation and know when to stop using it","text":"Derive the one-third approximation and know when to stop using it The one-third rule has a simple energy interpretation that is useful for implementation review. Consider a metal finger of length L with resistance per length r, contacted at x = 0. If current leaves uniformly through distributed identical silicon elements, metal current is approximately I(x) = I 0 (1 − x/L). The dissipated metal power is the integral of rI(x)² along the finger. Integrating the square gives P = I 0 ²rL/3, so the effective additional resistance seen at the terminal is R wire /3. A wire feeding a lumped far-end load instead carries I 0 everywhere and dissipates I 0 ²R wire . The approximation assumes the silicon current is approximately uniform. If metal voltage drop appreciably changes local transistor overdrive or conduction, that assumption fails: more current may be concentrated near the terminal. The exact uniform-conductance distributed model quoted above captures this electrical redistribution but still does not include temperature-dependent conductivity, nonlinear transistor operation or a resistive return network. Numerical comparison. Let the aggregate ideal silicon resistance be 1 Ω and the metal finger's end-to-end resistance be 0.3 Ω. Then u = √0.3 ≈ 0.548 and the exact model gives 1.0981 Ω total resistance. The one-third approximation gives 1.1000 Ω, a total-resistance error of about 0.18%. If the metal resistance instead rises to 3 Ω, the exact model gives 1.8440 Ω while the approximation gives 2 Ω, an error of about 8.5%. These are model calculations, not measured process values. The example also shows why percentages must identify their denominator. A small error in the total resistance can be a larger fractional error in the added metal contribution. Implementations should compare the approximation against the exact model in the domain where they intend to use it. Changing to both-end contacts or multiple taps changes the current profile and effective resistance; it should trigger a new network evaluation rather than reuse the single-ended factor automatically.","url":"topic-rf.html#rf-one-third"},{"title":"RF, power and specialty-device boundaries · Power, high-voltage, JFET and NVM layout decisions","text":"Power, high-voltage, JFET and NVM layout decisions Power MOS. Optimize silicon, metal, via and package contributions together. Wider aggregate gate width may lower ideal channel resistance yet demand more gate drive and create a distributed gate-delay problem. Feed arrangements can improve switching uniformity while consuming contact area and adding capacitance. Source/body access affects both conduction and parasitic bipolar behavior. Evaluate the relevant pulse and electrothermal safe-operating-area conditions; a favorable temperature coefficient at one bias is not a general proof against current localization. Power bipolar. Collector and base access, emitter debiasing, ballasting and turnoff conditions govern current spreading. A shape suitable for steady linear operation can be poor during reverse-bias turnoff. Retain the qualified structure and its forward/reverse SOA evidence. Geometrically repeating a small-signal cell does not establish a safe power primitive. PDF pp. 485–505 Sense elements. A small SenseFET at the periphery may sample different temperature, source/drain drop or edge behavior from the main array. Choose its units and connections to represent the intended main-device quantity, then extract the actual terminal differences. Its drawn width ratio alone cannot establish current-sense accuracy over load and temperature. High voltage. Drift-region charge, depletion, field plates, well/body arrangement and oxide options determine field crowding and breakdown. Keep their exact qualified variants and bias restrictions. A field plate may resemble metal routing but participates in device operation; moving or filling it can change the model. Source and drain are often physically asymmetric. Model-aware transforms are mandatory. PDF pp. 669–685 Annular MOS and JFETs. Annular devices have curved channels, unequal inner/outer perimeters and process-specific stringer or isolation considerations. An average perimeter is not automatically the model's effective width. JFET channels and gates depend on specific implants, diffusions or epitaxial regions; their pinch-off, leakage and capacitance cannot be recovered from a generic planar-MOS footprint. Use recognized PCells and their compact/extraction models. PDF pp. 616–622 , 633–639 Nonvolatile memory and trim. Programming and erase voltages, charge storage, endurance and retention introduce scenarios absent from nominal analog operation. Respect their wells, antenna protection, isolation and access requirements. A trim line's rare operation does not excuse coupling into a high-impedance node during programming or subsequent use. Device variants and trimming architectures must be explicit design inputs. PDF pp. 623–632","url":"topic-rf.html#rf-specialty"},{"title":"RF, power and specialty-device boundaries · Generate candidates inside a verified capability envelope","text":"Generate candidates inside a verified capability envelope Declare the performance domain. Record frequency band, port impedance/reference, voltage/current waveforms, temperature, required lifetime and package context. This prevents a scalar nominal value from masquerading as a complete requirement. Select qualified geometry and transformations. Enumerate supported winding variants, contact schemes, power cells or high-voltage PCells. Reject unsupported shape changes explicitly. Reserve the full environment. Include EM exclusions, fill policy, guard/well regions, gate-drive access, power-return area and thermal space. These are part of the physical candidate, not decorations added afterward. Use staged evaluation. Apply fast conservative bounds first, then extracted RC, electromagnetic or electrothermal models as appropriate. Keep a clear label on each approximation and its calibrated range. Evaluate the circuit metric. Check gain/noise/oscillation behavior or power loss/sensing/SOA using the actual port and device models. A favorable standalone inductor Q or transistor on-resistance need not give the best circuit. Freeze and recheck the final geometry. Changes to leads, fill, shielding, contacts or neighboring power blocks can invalidate a previously characterized environment. Retain geometry hashes, model versions, ports and scenario results. Required characterization includes metal thickness and frequency-dependent conductivity, dielectric/substrate stack, accepted EM ports and models, coupling/exclusion rules, device SOA and pulse limits, thermal/package parameters, qualified high-voltage structures, special-device recognition and programming conditions. When that information is absent, the correct engine behavior is a capability boundary with a stated external qualification requirement, not an invented penalty weight.","url":"topic-rf.html#rf-proof"},{"title":"Robust design, variation and yield · Optimize a manufactured population under a defined operating contract","text":"Optimize a manufactured population under a defined operating contract A robust layout protects electrical behavior when fabrication and operating conditions depart from nominal. This requires more than a process-corner pass, more than matching geometry, and more than a large nominal margin. Define the random variables, required operating domain and complete acceptance event before choosing a robustness score. Otherwise the optimizer can improve a number that does not answer the user's engineering question. Graeb distinguishes design variables xd, statistical fabrication variables xs, and range variables xr such as supply and temperature. The yield contract is: Y(xd) = Prxs[∀ xr ∈ Tr: every specified lower and upper performance bound passes]. A statistical draw describes a manufactured instance; that same instance must work throughout the specified operating domain. Randomly choosing one temperature per manufactured sample answers a different question. Similarly, checking a few named process corners does not define a probability distribution or quantify local mismatch. These analyses can complement each other, but their results need separate labels. Design Centering, PDF pp. 46–50 , pp. 89–95 . Design centering means adjusting controllable design choices to improve this probability. It is not geometric centering of devices. Common-centroid placement is one technique that may improve selected mismatch mechanisms; its value is established by how it changes the circuit's acceptance region and uncertainty model.","url":"topic-robust-design.html#robust-contract"},{"title":"Robust design, variation and yield · Global process, local mismatch and spatial fields are different models","text":"Global process, local mismatch and spatial fields are different models For a simple matched pair, write q1 = μ1 + g + l1 and q2 = μ2 + g + l2 . Here g is a shared die-level variation and l1, l2 are local deviations. Subtracting gives q1 − q2 = μ1 − μ2 + l1 − l2 ; the shared term cancels exactly in this parameter difference. If σg = 20 mV and independent local σl = 3 mV, each device has standard deviation √409 ≈ 20.22 mV, but the difference has standard deviation √18 ≈ 4.24 mV. Treating the device totals as independent would predict about 28.6 mV and lose the cancellation. This cancellation is conditional. An actual current difference may have unequal sensitivities to the shared parameter because the devices differ in geometry, bias or temperature. Propagate the full circuit response rather than assuming every globally varying quantity cancels in every matched output. The book introduces shared and local parameter spaces as a modeling foundation; PDK definitions determine the real decomposition. PDF pp. 63–64 . Shared process variation and local mismatch A common global variation enters two device rectangles while each receives its own local variation. Subtracting matched responses can cancel the shared component while local mismatch remains. Shared process term g Device 1: μ1 + g + l1 Device 2: μ2 + g + l2 local realization l1 local realization l2 Difference = (μ1 − μ2) + (l1 − l2) Download this SVG Original block schematic for a simplified additive model. Shared circuit-output sensitivity must be checked; equal parameter perturbations need not produce equal output perturbations. A spatial field adds another structure: a slowly varying process or temperature field is sampled at each unit's location. Derived moment condition: for a difference of weighted responses, cancellation of a common affine spatial field requires equal zeroth and first moments of the comparison weights. For ratio matching, normalize each group's weights by its intended total before comparing centroids; raw unit counts need not be equal. This conclusion assumes the same local field and linear response coefficient for the compared unit contributions. It does not cancel arbitrary second-order fields, unequal edge environments or a routing-induced bias difference. Random area-dependent mismatch also remains. The physical action follows from the mechanism: use larger matched units to reduce a calibrated random term, spatial arrangement for appropriate gradients, and equivalent surroundings or routing for other systematic terms. Do not use one spacing penalty as a universal replacement for all of them. Lampaert, PDF pp. 53–55 .","url":"topic-robust-design.html#global-local"},{"title":"Robust design, variation and yield · Build a valid covariance model before propagating it","text":"Build a valid covariance model before propagating it Let Cij = Cov(xi, xj), with diagonal entries σi². A valid covariance matrix is symmetric positive semidefinite. Pairwise correlation magnitudes below one do not suffice: for three unit-variance variables with all pairwise correlations −0.9, the vector (1,1,1) has negative quadratic variance, so the matrix is impossible. Test the matrix as a whole. For Gaussian variables, a factorization C = AAᵀ supports samples x = μ + Az with independent standard normal z. Exact common variables can make C singular; use a reduced-rank factorization. When parameters have positive support or non-Gaussian distributions, a latent normal transformation may be useful, but its validity and transformed sensitivities must be established. Correlation does not specify the whole joint distribution outside the Gaussian case. Design Centering, PDF pp. 51–63 . For locally linear outputs f ≈ f0 + S(x − μ) , propagation gives Cov(f) ≈ S C Sᵀ . The off-diagonal output terms matter because yield requires simultaneous success. Computing one standard deviation per performance metric discards the dependence needed for a joint pass probability. Attach covariance to stable device and latent-variable identities, so flattening or merging fingers does not redraw shared global variation independently. Action: require every statistical model to declare population scope, units, supported device types, bias and temperature domain, geometry dependence and correlation structure. Cache it against layout and PDK revisions. This protects the optimizer from improving a layout under a stale covariance model after area, spacing or unit count changes.","url":"topic-robust-design.html#covariance-validation"},{"title":"Robust design, variation and yield · Worst-case distance measures margin relative to sensitivity","text":"Worst-case distance measures margin relative to sensitivity For one upper-bounded linear Gaussian metric f = f0 + gᵀδ , its standard deviation is σf = √(gᵀCg) . Define signed margin distance β = (U − f0)/σf . A positive β means nominally passing; a negative β means nominally failing. The exact one-bound pass probability in this linear Gaussian model is Φ(β). For a lower bound, use β = (f0 − L)/σf . Zero σf requires separate deterministic or higher-order treatment rather than division by zero. A nominal margin of 10 mV with σ = 5 mV gives β = 2. A smaller 8 mV margin with σ = 2 mV gives β = 4. The second design is much more robust to that modeled variation despite its smaller nominal headroom. This is why a layout change that slightly worsens nominal gain can still be valuable if it reduces sensitivity or mismatch enough. Design Centering, PDF pp. 111–112 , pp. 154–158 . For nonlinear metrics, find the nearest failure boundary in whitened variables z: minimize ||z|| subject to reaching the specified bound, with operating conditions chosen according to the worst-case contract. Linearization at that point gives a local probability approximation. Multiple failure surfaces, two-sided mismatch failures and interior operating extrema require multiple starts or other search mechanisms. A nominal sensitivity of zero can hide a quadratic failure mechanism; it does not prove immunity. Minimizing over supply and temperature is nested inside the acceptance event. If a response is proven monotone over the specified range, its worst case is at a boundary; otherwise a corner-only scan can miss an interior failure. Store evidence for monotonicity or report the actual sampled domain. The book explicitly distinguishes box-linear, ellipsoid-linear and general nonlinear worst-case analyses. PDF pp. 103–124 , pp. 148–153 .","url":"topic-robust-design.html#worstcase-distance"},{"title":"Robust design, variation and yield · Why “three sigma” needs a complete sentence","text":"Why “three sigma” needs a complete sentence A tolerance ball, box and half-space have different probabilities In two standardized dimensions a radius-three circle lies inside a plus-or-minus-three box. The one-sided bound on the first coordinate is a different region extending beyond both. z1 = 3 0 z2 Ball: z1² + z2² ≤ 9 Probability ≈ 98.889% Box: |z1| ≤ 3 and |z2| ≤ 3 Probability ≈ 99.461% Half-space: z1 ≤ 3 Probability ≈ 99.865% Here z1 and z2 are independent N(0, 1). Download this SVG Original quantitative diagram in standardized coordinates. The illustrated probabilities refer to the named regions, not to a real circuit's yield. For n independent standardized Gaussian variables, a radius-β ball has probability Pr[χ²n ≤ β²] . A symmetric ±β box has probability [2Φ(β) − 1]ⁿ . A single normalized linear upper bound has probability Φ(β), independently of the number of underlying Gaussian variables because the projection is one dimensional. These are different regions, so their different probabilities are expected. The source's tolerance-class chapter derives the distinction directly. Design Centering, PDF pp. 133–145 . Joint circuit yield is the probability of the intersection of all acceptance regions. Its value is at most the smallest individual pass probability. A union bound gives a conservative lower bound Y ≥ 1 − Σj Pr(failure j) , clipped to zero; independence is not required. Multiplying pass probabilities needs independence and should not be the default for several responses of the same circuit. Maximizing the minimum β is a useful bottleneck objective but does not exactly maximize joint yield. For two independent one-sided linear metrics, β = (3,3) gives joint yield about 0.997302. β = (2.9,8) gives about 0.998134, even though its minimum distance is smaller. The best objective depends on whether the contract prioritizes the weakest margin, actual joint probability, or another explicit preference. The source makes the distinction between maximal inscribed tolerance regions and exact design centering. PDF pp. 179–180 .","url":"topic-robust-design.html#region-probability"},{"title":"Robust design, variation and yield · Validate yield with an uncertainty statement","text":"Validate yield with an uncertainty statement Ordinary Monte Carlo draws independent manufactured instances and evaluates the complete acceptance event. For K passes in N draws, Ŷ = K/N and Var(Ŷ) = Y(1−Y)/N . Increasing accuracy by a factor of ten requires roughly a hundred times as many samples at fixed yield. The dimensionality does not appear directly in this Bernoulli variance formula, but circuit simulation and operating-domain verification still become more expensive with problem complexity. PDF pp. 125–133 . Zero observed failures is not a perfect-yield certificate. Under independent draws, Pr(no failures | p) = (1−p)ᴺ . Equating this to 0.05 yields a one-sided 95% upper bound pupper = 1 − 0.05^(1/N) . At N = 3000, it is about 0.1%. Demonstrating a one-part-per-million upper bound this way requires roughly three million clean samples. This is a derivation of estimator limits, not a demand to run that many samples for every exploratory layout. Use a hierarchy of evidence. Cheap linear models screen moves. Geometric failure searches find likely failure regions. Importance sampling draws more frequently near failures and reweights by true density divided by proposal density. Independent finalist validation checks the model and estimator assumptions. An importance proposal must cover all important failure modes; a narrow proposal around one mismatch direction can miss the opposite direction. Weighted samples require weighted variance analysis, not the ordinary Bernoulli formula. PDF pp. 127–132 . Record simulator nonconvergence and invalid operating points explicitly. They are unresolved outcomes until a defined analysis determines their status. Keep optimization samples separate from held-out validation; repeatedly choosing the best candidate using the same small sample can overfit the sample noise. Common random draws are useful for paired comparisons, but they do not make multiple reused comparisons independent experiments.","url":"topic-robust-design.html#sampling-confidence"},{"title":"Robust design, variation and yield · Make physical design choices that change the failure mechanism","text":"Make physical design choices that change the failure mechanism When offset yield is poor, determine whether the dominant cause is random local variation, a systematic spatial field, unequal electrical environment, or bias sensitivity. Larger unit area can reduce calibrated random mismatch while increasing parasitic capacitance. Closer placement can help spatial matching while creating congestion or thermal interaction. A common-centroid arrangement may improve a gradient term while lengthening sensitive cross-connections. Every action therefore needs both an intended mechanism and a post-layout check of its competing effects. The source's geometric optimization example improves the weakest margins while allowing strong nominal metrics to degrade. Its trust-region method controls the size of changes because local response models eventually become inaccurate. For an engine, optimize a vector of signed failure distances or estimated joint yield together with area/power objectives, then validate actual candidates rather than assuming a local mathematical step is a legal layout change. PDF pp. 173–179 . At high yield, the raw probability objective becomes nearly flat. Distance objectives or temporarily inflated tolerances can expose remaining improvement. Inflating covariance is a search technique, not a claim that the foundry process has changed. Final evaluation must return to the original process model. Similarly, choosing larger devices or a different allowed device flavor may change statistical behavior; directly tuning unavailable foundry parameters is not an actionable layout decision.","url":"topic-robust-design.html#robust-optimization"},{"title":"Robust design, variation and yield · Philis integration and independent checks","text":"Philis integration and independent checks The annotator's placement (line 53) emits matching, thermal, centroid, symmetry and isolation-related requirements from recognized structures. This is a starting point for identifying likely mechanisms, not yet a statistical performance model. The constants near lines 37–43 include a thermal difference, proximity scale and gradient share; preserve their status as tuning defaults until calibrated to a PDK, scenario and electrical specification. Proposed extension: give each statistical evaluation an immutable context containing global latent variables, local/spatial covariance, operating domain, model revisions and acceptance specifications. Geometry changes request a new uncertainty model when required. The optimizer can then compare robust candidates without losing which uncertainties were actually analyzed. Meaningful checks include the exact shared-versus-local pair example, a correlated linear output with known variance, a nonlinear response with zero nominal derivative but nonzero quadratic variation, and a response with a worst temperature inside the allowed interval. Check joint yield against an independent low-dimensional integral before applying the implementation to circuit simulations. Compare statistical predictions with extracted circuit behavior on unseen variants and layouts. Keep defect yield separate from parametric yield. Critical-area models concern shorts or opens from physical defects; process/mismatch models concern specification variation in otherwise connected circuits. Multiplying their yields requires an independence or conditional model. A final report should identify each mechanism, evidence and unresolved uncertainty rather than compressing every form of manufacturability into one unexplained percentage.","url":"topic-robust-design.html#robust-engine"},{"title":"Search algorithms and constraint-aware moves · Search must protect validity while spending computation on useful alternatives","text":"Search must protect validity while spending computation on useful alternatives Analog placement combines discrete choices, continuous dimensions, equality constraints, forbidden geometric regions and expensive electrical evaluations. No single optimizer removes that structure. A useful architecture assigns each kind of decision to a method that can handle it: constructive moves for exact relationships, graph or linear compaction for fixed topology, simulated annealing or enumeration for discrete alternatives, and circuit analysis for deciding whether a geometric improvement matters electrically. The sources repeatedly separate feasibility from optimization. Lampaert uses constrained groups and performance penalties within annealing; the topological volume develops encodings that avoid spending most of the run rejecting invalid arrangements; Graeb's sizing book derives constrained numerical methods and carefully distinguishes local stationary solutions from global optimization. The engine should preserve these distinctions in its reports. Lampaert, PDF pp. 99–107 ; Topological Approaches, PDF p. 25 ; Design Centering, PDF pp. 188–195 . Proposed decision order: first determine whether the candidate is physically and electrically meaningful, then whether required budgets pass, then compare the chosen quality objectives. A cheap proxy may rank candidates for deeper evaluation, but a low proxy value must never erase an open connection, a violated equality or an unsupported model.","url":"topic-search-algorithms.html#search-contract"},{"title":"Search algorithms and constraint-aware moves · Preserve exact constraints with the move itself","text":"Preserve exact constraints with the move itself Consider a matched pair whose center coordinates satisfy xA + xB = 2a and yA = yB . Moving A independently breaks symmetry; subsequently projecting B can collide with a fixed block. Moving the axis by δ translates both partners by δ and preserves the equality. Changing pair separation by moving A left and B right also preserves it. Swapping both members of two compatible pairs can preserve a complete group. These moves retain useful degrees of freedom without repeatedly leaving the constraint surface. A compound pair move preserves symmetry Initial mirrored rectangles, an invalid one-sided move, and a valid pair expansion are compared about a dashed symmetry axis. Initial state Move only A Move the pair A B A B A B xA + xB = 2a Equality broken Equality preserved External clearance, pin access and other groups still require validation. Download this SVG Original rectangular teaching example. A compound move preserves one invariant; it does not automatically preserve every neighboring constraint. Group preservation is preferable when a requirement is exact and has a compact parameterization. Projection remains useful for general constraints, but the complete affected component must be rechecked after projection. Sequentially projecting incompatible equalities can cycle; projection against one symmetry axis can invalidate another. Use a simultaneous solve for coupled linear equalities, or explicitly declare which group decomposition is assumed. Fixed devices must participate in that solve rather than being restored afterward without rechecking the equality. Philis already centralizes detailed-placement trials in Sa::trial (line 140) and exposes project_hard (line 348). This is the correct integration boundary for compound moves and candidate auditing. A trial transaction should include coordinates, orientation, selected geometry variant, pin offsets, shared axes, branch state and every derived cache affected by the mutation. The reason is reproducibility: a rejected reshape must not leave a changed terminal position influencing later scores.","url":"topic-search-algorithms.html#feasible-moves"},{"title":"Search algorithms and constraint-aware moves · Lexicographic feasibility is clearer than an enormous penalty weight","text":"Lexicographic feasibility is clearer than an enormous penalty weight A finite penalty alone does not establish hard feasibility: improvement in another objective can outweigh a violation unless a sufficient exact-penalty bound has been proved for the modeled problem. Merely increasing an empirical weight supplies no such proof. Even an exact-penalty result about the final optimum does not make every intermediate search state feasible. A lexicographic key first compares hard violation status, then a defined violation magnitude, then required-budget violations, and finally the feasible objective. During an infeasible initialization, this gives a controlled repair policy. Once feasible, accepted moves remain feasible under the checks that define the key. Philis's detailed placer explicitly compares a gate key containing violated hard batches, hard residual plus encroachment, and budget residual before the Metropolis decision. accept (line 379) therefore expresses stronger intent than a flat weighted sum. However, batch count depends on batching: combining ten requirements into one batch can change a lexicographic count. Use stable semantics for violation counts and ensure aggregate residuals do not hide one worsened mandatory constraint behind another improved one. Action: distinguish an exploratory infeasibility score from a release predicate. The former may aggregate residuals to find a route back toward legality; the latter checks each mandatory constraint against its own tolerance. This permits useful repair without allowing an aggregate zero or a favorable average to masquerade as complete compliance. Record numerical tolerances in physical units or normalized units with a documented scale. Some budget tradeoffs are real. Two wire capacitances can share one bandwidth allowance, so requiring both individual caps to decrease may unnecessarily reject a valid move. Other constraints are independent obligations, such as two separate output voltage limits. The constraint system must say which residuals are pooled before the optimizer chooses a gate. See performance budget derivation for the electrical distinction.","url":"topic-search-algorithms.html#lexicographic"},{"title":"Search algorithms and constraint-aware moves · What simulated annealing provides, and what it does not","text":"What simulated annealing provides, and what it does not For a fixed scalar energy E and a symmetric proposal distribution, ordinary Metropolis acceptance is one for ΔE ≤ 0 and exp(−ΔE/T) otherwise. Temperature has the same units as E. If the objective changes from nanometer wirelength to a normalized electrical loss, the old temperature schedule is not automatically meaningful. Lampaert discusses estimating initial temperature from typical uphill moves and an intended initial acceptance rate. A useful approximation is T0 = −mean(positive ΔE)/ln(p0) ; it is a calibration heuristic, not an exact guarantee of the aggregate rate. Lampaert, PDF pp. 117–118 . For deliberately asymmetric proposals, Metropolis–Hastings would include the reverse/forward proposal ratio to target the usual equilibrium distribution. Repair maps can make proposals many-to-one and asymmetric. A practical annealer may still use them as an effective optimization heuristic, but then textbook stationary-distribution or convergence arguments need additional work. Likewise, a finite cooling schedule and finite move budget do not establish global optimality. The asymptotic theorem is not a product guarantee. Keep the energy definition fixed during an inner annealing epoch. Changing penalties, thermal fields or sensitivity coefficients while comparing a cached previous score with a newly evaluated score means ΔE no longer compares the same function. Philis's Sa::prices field (line 85) explicitly holds prices read-only during the anneal. Extend this discipline to all model revisions: close an epoch, update models, rescore the incumbent and archive, then resume search. while computation remains: freeze a model snapshot and objective scales for each trial in this epoch: propose an atomic move or coupled branch transition generate geometry and preserve exact group relations evaluate mandatory constraints and complete gate key if gate improves: accept and invalidate affected caches if gate ties: apply the documented annealing rule otherwise: restore the full prior candidate route/extract selected candidates and audit model errors refresh models when needed; rescore before the next epoch Maintain an archive of feasible nondominated candidates as well as the current annealing state. An uphill excursion is useful for exploration but should not destroy the best known deliverable. Compare several seeds using identical time budgets; report distributions of routed electrical outcomes rather than one lucky best placement.","url":"topic-search-algorithms.html#annealing"},{"title":"Search algorithms and constraint-aware moves · Disconnected legal regions require discrete transitions","text":"Disconnected legal regions require discrete transitions Some spacing rules are disjunctions, not lower bounds. In the topological volume's deep-trench example, a pair may share a region within a maximum stretch or be isolated beyond a minimum separation; intermediate gaps are forbidden. If legal gap g satisfies 0 ≤ g ≤ smax or g ≥ d , with d > smax, continuous small steps that never worsen hard legality cannot travel between the two components. No clever temperature rescues a move rejected before the energy test. Topological Approaches, PDF pp. 113–115 , p. 136 . Sharing and isolation are disconnected legal choices A gap scale shows a legal shared region from zero to maximum stretch, an illegal intermediate band, and a separate isolation region. A compound branch change jumps between legal endpoints. Two legal components of a spacing rule share forbidden isolate 0 smax d gap g Change branch and realize its geometry in one trial. Then check neighbors, symmetry and total trench stretch. Illustrative rule shape; smax and d require a process-specific definition. Download this SVG Original diagram of a disjunctive rule. The successful move crosses the forbidden interval in representation space without accepting an illegal intermediate layout. Action: propose a branch change together with a legal endpoint geometry. Philis already carries Layout::branch and DtiBand (line 18). Keep branch identity stable across constraint reordering, and verify that the move realizes its declared branch. If one device has left and right sharing stretches, constrain their sum when the physical rule limits total stretch; checking each side independently can permit an impossible total. A bounded mixed-integer formulation makes the logic explicit. With z ∈ {0,1}, 0 ≤ g ≤ gmax, require g ≥ dz and g ≤ smax + (gmax−smax)z . z = 0 selects sharing; z = 1 selects isolation. The formulation requires gmax ≥ d. Tight bounds improve numerical conditioning. An arbitrarily enormous big-M value can weaken relaxation and hide numerical violations.","url":"topic-search-algorithms.html#disjunctions"},{"title":"Search algorithms and constraint-aware moves · Use exact optimization where the model is small enough to justify it","text":"Use exact optimization where the model is small enough to justify it For a fixed ordering and fixed dimensions, minimize width or height subject to linear spacing, symmetry and alignment equations. A feasible LP with an optimality certificate solves that modeled coordinate subproblem. Rectangular area W×H is bilinear, so ordinary linear programming does not directly minimize area unless one dimension is fixed, an alternative linear objective is chosen, or the problem is otherwise reformulated. Enumerating several aspect-ratio bounds is often clearer than pretending a weighted W+H objective is identical to area. For tiny groups, enumerate orientations, variants or ordering choices and solve the coordinate subproblem for each. Keep the nondominated alternatives. This provides a useful oracle for evaluating a heuristic placer, and can generate reliable templates for recurring motifs. The hierarchical synthesis chapter uses bottom-up variant enumeration and compaction for small clusters, while recognizing that large combinations become expensive. Topological Approaches, PDF pp. 130–147 . Mixed-integer models can select separating directions for rectangles with four binary choices and bounded inequalities. They can also choose contact counts, legal variants and disjunctive branches. Their result is globally optimal only relative to the finite model, domain and solver termination certificate. A time-limited incumbent is a feasible solution with a bound or gap if the solver provides one; an interrupted search with no incumbent does not prove infeasibility. Continuous performance optimization needs similar discipline. Local linear or quadratic models support useful steps, but a trust region limits how far a step can extrapolate. Evaluate the true model after the step and compare predicted with actual improvement. A good coordinate LP cannot certify a circuit metric computed by an invalid sensitivity model. Design Centering, PDF pp. 174–179 .","url":"topic-search-algorithms.html#exact-subproblems"},{"title":"Search algorithms and constraint-aware moves · Experiments that tell whether the search is improving the engine","text":"Experiments that tell whether the search is improving the engine First use tiny cases with known answers: a two-pair symmetry problem, a three-block packing, and a shared-versus-isolated gap with legal solutions in both components. Enumerate their discrete choices independently. Verify that compound moves can reach both legal branches, that rejected moves restore pin geometry as well as coordinates, and that every recorded incumbent passes direct geometry checks. These tests target the failure mechanisms that matter. Then compare search variants on identical generated cells, objective scales, PDK rules and evaluation budgets. Report attempted moves, legal proposals, accepted moves, model refreshes, routed completion rate and final electrical margins. A lower placement cost accompanied by more routing failure is a regression. A faster run that silently restricts templates needs its search-domain change reported alongside the speedup. Finally retain explainable termination states: verified feasible result, feasible result with unresolved electrical model accuracy, budget exhaustion, conflicting constraints, and proven infeasibility of a named subproblem. These states give the next stage enough information to widen the search, relax an explicitly optional preference, or return a justified failure. They prevent a heuristic stopping rule from becoming an unjustified claim of optimality.","url":"topic-search-algorithms.html#search-validation"},{"title":"Signal routing, symmetry and shielding · Define the routing contract per net and per relationship","text":"Define the routing contract per net and per relationship A useful net description includes terminal roles, expected signal range and spectrum, DC and transient current, source/load impedance, allowed R/C, permitted layers, shielding needs, and related nets. Relationships include differential partners, a Kelvin sense and its force path, an aggressor/victim pair, and the separate plates of a capacitor array. Net classification is a starting point; the circuit condition sets the actual budget. Why. A high-impedance bias node and a low-impedance output can have similar geometry but very different sensitivity to the same injected charge. A clock's edge rate can matter more than its repetition frequency. A long quiet route may be safer than a short route alongside a fast switching net. The performance survey describes symmetry, exact per-layer route matching, critical-net shielding, and current-flow constraints as useful techniques, while warning that geometric constraints alone do not guarantee circuit performance. Routing survey, PDF p. 2","url":"topic-signal-routing.html#contract"},{"title":"Signal routing, symmetry and shielding · Route scarce electrical resources deliberately","text":"Route scarce electrical resources deliberately Reserve critical pin access and protected device regions. If every legal escape is consumed, a nominally good floorplan becomes unroutable. Reserve supplies, quiet returns, and guard/shield connections. Signal protection depends on these networks being physically realizable. Route constrained pairs or bundles with coupled decisions. Their layer/width/topology choices are related, so independent shortest paths can make later matching expensive. Route remaining nets with congestion negotiation. Keep analog costs tied to measured budget use rather than one unexplained global penalty. Extract and repair the limiting electrical violations. A successful DRC route can still exceed a sensitive-node budget. This is a policy template, not an unconditional ordering. A large supply mesh or fixed external pin may require a different schedule. The reason for each reservation should be the scarcity or sensitivity it protects.","url":"topic-signal-routing.html#priorities"},{"title":"Signal routing, symmetry and shielding · Differential matching: corresponding paths and surroundings","text":"Differential matching: corresponding paths and surroundings A common-mode disturbance becomes differential error when the two sides respond differently. Geometrically mirroring the routes helps make their electrical models alike, but only if layers, widths, vias, loading, and nearby conductors also correspond. A matched pair whose routes see different clocks is not electrically symmetric. Length equality is only one route-matching feature Two equal-length routes use the same layers and via count but have different per-layer lengths, leading to potentially different resistance and capacitance. Length equality is only one route-matching feature Same total length, different stack usage pin pin pin pin Route A: 100 drawn units on layer 2; two transitions Route B: 280 drawn units on layer 2; two transitions layer 1 layer 2 via Download this SVG Original illustrative diagram; not PDK-valid or to scale. Equal total length, layer set and via count can hide different R/C because the per-layer length allocation differs. Evaluate actual terminal RC and neighboring geometry. Constraints. Preserve terminal correspondence; choose compatible layer/width sequences; match contact/via strategies; constrain extracted resistance and capacitance imbalance; and compare coupling from each significant aggressor to both sides. Derive tolerances from offset, CMRR, settling, or timing requirements. Technique. Route a template and its reflected partner when obstacles permit. Otherwise search paired states or maintain a shared electrical budget while allowing asymmetrical geometry. Re-extract both sides after a repair. Matching only the set of layers misses a case where one side spends most of its length on a resistive layer and the other on a low-resistance layer. Counterexample. Two 100 µm routes can use the same two metals yet have different allocations, via stacks, widths, and neighbors. Their total lengths and layer sets match while their terminal RC does not. Philis's current Differential predicate cannot distinguish all such cases.","url":"topic-signal-routing.html#differential"},{"title":"Signal routing, symmetry and shielding · Translate crosstalk into an electrical budget","text":"Translate crosstalk into an electrical budget The following estimates are derived here from the linear capacitor law and charge conservation. For a simple coupling capacitor Cc, injected current is i=Cc·d(Vaggressor−Vvictim)/dt . If a fast transition couples into a nearly floating victim with capacitance Cv to quiet reference nodes, excluding the coupling capacitance Cc, the instantaneous charge-sharing estimate is ΔVvictim≈Cc/(Cv+Cc)·ΔVaggressor . For the instantaneous step, conservation of victim charge gives Cv·ΔVvictim+Cc(ΔVvictim−ΔVaggressor)=0 , which yields the stated ratio. This is an illustrative limit, not a universal noise formula: driver impedance, nonlinear clamps, finite edge rate, other couplings, and circuit dynamics alter the response. For frequency-domain small-signal analysis, the victim response depends on both the coupling and the victim impedance. A constraint on minimum spacing is therefore an indirect way to reduce Cc; a noise constraint needs aggressor waveforms or spectra and a transfer model. Multiple aggressors can add coherently or partly cancel depending on phase and correlation. Do not sum their powers as though they were independent unless that assumption is justified. Example. With illustrative Cc=1 fF, Cv=19 fF, and a 1 V fast edge, the simple charge-sharing estimate is 50 mV. The same geometry connected to a low-impedance node can produce a much smaller voltage excursion. The victim's electrical role changes the allowable coupling by orders of magnitude. Router actions. Increase separation on important parallel runs, shorten overlap, change layers when the stack model supports improvement, move the aggressor, or add a correctly referenced shield. Choose based on estimated noise reduction per routing resource and confirm by extraction/simulation.","url":"topic-signal-routing.html#coupling"},{"title":"Signal routing, symmetry and shielding · A shield is a connected conductor with costs","text":"A shield is a connected conductor with costs A grounded or otherwise quiet shield can intercept electric-field coupling. Its own impedance determines how quiet it remains. A floating shield can transfer charge; a shield tied through a long inductive or resistive path can carry noise; a shield connected to a noisy rail can become an aggressor. The label “shield” provides no electrical guarantee. A shield needs a quiet electrical return An aggressor and victim rectangle are separated by a shield. Coupling to the shield and its return impedance remain part of the network. A shield needs a quiet electrical return Aggressor: switching voltage Shield conductor Victim: sensitive node Coupling to shield Added victim loading Quiet reference Return Z(f) Evaluate the shield voltage and return path as well as physical coverage. Download this SVG Original illustrative diagram; not PDK-valid or to scale. Grounding is an electrical boundary condition. A floating or noisy shield cannot be awarded the benefit of an ideal reference conductor. Constraints. Name the reference net, preserve continuity, bound connection impedance over the relevant spectrum, respect legal spacing, and account for added victim-to-shield capacitance. A differential pair may require balanced shield geometry. The substrate chapter explains analogous dependence on guard-ring and package return impedance. Tradeoff. A shield can lower crosstalk while increasing victim loading and consuming a track. At a sensitive pole this can reduce bandwidth. It may also change density or the over-device environment. Do not impose blanket shielding on every sensitive device; the device-specific chapters explain when metal coverage itself can affect matching. Enforcement. Treat the shield and its tie points as routable objects with connectivity checks and extraction. Reserve the resource before detailed routing; an annotation that merely raises spacing cost does not construct a shield.","url":"topic-signal-routing.html#shielding"},{"title":"Signal routing, symmetry and shielding · Route topology controls voltage distribution","text":"Route topology controls voltage distribution A chain, star, tree, and mesh distribute resistance differently. A source connection shared by many transistor fingers accumulates branch currents toward the common node; moving that node changes local source voltages. A capacitor-bank trunk can make the far units settle later. A bias tree with unequal shared impedance can couple nominally independent blocks. Constraint. Bound terminal voltage differences or path transfer errors rather than minimizing only total wire length. Technique. Choose balanced trees, local stars, fishbone structures, multiple access points, or parallel routes according to the current distribution. Reason. These structures control shared impedance and which currents flow through each segment. Limit. A geometrically balanced tree does not have equal delays if its loads differ. The CC review explicitly analyzes the small-signal source currents and local AC-ground location in different array patterns. This is why internal macro routing belongs in the same electrical model as global routing. Source-resistance analysis, PDF pp. 6–7","url":"topic-signal-routing.html#topology"},{"title":"Signal routing, symmetry and shielding · Special handling for high-impedance and charge-storage nodes","text":"Special handling for high-impedance and charge-storage nodes High-impedance nodes are sensitive to capacitive loading, leakage, and injected charge. Sample-and-hold nodes and capacitor top plates can also be sensitive to dielectric absorption, parasitic voltage dependence, and switching feedthrough. Minimize unnecessary metal exposure and identify every nearby switching conductor. Keep test structures, fill, and access stubs in the model. Technique. Prefer direct routes with controlled surroundings, protected layers where justified, short device access, and deliberate reference conductors. Constrain parasitic ratios for charge-domain networks rather than demanding equal absolute parasitics on unequal intended capacitances. Use extraction to distinguish top/bottom plate relationships. The DAC chapter develops these rules in detail. Capacitor terminal parasitics, PDF pp. 2–4 For precision sense connections, separate force and sense paths where the circuit architecture requires Kelvin behavior. The sense route should not include uncontrolled voltage drop from the force current. This is a connectivity/topology requirement, not merely a request for a wider wire; see power and reference distribution .","url":"topic-signal-routing.html#sensitive"},{"title":"Signal routing, symmetry and shielding · Put the right information into route costs","text":"Put the right information into route costs Search costs can combine congestion, length, via use, estimated R/C, coupling, and budget criticality. Normalize physical residuals and retain their identities. A route that is short but consumes all of a critical capacitance budget should have a different marginal cost from an equally long insensitive rail. A local edge cost is only an approximation when an objective depends on a whole route graph, pair matching, or aggregate coupling. Maintain route-level state and reevaluate completed candidates. If an optimization introduces signed sensitivity rewards, do not feed negative edges into an algorithm that assumes nonnegative edge costs without changing the algorithm or proving an equivalent safe formulation. Constraint interaction. Width changes spacing and congestion. A new layer adds vias. A shield adds capacitance and a return route. A matched detour changes both sides' performance. Price these consequences or reserve them explicitly; treating each repair as independent can make the engine oscillate between violations.","url":"topic-signal-routing.html#costs"},{"title":"Signal routing, symmetry and shielding · Philis additions and acceptance tests","text":"Philis additions and acceptance tests The current global router and detailed router already have negotiation and targeted repair mechanisms. Extend their measured state with terminal RC, corresponding-path signatures, per-layer coupling models, and explicit shield connectivity. Preserve the existing same-layer spacing and aggregate-coupling checks as useful low-cost screens, with their physical scope stated. Test a length-matched but RC-mismatched pair; two individually acceptable aggressors that together exceed the victim budget; a floating versus well-tied shield; a layer change that increases via resistance; unequal loads on a symmetric tree; and an internal macro route that dominates the external route. Simulate the corresponding circuit error after each repair. A useful new constraint should distinguish a known harmful layout from a harmless one without systematically rejecting valid alternatives.","url":"topic-signal-routing.html#engine"},{"title":"Substrate noise and isolation · Evidence and boundaries","text":"Evidence and boundaries This chapter synthesizes all nine main chapters and technical appendices of the supplied substrate book, with emphasis on extraction, noise generation, circuit sensitivity, optimization, circuit failure mechanisms, isolation tradeoffs, and measurement. The original numerical study curves are process-specific. The proposed algorithm, error-budget contract, SVGs, and numerical differential example are instructional synthesis, not claimed foundry guidance. The linear substrate model must declare where nonlinear injection, package behavior, or insufficient characterization prevents an isolation signoff claim.","url":"topic-substrate-isolation.html#coverage"},{"title":"Thermal fields, gradients and package effects · Validation and evidence limits","text":"Validation and evidence limits Test single-source distance scaling; equal-radius pairs; two-heater mode changes; finite-source near-field behavior; die-edge/package asymmetry; unequal self-heating in matched devices; and transient bursts with different thermal time constants. Compare group-weighted electrical error with a circuit model, not only maximum temperature. Verify that power scaling produces the expected response in the linear model and that the solver recovers a declared boundary temperature at zero power. Compare the calibrated fast model against a layered or numerical reference before claiming quantitative temperature margins. This chapter explicitly separates the current half-space heuristic, general heat-flow synthesis, and the supplied literature’s thermal/reliability evidence.","url":"topic-thermal.html#coverage"},{"title":"Experiments that demonstrate better layouts · Write a falsifiable claim for every improvement","text":"Write a falsifiable claim for every improvement Turn “common centroid is better” into a conditional statement: “for equal electrical unit weights in a linear spatial parameter field, the generated layout equalizes the first moments and reduces that field's ratio-error contribution.” Then test a quadratic field, unequal edge environments and routed parasitics, where the same certificate is insufficient. This structure distinguishes a valid technique from a universal claim the sources do not make. Gradient-cancellation motivation, PDF pp. 1–3 Limits and tradeoffs, PDF pp. 6–7 Similarly, “wider routes improve performance” needs a circuit, metric and operating domain. Widening reduces geometric resistance but can add capacitance or force detours. The wire-sizing paper provides explicit cases where uniform width changes improve some metrics and worsen others. Test the actual constraint and its competing effects. Uniform-width comparison, PDF p. 19 A useful experiment record states the mechanism, expected direction under its assumptions, intervention, unchanged comparison conditions, measured outcomes, possible confounders and acceptance threshold. Thresholds should follow a specification or model accuracy requirement, not be chosen after observing the result.","url":"topic-validation.html#hypothesis"},{"title":"Experiments that demonstrate better layouts · A discriminating experiment catalog for Philis","text":"A discriminating experiment catalog for Philis Mechanism / proposed feature Experiment Evidence that would support it Electrical rather than box centroids Change only halo/outline dimensions around fixed internal active units; then move a real unit. The first change leaves electrical moments invariant; the second changes them predictably. Linear versus higher-order gradients Compare AABB, ABAB, ABBA and balanced multirow patterns under separate polynomial basis fields. Only the certified moments vanish; remaining quadratic/mixed terms remain visible. Random mismatch and segmentation Compare equal total active area with different legal subdivisions under independent and correlated unit models. Independent-area predictions remain consistent; correlated results follow dᵀΣd rather than the unit count alone. Orientation / LDE Keep centroids fixed while reversing source/drain direction or changing one member's STI/well distance. The relevant signed-orientation/environment checker detects the change and a qualified model predicts its effect. Differential routing Use equal total length with unequal layers, vias, branches or neighboring aggressors. The electrical route checker distinguishes the pair even when the old length check ties. Shielding Compare no shield, a grounded low-impedance shield and a poorly returned shield under the same aggressor excitation. Extracted/simulated victim error includes reduced mutual coupling, added load and shield-return effects. Wire sizing Compare uniform narrow/wide routes and selective sizing at equal topology and legal geometry. The selected widths improve the declared metric while all required metrics and EM rules pass. Substrate isolation Vary contact/guard return impedance while holding geometric separation fixed. The model responds to the electrical return path; distance-only scoring cannot explain all results. Thermal placement Move a powered aggressor relative to a matched pair; then remove valid power data. Predicted differential heating tracks a qualified reference; missing data become unknown rather than a cool layout. EM and via capacity Compare a uniform wire, a neck, a branch junction and an unevenly fed via array across temperature. Local current density/capacity and hotter-condition degradation are captured; total via count alone is insufficient. Antenna Use a MOS with distinct G and D nets and vary process-stage connectivity to a protection element. Only the actual gate and available stage-specific protection determine its exposure. DAC arrays Compare spiral, chessboard and block families with fixed units, PDK and switch/testbench assumptions. Code linearity, terminal RC and settling reveal the matching/routing tradeoff. Performance objectives Move equal capacitance between a high-impedance node and an insensitive node. Circuit ranking changes even though total C does not. Verification coverage Deliberately omit a required recognizer, model, rule or simulation result. The corresponding certification becomes incomplete/failed and identifies the missing evidence. Constraint propagation Merge, rotate and retarget a matched macro while retaining original device identities. Required semantics survive; duplicate or missing physical weights do not silently cancel. These are proposed discriminating experiments, not claims that the current engine passes them or that they were run during handbook writing. The detailed chapters supply the equations, scope and expected failure modes behind each row.","url":"topic-validation.html#matrix"},{"title":"Experiments that demonstrate better layouts · Use a validation ladder from arithmetic to complete circuits","text":"Use a validation ladder from arithmetic to complete circuits Level 1: mathematical and semantic checks. Verify moment algebra, units, covariance transformations, terminal identities and graph equivalence on small examples with known answers. These can expose an incorrect implementation before an expensive layout run. For the higher-order pattern, explicitly test every monomial of total degree up to the claimed order; do not test only one convenient gradient direction. Level 2: isolated physical structures. Use device arrays, line/via structures, coupled routes, guard rings or resistor/capacitor cells with controlled geometry. Compare analytic limits and an independent qualified extraction/model source. Sweep the parameter that activates the mechanism, including boundary conditions where the approximation should break down. Level 3: circuit blocks. Use mirrors, differential pairs, OTAs, resistor ratios, references and capacitor DACs with fixed testbenches. Evaluate the metrics that motivated the constraint. A mirror may need current-ratio error over output voltage; an OTA needs more than gain; a reference may be limited by temperature coefficient or thermoelectric offset. Level 4: complete flow. Run annotation, cell generation, placement, routing, physical checks, extraction and simulation. Include legal-route success rate and model coverage, not just the best successful circuit. This reveals interface failures that isolated rule tests miss. Level 5: process/manufacturing evidence. Compare to foundry-qualified decks, characterized models and measurements where available. Textbook equations and simulations do not independently establish silicon yield. This stage is a required external evidence source for manufacturing claims, not work completed by these notes.","url":"topic-validation.html#levels"},{"title":"Experiments that demonstrate better layouts · Make comparisons fair and reproducible","text":"Make comparisons fair and reproducible Hold the schematic, authorized sizing space, process data, legal geometry policy, testbenches and metric definitions constant unless the experiment intentionally studies one of them. If a new method uses larger devices or a looser outline, disclose that as a changed design space. Compare at equal compute/evaluation budgets as well as, where useful, equal final quality. Use several seeds for stochastic search. Report the distribution of feasible quality, feasibility rate, runtime and failure reasons. The best of many runs can overstate typical performance; an average over only successful runs hides inability to route difficult examples. For timing, distinguish wall time from total CPU/GPU work and number of expensive extraction/simulation calls. Cache keys should include schematic, emitted geometry, PDK/deck/model versions, extraction settings, stimulus and measurement script. Store unrounded raw metrics and derived scores so a changed weighting policy does not require pretending old measurements were different. Keep versioned candidate records; source hashes in coverage serve a similar purpose for this handbook. For layout-aware ML, split datasets at the level matching the claim. Random variants from the same topology support interpolation claims. Transfer to new circuits, sizes, processes or test modes needs corresponding held-out groups. Check repeated or near-identical geometries across splits; leakage can make a model appear predictive while it is memorizing layouts. Metric-dependent transfer, PDF p. 4","url":"topic-validation.html#controls"},{"title":"Experiments that demonstrate better layouts · Use ablations to learn which constraint helped","text":"Use ablations to learn which constraint helped Compare the full method to variants removing one new constraint or model at a time, while preserving feasibility requirements. For example, compare route-length matching alone to route-length plus per-layer/via matching, then to extracted terminal RC matching. Measure both runtime and electrical results. If a new geometric rule improves its own score but does not improve any intended metric, it may still be useful as a conservative screen, but that is a narrower claim. When mechanisms interact, a one-at-a-time ablation is insufficient. A guard ring's benefit depends on return impedance; common centroid's value depends on the gradient model; wire widening depends on node sensitivity and channel congestion. Add a small factorial or structured sweep for these interacting variables. Report the domain where the effect changes sign rather than forcing one universal ranking. Also include counterexamples. A no-gradient scenario should not reward costly gradient cancellation merely because the pattern looks disciplined. A low-impedance route may tolerate added capacitance that would be fatal on a high-impedance node. A dummy or shield lacking the connection or environment required by its qualification should not receive the corresponding credit; some qualified dummy/fill structures are intentionally floating. These cases check the evaluator's reasons for taking an action.","url":"topic-validation.html#ablation"},{"title":"Experiments that demonstrate better layouts · Quantify uncertainty in yield and small improvements","text":"Quantify uncertainty in yield and small improvements If n independent Monte Carlo samples contain no failures, that does not prove zero failure probability. Under a binomial model, a one-sided confidence bound at level 1−α gives p fail ≤ 1−α 1/n , approximately −ln(α)/n for a small bound. At 95% confidence this is about 3/n. Thus 1,000 passing samples support a bound near 0.3%, not parts-per-billion reliability. The derivation follows P(no failures) = (1−p) n ; independence and representative sampling are assumptions. For paired layout comparisons, use common characterized random samples when appropriate to reduce comparison noise, while still evaluating the joint pass event for each layout. Do not independently push every correlated parameter to its worst marginal quantile and call that a statistically meaningful sample. Importance sampling or worst-case distance methods need their own assumptions and estimator checks, as discussed in robust design . A tiny nominal metric improvement below extraction or simulation uncertainty is not convincing evidence. Estimate solver tolerance, model approximation and run-to-run variation where relevant. Report a practical effect size and margin to required limits. A repeatable increase in feasibility or margin can matter more than a small best-case objective gain.","url":"topic-validation.html#statistics"},{"title":"Experiments that demonstrate better layouts · Reuse independent checks already present in the repository","text":"Reuse independent checks already present in the repository The repository includes DRC cross-check tooling , LVS cross-check tooling , and PEX cross-check tooling . Build validation around these independent comparisons where their PDK/device scope is appropriate. Their existence is not proof that every new device or effect is covered. In particular, xcheck_pex.py independently recomputes the same per-rectangle ground-capacitance formula from GDS and checks a heuristic total/ground-capacitance band; it does not independently validate resistance, coupling, field accuracy or reduced-network poles. The DRC comparison uses the same local deck, so agreement does not prove foundry-rule completeness. Inspect the actual invocation, model and reference setup for each experiment. Separate the reusable validator from the optimizer being evaluated. A moment checker that simply reads the optimizer's reported score cannot independently verify its internal-unit mapping. Recompute from emitted unit identities and coordinates. Likewise, extract the final routed geometry when testing a width change; do not validate it using only the width value requested before legalization.","url":"topic-validation.html#existing"},{"title":"Experiments that demonstrate better layouts · Report improvement in the language of the design contract","text":"Report improvement in the language of the design contract A useful result says which required metrics passed, how their worst-case margins changed, how many candidate runs were feasible, and what area/power/runtime costs accompanied the change. Show the nondominated alternatives when tradeoffs remain. Do not aggregate unrelated circuits into a single percentage without explaining normalization and weighting. Keep the conclusion bounded by evidence: “reduced simulated offset variance under the characterized model,” “improved worst-bit settling at equal capacitance,” or “detected terminal-index failures that the previous test hid.” Those claims directly support better engine decisions. A source paper's reported speedup, matching improvement or yield number becomes a hypothesis to reproduce under Philis's conditions, not a default expectation.","url":"topic-validation.html#report"},{"title":"Verification coverage and layout certificates · Different checks answer different questions","text":"Different checks answer different questions Check Its question Why another check is still needed Intent / annotation Do matched groups, net classes, voltage domains and allowed transformations correspond to the intended circuit? Correct intent can still be implemented incorrectly. Geometry DRC Do drawn shapes satisfy the supported process geometry rules? Legal shapes can connect the wrong circuit or perform poorly. Connectivity / LVS Does the recognized extracted device graph match the intended graph and relevant parameters? Correct topology can violate voltages, current density or analog specifications. ERC / reliability Are electrical domain, well, oxide, current and other required conditions valid? Many require scenarios and models beyond connectivity. Parasitic extraction What additional physical network does the geometry produce within the extractor's scope? A network alone is not a pass/fail circuit result. Post-layout simulation Does that realized circuit meet its metrics for the specified modes and conditions? Nominal simulation does not establish statistical yield or lifetime. Variation, aging and manufacturing review Do required margins survive characterized uncertainty, mission history and final processing? The answer remains conditional on model and coverage quality. This layered approach follows the layout/verification/performance feedback described in Lampaert and the modern automation survey. The explicit certificate schema below is a proposed Philis design. Layout verification loop, PDF pp. 18–20 Performance gap, PDF pp. 1–3 See also constraint systems .","url":"topic-verification.html#layers"},{"title":"Verification coverage and layout certificates · Use result states that distinguish absence of evidence from success","text":"Use result states that distinguish absence of evidence from success Every rule evaluation should return an applicability decision, a result and supporting evidence. Useful states are pass , fail , not applicable with a reason , unknown because required data are missing , and evaluation failed . “Not applicable” is justified by the physical scope: an oxide antenna rule may not apply to a graph with no exposed MOS gates. An empty gate table caused by a mapping bug is unknown or an error, not non-applicability. For a required rule, missing models, unsupported device recognition or failed execution should block the corresponding certification. The optimizer may continue exploring and label the candidate provisionally feasible under available checks, but the final report must retain the gap. A default numeric zero is especially dangerous for power, current, coupling or mismatch because it resembles a physically excellent result. A report needs rule identity and version, severity/tier, affected objects, physical units, measured quantity, limit, normalized residual, model inputs, scenario, evidence location, and applicability explanation. Retain raw measurements even when the ranking uses normalized residuals. This makes two violations comparable without hiding whether one is a nanometer spacing shortfall or a statistical offset limit. When constraints conflict, report a small explanatory subset if available: a fixed outline, a required symmetry island and a routing corridor may collectively be infeasible even though each is reasonable alone. If the search simply failed, say that instead of presenting an unproved infeasibility diagnosis. Metaverification—checking that constraints and their mappings remain consistent—is developed in the topological survey's final chapter. Chapter 7, §7.4.5, metaverification, PDF pp. 291–293 §7.5.2, conflict checking, PDF pp. 296–298 The distinction between a failed search and a proof of infeasibility is our methodological qualification.","url":"topic-verification.html#coverage"},{"title":"Verification coverage and layout certificates · Verify the emitted geometry, including transformations and late changes","text":"Verify the emitted geometry, including transformations and late changes Placement boxes and routing centerlines are approximations. Check the actual expanded polygons, cuts, enclosures, pin-access patches, device shapes, guard contacts, dummies and fill. Width-dependent spacing can make a route illegal after widening; a legal via center can still lack enclosure; a rotated macro can invalidate pin access or directional device rules. These changes are reasons to recheck the realized shapes, not reasons to ban useful variants. Manufacturing rules depend on process stage, net relationship, width, length, voltage domain, density window and connectivity. Represent those conditions rather than reducing every rule to a single minimum spacing. Antenna checking needs the conductor connectivity present at the relevant etch stage. Final connectivity through upper metal may not have existed when lower metal accumulated charge. See manufacturability and protection . Late fill, density repair, shielding, redundant vias and power reinforcement change parasitics and sometimes topology. Treat them as candidate mutations with invalidated dependent results. A pre-fill performance pass does not certify a post-fill layout when added capacitance or stress matters. For efficiency, use localized incremental checks only when their dependency region is complete; retain a full final check of the accepted geometry.","url":"topic-verification.html#geometry"},{"title":"Verification coverage and layout certificates · Preserve device identity through generation and LVS","text":"Preserve device identity through generation and LVS The reference circuit and generated device must agree on dimensions, finger multiplicity, series/parallel composition, legal source/drain interchange, body connections and recognized model family. A macro containing several schematic devices needs a mapping to its internal physical units. A bounding box or a single macro identifier cannot establish the equivalence of every terminal. LVS recognition limits must be visible. If a device family has no recognizer, omitting it from the reference may allow the supported subgraph to compare successfully while leaving the omitted device unchecked. State the number and identities of such devices and whether the requested certification requires them. This is particularly relevant to new passive structures or special devices whose electrical function cannot be inferred from arbitrary metal rectangles. Use adversarial circuits when validating the graph mapping. A diode-connected MOS has G=D and can hide a gate/drain swap. A distinct-gate, distinct-drain device exposes it. Similarly, symmetric test circuits can conceal source/drain or bit-label permutations; add an asymmetric reference with known terminals. The purpose is to test semantic preservation, not just whether a familiar example yields a clean report.","url":"topic-verification.html#connectivity"},{"title":"Verification coverage and layout certificates · Check extraction quality before using it as the optimization target","text":"Check extraction quality before using it as the optimization target At the network level, check units, connectivity, nonnegative passive element values within the extractor's formulation, matrix symmetry where reciprocal physics requires it, and proper treatment of reference nodes. A coupling capacitor between i and j contributes +C to the two diagonals and −C to the off-diagonals. Shunt capacitance and mutual capacitance must not be conflated or double counted. Use structures that isolate mechanisms: one straight wire on a known stack, two parallel wires with a spacing sweep, a layer crossing, a via chain, a branched resistive net and a capacitor with each plate separately accessible. Compare to analytic limits or an independent qualified extractor. Monotonicity is conditional: changing a wire's width can reduce its resistance while increasing its capacitance, so “all extracted metrics improve with width” is an invalid test. Then check circuit consequences: equal total C placed on different nodes should produce different behavior when node sensitivities differ. Equivalent series/parallel resistor networks should preserve the appropriate DC result after valid reduction. Reduction must preserve the ports and bandwidth required by the testbench; a lumped approximation that matches DC can still lose settling poles. An independent extractor disagreement is useful evidence, not automatically a failure of one implementation. Align device recognition, stack parameters, coupling cutoff, reduction policy and reference conditions first, then compare the remaining difference and its performance impact. See parasitic networks for the physical model.","url":"topic-verification.html#pex"},{"title":"Verification coverage and layout certificates · Verify modes, corners, statistics and mission conditions explicitly","text":"Verify modes, corners, statistics and mission conditions explicitly Supply sequencing, startup, shutdown, calibration, sampling phases and overload can create terminal voltages or current paths absent from the nominal operating point. For each required check, identify the scenarios that excite its mechanism. EM may require average/DC, RMS and peak currents under different rules; oxide stress depends on terminal voltage history; substrate noise depends on switching spectra and return impedance. One nominal DC solution does not substitute for these. Electrical performance verification needs clearly measured gain, bandwidth, phase margin, noise, swing, settling, distortion, offset or converter linearity as appropriate to the circuit. Joint yield concerns the probability that all required metrics pass simultaneously. Taking the smallest of several single-metric yields or calling a 3σ value “99.7% circuit yield” generally ignores their dependence and combined failure event. Robust design gives the corresponding models and limits. Use model-qualified temperatures and lifetime conditions for aging or reliability claims. An EM screen based on room-temperature current density is useful evidence only for that screen. Likewise, a point-source thermal placement estimate can guide separation but does not replace a package-aware thermal solution where temperature margins are tight.","url":"topic-verification.html#electrical"},{"title":"Verification coverage and layout certificates · What Philis currently checks, and where the report needs stronger evidence","text":"What Philis currently checks, and where the report needs stronger evidence signoff runs the DRC/ERC/LVS/PEX engine, turns reported violations into hard violations, and treats skipped/refused requested stages or engine failures as failures. It also detects label shorts and reruns label-free checks to gather other geometry findings. These are useful safeguards already present; the handbook does not propose them as missing features. Two narrower coverage gaps remain in the reviewed implementation. Devices without deck recognizers are reported to stderr and left out of the LVS reference. Individual rules skipped inside a running stage are logged once rather than recorded as structured coverage in the candidate report. A future certificate should expose both sets per run and compare them to the coverage required for that circuit. The current scalar cost is checker.total_cap_ff() ; a successful PEX stage still needs the electrical evaluations described above. Report::feasible requires both hard and budget violation lists to be empty. Report::lex ranks by hard count, summed budget margins and cost. Preserve the difference between a ranked provisional candidate and a fully certified output. In particular, a boolean budget failure with zero stored residual could be present while adding zero to the budget sum; validate invariants on required budget-result construction.","url":"topic-verification.html#current"},{"title":"Verification coverage and layout certificates · A reviewable final layout certificate","text":"A reviewable final layout certificate A proposed output bundle contains the final geometry hash; schematic and generated-device mapping; PDK/deck/model versions; declared objective and required scenarios; per-rule and per-device-family coverage; all raw and normalized margins; post-layout metric vector; variation/reliability evidence where required; and the search termination reason. Include links to located failures and to the source/rationale of generated constraints. This is useful for the engine itself as well as the designer. It prevents a later optimization pass from accepting a candidate on stale checks, makes missing model data actionable, and allows a different checker to reproduce the claims. The final result should say exactly which meaning of “optimal” it supports: for example, the lowest-area verified candidate among the evaluated layouts, under a specified electrical contract.","url":"topic-verification.html#certificate"},{"title":"Performance-driven wire sizing · Derive the competing constraints","text":"Derive the competing constraints For a uniform metal strip, resistance scales approximately as R□L/W . Increasing W can reduce series voltage drop and source degeneration. At the same time, larger conductor area and changed neighboring gaps can increase shunt or coupling capacitance. The effect on the circuit depends on where the wire sits: a low-impedance source path may benefit, while a high-impedance pole may become slower. Current-density and voltage-drop limits provide lower-width requirements; routing capacity, spacing, and capacitance can impose upper practical limits or discrete alternatives. Width-dependent design rules mean a route that is legal at one width may fail after widening even if its centerline does not move. Physical motivation, PDF pp. 2–4 Selective width is an electrical design decision Three route widths trade resistance against capacitance and consumed channel space. A driver-resistance model can have an interior optimum. Selective width is an electrical design decision Narrow higher R, smaller area load Medium a possible compromise Wide lower R, larger area load Legal widths + node sensitivity + via capacity + routing space Download this SVG Original illustrative diagram; not PDK-valid or to scale. Increasing width helps resistance and often current capacity, but capacitance and geometry must be reevaluated. No universal widest-is-best rule follows. Constraint representation. Store a legal option set by layer and path type, including widths, spacing envelopes, via/access strategies, and characterized RC. For advanced gridded processes the options may be parallel tracks rather than arbitrary widths. For another process, continuous drawn widths snapped to a grid may be legal. The PDK determines the set.","url":"topic-wire-sizing.html#derivation"},{"title":"Performance-driven wire sizing · A simple worked tradeoff","text":"A simple worked tradeoff Assume a toy first-order objective τ(w)=(Rdriver+a/w)(Cload+bw) , with positive constants and a continuous width w. Expanding gives Rdriver·Cload + Rdriver·bw + a·Cload/w + ab . Differentiating gives an interior stationary point: w* = √[a·Cload / (Rdriver·b)] when Rdriver>0 and b>0, then clip to feasible bounds and compare legal discrete neighbors. This is an original illustrative derivation, not the objective used by the wire-sizing paper. It explains why a finite optimum can exist: one term decreases with width and another increases. If Rdriver approaches zero or if capacitance barely changes, the tradeoff changes. In a real analog circuit, multiple nodes, poles, nonlinear behavior, noise, and matching invalidate this one-pole shortcut as a final selector. Engine use. Such a model can rank early candidates or initialize a discrete search. Validate its predictions against the extracted circuit and abandon it when topology or operation changes. Do not hardcode the derived optimum as a universal wire-width rule.","url":"topic-wire-sizing.html#rc-model"},{"title":"Performance-driven wire sizing · Separate reliability minima from performance preferences","text":"Separate reliability minima from performance preferences A wire required to carry a certain mission-profile current cannot be narrowed merely because the capacitance score improves. Derive its minimum viable cross-section from the appropriate process current-density model, temperature, lifetime, and waveform statistic. Then check the complete path, including contacts, necks, and vias; a wide trunk with a narrow escape is not reliable. Within that feasible set, circuit performance may favor a particular width. Treat EM, IR drop, and transient voltage errors as explicitly named requirements, and RC-driven optimization as a separate decision. The EM chapter explains why average, RMS, and peak currents are not interchangeable, while power distribution explains segment currents.","url":"topic-wire-sizing.html#hierarchy"},{"title":"Performance-driven wire sizing · Choose the granularity of the sizing variables","text":"Choose the granularity of the sizing variables Per-net width is simple and reduces the search space, but a net can contain a high-current trunk, low-current leaves, and sensitive access segments. Per-segment width is more flexible, but adds variables, discontinuities, and contact transitions. Shared variables for matched routes preserve a useful symmetry, but equal width alone does not guarantee equal electrical paths. Recommended progression for Philis. Begin with a small set of critical net or route-group variables. Add segment granularity only where extraction shows that a uniform width wastes capacitance or leaves a bottleneck. Group corresponding differential segments and maintain legal transitions. This concentrates simulation effort on changes with a physical reason. The supplied wire-sizing paper uses discrete per-net choices, assigned after global routing and before detailed routing, with equal choices for symmetric nets. It demonstrates a useful tractable formulation rather than proving per-net sizing is always sufficient. Problem formulation and flow, PDF pp. 5–6","url":"topic-wire-sizing.html#where"},{"title":"Performance-driven wire sizing · Parallel tracks are a topology, not just a width number","text":"Parallel tracks are a topology, not just a width number Two parallel conductors can reduce resistance when both are well connected at the needed terminals. Their effective resistance depends on lengths, contacts, current division, and any cross-connections. If only one track reaches a narrow pin escape, that access remains a bottleneck. Multiple parallel tracks also have different coupling and spacing behavior from one contiguous wide rectangle. The paper realizes 1×, 2×, and 3× choices with parallel routing on a gridded process. The DAC paper similarly adds parallel wires and observes diminishing returns as capacitance grows and the critical bit changes. Carry the realized route graph through extraction instead of multiplying an old resistance by 1/N. Physical realization, PDF pp. 15–16 Parallel routing and shifting bottlenecks, PDF pp. 11–12","url":"topic-wire-sizing.html#parallel"},{"title":"Performance-driven wire sizing · A practical sizing algorithm","text":"A practical sizing algorithm Establish a legal routed baseline and its circuit metrics. This gives an actual bottleneck and a comparison target. Identify candidate paths using sensitivity and current analysis. Reason: a small number of paths often matter more than most wires. Build legal width/topology options with their spacing and via requirements. Reason: unconstrained numerical choices may be impossible to draw. Evaluate a small discrete neighborhood. Change one or a coordinated set of corresponding paths, reroute locally, extract, and simulate. Include both narrower and wider legal alternatives where reliability allows. Retain feasible improvements and nondominated alternatives. Do not let improved gain buy a failed phase margin or lifetime limit. Refresh sensitivities and the critical-path set. The former bottleneck may no longer limit the circuit. Stop according to evaluation budget and measured improvement. Report the best observed candidate and search coverage, not an unsupported global-optimum claim. This initial algorithm needs no learned model. It establishes a reference for more sophisticated methods and creates trustworthy training data.","url":"topic-wire-sizing.html#algorithm"},{"title":"Performance-driven wire sizing · When a surrogate can help","text":"When a surrogate can help The paper's WAGN model uses device/pin/Steiner-node features and route features including width and via count. It produces a soft classification score denoted as the probability that a normalized FOM is below one chosen threshold. Calibration and manufacturing yield are not established by that notation. Search runs cheaply on that classifier, then a shortlist is simulated. Preserve the final simulation step: optimization actively seeks regions where a surrogate may be overconfident. Graph features, PDF p. 8 Two-stage optimization, PDF pp. 12–13 If using continuous relaxation, legalize the result to discrete choices and re-evaluate the resulting geometry. Partial floor/ceiling enumeration grows exponentially with the number of ambiguous variables, so cap it or use a structured discrete search. A route that falls back to a narrower width because of congestion must be rescored; the predicted result for the unbuilt wider route is invalid. Adoption criterion. Compare total data generation, training, routing, extraction, and simulation cost at equal achieved specification quality. The paper's speedups are tied to its hardware, circuits, and evaluation budgets. Generalization to a different PDK or circuit family requires evidence. Runtime accounting, PDF pp. 18–19 Declared limitations, PDF p. 23","url":"topic-wire-sizing.html#surrogate"},{"title":"Performance-driven wire sizing · Philis implementation boundary","text":"Philis implementation boundary DetailedCfg already carries base width, signal/supply fattening, width-dependent spacing, via-array spacing, and net-current input. Extend it with a candidate option map rather than replacing these rules. Use the route graph to propagate chosen dimensions into every segment, escape, and contact. The current current-per-width constant is a coarse process assumption, and the final winner cost is total capacitance. Introduce layer- and mission-specific reliability data and an electrical performance evaluator before interpreting the sizing result as optimal. Source/supply classification is already wired into the main flow; operating currents require a distinct data path.","url":"topic-wire-sizing.html#engine"},{"title":"Performance-driven wire sizing · Tests that justify the feature","text":"Tests that justify the feature Use a resistance-dominated source connection, a capacitance-sensitive high-impedance node, and a mixed RC-sensitive compensation path. Include a width-dependent spacing threshold, a pin-access neck, an asymmetric via array, and two matched nets sharing a width variable. Check that increasing width can help the first circuit, hurt the second, and require a tradeoff in the third. Measure extracted R/C and circuit metrics after physical realization. Compare against uniform minimum width, uniform wider width, current-only sizing, and the existing router at equal evaluation budgets. A useful optimizer should explain why its selected paths differ, not merely report that a scalar FOM increased.","url":"topic-wire-sizing.html#tests"},{"title":"Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches · Choose a search space that preserves intent, then verify what that search space omits","text":"Choose a search space that preserves intent, then verify what that search space omits Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor, Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches (2011). This is an edited collection with seven technically different contributions, not one algorithm. Its placement chapters explain how representations change which layouts are reachable and how much effort is spent exploring illegal candidates. Its routing and design-flow chapters explain why geometry alone is insufficient: connectivity, parasitics, sizing, technology migration and constraint semantics must remain consistent. Engine-level conclusion. Philis should preserve exact constraints through suitable representations and moves, retain multiple useful realizations, and postpone irreversible choices until their electrical and routing consequences are understood. “Optimal” always means optimal within a stated candidate space, objective and model. A compact symmetric packing is not automatically the best routed circuit.","url":"topological.html#thesis"},{"title":"Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches · Coverage and chapter map","text":"Coverage and chapter map Substantive sections were read in every chapter, emphasizing definitions, methods, limitations and their application to Philis. Chapter 1’s contour-data-structure implementations and some of Chapter 5’s simplex derivations were surveyed rather than reproduced line by line. The reading includes chapter conclusions and selected experiments; experiments were not rerun, and references cited within the book were not independently reviewed. The supplied 311-page scan omits some printed blank pages, so a constant printed-to-PDF offset is unsafe. All source links use one-based PDF positions. Chapter / author PDF pages Main result examined 1. Florin Balasa: device-level topological placement 17–74 Symmetric-feasible sequence pairs and trees, contour structures, move restrictions, multiple-group feasibility. 2. Mark Po-Hung Lin and Yao-Wen Chang: hierarchical placement 75–108 ASF-B* representatives, connected symmetry islands, HB* hierarchy, contours, perturbations and extensions. 3. Strasser, Eick, Graeb and Schlichtmann: enhanced shape functions 110–160 SMP graph/HSMPG tree, constraint generation, LP/MIP packing, deterministic hierarchical enumeration. 4. Günhan Dündar and Ahmet Unutulmaz: routing 162–214 Grid/tile/topological models, physical connectivity, net splitting, symmetry, shielding, bounds, templates, RF and arrays. 5. Said, Dessouky, El-Adawi, Abbas and Shahein: retargeting 215–252 Constraint extraction, graph compaction, coefficient-graph simplex, hierarchy, replacement and parasitic preservation. 6. Castro-López, Roca and Fernández: layout-aware sizing 253–278 Geometric parameter selection, template shape functions, direct extraction versus sampled parasitics, simulation feedback. 7. Jerke, Lienig and Freuer: constraint-driven methodology 279–307 Classification, management, derivation, transformation, sensitivity, conflicts and meta-verification. Raster inspection checked the symmetric-feasible sequence-pair condition on PDF p. 49, center-based symmetry equations on p. 113, DTI disjunctive constraints on p. 136, and enhanced-shape merging on p. 139. These checks matter because the extracted text corrupts mathematical operators and superscripts.","url":"topological.html#coverage"},{"title":"Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches · A comparison to use before selecting an algorithm","text":"A comparison to use before selecting an algorithm Approach Why it is useful What its optimum does not establish Best initial experiment in Philis Free-coordinate search Flexible continuous positions, sharing opportunities, natural physical costs. Fast progress or automatic final legality; overlap and repair can dominate. Measure rejected moves, repair displacement and final routing quality of the existing GP/DP baseline. Symmetric-feasible sequence pairs Eliminate many symmetry-invalid orderings before geometric construction. Completeness of a sufficient-condition subset, electrical feasibility, or globally best placement. Generate alternative legal seeds for strongly symmetric blocks. ASF-B*/HB* symmetry islands Pack representative half-groups efficiently; retain hierarchy and rectilinear outlines. That abutting/connected islands are always electrically desirable or that all legal layouts are reachable. Use connected subcircuits with routing halos and several island shapes. Plantage enhanced shape functions Deterministic local enumeration, multiple aspect ratios, explicit linear/disjunctive constraints. Global exhaustive search once hierarchy and pruning restrict choices. Enumerate small matched groups and keep a contextual candidate frontier. Compaction/retargeting Reuse verified physical organization and repair dimensions for another process. Electrical equivalence after changing PDK or dimensions. Resize a known layout while tracking preserved versus reverified intent. Layout-aware template sizing Fast, repeatable geometry makes parasitic feedback practical during sizing. Quality outside the template family or validity of an out-of-domain surrogate. Co-optimize fingers/shape for a recurring amplifier template. The first three chapters differ in scope and admissible layouts, so their runtime and area tables cannot justify replacing Philis wholesale. Comparison requires identical constraints, allowed rotations, primitive variants, routing assumptions, runtime budgets and electrical acceptance tests. Chapter 1 experiments, PDF pp. 69–71 ; Chapter 2 comparisons, PDF pp. 96–101","url":"topological.html#comparison"},{"title":"Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches · 1. Balasa: make symmetry feasibility a property of the encoding","text":"1. Balasa: make symmetry feasibility a property of the encoding Source finding. A sequence pair (α, β) specifies relative orders. In this chapter’s convention, A preceding B in both sequences puts A left of B; A preceding B in α and following B in β puts A above B. A packer converts the ordering to coordinates through horizontal and vertical constraints. The author’s central observation is that fast packing is of little use when most generated codes violate the analog symmetry requirements. PDF pp. 25–26 , PDF pp. 48–51 For distinct members x and y of one symmetry group about a common vertical axis, the chapter gives the following sufficient symmetric-feasible condition (§1.3, Eq. (1.1)): pos α (x) < pos α (y) ⇔ pos β (sym(y)) < pos β (sym(x)). sym(x) denotes the partner of x; a self-symmetric device maps to itself. Mirrored partners reverse the corresponding order in the other sequence. This is explicitly a sufficient condition: the next page discusses legal symmetric placements excluded by it. Treat “outside this subset” as unsearched, not physically impossible. Multiple symmetry groups also need compatible interleaving/nesting; a group-wise condition alone does not license arbitrary intermingling. Nested groups require an appropriate dependency order. PDF pp. 49–50 , PDF pp. 60–62 The chapter compares segment trees, red-black interval trees, deterministic skip lists and a specialized priority queue for contour/LCS evaluation. Asymptotic packing improvements are valuable only after the encoding, symmetry handling and update workload are fixed. Different representations also have different redundancy and move locality. Fewer codes do not guarantee better quality under finite search. Action and reason. Prototype a symmetry-preserving topological seed generator for blocks where the existing coordinate optimizer spends much effort repairing overlap or symmetry. Keep physical scoring and verification common to both approaches so the experiment measures representation quality rather than different objectives. Tradeoff and validation. Record reachable-layout restrictions and retain a fallback search outside the sufficient-condition subset. Enumerate tiny examples to verify the pair-order condition, packer and legal move invariants; include multiple and nested axes. Then compare completed routes and extracted margin under equal wall-clock budgets. The current separate_overlaps entry point (line 17) is a useful place to measure how much postprocessing a topological seed might avoid. Orientation caveat. This chapter distinguishes mirror symmetry, identical-orientation “perfect” symmetry and self-symmetry. Identical orientation can better protect anisotropic-process matching while making geometric mirror routing impossible. Preserve electrical terminal correspondence instead of equating mirror placement with identical device orientation. PDF pp. 21–22","url":"topological.html#sequence-pairs"},{"title":"Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches · 2. Lin and Chang: connected symmetry islands make hierarchy efficient","text":"2. Lin and Chang: connected symmetry islands make hierarchy efficient Source finding. A symmetry island is a connected placement in which each module abuts another member of the group. The automatically symmetric-feasible B* tree packs one representative from each pair and half of each self-symmetric module, then reconstructs the other side. Self-symmetric representatives must touch the symmetry axis; this becomes a boundary restriction on the tree. The hierarchical HB* tree embeds these islands among nonsymmetric modules and retains rectilinear contours rather than wasting every indentation inside a rectangular bounding box. PDF pp. 79–87 In Chapter 2’s B* convention, a left child starts to the right of its parent, x child = x parent + w parent ; a right child has the same x coordinate, and a contour determines y. Other chapters use their own traversal/packing conventions. Copying a child rule from one chapter and a compactor from another without reconciling them is unsafe. The reported linear packing bound is for the specified representation and contour operations; it is not a bound on a complete electrical optimizer. PDF pp. 80–85 , PDF pp. 90–91 The search perturbs hierarchy and island interiors, preserving boundary restrictions. If an island’s outline changes, contour nodes and dependent modules must be updated. Extensions align multiple axes, nest groups, and insert nonsymmetric clusters within symmetric arrangements. Wells and guard rings still need reserved space; “connected” should be interpreted using legal module envelopes, not by forcing active geometry to touch. PDF pp. 91–96 , PDF pp. 100–106 Action and reason. Use recognized hierarchy as a collection of alternative physical group shapes, including outlines, access sides, routing reserves and legal orientations. Pack whole groups while permitting coordinated changes inside them. This reduces unrelated movement and preserves close matching structures without flattening every constraint into pairwise penalties. Tradeoff. An island policy can exclude useful space for routing, shielding or a central heat source. A proximity preference is not automatically an abutment requirement. Allow explicit exceptions and compare multiple group candidates; the chapter itself extends the framework beyond strict islands. Validation. Test contour changes that remove an indentation, create a dangling child, rotate a self-symmetric representative, and combine different well domains. Check both overlap freedom and pin access. In Philis, Block (line 8) supplies a natural logical hierarchy, but a logical block should acquire a physical interface before it becomes an indivisible island.","url":"topological.html#hierarchical-trees"},{"title":"Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches · 3. Plantage: derive hierarchy, enumerate small problems, retain several answers","text":"3. Plantage: derive hierarchy, enumerate small problems, retain several answers Source finding. The SMP multigraph distinguishes matching from symmetric partners, matching within a recognized building block, proximity within a block, symmetry itself, and general netlist proximity. An ordered clustering process turns those requirements into an HSMPG tree, then generates variant, alignment, symmetry and common-centroid constraints. This is useful because a transistor can participate in conflicting relationships, such as matching its differential counterpart and matching a current-mirror partner. PDF pp. 119–129 The proposed priority favors differential matching over matching within each branch, then block proximity, symmetry and general net proximity. The authors justify this by the relative effects on offset versus common branch behavior, and explicitly allow simulation-derived priorities. That ordering is an application-dependent heuristic, not permission to decide universally that offset matters more than gain. Action and reason. Preserve every recognized relationship, its origin and the reason it wins or loses during clustering. Use circuit specifications and sensitivity when relationships conflict. Avoid silently discarding a mirror relationship merely because a differential-pair grouping was selected first. Tradeoff and validation. An overlapping relationship graph is more expressive than a tree, but local enumeration needs a tractable decomposition. Keep crossing constraints at the parent interface, compare plausible decompositions on small examples, and verify the emitted hierarchy against the original graph. recognize (line 175) and placement (line 53) are the relevant existing recognition-to-requirement boundary. LP and MIP packing. Given an ordering, linear spacing inequalities and symmetry/common-centroid equalities can determine compact coordinates. For a vertical symmetry axis x s , with lower-left coordinates and dimensions: [(x i + w i /2) + (x i′ + w i′ /2)]/2 = x s ; y i + h i /2 = y i′ + h i′ /2. These are center equations; copying them as corner equalities changes the meaning for unequal dimensions. A common-centroid equality additionally requires choosing the correct electrical weights. The chapter’s geometric group center is area-weighted; that equals the required electrical centroid only under appropriate unit-device assumptions. PDF pp. 112–115 Deep-trench-isolation devices illustrate a genuinely disjunctive spacing rule: sharing is permitted over a small interval, intermediate distances are forbidden, and sufficiently separated trenches are permitted again. The chapter adds binary branch variables and shared stretch constraints, rather than pretending one convex penalty represents the legal set. The full model also limits a device’s total left/right stretch, so independently legal pair distances can still be jointly impossible. PDF pp. 135–137 Action and reason. Keep explicit branch state for disjunctive technology constraints and coordinate branch changes across neighbors sharing a resource. Philis’s DtiBand (line 18) is already a branch-based entry point. Compare its pair abstraction with the actual foundry rule before generalizing the book’s DTI example. Validation. Check both interval endpoints, the forbidden gap, a three-device chain sharing a middle stretch budget, and an impossible combination with symmetry. A solver timeout or rejected local branch change is not proof of global infeasibility. If using big-M formulations, derive finite bounds from the die and dimensions; oversized constants harm numerical conditioning. Enhanced shape functions. A conventional shape function retains a width/height frontier. A horizontal composition gives (w 1 +w 2 , max(h 1 ,h 2 )); vertical composition gives (max(w 1 ,w 2 ), h 1 +h 2 ). Enhanced shapes also retain their B* trees, allowing contours to fit together more tightly. Plantage enumerates small basic groups and their compatible variants, then combines results bottom-up. This is deterministic hierarchy-guided enumeration, not an exhaustive enumeration of every full-circuit layout. Composition preserves child constraints only under the stated assumptions about additional parent constraints. PDF pp. 137–146 Action and reason. Retain a frontier of useful group variants rather than selecting one smallest-area candidate early. Extend the interface to include pin locations, access, critical parasitics, contours and environment obligations. A slightly larger shape can give the parent a shorter sensitive route or a better substrate separation. Tradeoff and validation. More dimensions enlarge the frontier. Use bounded diversity or documented approximate pruning, and measure what was pruned. Width/height dominance alone is safe only when the discarded state is replaceable in every relevant parent context; it is not sufficient once pin topology or parasitics differ. Validate against exhaustive small-parent compositions, including two children with equal dimensions but different pin access.","url":"topological.html#enhanced-shapes"},{"title":"Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches · 4. Dündar and Unutulmaz: a routing representation must carry physical connectivity","text":"4. Dündar and Unutulmaz: a routing representation must carry physical connectivity Source finding. Uniform grids make implementation straightforward but trade memory for geometric resolution. Nonuniform grids adapt to obstacles; tile/corner-stitching representations retain free space efficiently but complicate escape points and updates. Topological representations express ordering well but lack the exact neighbor geometry needed for parasitic extraction. This argues for a hybrid architecture, not for using one representation in every stage. PDF pp. 168–174 The chapter distinguishes net membership, current-class subnets and physically connected groups. Terminals belonging to one net are not necessarily connected yet. Rip-up can split a physical group even though the netlist is unchanged. High- and low-current portions of a shared ground should sometimes use separated return paths, because voltage drop on shared resistance modulates a sensitive reference. Net splitting here is a routing topology decision and must preserve the intended electrical node. PDF pp. 172–175 Action and reason. Make incremental physical connectivity and branch currents first-class routing state. Recompute affected current paths after a branch or shared segment changes; verify opens independently from budget checks. A zero-length unrouted net must not count as an electrically successful route simply because its parasitic score is small. Tradeoff and validation. Fully dynamic connectivity is complex; reconstruct affected nets when that is simpler. Test insertion, rip-up, via removal, preconnected primitive terminals, separate sensitive returns and multi-terminal current redistribution. Philis’s ParasiticBudget::satisfied deliberately lets zero length pass (lines 30–33), so the independent open check is part of the acceptance contract. Mirror routing can route one side against obstacles mirrored from both sides, but crossing differential nets need a balanced connector and matched layer transitions. Equal wirelength alone does not equalize coupling to asymmetrical neighboring nets. Shields need an appropriate low-impedance reference path; a floating or noisy shield can worsen the problem. Extraction accuracy should increase from simple estimates to detailed models as candidates mature; the chapter separately discusses RF interconnects, where inductance and transmission-line properties can be essential. PDF pp. 175–178 , PDF pp. 204–209 The routing survey distinguishes minimizing raw parasitics, enforcing allocated parasitic bounds, and optimizing sensitivity-derived performance costs. It also reviews interleaving routing with placement, constructing wires as movable layout objects, and templated routes. The LDS template example is particularly useful: independently preserving horizontal and vertical relations from one original layout can allow new overlaps after resizing; additional relations are needed to guarantee valid regenerated instances. Templates encode useful knowledge only if their valid parameter domain is checked. PDF pp. 182–193 , PDF pp. 198–203 Action and reason. Use global routing to evaluate selected placement candidates and return congestion/electrical failure locations to placement. Keep successful local templates as reusable alternatives with parameter-domain validation. This targets repeated placement/routing incompatibility without requiring a monolithic optimizer. Validation. Compare final connectivity, DRC, extracted matching, area and runtime against the sequential baseline. Include a template resized until an old visibility relation ceases to apply, and a matched route with a one-sided obstacle. The TrackGrid representation around line 394 is a current concrete grid choice to evaluate, not evidence that all analog routing must become gridless.","url":"topological.html#routing"},{"title":"Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches · 5. Said and colleagues: compaction preserves design knowledge only if that knowledge is explicit","text":"5. Said and colleagues: compaction preserves design knowledge only if that knowledge is explicit Source finding. Retargeting starts with an existing netlist/layout, target device dimensions, target technology and optional constraints. Layer mapping, recognition and constraint extraction precede compaction. A basic difference constraint x j −x i ≥ d ij fits a directed graph; longest paths in an acyclic ordering find the minimum legal coordinates. However, symmetry such as x b −x a = x d −x c involves four variables and does not fit an ordinary pairwise difference graph. The chapter’s coefficient-graph simplex retains general linear constraints while exploiting graph structure. PDF pp. 217–221 , PDF pp. 227–238 Important practical limitations include stale edge order after orthogonal compaction, long wires created by independently pushing edges, conditional wide-wire rules, well-proximity/STI effects, changed device aspect ratios, hierarchy and full device replacement. A source well larger than the minimum might embody deliberate electrical intent; shrinking it blindly can destroy that intent. Via-array aggregation can reduce constraints, but reconstruction must retain required current capacity and legal enclosure. PDF pp. 241–247 Action and reason. Add a controlled reuse mode that preserves chosen topology, matching environment and route relationships while permitting PDK-driven dimensions and explicitly allowed variants. Prefer an established LP/MIP backend initially over reimplementing the specialized simplex solely for historical runtime claims. Tradeoff and validation. Preservation limits optimization freedom and is not proof of electrical portability. Mark each invariant as preserved, transformed or reverified; regenerate constraints after geometry changes. Run target-PDK DRC/LVS, extraction and performance checks, including changed well margins and a via array whose old cut count is insufficient in the new process. Report an infeasible preserved topology distinctly from an infeasible circuit.","url":"topological.html#retargeting"},{"title":"Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches · 6. Castro-López and colleagues: size the circuit that will actually be built","text":"6. Castro-López and colleagues: size the circuit that will actually be built Source finding. The chapter combines electrical sizing with a geometric-constraint module. For each candidate sizing, the module chooses geometric parameters such as finger counts and primitive shapes under a fixed template; the layout is generated or its parasitics estimated, then circuit performance is simulated. A bottom-up shape-function pass and top-down selection enforce aspect ratio and dimension goals. This recognizes that minimizing ΣWL does not necessarily minimize final layout area, especially after routing and spacing. PDF pp. 259–268 Two parasitic approaches are compared: generate and extract actual template instances, or use analytical device geometry with precharacterized routing tables. A naïve table with m samples along n independent dimensions requires m n entries. The authors first identify important parasitics and then sample their influential parameters more densely. That is a useful strategy, but a table remains valid only for its technology, template, model conventions and characterized parameter domain. Its validity is not unconditional across future sizing runs. PDF pp. 272–275 Action and reason. Feed Philis-generated geometry/parasitics into an outer sizing loop, first for recurring small blocks. Begin with direct extraction on shortlisted candidates to establish trustworthy data, then build a reduced surrogate only where generation cost justifies it. Track internal device parasitics separately from interconnect to prevent double counting. Tradeoff and validation. Template reuse trades generality for predictable runtime and may miss better topologies. Preserve alternatives for electrically sensitive geometric parameters, even when one is smaller. Validate against held-out extracted instances and full circuit metrics; include unseen finger counts and near-boundary aspect ratios as deliberate out-of-domain tests. A simulation failure is an invalid/unknown candidate, not a numerical value to optimize through. The reported experiments demonstrate nominally passing circuits that fail after extraction and improvements when geometry and parasitics participate in sizing. They support the architecture, not universal numerical speedups or signoff guarantees. For example, §6.5.2 says approach A takes approximately 15% more CPU time than approach B. Table 6.8 gives 880.3 s versus 612.31 s, about 43.8% more total time; because the iteration counts differ (3044 versus 2437), the per-iteration ratio is about 15.1% more. The prose is thus consistent with per-iteration overhead, not the stated totals. Specify the denominator and do not turn either historical comparison into a performance promise for Philis. PDF pp. 274–276","url":"topological.html#layout-aware-sizing"},{"title":"Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches · 7. Jerke, Lienig and Freuer: constraints require a lifecycle and a verification plan","text":"7. Jerke, Lienig and Freuer: constraints require a lifecycle and a verification plan Source finding. The chapter separates technology, functional/electrical, methodological/geometric and commercial constraints. A required objective is a constraint; an optional “constraint” is an objective. It identifies five necessary services: management, derivation, transformation, sensitivity analysis and verification. DRC/LVS do not verify all analog intent, and a constraint that cannot be traced across hierarchy may be silently lost. PDF pp. 281–287 A higher-level limit can admit several transformations. An IR-drop bound can be met by changing placement distance, layer, width or routing topology. Fixing one transformation too early discards alternatives; a generated bound therefore needs its parent relation and validity assumptions. Constraint sensitivity also depends on the current design: a width change may matter greatly for a narrow wire and little after widening. Derivatives of quantities with unlike units require normalization before ranking. PDF pp. 288–291 Action and reason. Extend requirement metadata with stable identity, source, parent specification, derived-from links, units, scope, assumptions, enforcement tier, calibration/version and verification method. Keep the original electrical constraint even after deriving a cheap geometric proxy. This makes failures explainable and allows the engine to revise allocations without discarding the actual requirement. Tradeoff and validation. More metadata and dependency tracking create engineering cost, but they prevent stale decisions after hierarchy collapse, variant changes or technology updates. Test rename/split/merge transformations, inherited shield constraints and conflicting spacing/symmetry requirements. Requirements currently identifies priced batches by kind and ordinal according to its lines 15–17 contract; persistent provenance should not accidentally depend on reorderable list position. The proposed constraint-engineering system uses logic rules, specialized solvers and adapters to external tools. Its lasting idea is decomposition: a high-level requirement selects relevant objects, obtains electrical/geometric facts, and evaluates the necessary relation. Philis does not need to adopt Prolog to preserve that architecture. Cheap structural failures can precede expensive simulation; static final verification must use a frozen design/constraint snapshot; dynamic optimization checks need cache invalidation after relevant changes. PDF pp. 291–298 Action and reason. Produce a coverage report for final verification: which constraints were checked, against which model, which were unsupported, and which depended on uncalibrated proxies. Diagnose conflicting subsets where possible. This distinguishes “all implemented checks passed” from “all intended circuit requirements passed.” Validation. Inject a missing model, a stale geometry cache, an inconsistent pair of constraints and an unsupported inherited requirement; each must produce an explicit result. Do not automatically relax true technology or user requirements to make the optimizer finish. Relax a derived proxy only by revisiting its valid transformation and rechecking its parent requirement.","url":"topological.html#constraint-system"},{"title":"Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches · Practical order of adoption","text":"Practical order of adoption Constraint provenance and verification coverage first: they make every subsequent representation experiment comparable and explainable. Small-group candidate frontiers second: they directly exploit existing recognized blocks without forcing a global algorithm rewrite. Topology-preserving moves and optional topological seeds next: adopt them where measured repair/rejection cost demonstrates a need. Routing feedback and physical connectivity contracts: these connect packing quality to actual circuit feasibility. Template-aware sizing and reuse modes: introduce them after consistent geometry/extraction interfaces exist. The decisive benchmark should report completed, verified routed layouts; specification margins across required conditions; runtime and search variability; and the exact candidate-space restrictions. A representation’s packing speed, or a visually pleasing symmetry pattern, cannot substitute for those outcomes.","url":"topological.html#priority"},{"title":"Coverage · What was read, and what the notes claim","text":"What was read, and what the notes claim The corpus contains 2,710 physical PDF pages. The complete text was extracted for navigation and reading. Every source and every main book chapter is represented, with deeper attention to material applicable to automated analog P&R. The books were not all read line by line: chapter surveys, targeted deep reading and selective visual checks are distinguished in each source companion. No claim is made that every exercise, bibliography entry or repeated image description was independently studied. The Hastings file includes 592 pages of accessible descriptions after its index. These largely describe figures and equations already in the book; the physical page total should not be interpreted as 2,710 pages of independent technical chapters. Source links use physical PDF positions because printed-page offsets vary across the scans. The notes separate source findings, derivations made here, illustrative calculations, current-code observations and proposed extensions. Historical process dimensions and reported benchmark gains are not treated as current PDK rules or measured Philis results. References inside a supplied book are not automatically additional sources reviewed here.","url":"coverage.html#scope"},{"title":"Coverage · How to assess the evidence","text":"How to assess the evidence The subsequent semantic audit checks the notes’ theory and original citation occurrences against the supplied PDFs. It corrects source locations, equations, assumptions and attribution; it does not certify every proposition in every book or review engine implementation. Theory provenance separates source results, our derivations and proposed software policies, with reusable comment references. Technical claims link directly to the relevant supplied PDF pages. Important extraction ambiguities were checked against rendered pages, especially superscripts, formulas, tables and diagrams. The source companions record those checks and apparent source inconsistencies. Equations omitted by extraction were not filled in by guessing. All teaching diagrams are original code-native SVGs. They explain topology, physical mechanisms or mathematical examples; they are not legal PDK layouts, extracted simulation results or reproductions of the books' drawings. Individual SVGs can be downloaded from the chapters. Foundry rule decks, calibrated mismatch/correlation data, compact models, extraction stacks, reliability limits, package models and circuit testbenches remain the process-specific inputs needed to turn this handbook into production constraints. The supplied corpus does not define one universal optimum or establish complete coverage of every modern process.","url":"coverage.html#method"},{"title":"Coverage · Source inventory and reading map","text":"Source inventory and reading map SHA-256 identifies the exact supplied file. Open the companion for chapter/page coverage, model limits and application to the engine. The machine-readable source manifest contains the same identities. The Art of Analog Layout Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024 · 1,568 PDF pages Physical mechanisms, device construction, matching, protection and die assembly. Reading coverage and limits · Supplied PDF Exact file identity The Art of Analog Layout 3ed 2023 -- Ray Alan Hastings -- 3, Hoboken, NJ, 2023 -- Pearson -- isbn13 9780138038397 -- 6bbbd57a3d28d8899c01a9f3ed055000 -- Anna’s Archive.pdf SHA-256: 8f6d5e7f9844f5ec2b8367af801a57cec081cc0dc255e09a37a4f74f04b38b01 Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999 · 185 PDF pages Performance budgets, module generation, placement, routing and defect-aware design. Reading coverage and limits · Supplied PDF Exact file identity Analog Layout Generation for Performance and -- Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen, Willy Sansen (auth_) -- The Springer International Series in -- isbn13 9780792384793 -- 4ebd732bf3deace6ef0efade488ea6ab -- Anna’s Archive.pdf SHA-256: 0261ef97393069806fb14ce6617a47b60b27226f92a8d06c00bff948ab3d0d4e Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological Approaches Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011 · 311 PDF pages Topological placement, hierarchy, routing, retargeting, sizing and constraint management. Reading coverage and limits · Supplied PDF Exact file identity Analog Layout Synthesis : A Survey of Topological Approaches -- Florin Balasa (auth_), Helmut E_ Graeb (eds_) -- 1st ed_ 2011, New York, NY, 2011 -- isbn13 9781282972636 -- a373d8a6c22644f15cf2c13836981ad7 -- Anna’s Archive.pdf SHA-256: bcbf2f98625fe3f77974c3463ceca887d50200128b3b0afbb2f3e8f32dcadb7d Analog Design Centering and Sizing Helmut E. Graeb · 2007 · 210 PDF pages Sensitivity, uncertainty, worst cases, statistical yield and robust optimization. Reading coverage and limits · Supplied PDF Exact file identity Analog Design Centering and Sizing -- Helmut E_ Graeb -- 2007, 2007 -- Springer London, Limited -- isbn13 9781280943683 -- 2c21c02d0baade22d10875dc1e68fe8a -- Anna’s Archive.pdf SHA-256: f6786c92444dce4f6d2bdc3982ea199ae6ed126ded53ecd3a622eaaf25219756 Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook · 194 PDF pages Noise transport, contact networks, isolation, sensitivity and measurement. Reading coverage and limits · Supplied PDF Exact file identity Substrate Noise : Analysis and Optimization for IC Design -- Edoardo Charbon; Ranjit Gharpurey; Paolo Miliozzi; Robert G_ -- 1st ed_ 2001, New York, -- isbn13 9780306481710 -- bb6b7e76395a141e9e9ab9f238fbdb67 -- Anna’s Archive.pdf SHA-256: b2d11867b5cb5b0313c2d52bc7a4e1d5df6aedc035eba1bbd4875161f79721bb Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018 · 171 PDF pages Current density, thermal effects, vias, stress evolution and reliability-aware routing. Reading coverage and limits · Supplied PDF Exact file identity Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit -- Jens Lienig,Matthias Thiele (auth_) -- 1st ed_ 2018, Cham, 2018 -- Springer International -- isbn13 9783030088118 -- 441cc50ea08db83725d64361b9c0688e -- Anna’s Archive.pdf SHA-256: dece9109bf0d2f1f2c5589cc93bffca5363a9d6f3dc1bd4def16e7fa75b2373b Matching properties of MOS transistors Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988 · 4 PDF pages The supplied four-page conference paper on local area and spatial mismatch; not the 1989 JSSC paper. Reading coverage and limits · Supplied PDF Exact file identity Matching_properties_of_MOS_transistors.pdf SHA-256: 90db554da931758a0a570ab5ebfe515b19ed2f4726093de48b980fe157ab601a Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. McAndrew · JSSC 2003 · 7 PDF pages Bias- and geometry-dependent mismatch and the limits of simplified scaling rules. Reading coverage and limits · Supplied PDF Exact file identity Understanding_MOSFET_mismatch_for_analog_design.pdf SHA-256: 7522e7aa51c90edb18dd4e05fe548e56d09b57689b7095fac277ece9a36eed7b Common-Centroid Layout for Active and Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121 · 8 PDF pages Gradient matching versus LDE, random variation and routed electrical performance. Reading coverage and limits · Supplied PDF Exact file identity Common-Centroid_Layout_for_Active_and_Passive_Devices_A_Review_and_the_Road_Ahead.pdf SHA-256: 7a2c661c60de7f7a6aa82ebcb3dcb53e0f891e8580090834ec92ca0f2dce5c07 An Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation Xin Dai, Chengming He, Hanqing Xing, Degang Chen and Randall Geiger · ISCAS 2005 · 4 PDF pages Recursive higher-order cancellation and a directly testable spatial-moment certificate. Reading coverage and limits · Supplied PDF Exact file identity An_N_sup_th__order_central_symmetrical_layout_pattern_for_nonlinear_gradients_cancellation.pdf SHA-256: e725d52e345aeda2b2f13593bc5070b12cfdb6b07775ff2698ef5b595c556476 Constructive Placement and Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795 · 14 PDF pages Spiral/chessboard construction, terminal routing, bridge geometry and matching/settling tradeoffs. Reading coverage and limits · Supplied PDF Exact file identity Constructive_Placement_and_Routing_for_Common-Centroid_Capacitor_Arrays_in_Binary-Weighted_and_Split_DACs.pdf SHA-256: bfa289efdd2d642f2a4993a072b30f4f8b0bf409689fe8d89824c4c7e2165b62 Performance-Driven Analog Layout Automation: Current Status and Future Directions Peng Xu, Jintao Li, Tsung-Yi Ho, Bei Yu and Keren Zhu · ASP-DAC 2024 · 7 PDF pages Electrical feedback, model costs, transfer limits and multiobjective search. Reading coverage and limits · Supplied PDF Exact file identity Performance-Driven_Analog_Layout_Automation_Current_Status_and_Future_Directions_Invited_Paper.pdf SHA-256: f720e36240f8fdf85504169588be8ec550ac9398607170d20ca56cb0e50de902 Performance-driven Wire Sizing for Analog Integrated Circuits Yaguang Li, Yishuang Lin, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind Sharma, Sachin S. Sapatnekar, Ramesh Harjani and Jiang Hu · supplied manuscript (PDF metadata dated 2022) · 27 PDF pages Graph-based prediction and discrete wire sizing with detailed evaluation; manuscript-specific evidence. Reading coverage and limits · Supplied PDF Exact file identity todaes22yl.pdf SHA-256: 9f734d42fa437e7828757c840aa63ae15f83217328ae0b0c64331ca2be507929","url":"coverage.html#inventory"},{"title":"Coverage · Known source boundaries and apparent inconsistencies","text":"Known source boundaries and apparent inconsistencies The supplied Pelgrom item is the four-page ESSCIRC 1988 paper, not the longer 1989 JSSC article. The wire-sizing file is identified as the supplied 27-page manuscript; final-publication metadata or a DOI is not invented. The nth-order paper uses 2 n units per device for its stated construction; text extraction loses that superscript. The DAC paper's N-dependent settling-frequency expression is distinguished from an ordinary first-order −3 dB pole. The performance survey's runtime percentages are internally inconsistent; only the supported qualitative lesson is used. Apparent equation issues in the centering, electromigration and Hastings texts are flagged with corrected derivations or explicit uncertainty in their companions. They are not silently propagated into engine recommendations.","url":"coverage.html#scope-boundaries"},{"title":"Coverage · Repository context and artifact validation","text":"Repository context and artifact validation Code observations reflect commit dad330c42f129cb0bca51427d308745f2d168324 plus the user's existing working-tree changes during this review. The handbook changes only ref/Notes/ ; it does not implement the proposed engine changes. These code observations belong to the earlier authoring snapshot. They were excluded from the subsequent theory/reference audit requested by the user and are not recertified as current implementation facts. The notes include proposed experiments and acceptance criteria. They do not claim that new Philis benchmarks, silicon measurements or source-paper experiments were run. Artifact checks cover local links, PDF-page bounds, HTML structure, SVG validity/accessibility, script syntax and educational moment arithmetic; see the generated validation report . The delivered validator can recheck structural integrity after edits; the JavaScript example checks can be rerun with Node.js. Read the implementation roadmap for observed gaps, dependencies and concrete acceptance evidence. Use the constraint catalog to navigate by the physical obligation rather than by book title.","url":"coverage.html#code"}];
